window.ATLAS_ENTRIES.push(...[{"id": "fm-how-a-paraglider-flies", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 50.5, "lon": 9.94, "name": "How does a paraglider fly with no engine and no frame?", "place": "Explained over the Wasserkuppe, Germany", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A bag of nylon and 300 metres of string becomes a wing the moment air fills it.", "body": ["A paraglider is a ram-air wing. Air enters open cells along the leading edge, pressurises the canopy and gives it an aerofoil shape. The pilot hangs 7 to 10 metres below on lines, and the weight of pilot and harness keeps the lines tight and the wing loaded. There is no rigid structure at all: internal pressure and line tension do the job a spar does on an aeroplane.", "The wing always flies slightly downhill through the air. Gravity pulls it forward and down, the aerofoil converts that forward motion into lift, and the result is a steady glide. A recreational wing sinks at roughly 1.1 metres per second at 36 km/h and glides about 9 metres forward for every metre lost. To climb, the pilot must find air rising faster than the wing sinks.", "The Wasserkuppe in the Rhön hills is where German gliding began in the 1920s and is still home to a gliding school and museum. The same soaring slopes that taught the first sailplane pilots now host paragliders, which is why it is a fitting place to grasp the basic idea: a wing needs no engine, only moving air and a way to stay ahead of its own sink."], "rel": ["fm-lift-and-drag", "fm-angle-of-attack", "fm-glide-ratio", "lilienthal"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}]}, {"id": "fm-lift-and-drag", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.89, "lon": -117.25, "name": "What are lift and drag on a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Torrey Pines, United States", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "At trim speed a good wing makes about nine units of lift for every one of drag.", "body": ["Lift is the force the wing produces at right angles to the airflow. It comes from the curved aerofoil deflecting air downward as the wing moves through it. Drag is the force pulling backward along the airflow, and on a paraglider it is unusually large because the pilot, harness and many metres of exposed line all sit in the wind. Lines and pilot can account for a large share of total drag.", "The ratio between the two decides how far you glide. Flybubble's class comparison puts lift at roughly nine times drag for a mid EN B wing at trim, which is why such a wing glides about 9:1. Drag rises steeply with speed, so pushing the speed bar buys speed at the cost of a steeper glide. Sitting upright in the harness or trailing a loose leg adds drag you will never see on an instrument.", "Torrey Pines, the sea cliff north of San Diego, is one of the oldest soaring sites in the world and a clean laboratory for these forces. The onshore wind is smooth and steady, so a pilot can feel how a little brake raises lift and sink together, and how a slightly faster wing sits further out in front of the cliff."], "rel": ["fm-how-a-paraglider-flies", "fm-glide-ratio", "fm-angle-of-attack"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}, {"t": "NASA Glenn, Inclination effects on lift", "u": "https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/foilinc/"}]}, {"id": "fm-angle-of-attack", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.94, "lon": 6.86, "name": "What is angle of attack and why does every manoeuvre come back to it?", "place": "Explained over Planpraz, Chamonix, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Brakes raise it, speed bar lowers it, and too much of it is a stall.", "body": ["Angle of attack is the angle between the wing's chord line and the direction the air is actually hitting it. Raise it and the wing makes more lift, up to a point. NASA's teaching material notes that for thin aerofoils lift rises roughly in step with angle up to about 10 degrees, after which airflow begins to separate from the upper surface and lift collapses. That separation is a stall.", "On a paraglider the pilot changes angle of attack with the brakes, which pull the trailing edge down, and with the speed bar, which pulls the front risers down and tilts the wing nose-down. Brakes mean higher angle, slower flight and more sink in a turn; speed bar means lower angle, faster flight and a wing closer to a frontal collapse. Gusts change it too, which is why active flying exists.", "Planpraz above Chamonix, at about 2,000 metres under the Aiguilles Rouges, is a launch where pilots feel angle of attack in their hands: a thermal pitching the wing back, then the surge forward as it exits. Understanding that one angle explains stalls, spins, collapses and speed makes every other topic in this section easier."], "rel": ["fm-stall", "fm-brakes", "fm-speed-bar", "fm-active-flying"], "src": [{"t": "NASA Glenn, Inclination effects on lift", "u": "https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/foilinc/"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-glide-ratio", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.79, "lon": 11.72, "name": "What is a glide ratio and how far can a paraglider really go?", "place": "Explained over Bassano del Grappa, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "From 1,000 metres a school wing reaches about 9 kilometres in still air, and real air is never still.", "body": ["Glide ratio is horizontal distance divided by height lost. A ratio of 9:1 means 9 metres forward for each metre down. Recreational wings sit around 9 to 9.5 in still air, EN C and D wings around 10 to 10.5, and competition wings claim 11 or more. Those figures come from manufacturers' best cases; Flybubble notes that in real turbulent air the classes bunch much closer together.", "Glide over the ground is what matters for landing out safely, and it depends on wind. A 9:1 wing flying into a 20 km/h headwind manages only about 4:1 over the ground, while a tailwind stretches it. Pilots use a simple check: pick a point ahead and watch whether it moves up or down in your field of view. If it climbs, you will not reach it.", "Bassano del Grappa, where the Venetian plain meets the pre-Alps, is a classic place to learn this. Launches above Semonzo sit roughly 1,000 metres over a flat landing field, and the long ridge gives pilots hours of practice judging whether the next spur, or the landing, is within glide."], "rel": ["fm-polar-curve", "fm-speed-to-fly", "fm-lift-and-drag"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}, {"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "fm-polar-curve", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.41, "lon": 8.14, "name": "What is a polar curve?", "place": "Explained over Fiesch, Switzerland", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "One curve on a graph tells you the slowest, the flattest and the fastest your wing can fly.", "body": ["A polar curve plots sink rate against airspeed for a given wing and load. The lowest point is minimum sink, the speed to use when climbing in weak lift. A line from the origin that just touches the curve marks best glide, usually close to trim speed with hands up. Beyond that, as the speed bar comes on, sink grows faster than speed and the glide gets steeper.", "Flybubble's estimates for a mid EN B wing give a sink of about 1.1 metres per second at a 36 km/h trim and about 1.9 metres per second at a 48 km/h top speed. Paraglider polars are hard to measure precisely because the wing is soft and air is rarely calm, so treat published curves as a guide, not a promise.", "Fiesch in the Swiss Goms valley is the launch point for long Alpine flights along the Rhône, where the difference between best glide and best speed decides whether you cross the next pass. The polar is the mental model pilots carry when choosing how hard to push."], "rel": ["fm-glide-ratio", "fm-trim-speed", "fm-speed-to-fly"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider weight ranges: the numbers", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-weight-ranges-the-numbers"}]}, {"id": "fm-trim-speed", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.65, "lon": 24.75, "name": "What is trim speed?", "place": "Explained over Sopot, Bulgaria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Hands up, feet off the bar: the speed the designer chose for you.", "body": ["Trim speed is the airspeed a wing settles at with no brake and no speed bar, hands fully up. Manufacturers set it with riser and line lengths so that it sits near best glide in still air. Typical values are 32 to 40 km/h, with most modern EN B wings around 36 km/h. It rises with wing loading: Flybubble's example shows a 36 km/h trim at the bottom of a weight range becoming about 39 km/h at the top.", "Trim matters for safety as much as performance. Flying with a few centimetres of brake held on feels secure but takes the wing slower, closer to stall and further from best glide. Ozone's manuals describe trim as the hands-up position and warn that only a few centimetres of brake can keep a wing in a stall once it is there. In turbulence the right default is near trim, with brakes used for corrections.", "Sopot, under the Balkan range in central Bulgaria, hosts competitions and long ridge runs where pilots spend hours gliding at or near trim between climbs. Lines shrink and stretch with use, so a wing's trim drifts over time; a line check every year or 100 hours keeps it where the designer intended."], "rel": ["fm-polar-curve", "fm-speed-bar", "fm-brakes", "fm-wing-loading"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider weight ranges: the numbers", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-weight-ranges-the-numbers"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-speed-bar", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -30.75, "lon": 150.72, "name": "How does the speed bar work and when should you use it?", "place": "Explained over Manilla, Australia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Push with your feet and the wing goes 10 to 20 km/h faster, but closer to a collapse.", "body": ["The speed bar is a foot stirrup on the harness, linked by lines and pulleys to the front risers. Pushing it shortens the A and B risers, tilting the wing nose-down and lowering the angle of attack. Brakes can only slow a wing; the bar is the only way to fly faster than trim. Full bar is reached when the pulleys meet, adding roughly 12 to 25 km/h depending on class.", "Half bar costs little glide or stability on most wings, and manufacturers say so in their manuals. Full bar is different: the wing is at its lowest angle of attack and a gust can fold the leading edge. Advance, Ozone and others tell pilots to release the bar immediately on any collapse, never to brake while accelerated, and not to use full speed close to the ground or in rough air.", "Use the bar to escape sink, to penetrate a headwind, or to leave a ridge when the wind picks up. Over the flatlands of Manilla in New South Wales, pilots cover long distances by bar-gliding between thermals at 45 to 50 km/h. The habit to build early is feet on the bar during every glide, ready to push or release."], "rel": ["fm-trim-speed", "fm-speed-to-fly", "fm-frontal-collapse", "fm-angle-of-attack"], "src": [{"t": "Advance Iota DLS manual, speed system", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/iota_dls/1127"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}]}, {"id": "fm-brakes", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.83, "lon": 13.21, "name": "What do the brakes actually do on a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Castelluccio di Norcia, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Not really brakes but a way of bending the trailing edge, and too much is a stall.", "body": ["The brake handles pull lines attached along the trailing edge. Pulling one side increases drag and lift on that side, so the wing yaws and rolls into a turn. Pulling both slows the wing, raises the angle of attack and increases sink until, with enough travel, the wing stalls. On an EN A wing the full travel is around 85 centimetres; stall usually arrives somewhere in the last third.", "Manufacturers give useful reference points. About 20 to 30 centimetres of brake gives minimum sink for thermalling. Light, constant pressure of around 20 centimetres is the default for active flying. In a parachutal stall, a few centimetres of brake is enough to hold the wing stalled, which is why the correct answer there is hands up. Starting a turn from deep brake risks a spin.", "Castelluccio di Norcia, on its high plain in the Sibillini mountains, is a gentle slope where schools let pilots play with brake travel in smooth evening air. The lesson pilots get wrong most often is flying permanently braked because it feels safer. It is slower, sinks more and sits nearer the stall."], "rel": ["fm-stall", "fm-angle-of-attack", "fm-weight-shift", "fm-active-flying"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-weight-shift", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.67, "lon": 12.4, "name": "Why do instructors keep saying weight shift first?", "place": "Explained over Kössen, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Leaning in the harness turns the wing without slowing it, which is why it comes before the brake.", "body": ["Weight shift means moving your body sideways in the harness so that more of your weight hangs from one side of the wing. That side flies slightly faster and lower, and the wing banks and turns. Ozone's manuals describe it as the first input for any change of direction, followed by smooth brake to set the bank angle and radius. Unlike brake, it does not raise the angle of attack or add sink.", "The same movement is the core of collapse recovery. When one side folds, the wing turns towards the collapsed side; shifting weight towards the open side, with just enough brake to hold direction, is usually enough for the wing to reinflate on its own. Too much brake on the flying side, which is the common error, can stall the whole wing.", "Kössen in Tyrol, with its grassy launch on the Unterberghorn and wide landing meadows, is a school site where pilots first feel how a loose chest strap lets weight shift work. A chest strap set too tight numbs the input, and manufacturers note it can also make a spiral dive harder to exit."], "rel": ["fm-brakes", "fm-asymmetric-collapse", "fm-thermalling", "fm-spiral-dive"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}]}, {"id": "fm-big-ears", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.77, "lon": 10.8, "name": "What are big ears and why are they not as harmless as they look?", "place": "Explained over Malcesine, Lake Garda, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The beginner's descent method has a hidden trap, and the fix is to keep a foot on the bar.", "body": ["Big ears means pulling the outer A-lines so the wingtips fold under. The wing keeps flying straight at nearly the same airspeed but with less area, so sink rises to about 2.5 to 3.5 metres per second, or 4 to 6 with speed bar added. It is the gentlest way down and the easiest to learn. Pilots use it to lose height, to get out of strong lift, or to push forward from a ridge in rising wind.", "The trap is deep stall. With the tips folded the angle of attack is higher than normal. USHPA's accident review describes pilots who, struggling to reopen the ears, pulled big symmetric brake and dropped into a parachutal stall, then stalled fully. Its advice: keep feet on the speed bar while pulling ears, add a little bar once they are in, clear one ear at a time, and if the wing is not flying normally afterwards, use the bar.", "Ozone adds never to spiral with ears in, because line loads can exceed breaking strength, and not to use ears in rain. Malcesine, on the east shore of Lake Garda below Monte Baldo, is where many pilots first practise this over water during an SIV course, with a boat below and an instructor on the radio."], "rel": ["fm-parachutal-stall", "fm-b-line-stall", "fm-spiral-dive", "fm-siv-courses"], "src": [{"t": "USHPA Accident Review Committee, Big ears", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/arc-big-ears.aspx"}, {"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "fm-b-line-stall", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.96, "lon": 8.47, "name": "What is a B-line stall?", "place": "Explained over Emmetten, Lake Lucerne, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Pull the second riser row and the wing stops flying but keeps its shape, dropping 6 metres a second.", "body": ["Pulling the B-risers down by 15 to 20 centimetres breaks the airflow over the wing. It stops flying forward, stays roughly open overhead, and descends vertically at about 6 metres per second, with some wings reaching 8 to 10. There is little G-force and no turning, which once made it a popular way to escape cloud suck or get down fast. Exit is a smooth, symmetric release of both risers; the wing surges forward and resumes flight.", "The method is now used less. It puts heavy loads on the internal structure and lines, and some wings are slow to leave the stall, hanging in a parachutal state after release. Ozone does not recommend it at all on its two-liner LM7, and manufacturers of A wings describe it as an emergency measure only. If the wing does not fly after release, the answer is hands up, then a touch of speed bar, never brake.", "Emmetten above Lake Lucerne is a well-known SIV venue where this is taught over water. Pilots who learn it there learn the sequence that applies to every descent technique: enough height, a clear airspace below, look up through the exit, and never add brake until the wing is clearly flying again."], "rel": ["fm-big-ears", "fm-spiral-dive", "fm-parachutal-stall", "fm-deep-stall-recovery"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "fm-spiral-dive", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.78, "lon": 6.22, "name": "What is a spiral dive and why is the exit the dangerous part?", "place": "Explained over Doussard, Lake Annecy, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Fastest way down, 15 metres a second at 2 to 3 G, and the exit is the hard part.", "body": ["A spiral dive is a steep, continuous turn where the wing points towards the ground and the pilot swings far out to the side. Sink rates above 8 metres per second are routine and nose-down spirals can pass 20. Skynomad quotes airspeeds up to about 100 km/h and loads of 2 to 3 G, enough to cause tunnel vision or blackout. It is entered by looking into the turn, leaning in, and pulling the inside brake progressively until the wing drops into the dive.", "The exit is where pilots get hurt. Ozone's manuals describe it: shift weight to the outside, release the inside brake slowly, apply outside brake until the wing decelerates, then let it climb and settle. Releasing everything at once produces a violent climb and surge. Some wings, especially at high sink rates, with a tight chest strap, or outside the weight range, stay locked in the spiral and need firm outside brake to come out. Never spiral near the ground.", "Doussard at the south end of Lake Annecy is the landing for the Forclaz launch and a long-standing SIV ground, where pilots learn spirals over deep water. The rule from instructors there is simple: build the spiral in stages, watch the altimeter, and start the exit with plenty of height to spare."], "rel": ["fm-big-ears", "fm-b-line-stall", "fm-wingovers", "annecy"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}]}, {"id": "fm-wingovers", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.76, "lon": 7.63, "name": "What are wingovers and what do they teach?", "place": "Explained over Thun, Lake Thun, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Swinging higher each side of the wing teaches timing, and a mistimed one folds half the glider.", "body": ["A wingover is a series of linked, banked turns where the pilot swings out past the side of the wing, each swing higher than the last. It is built from weight shift and brake applied in rhythm with the pendulum: pull as you rise, release as you drop. Skynomad describes the first stage as a sharp brake and weight shift that turns the wing about 45 degrees, with the reversal timed between the low and high points of the swing.", "Done well it teaches the roll and pitch timing that active flying needs. Done badly it is one of the commonest causes of large asymmetric collapses and cravats, because the outside wing unloads at the top of the swing. Ozone warns that uncoordinated wingovers can fold the wing and must never be performed near the ground. Flybubble suggests at least 200 metres clearance for practice.", "Thun, at the outflow of the lake of the same name, is the scene for many Swiss SIV courses, with pilots towed or launched from the Niesen and Niederhorn to practise over water. Start small, build the amplitude slowly, and stop the moment the wing feels light on the high side."], "rel": ["fm-pitch-and-roll", "fm-asymmetric-collapse", "fm-cravat", "fm-siv-courses"], "src": [{"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}, {"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider control: stall, spin, collapse", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-control-stall-spin-collapse"}]}, {"id": "fm-stall", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.4, "lon": 6.93, "name": "What happens when a paraglider stalls?", "place": "Explained over Villeneuve, Lake Geneva, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Pull both brakes to the bottom for about four seconds and the wing falls behind you.", "body": ["A full stall happens when the angle of attack is so high that airflow separates and the wing stops producing lift. On a paraglider it is reached by holding both brakes deep, roughly four seconds of full brake from trim according to Flybubble. The wing drops back behind the pilot, deflates partly, and the pilot falls at around 8 metres per second under a flapping canopy.", "The danger is not the stall itself but the exit. Releasing the brakes abruptly lets the wing shoot forward and can dive it below the pilot, with a frontal collapse or worse. The taught method is to keep the wing stable overhead with brakes held, then raise the hands symmetrically and steadily and check the surge with a short, dosed brake. Asymmetric release turns a stall into a spin.", "Villeneuve, on the eastern tip of Lake Geneva below the Rochers de Naye, is among the oldest SIV grounds in Europe, and the full stall is taught there as the master key: the recovery for cravats, locked spirals and some deep stalls starts from it. Nobody should try it outside an SIV course with water below and an instructor on the radio."], "rel": ["fm-angle-of-attack", "fm-spin", "fm-parachutal-stall", "fm-siv-courses"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider control: stall, spin, collapse", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-control-stall-spin-collapse"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}, {"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-spin", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.65, "lon": 13.9, "name": "What is a spin and why does it bite thermalling pilots?", "place": "Explained over Gerlitzen, Ossiacher See, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Stall one half of the wing and it pivots round the stalled side, usually while turning slow and flat.", "body": ["A spin, often called a negative spin, is a stall of one side of the wing only. The stalled side loses lift and gains drag, so the wing rotates around it, backwards on the stalled side, while the pilot is flung round in a wide circle. Dennis Pagen's article for USHPA describes it as similar to a spiral in steepness and disorientation, with a sink rate of about 5 metres per second.", "Nearly every inadvertent spin Pagen witnessed came the same way: a pilot thermalling slowly in a flat turn, with deep inside brake, when a gust or an extra pull stalled the inside wing. Recovery is to release the inside brake at once while keeping weight shift. Do not try to slow the outside wing; that can reverse the spin. The warning signs are a sudden loss of brake pressure and the wing starting to rotate towards the turn.", "Gerlitzen above the Ossiacher See in Carinthia is a thermic Alpine site and an SIV venue, where spins are taught over the lake. Ozone's manuals put the rule plainly: never start a turn at minimum speed with the brakes fully applied. Thermal with the wing flying, not hanging."], "rel": ["fm-stall", "fm-thermalling", "fm-brakes", "fm-cravat"], "src": [{"t": "USHPA, Spins by Dennis Pagen", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/spins-hg-pg-article.aspx"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider control: stall, spin, collapse", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-control-stall-spin-collapse"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-asymmetric-collapse", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 4.41, "lon": -76.15, "name": "What is an asymmetric collapse and what should you do?", "place": "Explained over Roldanillo, Colombia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Half the wing folds, the glider turns towards it, and the first job is to fly straight.", "body": ["An asymmetric collapse is when turbulence drops the angle of attack on one side far enough that the leading edge folds under. Anything from a tip to 70 per cent of the span can go. The wing yaws and rolls towards the folded side and the pilot swings out. Certification tests use a 75 per cent collapse along a 45 degree line, and DHV test pilots note that real collapses often feel harder than the test.", "The response in every manual is the same order: keep direction first. Shift weight away from the collapse and apply just enough brake on the flying side to stop the turn, watching for obstacles. On most wings that is enough and the side reinflates by itself. If not, give long, smooth pumps of about one to two seconds on the collapsed side. Too much outside brake stalls the flying half; too fast a pump does nothing.", "Roldanillo in Colombia's Cauca valley hosts world-class competitions in strong, punchy thermals where tip collapses are a daily event. Skynomad suggests practising recoveries with at least 200 metres of ground clearance, and a 50 per cent collapse typically turns the wing about 45 degrees and costs around 50 metres. Collapses near the ground are the ones that injure."], "rel": ["fm-frontal-collapse", "fm-cravat", "fm-active-flying", "fm-weight-shift"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Safety Journal", "u": "https://dhv.de/en/safety/safety-journal"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}]}, {"id": "fm-frontal-collapse", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 19.19, "lon": -100.13, "name": "What is a frontal collapse?", "place": "Explained over Valle de Bravo, Mexico", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The whole leading edge tucks under at once, usually on speed bar, and the fix is mostly patience.", "body": ["A frontal, or symmetric, collapse is the leading edge folding under across the full span. It happens when a gust or sink drops the angle of attack below zero, which is most likely in accelerated flight. The wing stops flying for a moment, the pilot drops, then the canopy reopens with a surge. Manufacturers say most frontals reinflate without input, and that 15 to 20 centimetres of symmetric brake speeds the reopening.", "The sequence that matters is: release the speed bar first, give a short, sharp check on both brakes as the nose tucks, then let the wing surge forward and fly. Flybubble warns that pumping too hard or too long converts a frontal into a full stall, and Ozone tells pilots to confirm the wing has airspeed and is not parachutal before any further input. A frontal with a tip left inside becomes a cravat.", "Valle de Bravo, two hours west of Mexico City, delivers strong winter thermals and long glides on bar between them, exactly where frontals occur. The lesson pilots take home is that the bar is a tool for smooth air, and on a glide through rough air you fly half bar at most with hands ready."], "rel": ["fm-speed-bar", "fm-asymmetric-collapse", "fm-stall", "fm-parachutal-stall"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider control: stall, spin, collapse", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-control-stall-spin-collapse"}]}, {"id": "fm-cravat", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.32, "lon": 12.8, "name": "What is a cravat and how do you clear one?", "place": "Explained over Zell am See, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wingtip caught in its own lines turns a collapse into something that will not reopen on its own.", "body": ["A cravat is a wingtip that has gone through the lines and stayed tangled there after a collapse. The trapped tip acts as a brake on one side, so the wing wants to turn, and if nothing is done the turn tightens into a spiral. Flybubble suggests that with a small cravat, up to about 40 per cent of the wing, a pilot can land safely in stable conditions. Larger ones need action.", "The order in every manual is the same. First stabilise the wing and hold direction with weight shift and opposite brake. Then pull the stabilo line, the outermost line on the tip, which on most wings is colour-coded. If that fails, strong deep pumps on the cravated side while leaning away may free it. A full stall clears big cravats but only with height and SIV practice. If rotation accelerates and cannot be controlled, throw the reserve.", "Zell am See, where the Schmittenhöhe drops to the lake, is a regular SIV venue for Austrian and German schools, and cravats induced on purpose over water are part of the syllabus. Pilots get it wrong by fighting the turn with more and more brake until the flying side stalls; direction first, then the tip."], "rel": ["fm-asymmetric-collapse", "fm-stall", "fm-spiral-dive", "fm-siv-courses"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider control: stall, spin, collapse", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-control-stall-spin-collapse"}]}, {"id": "fm-parachutal-stall", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.28, "lon": 9.41, "name": "What is a parachutal stall?", "place": "Explained over Ebenalp, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": true, "hook": "The wing looks perfect overhead, the air goes quiet, and you are sinking at 6 metres a second.", "body": ["A parachutal stall, also called deep stall, is a state in which the wing stays open and looks normal but has stopped flying forward. Airflow has separated across the whole surface and the canopy descends vertically at around 6 metres per second, much like a round parachute. There is no wind on the face, the brakes feel light, and the vario shows steady sink. It is easy to miss until the ground arrives.", "It follows big ears, a B-line stall, a slow exit from a collapse, or a wing that is old and porous or out of trim. Cold, dense air and flying at the bottom of the weight range make it more likely. The DHV Safety Journal describes an accident on the Ebenalp where a class 1-2 wing sank at about 6 metres per second in a parachutal stall before fully stalling 2 metres above the ground.", "The DHV safety officer's conclusion from that case is the one to remember: do not touch the brakes. A few centimetres of brake in a parachutal stall can tip the wing into a full stall. Hands up, then speed bar or push the A-risers. The Ebenalp above Appenzell, with its cliff-top inn and cable car, is a busy flying site where this lesson was learned the hard way."], "rel": ["fm-deep-stall-recovery", "fm-big-ears", "fm-b-line-stall", "fm-stall"], "src": [{"t": "DHV Safety Journal", "u": "https://dhv.de/en/safety/safety-journal"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider control: stall, spin, collapse", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-control-stall-spin-collapse"}, {"t": "USHPA Accident Review Committee, Big ears", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/arc-big-ears.aspx"}]}, {"id": "fm-deep-stall-recovery", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.25, "lon": 13.58, "name": "How do you recover from a deep stall?", "place": "Explained over Kobarid, Slovenia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Hands all the way up, count a few seconds, then push the bar: brake is the wrong reflex here.", "body": ["Every manufacturer's manual gives the same three steps. First, raise both hands fully so the trailing edge is completely released; most wings recover on their own within a few seconds. Second, if nothing happens, lower the angle of attack: push the speed bar, or push the A-risers forward. Third, confirm the wing has airspeed, feel the wind on your face and see the brakes load up, before using them again.", "What pilots get wrong is the reflex to brake. A deep stall feels like sinking, sinking feels like the wing needs more lift, and more brake is how you get lift in normal flight. In a deep stall it does the opposite and the wing falls back into a full stall. The other error is asymmetric input, which turns the stall into a spin. Keep the hands level and together.", "If the wing is low, say under 100 metres, and still not flying, the reserve is the answer; a deep stall at 6 metres per second is a survivable landing only on soft ground with a perfect roll. Kobarid in the Soča valley, with its Stol and Kobala launches, is a site with long, smooth evening glides where pilots on SIV-style coaching practise the hands-up discipline."], "rel": ["fm-parachutal-stall", "fm-speed-bar", "fm-stall", "fm-siv-courses"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Ozone LM7 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2019/12/LM7-manual-EN-v1.0.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA Accident Review Committee, Big ears", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/arc-big-ears.aspx"}]}, {"id": "fm-wing-loading", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.3, "lon": 5.88, "name": "What is wing loading and where in the weight range should you fly?", "place": "Explained over Saint-Hilaire-du-Touvet, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": true, "hook": "Twenty kilos of difference changes trim speed by about 10 per cent and the whole feel of the wing.", "body": ["Wing loading is the all-up weight, pilot plus harness, reserve, clothing and wing, divided by the wing's area. Every certified wing has a weight range, typically 20 kilograms wide, such as 75 to 95 or 90 to 110. Outside that range the certification no longer applies, and in a competition a weigh-in would disqualify you. The first step is to weigh yourself with everything you fly with, including water.", "Flybubble's worked example shows what the range does. A wing with 36 km/h trim at the bottom of the range flies at about 39 km/h at the top, with sink rising from about 1.1 to 1.2 metres per second; the still-air glide angle stays the same. Heavier loading makes the wing more responsive and more pressurised, and also quicker and sharper when something goes wrong. Advance's manuals say higher loading means more demanding handling.", "The usual advice is the middle to upper third for mountain sites with strong climbs, and the middle for weak flatland lift. Saint-Hilaire-du-Touvet above Grenoble, home of the Coupe Icare each September, is where many pilots first see the competition weigh-in and realise their winter kit has moved them up a size."], "rel": ["fm-trim-speed", "fm-aspect-ratio", "fm-polar-curve"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, Paraglider weight ranges: the numbers", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/paraglider-weight-ranges-the-numbers"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-aspect-ratio", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.32, "lon": 5.72, "name": "What does aspect ratio tell you about a wing?", "place": "Explained over Laragne, Chabre, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A long thin wing glides further and folds more dramatically; 4.5 is a mountain wing, 6 is a two-liner.", "body": ["Aspect ratio is span squared divided by area: a measure of how long and narrow the wing is. Flat aspect ratio counts the wing laid out on the ground; projected ratio counts the arc as it flies, and is lower. Nova lists its wings from 4.5 for a lightweight hike-and-fly model, 5.2 for an EN B, 5.5 for a high B, to 6.1 for its two-liners. Competition wings go higher still.", "Higher aspect ratio reduces drag from the wingtips and improves glide, which is why performance wings are long and thin. The price is behaviour when things go wrong. A narrow wing twists more easily, collapses travel further along the span, and the long tip is more likely to tangle in the lines and cravat. Nova's design note sums up the trade-off as as much as necessary, as little as possible.", "Chabre above Laragne-Montéglin in the southern French Alps hosts Paragliding World Cup and British championship tasks, where the high-aspect wings earn their keep on long glides. For most pilots a moderate ratio and a wing that stays solid in rough air is worth more than a tenth of a point of glide."], "rel": ["fm-cell-design", "fm-glide-ratio", "fm-cravat", "fm-wing-loading"], "src": [{"t": "Nova, Low aspect ratio", "u": "https://www.nova.eu/fr/innovation/low-aspect-ratio"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-cell-design", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 43.97, "lon": 6.51, "name": "Why do paragliders have cells, and what is a shark nose?", "place": "Explained over Saint-André-les-Alpes, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": true, "hook": "Thirty to seventy fabric pockets, some sealed, hold the pressure that makes cloth behave like a wing.", "body": ["A paraglider canopy is a top skin and a bottom skin joined by vertical ribs, which divide it into cells. Each cell has an opening at the leading edge where ram air enters and pressurises it. Cross-ports in the ribs let air flow between cells so pressure evens out and a collapsed section reinflates. A school wing like Ozone's Atom 3 has 31 cells; competition wings have far more, giving a smoother surface at the cost of weight and complexity.", "Since the 1990s designers have closed some cells near the tips for a cleaner profile and added rods, usually nylon, along the leading edge to hold the opening's shape. The shark nose, introduced by Ozone around 2011, moves the cell openings back under a slightly drooped nose, so internal pressure stays high across a wider range of angles of attack. The result is a wing that resists collapse better on speed bar and recovers more cleanly from deep stall.", "Saint-André-les-Alpes, on the Lac de Castillon in Haute-Provence, is a long-standing competition and test-flying ground where designers from the south of France fly prototypes in reliable thermals. Watching a wing inflate on launch there shows the whole system: cells filling, pressure building, and only then a shape that will fly."], "rel": ["fm-aspect-ratio", "fm-lines-and-risers", "fm-how-a-paraglider-flies"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-lines-and-risers", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 43.72, "lon": 6.98, "name": "What do the lines and risers do?", "place": "Explained over Gourdon, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A line less than a millimetre thick holds 56 kilograms, and the whole set holds you.", "body": ["Lines connect the canopy to the risers, the short webbing straps that clip to the harness. They are arranged in rows: A lines to the leading edge, then B, C and sometimes D towards the trailing edge, with brake lines running to the back. The rows let the pilot act on different parts of the wing: A risers for launching and big ears, B risers for a B-line stall, C risers for steering on a two-liner, and brakes for everyday control.", "Lines are made from ultra-high-molecular-weight polyethylene or aramid, usually sheathed near the risers and bare higher up to save drag. The thinnest common line, 0.66 millimetres across, breaks at about 56 kilograms force, and there are dozens of them. Lines shrink or stretch with use and UV exposure, which changes trim; manufacturers and federations recommend a line-length check at set intervals, and any change of line lengths, risers or maillons voids certification.", "Gourdon, a hilltop village above the Côte d'Azur with a launch at around 760 metres looking down to the sea, is a few kilometres from Ozone's base in the hills behind Nice and a regular test site. The pre-flight line check there is the same as anywhere: lines clear and untangled, risers straight, brake lines free to the trailing edge."], "rel": ["fm-cell-design", "fm-brakes", "fm-big-ears", "fm-trim-speed"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "Advance Iota DLS manual", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/iota_dls/1127"}]}, {"id": "fm-ground-handling", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.59, "lon": -1.21, "name": "Why is ground handling the skill that matters most?", "place": "Explained on the Dune du Pilat, France", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "Pilots who can hold a wing still in a 25 km/h breeze rarely have launch accidents.", "body": ["Ground handling is controlling the inflated wing while standing on the ground: bringing it up, holding it overhead, steering it with brakes and weight, and bringing it down. It teaches the feel of the wing through the harness, which is the same feel active flying relies on in the air. Most launch and landing accidents come from pilots who never learned to stop a wing that is pulling them where they do not want to go.", "The classic lesson is on a smooth, open slope in a steady wind of 10 to 25 km/h, with nothing hard to be dragged into. Practise facing the wing with A risers in one hand and brakes in the other, then switching to forward position, then back. Learn to kill the wing with the rear risers or C lines when it overpowers you. Helmet and gloves on, even for a session on the ground.", "The Dune du Pilat near Arcachon, Europe's tallest sand dune at about 106 metres and 2.7 kilometres long, faces the Atlantic sea breeze and is where French pilots go to practise for days on end. Sand is soft to fall on, the wind is laminar, and the dune is so well known for soaring that tandem schools operate there all summer."], "rel": ["fm-kiting", "fm-reverse-launch", "fm-forward-launch", "fm-active-flying"], "src": [{"t": "Dune of Pilat, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dune_of_Pilat"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-forward-launch", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 28.22, "lon": 83.98, "name": "How does a forward launch work?", "place": "Explained over Sarangkot, Pokhara, Nepal", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Face downhill, lean into the harness, and the lines should tighten within two steps.", "body": ["The forward, or alpine, launch is used in nil to light wind. The pilot lays the wing out in an arc, faces downhill with the A risers in hand, and runs. Ozone's manual says the lines should come tight within one or two steps; the wing then rises overhead as the pilot keeps a steady pull through the harness, not the arms. Once the wing is above, check it with a glance, dab the brakes if it overshoots, and keep running until the ground drops away.", "The common errors are pulling the A risers down instead of letting the body do the work, which stalls the leading edge; stopping to look up, which lets the wing fall back; and sitting down too early. If the wing comes up crooked, correct with a step under the high side and a little brake on the opposite side, or abort while you still can. A launch that feels wrong is a launch to stop.", "Sarangkot, the ridge above Pokhara at about 1,600 metres, is one of the busiest launches on earth, with hundreds of tandems a day in light morning air. Most take off with forward launches, and watching a good one there, three steps and a clean inflation, is the best demonstration a new pilot could ask for."], "rel": ["fm-reverse-launch", "fm-ground-handling", "pokhara", "fm-angle-of-attack"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "fm-reverse-launch", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.67, "lon": -122.49, "name": "When do you use a reverse launch?", "place": "Explained over Mussel Rock, Pacifica, United States", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "In a stiff breeze you face the wing, bring it up, and turn only when it is steady overhead.", "body": ["A reverse launch is used in light to strong wind. The pilot clips in facing the wing, with risers crossed, and lets the wind inflate it. Because you can see the canopy, you can correct it while it rises, kill it if a gust takes it, and only turn to face downhill once it is stable above your head. In strong wind Ozone advises taking a few steps towards the wing as it comes up, to soften the inflation.", "Mistakes come from rushing the turn. Turning while the wing is still climbing, or with one side low, leaves the pilot facing the hill with a wing already heading somewhere else. Too much A riser in a strong breeze pulls the wing up fast enough to lift the pilot off the ground before the turn. The fix is to practise on flat ground until the wing comes up and stops, reliably, before any launch is attempted.", "Mussel Rock, the coastal site south of San Francisco, is a wind-driven ridge where the sea breeze arrives at 25 to 35 km/h most afternoons and every launch is a reverse. Pilots there spend their first sessions on the beach learning to keep the wing parked, and only then walk up to the launch."], "rel": ["fm-forward-launch", "fm-ground-handling", "fm-kiting", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "fm-kiting", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -25.9, "lon": 153.09, "name": "What is kiting and how is it different from ground handling?", "place": "Explained on Rainbow Beach, Australia", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "Walking a wing up a dune on the wind, tips overhead, is flying with your feet on the sand.", "body": ["Kiting is keeping the wing flying overhead on the ground for an extended time, steering it, walking with it, and even letting it lift you for a step or two. Where ground handling covers every skill from laying out to killing the wing, kiting is the part where the wing stays up, and it is the best training for the feel of pitch and roll. Coastal pilots kite up to launch rather than carry the wing.", "The method is to stand facing the wing in a steady breeze with brakes in hand, keep the wing centred with small brake inputs and steps sideways, and use the rear risers if it starts to overpower. Good kiting is mostly footwork: move under the wing instead of pulling it over you. Beware dragging in wind over 25 km/h, and never kite near fences, roads or the edge of a cliff.", "Rainbow Beach in Queensland, with its coloured sand cliffs facing the Pacific, is an Australian soaring classic where pilots kite along the beach and up the dunes in the afternoon sea breeze. It shares the character of the Dune du Pilat: soft landing, laminar wind, and a slope that lets you fly a wing without ever quite leaving the ground."], "rel": ["fm-ground-handling", "fm-reverse-launch", "fm-pitch-and-roll"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Soaring and staying up on your paraglider", "u": "https://xcmag.com/other/soaring-and-staying-up-on-your-paraglider/"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-landing-flare", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.68, "lon": 7.86, "name": "How do you time a landing flare?", "place": "Explained over Interlaken, Switzerland", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Hands up to about a metre, then a progressive pull that finishes as your feet touch.", "body": ["The flare converts the wing's speed into a last burst of lift so that you touch down walking rather than running. Ozone's school manual puts it simply: set up early, no tight turns below 30 metres, fly hands up to about 1 metre above the ground, then flare progressively. In nil wind the flare is long and strong; in a good breeze it is only enough to soften the touchdown. If the wing starts to climb, ease the brakes 10 to 20 centimetres.", "Skynomad's analysis gives the timing: pilot and wing each take about a second to respond, so at a 1 metre per second descent you start around 2 metres up, higher if you are coming down faster. The brake pull should finish as you touch. Beginners should start a little higher and pull more slowly to widen the margin. Flaring too early leaves you stalled a few metres up; flaring too late means running.", "Interlaken's Höhematte, the big meadow in the middle of town where tandems from the Beatenberg land all day, is possibly the most-watched landing field in the Alps. It is flat, grassy and often in light wind, which makes it a good place to see a long, committed flare done properly."], "rel": ["fm-wind-gradient", "fm-top-landing", "fm-brakes", "fm-stall"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Paragliding landings", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/paragliding-landings"}]}, {"id": "fm-top-landing", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 52.52, "lon": -2.86, "name": "How do you top land on a ridge?", "place": "Explained over the Long Mynd, United Kingdom", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Come in low across the slope, never downwind, and stop before the air starts rushing over the back.", "body": ["Top landing means landing on the hill you launched from, in the lift band near the crest. Flybubble's guide describes the approach: lose height out in front, then come in low and across the slope on a soaring pass, drift gently back, and turn into wind at the chosen spot. The wing should stay pointed mostly into wind on final. Legs down early, quarter brakes to hold a slow steady approach.", "The hazards are all behind the crest. Wind accelerates over the top and down the back, and just behind the crest the air is sinking; a pilot who drifts too far back lands hard or is carried into the rotor. Flybubble's author estimates about 10 km/h more wind from the crest fence to the road behind, and advises against staying up on the crest at all when the base wind is 30 km/h or more.", "Mistakes pilots make: approaching downwind with huge ground speed, pulling the slope-side brake during the crossing, and letting the wing drop downwind after landing so it drags them. The Long Mynd in Shropshire, a long heather-topped ridge facing the Welsh wind, is a British top-landing classic where club pilots learn to fly out, come back low and step onto the grass."], "rel": ["fm-ridge-soaring-rules", "fm-rotor", "fm-landing-flare", "fm-ground-handling"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, How to topland safely", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/topland-paraglider"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Soaring and staying up on your paraglider", "u": "https://xcmag.com/other/soaring-and-staying-up-on-your-paraglider/"}]}, {"id": "fm-thermalling", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.47, "lon": -5.33, "name": "How do you core a thermal, and how steep should you bank?", "place": "Explained over Piedrahita, Spain", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Most pilots circle too flat and too wide, and the core climbs away from them.", "body": ["A thermal is a column or bubble of rising air with a strong core and weaker edges. The aim is to circle inside the core at the bank angle that gives the best climb. Enter, count a few seconds into the lift, then turn. Centring is a repeated correction: widen the turn as lift weakens, tighten as it strengthens, and shift the circle towards the strongest part on each orbit. Many pilots use the rule of straightening briefly towards the best lift.", "Bank angle is a trade-off. Steeper gives a tighter circle that fits in a narrow core, but costs sink: at 45 degrees of bank the load is 1.41 G and stall speed is about 20 per cent higher; at 60 degrees the load is 2 G. The gliding article behind these figures notes that newcomers often circle under 30 degrees while believing they are at 40 or more. Thirty to forty-five degrees suits most paraglider thermals.", "Safety in the thermal is about airspeed. Spins happen to pilots turning slow and flat with deep inside brake; keep the wing flying, lead with weight shift, and use the outside brake to control the bank. Piedrahita in the Sierra de Gredos, west of Madrid, has strong, reliable thermals and a long competition history, which is why it draws pilots learning to climb efficiently."], "rel": ["fm-spin", "fm-weight-shift", "fm-speed-to-fly", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "British Gliding Association, Thermalling: secrets of success", "u": "https://www.gliding.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2015/06/Thermalling-secrets-of-success-SG-article.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA, Spins by Dennis Pagen", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/spins-hg-pg-article.aspx"}]}, {"id": "fm-ridge-soaring-rules", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 50.88, "lon": -0.21, "name": "What are the rules of the air for ridge soaring?", "place": "Explained over Devil's Dyke, United Kingdom", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "Ridge on your right has right of way, turns go away from the hill, lowest on final lands first.", "body": ["Ridge soaring means flying back and forth in the band of lift where wind is pushed up a slope. The rules exist because twenty wings share one strip of air. Head-on, both pilots turn right; the pilot with the ridge on their right keeps course and the one with the ridge on the left moves out. Overtake with a wide berth, on either side, and on a busy hill it is often safer to turn back than to pass. All turns are made away from the hill.", "Thermals near the ridge are where rules collide. The first pilot in a thermal sets the direction of turn and everyone joining must circle the same way and give way to those already in it. Flybubble's analysis advises not to start thermalling near the ridge unless you can complete a full 360 in clear air, and soaring pilots should route around circling traffic rather than along the ridge downwind of it.", "Landing: a wing on final has right of way over anything on the ground, and the lowest wing on final goes first, provided it does not cut in. Devil's Dyke on the South Downs, a short drive from Brighton, is one of Britain's busiest soaring sites and the place where these rules are tested every windy weekend. Every pilot has the duty to avoid a collision, regardless of who has priority."], "rel": ["fm-top-landing", "fm-thermalling", "fm-rotor", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Irish Hang Gliding and Paragliding Association, Rules of the air", "u": "https://ihpa.ie/index.php/home/flight-safety/rules-of-the-air"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Traffic rules: thermalers vs soarers", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/traffic-rules-thermalers-vs-soarers"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Soaring and staying up on your paraglider", "u": "https://xcmag.com/other/soaring-and-staying-up-on-your-paraglider/"}]}, {"id": "fm-speed-to-fly", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -4.97, "lon": -39.02, "name": "What is speed to fly and what did MacCready work out?", "place": "Explained over Quixadá, Brazil", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "The pilot who glides worse but arrives at the next thermal first often ends up higher.", "body": ["Speed to fly is the airspeed that gets you furthest, or there fastest, given the air you are in. The basic rule: fly at trim in still air, speed up in sink and headwind, slow to minimum sink in lift. Flybubble's figures for a mid EN B wing put the speed bar threshold at about 1.3 metres per second of sink or a 20 km/h headwind; for an EN C wing the thresholds are lower, 0.7 metres per second and 15 km/h.", "Paul MacCready, the American aeronautical engineer, added the insight that the climb you expect next also sets your glide speed. If the next thermal is strong, fly faster between climbs and accept a steeper glide, because the time saved is worth more than the height lost. Flybubble's example: a pilot gliding at 48 km/h loses more height than one at 36 km/h but reaches a 5 metre per second climb sooner and finishes about 100 metres higher.", "The catch, which Flybubble and glider pilots repeat, is that MacCready assumes you reach the next thermal. Set the ring too high and the ground arrives first. Quixadá in Ceará, with its tailwind convergence and long cloud streets, is where world distance records of over 500 kilometres have been flown, and where the balance between pushing on and staying up is decided every afternoon."], "rel": ["fm-polar-curve", "fm-glide-ratio", "fm-speed-bar", "fm-dolphin-flying"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}, {"t": "Chess in the Air, Misapply MacCready theory at your own peril", "u": "https://chessintheair.com/this-brilliant-man-can-get-you-in-trouble-misapply-maccready-theory-at-your-own-peril/"}]}, {"id": "fm-dolphin-flying", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -35.98, "lon": 142.91, "name": "What is dolphin flying?", "place": "Explained over Birchip, Australia", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": true, "hook": "Under a cloud street you stop circling, slow in lift and speed in sink, and never turn at all.", "body": ["Dolphin flying is cross-country flying without circling. Under a line of lift such as a cloud street or a convergence, the pilot flies straight, pulling the brakes to minimum sink in rising air and pushing the bar in sinking air so that the net height stays roughly level. The name comes from the porpoising flight path. Cross Country magazine's cloud street guide notes that it takes a lot of practice to judge the speed changes.", "Two refinements matter. Near cloudbase, speed up and let the sink rate rise so you are not pulled into cloud; lower down, slow to minimum sink to make the most of the lift. The aim is to stay in the strongest band, usually just under base. Cloud streets form when wind direction holds steady with height and are spaced at roughly 2.5 to 3 times the height of the cloud tops, so with tops at 3,000 metres the next street is 7 to 9 kilometres away.", "Birchip in the Mallee of north-west Victoria is open wheat country where Australian pilots fly long flatland distances under streets, and where the technique is used for hours at a time. The risk is the obvious one: a pilot who dolphins too fast through a weak patch and finds nothing beyond it lands in a paddock. Keep a landing in reach."], "rel": ["fm-speed-to-fly", "fm-thermalling", "k-cloudbase", "fm-speed-bar"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Thermal flying part 3: cloud streets over flatlands", "u": "https://xcmag.com/fly-better/paragliding-techniques-paramotoring-skills/thermal-flying-part-3-cloud-streets-over-flatlands/"}, {"t": "Flybubble, Speed to fly basics", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/speed-to-fly-basics"}]}, {"id": "fm-wind-gradient", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.48, "lon": -111.9, "name": "What is wind gradient and why does it catch pilots on final?", "place": "Explained over Point of the Mountain, Utah, United States", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "The wind drops in the last 20 metres, and so does your airspeed, right where height is gone.", "body": ["Wind gradient is the slowing of wind close to the ground as friction with the surface drags on the air. A pilot descending into wind at a steady airspeed is flying into air that moves more slowly with every metre lost. Groundspeed stays the same for a moment, airspeed falls, and the wing sinks and nears the stall. Behind trees, buildings or hedges the gradient is sharper and the air behind them may be turbulent as well.", "The safe answer is to fly final with hands up, at trim or close to it, so the wing carries speed through the gradient, and to keep the last turn high. Skynomad's landing guide warns against slow flight and sharp turns near upwind vegetation, and against playing with the stall in gusts or sink. The error pilots make is to hold brake on final because the ground looks fast, then flare from a wing that has already lost its energy.", "Point of the Mountain, the gravel ridge south of Salt Lake City, has a north side flown in the morning and a south side in the afternoon in reliable wind, with a wide, flat landing below. It is a training site for exactly this: the wind at 100 metres is clearly stronger than at the ground, and the gradient is felt on every landing."], "rel": ["fm-landing-flare", "fm-rotor", "fm-stall", "fm-trim-speed"], "src": [{"t": "Skynomad, Paragliding landings", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/paragliding-landings"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-rotor", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 28.13, "lon": -16.72, "name": "What is rotor and how far behind a hill does it reach?", "place": "Explained over Ifonche, Tenerife, Spain", "icon": "wave", "hidden": true, "hook": "Air pouring over a ridge rolls over on the lee side, and a wing in it can fold completely.", "body": ["Rotor is the turbulent, tumbling air on the downwind side of a hill, ridge, building or tree line. Wind flowing over the obstacle separates from it and curls back towards the ground in a horizontal roll, so a pilot in it meets rapid changes in direction and strength, often with air moving down. The Cumbria Soaring Club's mountain guide gives the physics: double the wind speed and the energy in the air quadruples.", "Bruce Goldsmith, writing in Cross Country, puts the limits in numbers: lee-side flying in a 5 km/h wind is little problem, and above 20 km/h it is likely to be extremely hazardous. Larger hills give more shelter, and the edge of the protected lee is often the worst place of all. A sudden change of groundspeed is the warning sign; the right response is to leave at once, into wind and away from the slope.", "Pilots get it wrong by drifting back over launch in rising wind, by landing just behind a crest, or by probing the lee of a spur for a thermal. Ifonche in southern Tenerife is a famous lee-side site that works because the island shelters it from a trade wind of up to 50 km/h, with the sea breeze smoothing the air below. It is the exception that proves the rule, and local guidance is needed to fly it."], "rel": ["fm-top-landing", "fm-turbulence-response", "fm-wind-gradient", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Bruce Goldsmith: Flying in the lee", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=149"}, {"t": "Cumbria Soaring Club, Mountain flying", "u": "https://cumbriasoaringclub.co.uk/kb/mountainfly.php"}, {"t": "Flybubble, How to topland safely", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/topland-paraglider"}]}, {"id": "fm-turbulence-response", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.05, "lon": 76.72, "name": "What should you do when you hit turbulence?", "place": "Explained over Bir Billing, India", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Keep the wing overhead, keep it flying, and resist the urge to pull a lot of brake.", "body": ["Turbulence is air changing speed or direction over short distances: the edges of thermals, rotor behind terrain, shear between wind layers, or wake from another wing. On a paraglider it shows as the wing pitching forward or back, rolling, or going soft on one side. Skynomad's active flying mantra covers the whole response: stop the dive, let it fly, keep direction.", "In practice that means light contact on both brakes, about 20 centimetres, so you feel pressure changes; a short, firm check when the wing surges ahead, released immediately; hands up when the wing drops behind; weight shift to the loaded side if one half goes light. Skynomad is blunt that too much brake is worse than none, since the common accident chain is turbulence, big brake, stall or spin. Slow down in strong turbulence by coming off bar, not by braking deep.", "If the air is bad enough that you are scared, head for smoother air: out from the slope, away from the lee, towards the landing field while you still have height. Bir Billing in Himachal Pradesh is famous for its long autumn flights and equally for rough midday air over the lower ridges, which is why pilots there launch early or wait for the afternoon to settle."], "rel": ["fm-active-flying", "fm-pitch-and-roll", "fm-asymmetric-collapse", "bir-billing"], "src": [{"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}, {"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}]}, {"id": "fm-pitch-and-roll", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.21, "lon": 12.79, "name": "How do you control pitch and roll on a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Meduno, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "You hang 7 metres below the wing like a pendulum, so timing beats strength every time.", "body": ["Pitch is the wing moving forward or back relative to the pilot; roll is it tipping side to side. Because the pilot hangs 7 to 10 metres below, the system is a pendulum, and every input arrives with a delay. Cross Country's pitching exercise shows the principle: in calm air with 200 metres of clearance, pull 20 centimetres of brake, release so the wing surges, then reapply as you swing under it. Starting and stopping the swing is all about brake timing.", "Pitch control in the real world is damping the surge after a thermal exit or a collapse. Brake as the wing dives ahead, release as it comes back overhead; hold the brake too long and you stall it as it pitches back. Never let the wing surge lower than about 45 degrees to the horizon in practice. Roll is controlled mainly with weight shift towards the loaded side, plus a short opposite-brake impulse to stop the swing.", "Meduno in Friuli, with its long ridge above the Tagliamento plain, is a smooth-air soaring site where Italian schools run pilotage courses on exactly this: pitch games, roll reversals, then linked turns. The pilot who can feel the pendulum and act a half-beat early is the one whose wing stays open in rough air."], "rel": ["fm-active-flying", "fm-wingovers", "fm-turbulence-response", "fm-brakes"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Pitch control", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=32209"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}]}, {"id": "fm-active-flying", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -18.85, "lon": -41.95, "name": "What is active flying?", "place": "Explained over Governador Valadares, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Keeping the wing overhead with small, constant corrections prevents most collapses before they start.", "body": ["Active flying is the continuous use of brakes and weight shift to keep the wing centred overhead and the lines loaded, so that turbulence changes the wing's angle of attack less than it would if the pilot sat still. Ozone's school manual sets the baseline: a light, constant brake pressure of around 20 centimetres, adjusted as needed, and no continuous deep brake in rough air. Your hands follow the pressure, not the other way round.", "The three habits, in Skynomad's words, are to stop the dive, let it fly and keep direction. When the wing surges ahead, check it; when it falls back, hands up; when one side goes light, lean to the heavy side and add a touch of brake on the light side. The pilot can detect pitch changes of 2 to 3 degrees through the brakes long before anything is visible, which is the whole point.", "The common error is over-controlling: big, slow pulls that leave the wing permanently braked and near the stall. Governador Valadares in Minas Gerais, with its Ibituruna launch at about 1,100 metres and strong tropical thermals, is a Brazilian cross-country classic where pilots fly five and six hours a day. Hands that stay light for that long are hands that are doing it right."], "rel": ["fm-pitch-and-roll", "fm-turbulence-response", "fm-asymmetric-collapse", "fm-brakes"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Atom 3 manual", "u": "https://cdn1.flyozone.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/1/2018/07/Atom-3-manual-EN.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad, Active flying", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/active-flying"}]}, {"id": "fm-siv-courses", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 36.55, "lon": 29.12, "name": "What is an SIV course and who should do one?", "place": "Explained over Oludeniz, Turkey", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Over water, on the radio, you fold, stall and spin your wing on purpose before the real one.", "body": ["SIV stands for Simulation d'Incidents en Vol, in-flight incident simulation. A course puts a pilot high over a lake or the sea, with a rescue boat below and an instructor on the radio, and works through collapses, big ears, B-line stalls, spirals, wingovers, and on later days full stalls, spins and cravat recovery. The point is that a pilot's first collapse should happen in a controlled setting, not at 50 metres over rocks.", "Most federations recommend SIV before moving up a class, and manufacturers of C and D wings say so in their manuals; Canada's HPAC makes a full course a prerequisite for its P4 rating. Courses typically run three to five days with a handful of flights each and small groups. Pilots should arrive with a current wing and reserve, a life jacket, and the honesty to say what they are not ready for. Manoeuvres are escalated one step at a time.", "Oludeniz on Turkey's Lycian coast is probably the best-known SIV ground in the world: a drive up Babadağ to launches at 1,700 metres or more, then a flight zone over the Mediterranean with warm water below. Lake Annecy, Lake Garda, Lake Thun and Villeneuve on Lake Geneva do the same job in the Alps. SIV teaches recovery, but its real lesson is how much height every recovery costs."], "rel": ["fm-asymmetric-collapse", "fm-stall", "fm-spiral-dive", "oludeniz"], "src": [{"t": "HPAC, SIV (Simulation d'Incidents en Vol)", "u": "https://hpac.ca/?p=653"}, {"t": "Jocky Sanderson, SIV courses Oludeniz", "u": "https://www.jockysanderson.com/pages/v2_siv"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Guide to Lake Garda", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-lake-garda-italy/"}]}, {"id": "wx-lapse-rate", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -3.07, "lon": 37.35, "name": "What is the lapse rate?", "place": "Explained over Kilimanjaro, Tanzania", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "The air cools as you climb, but how fast it cools decides whether you climb at all.", "body": ["Lapse rate is simply how quickly the air gets colder with height. In the standard atmosphere it falls about 6.5 degrees for every 1,000 metres. That is why Kilimanjaro, rising nearly 5,900 metres from the hot Tanzanian plains, can carry ice near the top while the base sits in heat.", "A parcel of dry air rising on its own cools faster, at about 9.8 degrees per 1,000 metres, because it expands as pressure drops. Once it reaches its dew point and cloud forms, condensation releases heat and the cooling slows, to a typical 5 degrees per 1,000 metres, with a range of roughly 3.6 to 9.2 depending on temperature.", "Pilots care because the gap between the real air and the rising parcel is the lift. If the surrounding air cools faster than 9.8 per kilometre, every warm bubble keeps rising and thermals are strong. If it cools slower than the saturated rate, a rising parcel soon finds itself colder than its surroundings and stops. That is a stable day, and a stable day is a sled ride."], "rel": ["wx-stability", "wx-inversion", "wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Lapse rate (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}, {"t": "The Skew-T log-P diagram (NWS)", "u": "https://www.weather.gov/media/ohx/PDF/TheskewTlogPdiagram.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-inversion", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.19, "lon": 5.72, "name": "What is a temperature inversion?", "place": "Explained over Grenoble, France", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "A lid of warm air that caps thermals, traps fog, and ends more flying days than rain.", "body": ["Normally the air gets colder as you climb. An inversion is a layer where it gets warmer instead. The Met Office describes it as a thin layer in the lower atmosphere where temperature rises with height, usually under high pressure when air from above sinks, dries and warms. That warm layer floats on the cooler air below and acts as a lid.", "Grenoble, in a bowl of mountains, shows what the lid does. In winter the valley fills with cold, murky air and fog while the summits above sit in sunshine. Walk up the Bastille hill and you can step out of the grey into warm, clear air within a few hundred metres.", "For pilots the inversion sets the day's ceiling. Thermals rise until they meet it, then stop, so the height of the inversion is the top of the lift. A low inversion in the morning often burns off by early afternoon as the ground heats. A strong one does not, and the day stays flat with hazy air underneath. Soundings and forecast sites show it as a kink in the temperature line.", "The trap is thinking a blue sky means a good day. Under a strong inversion the sky is often clear because nothing can rise high enough to make cloud. The air may look perfect and still give nothing above 300 metres."], "rel": ["wx-lapse-rate", "wx-stability", "wx-sounding-skew-t", "wx-high-pressure"], "src": [{"t": "Temperature inversion (Met Office)", "u": "https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/types-of-weather/temperature/temperature-inversion"}, {"t": "Lapse rate (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}]}, {"id": "wx-stability", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.68, "lon": 7.86, "name": "What makes the air stable or unstable?", "place": "Explained over Interlaken, Switzerland", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Push a bubble of air upward: does it keep going, or sink back? That answers everything.", "body": ["Imagine lifting a bubble of air a little way. If it cools faster than the air around it, it ends up heavier and sinks back. That is stable air. If it stays warmer than its surroundings, it keeps rising. That is unstable air, and it is what every thermal pilot is hoping for.", "Meteorologists put numbers on this. Air is absolutely stable when the real atmosphere cools slower with height than a cloudy parcel would, roughly less than 5 degrees per 1,000 metres. It is absolutely unstable when the atmosphere cools faster than 9.8 degrees per 1,000 metres. In between lies conditional instability, where dry air is stable but once a bubble makes cloud, the released heat keeps it going.", "Above Interlaken, between the two lakes and under the Eiger, a conditionally unstable spring day looks like neat cumulus with flat bases around 2,500 metres. The same sky on an absolutely unstable afternoon turns to towering congestus by two o'clock, and that is when the sensible pilot is already on the ground.", "Signs of stable air: haze, smooth lift, cloud in flat sheets, smoke drifting sideways. Signs of unstable air: crisp visibility, gusty ground wind, cumulus that keeps growing, smoke going straight up. The forecast will say the same thing in a sounding."], "rel": ["wx-lapse-rate", "wx-inversion", "wx-overdevelopment", "wx-sounding-skew-t"], "src": [{"t": "Lapse rate and stability (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}, {"t": "The Skew-T log-P diagram (NWS)", "u": "https://www.weather.gov/media/ohx/PDF/TheskewTlogPdiagram.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.36, "lon": -118.4, "name": "How do I work out cloudbase from the dew point?", "place": "Explained over Bishop, Owens Valley, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two numbers from the morning forecast tell you how high you can climb today.", "body": ["The dew point is the temperature the air has to cool to before its moisture condenses. A thermal rising from the ground cools as it expands, and when it reaches the dew point a cloud forms. The flat bottom of a cumulus is exactly that height. So the gap between surface temperature and dew point sets cloudbase.", "The rule of thumb: take the temperature minus the dew point in degrees Celsius, divide by 8 and multiply by 1,000. The result is cloudbase in metres above the ground. Roughly 125 metres for every degree of spread. A 30 degree afternoon with a 10 degree dew point gives a 20 degree spread, so about 2,500 metres above the valley floor.", "Owens Valley in California shows why the spread matters. The Sierra Nevada wrings moisture out of the Pacific air, so the valley is dry and the dew point is low. Spreads of 25 degrees or more are common in summer, putting cloudbase well above 3,000 metres over a valley that already sits at 1,200 metres.", "Two cautions. The result is above ground, so add the valley height to compare with your altimeter. And a dry day with a huge spread may have no cloud at all, in which case the thermals are still there but invisible: the blue day."], "rel": ["wx-lapse-rate", "wx-blue-thermals", "wx-cumulus-cycle", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Cloud base (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_base"}, {"t": "Dew point (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dew_point"}]}, {"id": "wx-cumulus-cycle", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.83, "lon": 13.21, "name": "How does a cumulus cloud grow and die?", "place": "Explained over Castelluccio, Italy", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The cloud you are flying towards may already be dying by the time you reach it.", "body": ["A cumulus is the visible top of a thermal. Warm air leaves the ground, rises and cools, and when it reaches its dew point the vapour condenses into droplets. Condensation releases heat, which warms the parcel and drives it higher still. The flat base marks the condensation level, the cauliflower top marks where the rising air is still going.", "Over the Castelluccio plateau in the Sibillini mountains, the cycle runs in plain sight. First wisps appear over the sunlit slopes in the late morning. They sharpen into crisp white puffs, then grow taller and darker underneath. Then the edges go ragged and soft, the base turns grey and lumpy, and the cloud slowly melts away as its thermal dies.", "Meteorologists name the stages. Cumulus humilis is the flat fair-weather puff. Mediocris has some vertical growth. Congestus is the towering cauliflower that can rain and that, with enough instability, becomes a cumulonimbus. For paragliders the sweet spot is the crisp, growing humilis or mediocris.", "The pilot's lesson is to read the edges, not the size. A sharp, hard-edged cloud is being fed and has lift under it. A soft, fraying cloud, however large, is decaying and the air under it is sinking. Fly to the one that is still being built."], "rel": ["wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "wx-overdevelopment", "wx-thermal-triggers", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Cumulus cloud (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulus_cloud"}, {"t": "The ten basic clouds (NOAA JetStream)", "u": "https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/clouds/ten-basic-clouds"}]}, {"id": "wx-overdevelopment", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.4, "lon": 8.13, "name": "What is overdevelopment?", "place": "Explained over Fiesch, Switzerland", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The good clouds keep growing until they join up, and the sky switches off.", "body": ["Overdevelopment, or OD, is when cumulus clouds grow faster and wider than the day can support. Instead of neat separate puffs, the clouds spread, merge and shade the ground. With the sun blocked, the thermals that built them switch off. The sky can go from perfect to dead in half an hour.", "Fiesch in the Swiss Alps, a classic big-distance site, shows the pattern. Moist unstable air arrives, the first clouds pop early, by midday they are congestus with dark bases, and by early afternoon they have spread into a grey lid with showers falling from the heaviest cells. The strongest pilots launch early and are landing while others are still waiting.", "The warning signs are an early start to cloud, bases that look lumpy rather than flat, tops that keep growing rather than levelling off, and clouds that spread sideways and touch. If virga or rain shafts appear under any cloud, the day has gone further than OD and is turning into storm weather.", "What pilots get wrong is waiting for it to clear. Sometimes the sun breaks through, the ground reheats, and a second cycle starts that is even more violent. The safe answer on an OD day is to fly early, stay in reach of a landing, and treat any cloud that is still growing in the afternoon as a storm in the making."], "rel": ["wx-cumulus-cycle", "wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "wx-virga", "wx-stability"], "src": [{"t": "Cumulus cloud (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulus_cloud"}, {"t": "Cumulonimbus cloud (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulonimbus_cloud"}]}, {"id": "wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -30.75, "lon": 150.72, "name": "What is cloud suck?", "place": "Explained over Manilla, Australia", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "In 2007 a paraglider was pulled to nearly 10,000 metres here and lived. Her teammate did not.", "body": ["Cloud suck is lift under a cloud so strong that a paraglider cannot descend out of it. Under a normal cumulus the lift fades near cloudbase. Under a growing congestus or cumulonimbus it does the opposite: it strengthens, because condensation inside the cloud releases heat and feeds the updraft. A glider with a sink rate of 1 metre per second has no answer to lift of 10.", "On 14 February 2007 near Manilla in New South Wales, German pilot Ewa Wisnierska was training for the World Championships when two thunderstorms merged around her. Her GPS logged climbs of up to 21 metres per second and a peak above 10,000 metres, where the air is far below freezing and holds less than half the oxygen of sea level. She passed out, was carried through hail and lightning, and landed about 60 kilometres away, alive. Chinese pilot He Zhongpin, caught in the same system, was killed by lightning.", "The cumulonimbus is the biggest cloud there is. Bases can be as low as 200 metres, tops normally reach 12,000 metres and sometimes 20,000. A single cell lives about 20 to 30 minutes, but the cold outflow from one cell triggers the next, so a storm can last hours.", "The only safe answer is distance. Leave the sky long before the clouds look threatening, and never fly on a day with thunderstorms forecast. Once in strong lift at cloudbase, big ears with speed bar or a spiral can buy a few hundred metres, but the air near a storm can exceed any descent technique a paraglider has."], "rel": ["wx-overdevelopment", "wx-anvil", "wx-gust-front", "wx-hypoxia"], "src": [{"t": "Ewa Wisnierska (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewa_Wi%C5%9Bnierska"}, {"t": "Miracle in the storm (Cumbria Soaring Club)", "u": "https://cumbriasoaringclub.co.uk/kb/miracle.php"}, {"t": "Cumulonimbus cloud (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulonimbus_cloud"}]}, {"id": "wx-gust-front", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.22, "lon": -110.97, "name": "What is a gust front?", "place": "Explained over Tucson, Arizona, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The storm can be 30 kilometres away and still knock you out of the sky.", "body": ["When a thunderstorm matures, rain-cooled air collapses out of it and spreads across the ground like water from a dropped bucket. The leading edge of that cold outflow is the gust front. The US National Weather Service defines it as the leading edge of gusty surface winds from thunderstorm downdrafts, often marked by a shelf cloud or a roll cloud.", "Tucson in the Arizona summer monsoon shows the full performance. A storm builds over the Catalina mountains, the base goes dark, and ten minutes later a wall of dust rolls across the desert from a storm that has not yet dropped a single raindrop on the city. Passage brings a sudden wind shift, a jump in pressure and a sharp drop in temperature.", "Outflow boundaries can travel hundreds of kilometres from the storm that made them and can persist for a day after it has died. Where two boundaries collide, new storms form. For a paraglider the danger is a wind that arrives in seconds, often from the opposite direction, with turbulence along the whole edge.", "The rule is simple: if you can see a cumulonimbus or hear thunder, land now, well before the dust line or shelf cloud reaches you. Pilots get caught because the storm looks far away and the air at launch is still calm. Calm air in front of a storm is the warning, not the reassurance."], "rel": ["wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "wx-anvil", "wx-virga", "wx-lightning-distance"], "src": [{"t": "Gust front (NWS glossary)", "u": "https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=GUST"}, {"t": "Outflow boundary (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Outflow_boundary"}]}, {"id": "wx-lenticular-wave", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -50.34, "lon": -72.26, "name": "What do lenticular clouds tell a pilot?", "place": "Explained over El Calafate, Argentina", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Those smooth flying-saucer clouds mean the whole sky is a standing wave. Gliders have ridden it to 22 kilometres.", "body": ["A lenticular is a lens-shaped cloud that sits still while the wind blows through it. It forms when stable air is pushed over a mountain range and bounces downwind in a series of standing waves, like ripples behind a rock in a stream. At each crest the air cools enough to condense, and the cloud appears, fixed in place, constantly forming on its upwind edge and evaporating on the downwind edge.", "Near El Calafate in Argentine Patagonia the Andes make some of the biggest waves on Earth. On 2 September 2018 the Perlan 2 glider rode them to 22,657 metres, a world altitude record for a glider, higher than any airliner. The pilots were Jim Payne and Tim Gardner.", "Wave needs three things: a wind blowing roughly across the ridge, a wind that strengthens with height, and a stable layer above the ridge top. The cloud may stack in several lenses if moist and dry layers alternate. Where there is wave above, there is almost always rotor below: a turbulent, rolling vortex under the first wave crest.", "For a paraglider the message is the opposite of a sailplane's. Lenticulars mean strong wind aloft and a rotor zone somewhere downwind of every ridge, usually near ridge height, where a wing can be collapsed or thrown. Lenticulars in the morning are a reason to stay on the ground, not a sign of a smooth day."], "rel": ["wx-rotor", "wx-wind-gradient", "wx-stability", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Lee wave (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_wave"}, {"t": "Perlan Project (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perlan_Project"}]}, {"id": "wx-rotor", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.36, "lon": 138.73, "name": "What is rotor?", "place": "Explained over Mount Fuji, Japan", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Rotor broke up a Boeing 707 near Fuji in 1966. A paraglider needs far less.", "body": ["Rotor is the rolling, turbulent air that forms downwind of a hill or mountain when the wind is strong. The wind flows over the crest, then curls back on itself in the lee, like water tumbling behind a weir. Its axis runs parallel to the ridge. Under a mountain wave the rotor sits beneath the first wave crest, roughly at ridge-top height, and is the most violent air in the system.", "On 5 March 1966 BOAC Flight 911, a Boeing 707, broke apart in flight near Mount Fuji in severe mountain wave turbulence. Fuji stands alone, 3,776 metres above the plain, and in a strong northwesterly it sheds rotor and wave for tens of kilometres downwind. Wikipedia's lee wave account lists this crash as a classic example.", "Rotor is often visible as a ragged line of cumulus fractus on the lee side, the roll cloud, with its base near the peaks and its top sometimes merging into the smooth lenticulars above. More often it is invisible. On a paragliding scale, every ridge, every spur and every line of trees makes its own rotor in proportion to the wind.", "The rules: never fly in the lee of a ridge in strong wind, never land behind an obstacle when the wind is more than light, and treat a smooth-looking lenticular sky as a rotor sky. Pilots get hurt in rotor low down on landing approach, not high up where it is first felt."], "rel": ["wx-lenticular-wave", "wx-wind-gradient", "wx-windsock", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Lee wave (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_wave"}]}, {"id": "wx-katabatic", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -67.01, "lon": 142.66, "name": "What is a katabatic wind?", "place": "Explained over Cape Denison, Antarctica", "icon": "wave", "hidden": true, "hook": "Cold air is heavy. On the windiest coast on Earth it simply falls off the ice cap.", "body": ["Katabatic means descending. Air in contact with a cold slope loses heat, becomes denser than the air beside it, and slides downhill under gravity. Every mountain valley makes a small one after sunset: the cool breeze that flows down from the slopes and pools in the valley bottom. On a big scale, whole ice sheets drain cold air to the sea.", "Cape Denison on Commonwealth Bay is the extreme. Douglas Mawson's expedition wintered there in 1912 and recorded an hourly mean of about 149 kilometres per hour on 24 March, with instantaneous gusts estimated near 270. Guinness World Records lists it as the fastest katabatic wind. Mawson called the place the Home of the Blizzard.", "Some famous named winds are katabatic in origin. The Bora of the Adriatic is a fall wind that arrives when a cold air mass is carried over the Dinaric mountains and pours down to the coast. Greenland's piteraq and the williwaws of Tierra del Fuego and Alaska are the same thing, and can exceed 200 kilometres per hour in narrow coastal valleys.", "For paragliders the everyday katabatic matters more than the record. The evening slope wind reverses the thermal flow: a launch that faced into a gentle anabatic breeze all afternoon suddenly has a tailwind, and the landing field in the valley gets a cool downslope flow from the opposite direction. Fly the last flight of the day with that reversal in mind."], "rel": ["wx-anabatic", "wx-bora", "wx-wind-gradient", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Fastest katabatic wind (Guinness World Records)", "u": "https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/404553-fastest-katabatic-wind"}, {"t": "Katabatic wind (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katabatic_wind"}]}, {"id": "wx-anabatic", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.77, "lon": 11.73, "name": "What is an anabatic wind?", "place": "Explained over Bassano del Grappa, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The gentle breeze up a sunny slope is the engine of almost every mountain flying day.", "body": ["Anabatic means ascending. Sun warms a slope, the slope warms the thin layer of air touching it, and that air becomes lighter than air at the same height out over the valley. It rises along the surface, and cooler air from the valley floor flows in underneath to replace it. The result is a breeze blowing up the hill all day while the sun is on it.", "Bassano del Grappa, where the Venetian plain meets the Alps, lives on this breeze. The south-facing slopes above the town warm from mid-morning, the upslope flow sets in, and launches that would be dead in still air fill with a steady headwind. That same flow is what lets Bassano run flying almost year round.", "Anabatic wind is the slope-scale cousin of the valley breeze, which is the larger flow up the whole valley towards the mountains. Both start an hour or two after sunrise, peak in the afternoon and die with the sun. Where the rising slope air reaches its dew point it builds the first cumulus of the day, usually over the highest sunlit ridges.", "The trap is a passing cloud. Shade the slope and the anabatic flow falters within minutes, so a launch wind that felt reliable can drop to nothing or even reverse. DHV's cloud-shadow article notes that thermals then tend to detach at the lower edge of the shadow. Watch the shadow line as much as the windsock."], "rel": ["wx-katabatic", "wx-thermal-triggers", "k-valley-breeze", "wx-windsock"], "src": [{"t": "Anabatic wind (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anabatic_wind"}, {"t": "Wolkenschatten (DHV magazine)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/07_wetter/Wetterwissen/DHVmagazin_Artikel/Wolken/8_2010_165_wolkenschatten.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-convergence", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.8, "lon": -122.3, "name": "What is convergence?", "place": "Explained over Puget Sound, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two winds meet and the air has nowhere to go but up, sometimes for a hundred kilometres.", "body": ["Convergence happens where two flows of air run into each other. Air cannot pile up on the ground, so it rises. That rising air gives lift without any need for a thermal, and if it is moist it makes a line of cloud along the join. On the global scale the trade winds converge near the equator in the Intertropical Convergence Zone, a belt of cloud and rain that circles the planet.", "Puget Sound in Washington State has a named one. Northwesterly wind off the Pacific splits around the Olympic Mountains, flows down the Strait of Juan de Fuca and up through the Chehalis gap, and the two branches meet over the Sound. The Puget Sound Convergence Zone produces a band of showers, or on a good day a line of lift, across the Seattle area while the sky clears on either side.", "Sea-breeze convergence is the version most paraglider pilots use. Where a sea breeze pushes inland and meets the prevailing wind, or where breezes from two coasts meet over a peninsula, a line of cumulus marks the join. Fly along it and you can stay up without turning. The same happens where valley winds from two valleys meet at a ridge.", "Convergence can also be rough. Where the two flows differ in strength the join is turbulent, and strong convergence under an unstable sky can set off a line of thunderstorms. Treat a convergence line as a resource in good air and a trigger line in bad air."], "rel": ["wx-thermal-triggers", "k-sea-breeze", "wx-trade-winds-hawaii", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Convergence zone (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_zone"}]}, {"id": "wx-cold-front", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 55.95, "lon": -3.19, "name": "What happens when a cold front passes?", "place": "Explained over Edinburgh, Scotland", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The best flying day of the week is often the day after the worst one.", "body": ["A front is the boundary between two air masses. At a cold front, dense cold air pushes under warmer air ahead of it and lifts it. The warm air rises fast, so a cold front brings a narrow line of showers or thunderstorms, a sharp wind shift, a drop in temperature and a rising barometer behind it. Cold fronts can move up to twice as fast as warm fronts.", "Over Edinburgh, sitting under the Atlantic storm track, the sequence is familiar. A mild, muggy southwesterly with low cloud, then a band of heavy rain, then within an hour the wind veers to the northwest, the sky clears to bright blue and the temperature falls several degrees. On a weather map the front is the line with blue triangles pointing the way it is moving.", "The day after is often excellent. The fresh polar air behind the front is cold at height, so the lapse rate is steep and the air is unstable: crisp cumulus, clear visibility, strong thermals. The price is wind, which is often strong for a day or two behind the front, and showers that can grow big in the afternoon.", "The risk is flying too close to the front itself. Squalls along the line can bring gusts far above the forecast mean, and the wind direction can swing through 90 degrees in minutes. Let the front go through, let the wind settle, then fly."], "rel": ["wx-warm-front", "wx-low-pressure", "wx-stability", "wx-gust-front"], "src": [{"t": "Weather front (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather_front"}, {"t": "High and low pressure (Met Office)", "u": "https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/how-weather-works/high-and-low-pressure"}, {"t": "Fronten (DHV magazine, Wetterwissen)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/07_wetter/Wetterwissen/DHVmagazin_Artikel/Grundlagen/06_2011_170_fronten.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-warm-front", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 51.9, "lon": -8.47, "name": "Can I fly ahead of a warm front?", "place": "Explained over Cork, Ireland", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The high cloud thickening from the west is a clock, and it is counting down.", "body": ["At a warm front, warm air slides up and over a wedge of colder air ahead of it. The slope is gentle, so the cloud spreads out hundreds of kilometres ahead of the surface front. The sequence is predictable: high wispy cirrus first, then a milky sheet of altostratus that puts a halo round the sun, then thick low stratus and steady rain. Warm fronts move slower than cold fronts because the cold air is heavy and hard to shift.", "Cork on the Atlantic edge of Ireland gets the full sequence many times a year. A clear morning with mares' tails high overhead, by lunchtime the sun is a pale disc, by mid-afternoon it is grey and drizzling, and the wind has backed to the south and strengthened.", "The early stage can be flyable. Under thin cirrus the ground still heats and thermals still work, though they weaken as the sheet thickens and the sun dims. Lift under a warm front is often smooth and broad because the whole air mass is being lifted. The air behind the front, in the warm sector, is usually stable, murky and poor for thermals.", "The danger is the wind. Ahead of a warm front the wind strengthens and the gradient tightens as the low approaches. Pilots launch in a light breeze under cirrus and find the landing field in 30 kilometres per hour an hour later. Fly early, keep the flight short, and land before the altostratus arrives."], "rel": ["wx-cold-front", "wx-low-pressure", "wx-haze-visibility", "wx-wind-gradient"], "src": [{"t": "Weather front (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather_front"}, {"t": "High and low pressure (Met Office)", "u": "https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/how-weather-works/high-and-low-pressure"}, {"t": "Fronten (DHV magazine, Wetterwissen)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/07_wetter/Wetterwissen/DHVmagazin_Artikel/Grundlagen/06_2011_170_fronten.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-high-pressure", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.47, "lon": -5.33, "name": "Is high pressure good for flying?", "place": "Explained over Piedrahita, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Sunny and settled, yes, but the same sinking air that clears the sky can cap your climb.", "body": ["In a high, or anticyclone, air sinks. The Met Office describes the descending air warming and drying, which suppresses cloud, so the weather is usually settled with light winds. In the northern hemisphere the wind circulates clockwise around a high. In summer that means warm, dry, sunny spells. In winter it means cold, calm days, overnight frost and fog.", "Piedrahita in the Sierra de Gredos, one of Spain's great cross-country sites, does its best work on the edge of a high. The sky is clear, the ground bakes, and the air is dry enough to put cloudbase above 3,000 metres. Summer flights of 200 kilometres are flown under skies with barely a cloud in them.", "The catch is the inversion. Sinking air in the centre of a strong high builds a warm lid that caps thermals low, often with haze underneath. Light winds and a flat, hazy sky under a big high can be a weak day despite the sunshine. The best flying is often on the flanks of the high, where the subsidence is weaker and a little instability survives.", "Read the pressure trend as well as the number. A high that is building brings settled days with a falling inversion each morning. A high that is breaking down lets instability back in, and the last day before a front is often the strongest thermic day, with a risk of overdevelopment by afternoon."], "rel": ["wx-inversion", "wx-low-pressure", "wx-blue-thermals", "wx-haze-visibility"], "src": [{"t": "High and low pressure (Met Office)", "u": "https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/how-weather-works/high-and-low-pressure"}, {"t": "Temperature inversion (Met Office)", "u": "https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/types-of-weather/temperature/temperature-inversion"}]}, {"id": "wx-low-pressure", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 64.15, "lon": -21.94, "name": "What does low pressure mean for pilots?", "place": "Explained over Reykjavik, Iceland", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Lows bring the fronts, the wind and the rain, but also the unstable air that makes the best thermals.", "body": ["In a low, or depression, air rises. The Met Office explains that as it rises it cools, moisture condenses and clouds and rain form, so the weather is unsettled and comes with weather fronts. Winds circulate anticlockwise around a northern-hemisphere low. Lows are born on the boundary between polar and tropical air and travel east along the storm track.", "Iceland sits under the Icelandic Low, a semi-permanent area of low pressure in the North Atlantic that spins up the depressions which cross Britain and Scandinavia. Reykjavik sees winds of gale force many times a winter, and the barometer here is a weather forecast for Europe three days later.", "For a paraglider pilot, a low is mostly a reason to stay home: wind above the limits, cloud down on the hills, rain. But the air masses around a low are what make flying good elsewhere. Behind the cold front the polar air is unstable and crisp. On the warm, sunny side of a slack low over land, the rising motion helps thermals climb high.", "What pilots misread is the pressure number itself. A low with 1,000 hectopascals at its centre can give a gentle day if the isobars are far apart, and a high of 1,025 can be unflyable if they are tightly packed on its edge. The spacing of the isobars is the wind, and the wind is what matters."], "rel": ["wx-high-pressure", "wx-cold-front", "wx-warm-front", "wx-wind-gradient"], "src": [{"t": "High and low pressure (Met Office)", "u": "https://weather.metoffice.gov.uk/learn-about/weather/how-weather-works/high-and-low-pressure"}, {"t": "Weather front (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weather_front"}]}, {"id": "wx-wind-gradient", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.59, "lon": -1.21, "name": "What is wind gradient?", "place": "Explained over the Dune du Pilat, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The wind at head height is not the wind at 50 metres, and that gap wrecks landings.", "body": ["Wind gradient is the way wind speed increases with height above the ground. Friction with the surface slows the lowest layer, and the effect fades with height, so the biggest change is in the first few hundred metres. Over rough ground or a city the surface wind can be 40 to 50 percent weaker than the wind aloft. Over open water or ice the loss is only 20 to 30 percent.", "The Dune du Pilat on France's Atlantic coast, Europe's tallest sand dune at over 100 metres, is a wind gradient laboratory. A smooth sea breeze arrives across open water with almost no gradient, hits the dune and the forest behind it, and the pilot who steps off the top in 25 kilometres per hour lands on the beach in 15.", "The hazard is on landing. As a wing descends through the gradient into slower air, its airspeed drops, so it loses lift and sinks faster at exactly the moment it is close to the ground. Sailplane pilots add speed on final approach for this reason. A paraglider pilot should keep hands up, keep airspeed, and avoid a long slow flare in strong wind.", "The gradient also means that launch conditions understate the day. A windsock on a low launch reading 20 kilometres per hour can mean 30 at ridge height. Check the wind on the top of the hill, or a forecast at ridge level, not only at the bottom."], "rel": ["wx-windsock", "wx-rotor", "wx-forecast-models", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Wind gradient (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_gradient"}]}, {"id": "wx-thermal-triggers", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 19.19, "lon": -100.13, "name": "What triggers a thermal?", "place": "Explained over Valle de Bravo, Mexico", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Warm air does not leave the ground on its own. Something has to kick it loose.", "body": ["A thermal starts as a layer of hot air clinging to the ground, like water on a tilted pane before the drop finally runs. Something has to break the layer: a gust, a tractor, a change in surface, a rock outcrop, a line of trees, a ridge crest. That is the trigger. Once the bubble separates it rises, and on a hot afternoon the layer near the ground is heated so steeply that the AMS glossary describes the lowest 100 metres as having a very steep lapse rate.", "Valle de Bravo in the Mexican highlands, at about 1,800 metres, is a thermal-trigger textbook. Dark pine forest, bare volcanic rock, the edge of the lake, a quarry, and the sharp ridge behind launch all work differently through the day. By early afternoon the sky is marked by cumulus over the same hills every day.", "Good triggers share two things: they heat faster than their surroundings, and they have an edge. Dark ploughed fields, south-facing rock, towns, dry riverbeds and quarries heat quickly. Ridge lines, forest edges, roads and the boundary between crop and fallow give the hot layer a place to detach. The upwind side of a slope usually releases, the downwind side collects.", "The mistake is to fly straight to the cloud and ignore the ground. The cloud marks where a thermal was a few minutes ago. The trigger below, allowing for wind drift, marks where the next one will be. Even a moving cloud shadow works as a trigger, releasing warm air along its edge."], "rel": ["wx-cumulus-cycle", "wx-blue-thermals", "wx-dust-devils", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Thermal (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal"}, {"t": "Dust devil (AMS Glossary of Meteorology)", "u": "https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Dust_devil"}, {"t": "Wolkenschatten (DHV magazine)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/07_wetter/Wetterwissen/DHVmagazin_Artikel/Wolken/8_2010_165_wolkenschatten.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-blue-thermals", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -23.7, "lon": 133.88, "name": "What are blue thermals?", "place": "Explained over Alice Springs, Australia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "No clouds, no markers, and some of the strongest lift you will ever find.", "body": ["A blue thermal is a thermal with no cloud on top. It happens when the air is so dry that a rising parcel never cools to its dew point before it stops at the inversion. The lift is just as real, often stronger because the ground is baking under cloudless sun, but there is nothing in the sky to show where it is.", "Alice Springs in central Australia is a blue-thermal factory. Red sand, rock and spinifex heat fast, the dew point sits far below the temperature, and the sky in winter and spring is an unbroken blue. Flights of hundreds of kilometres are flown here with no cloud markers at all.", "Without cumulus, pilots read the ground and the air. Soaring birds are the best marker there is. Dust devils show a thermal leaving. Shimmering heat haze over a field, a sudden drop in wind on the surface, a smell of hot earth rising past the harness, and the vario itself all count. Pilots fly from trigger to trigger, sun-facing slope to sun-facing slope, rather than cloud to cloud.", "The number to check in the forecast is the spread between temperature and dew point. If the spread times 125 metres gives a cloudbase higher than the inversion, the day will be blue. Blue days are not weak days, but they are lonely ones, and getting low far from a trigger is how they end."], "rel": ["wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "wx-thermal-triggers", "wx-dust-devils", "wx-inversion"], "src": [{"t": "Dew point (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dew_point"}, {"t": "Thermal (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal"}]}, {"id": "wx-dust-devils", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.2, "lon": -111.65, "name": "Are dust devils dangerous to paragliders?", "place": "Explained over Flagstaff, Arizona, USA", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "A thermal you can see, which is wonderful at 500 metres and lethal on launch.", "body": ["A dust devil is a thermal made visible. On a hot, calm afternoon the lowest 100 metres of air is heated so steeply that a column breaks loose and spins up as it rises, lifting dust and leaves into a whirling tube. The AMS glossary gives diameters of 3 to over 30 metres and heights averaging 200 metres, with rare ones reaching a kilometre. Most last under a minute. Big ones can last 20 minutes.", "Winds inside average around 70 kilometres per hour, and the largest exceed 100. A dust devil in Flagstaff, Arizona in 2000 was estimated at 120 kilometres per hour. Wikipedia notes they are considered a major hazard to paraglider pilots and skydivers, able to collapse a wing with no warning at a height too low to recover.", "High up, a dust devil is good news: it shows a strong thermal leaving the ground, and the column above it is often the best lift of the day. Low down it is the most dangerous air a paraglider meets. The core is rotating, the edges shear, and the whole thing moves across the ground at walking pace or faster.", "On launch, watch the whole field, not just the windsock. If a devil is tracking towards you, get the wing down and lie on it. In the air below 100 metres, fly away from the dust at full speed and expect a collapse. Pilots have been thrown to the ground on landing approach by devils they never saw because the ground was grass, not dust."], "rel": ["wx-thermal-triggers", "wx-blue-thermals", "wx-windsock", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Dust devil (AMS Glossary of Meteorology)", "u": "https://glossary.ametsoc.org/wiki/Dust_devil"}, {"t": "Dust devil (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust_devil"}]}, {"id": "wx-mistral", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": 43.95, "lon": 4.81, "name": "Mistral", "place": "Rhone valley, Avignon, France", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "The wind that drove Van Gogh mad and that no paraglider in Provence argues with.", "body": ["The Mistral is a cold, dry northerly that pours down the Rhone valley to the Mediterranean. It sets up when high pressure sits over the Bay of Biscay and low pressure over the Gulf of Genoa. Cold air is pulled south, squeezed between the Alps and the Massif Central, and accelerates through the Rhone corridor. Episodes last a day or two, often several, occasionally more than a week.", "Sustained speeds of around 50 kilometres per hour are typical, with 90 common in the valley and gusts above 100. The FAI explainer written for balloon pilots notes the flow can extend up to 3,000 metres. It is strongest in winter and spring, usually calmer at night, and brings brilliant clear skies: Provence gets 2,700 to 2,900 hours of sunshine a year largely because of it.", "Avignon sits in the throat of it. Trees along the valley lean permanently south, farmhouses turn blank walls to the north, and rows of cypress are planted as windbreaks. The clear air that follows is why the mountains stand out so sharply in the paintings made here.", "For pilots the Mistral is a no-fly wind. The sites along the Rhone, the Luberon and the Alpilles are unflyable while it blows, and the days after can still carry strong north wind aloft at ridge height even when the valley feels calm. The reward is the day it stops: dry, crisp, unstable air and high cloudbase."], "rel": ["wx-tramontane", "wx-bise", "wx-katabatic", "wx-high-pressure"], "src": [{"t": "Explainer: what is the Mistral and what does it mean for pilots? (FAI)", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/21115"}, {"t": "Mistral (wind) (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mistral_(wind)"}]}, {"id": "wx-tramontane", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 42.7, "lon": 2.9, "name": "Tramontane", "place": "Perpignan, Roussillon, France", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "It blows one day in three in Perpignan, and the record gust was over 190 kilometres per hour.", "body": ["The Tramontane is the Mistral's western twin. It is a northwesterly that funnels between the Pyrenees and the southern Massif Central, blowing from the Lauragais across Languedoc and Roussillon to the sea. Météo-France counts a Tramontane day when gusts reach at least 60 kilometres per hour, and by that measure it blows about one day in three.", "Between 1981 and 2010 Perpignan averaged 122 Tramontane days a year and Narbonne 144. Gusts reach 140 kilometres per hour and more, and at Cap Béar on the coast the record is 190.8, set on 24 January 2009. It is strongest in winter and spring and in the coastal strip between Narbonne and Perpignan.", "It arrives with high pressure over Spain or an Azores high stretching into the Bay of Biscay, often behind a cold front, with low pressure over the Gulf of Genoa. Météo-France noted that 2022 brought only 90 Tramontane days at Perpignan, the fewest since 1981, and projections suggest fewer days by 2100.", "The Roussillon flying sites, and the Spanish Catalan sites just across the border, shut down when it blows. Pilots arriving for the Pyrenees should read the Perpignan wind before the mountain forecast: a Tramontane day means strong north wind on every ridge of the eastern Pyrenees, with rotor in every south-facing bowl."], "rel": ["wx-mistral", "wx-bise", "wx-rotor", "wx-cold-front"], "src": [{"t": "Moins de jours de tramontane en 2022 (Météo-France)", "u": "https://meteofrance.com/actualites/moins-de-jours-de-tramontane-en-2022"}]}, {"id": "wx-bora", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 45.65, "lon": 13.78, "name": "Bora", "place": "Trieste, Italy", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "A city that strings chains along its pavements so people can stay on their feet.", "body": ["The Bora is a cold northeasterly that falls off the Dinaric mountains and the Karst plateau onto the Adriatic coast. It blows hardest when a cold high sits over the snowy interior behind the mountains and a low lies over the warmer sea. Dense cold air builds up behind the ridge, then spills over and accelerates downhill: a fall wind, a katabatic flow on a grand scale.", "In Trieste gusts usually exceed 120 kilometres per hour during a strong Bora and peak near 200. Chains and ropes are strung along downtown pavements so pedestrians can hold on. Along the Croatian coast it is stronger still. The official record is 248 kilometres per hour on the Maslenica bridge on 21 December 1998, and a 304 reading near the Sveti Rok tunnel in 2003 is unverified because the instruments were not calibrated that high.", "Locals name two kinds. Bora chiara, the clear Bora, blows under an anticyclone with blue skies. Bora scura, the dark Bora, comes with cloud on the hilltops and brings rain or snow. Roofs in Slovenian Karst villages and the Trieste province carry stones to hold the tiles down. It is most common in winter.", "The paragliding sites of the Slovenian coast and the Croatian islands are closed by the Bora and dangerous for a day either side of it. The wind arrives in violent gusts with lulls between, which is the pattern most likely to catch a pilot on launch."], "rel": ["wx-katabatic", "wx-mistral", "wx-tramontane", "wx-rotor"], "src": [{"t": "Bora (wind) (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bora_(wind)"}, {"t": "Katabatic wind (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Katabatic_wind"}]}, {"id": "wx-bise", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 46.2, "lon": 6.14, "name": "Bise", "place": "Geneva, Switzerland", "icon": "wave", "hidden": true, "hook": "Swiss forecasters measure it with two barometers 250 kilometres apart.", "body": ["The Bise is a cold, dry northeasterly that blows the length of the Swiss plateau between the Jura and the Alps and funnels into the Lake Geneva basin. MeteoSwiss describes it simply as a northeasterly wind over the Swiss plateau. It sets up when high pressure sits over northern or eastern Europe and low pressure over the Mediterranean.", "Forecasters gauge it by the pressure difference between Geneva and Güttingen in Thurgau, at the other end of the plateau. When Güttingen reads higher than Geneva the Bise starts, and the bigger the gap the stronger the wind. The gap between the mountains narrows towards the west, so the Bise is strongest around the lake: winter winds of about 100 kilometres per hour, with gusts of 154 recorded on La Dôle in the Jura.", "In summer it brings dry air and clear skies. In winter it traps cold, damp air under an inversion and the plateau disappears under a sheet of low stratus or fog for days, while the Alps above sit in sunshine. Strong winter Bise can coat the Geneva lakefront in thick ice from spray. A cloudy variant, the Bise noire, brings overcast and precipitation.", "For pilots the Bise means the lake sites and the Jura are blown out, while launches on the north side of the Alps can be in strong, cold, gusty north wind with rotor behind every ridge. The clear summer Bise day sometimes gives good flying in the Valais, sheltered to the south."], "rel": ["wx-mistral", "wx-inversion", "wx-tramontane", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Bise (MeteoSwiss)", "u": "https://www.meteoswiss.admin.ch/weather/weather-and-climate-from-a-to-z/bise.html"}, {"t": "Bise (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bise"}]}, {"id": "wx-harmattan", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 12, "lon": 8.52, "name": "Harmattan", "place": "Kano, Nigeria", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "A dry wind that fills the sky with Sahara dust and can hide the sun for days.", "body": ["The Harmattan is the dry northeasterly trade wind that blows out of the Sahara across West Africa towards the Gulf of Guinea. It is strongest from late November to mid-March. Crossing the desert it picks up fine dust, grains between 0.5 and 10 micrometres across, and carries a thick haze over Nigeria, Ghana, Burkina Faso, Senegal and their neighbours, and on for hundreds of kilometres over the Atlantic.", "Kano in northern Nigeria sits in the thick of it. Skybrary reports relative humidity dropping below 5 percent in the afternoon, days that start near 9 degrees and climb past 30, and visibility that falls to something comparable to heavy fog when the dust is dense. Nosebleeds and cracked lips are part of the season. Airlines lose flights every year to Harmattan haze.", "The dust layer sits in the lowest kilometre or two of the atmosphere. Where the Harmattan meets the moist southwest monsoon flow further south, it rides up over it to around 900 to 1,800 metres, and that boundary can spawn violent squalls. When the haze is thin the skies are clear and the air cool and dry.", "For a visiting pilot the Harmattan is a visibility problem rather than a wind problem. Surface winds are usually modest, but with the horizon gone and the sun a dull disc, judging height and distance becomes hard and thermals under the dust are weak. The cleaner flying in West Africa is in the shoulder seasons before and after it."], "rel": ["wx-saharan-air-layer", "wx-haze-visibility", "wx-trade-winds-hawaii", "wx-inversion"], "src": [{"t": "Harmattan (Skybrary)", "u": "https://www.skybrary.aero/articles/harmattan"}, {"t": "Harmattan (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmattan"}]}, {"id": "wx-himalayan-monsoon", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.22, "lon": 76.32, "name": "When does the monsoon stop Himalayan flying?", "place": "Explained over Dharamshala, Himachal Pradesh, India", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Three months of cloud and rain, then one of the clearest skies on Earth.", "body": ["The southwest monsoon is the seasonal wind that carries Indian Ocean moisture north across the subcontinent. It normally reaches the southwest coast at the start of June, covers the whole of India by mid-July, begins to withdraw in early September and has gone by early October. The Dhauladhar range above Dharamshala is the first wall it meets in the western Himalaya, and it rains here in enormous quantities.", "In the Bir Billing region a little to the east, which has the same weather, the monsoon months of July and August bring heavy rain, low cloud on the mountains and unstable air, and paragliding stops. Guides advise against flying then. Tandem operators run from mid-September to mid-July with the gap in between.", "The two cross-country seasons sit either side of the rains. October and November, after the monsoon has washed the dust out of the air, give cold, crisp skies and some of the best visibility of the year. Then March to May, before the heat and haze build, give strong spring thermals. Winter in between is cold but often flyable with short days.", "What catches visiting pilots is the pre-monsoon. Late May and June bring heat, haze, and afternoon storms that grow fast over the high peaks. The sky can look flyable at noon and be a line of cumulonimbus by three. Launch at 2,400 metres does not make a storm smaller."], "rel": ["bir-billing", "wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "wx-haze-visibility", "wx-overdevelopment"], "src": [{"t": "Monsoon of South Asia (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monsoon_of_South_Asia"}, {"t": "Bir Billing take-off (Flyappi)", "u": "https://flyappi.org/take_off/12970/"}, {"t": "Best time to visit Bir Billing (Capture a Trip)", "u": "https://www.captureatrip.com/blog/best-time-to-visit-bir-billing"}]}, {"id": "wx-trade-winds-quixada", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -4.97, "lon": -39.02, "name": "How do trade winds make 500-kilometre flights possible?", "place": "Explained over Quixadá, Ceará, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wind that never stops, blowing the same way every day, is a conveyor belt for paragliders.", "body": ["Trade winds are the steady easterlies of the tropics. Air sinks in the subtropical highs around 30 degrees latitude, flows back towards the equator, and the Earth's rotation bends it westward: northeast trades north of the equator, southeast trades south of it. They meet at the doldrums, the Intertropical Convergence Zone. They are strongest in the local winter.", "Quixadá in Brazil's Ceará state, a few degrees south of the equator, is where that wind became a sport. From the dry season, September to December, the trades blow from early morning to sunset, regularly above 50 kilometres per hour in November and December. Launch is at 460 metres on a granite dome, landings are around 300, and hired helpers hold the wing through 10 to 20 second lulls between gusts.", "Pilots fly downwind along the roads westward, Quixadá to Quixeramobim to Tauá to Picos, with cloudbase near 3,500 metres in November and temperatures touching 40 degrees. Flights of 200 to 300 kilometres are routine for the experienced. The world distance record was pushed to 564 kilometres in this region of northeast Brazil on 13 October 2016 by a Brazilian team flying together.", "The committed part is the launch and the landing, both in strong wind over rough ground. The rest is reading the cloud streets that line up along the wind. Nobody flies back: the retrieve is a long drive east into the wind at the end of the day."], "rel": ["wx-trade-winds-hawaii", "wx-convergence", "wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "wx-wind-gradient"], "src": [{"t": "Guide to Quixadá, Brazil (Cross Country magazine)", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-quixada-brazil/"}, {"t": "The dream team: 564 km paragliding world record in Brazil (Cross Country magazine)", "u": "https://xcmag.com/magazine-articles/the-dream-team-564km-paragliding-world-record-in-brazil/"}, {"t": "Trade winds (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_winds"}]}, {"id": "wx-trade-winds-hawaii", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 21.31, "lon": -157.66, "name": "What are the trade winds?", "place": "Explained over Makapuu, Oahu, Hawaii, USA", "icon": "wave", "hidden": true, "hook": "A northeasterly on most summer days, which is why Oahu's cliffs are soarable almost every afternoon.", "body": ["The trade winds are the planet's most reliable wind. Warm air rises at the equator, drifts poleward high up, sinks at around 30 degrees north and south in the subtropical high-pressure belts, and flows back towards the equator along the surface. The Coriolis effect deflects it west, so it arrives from the northeast in the northern hemisphere and from the southeast in the southern.", "Hawaii sits squarely in the northeast trade belt. From May to September the trades are prevalent 80 to 95 percent of the time, and the windward cliffs of Oahu, such as Makapuu, give smooth ridge soaring day after day over the Pacific. From October to April the belt shifts south of the islands, winds are lighter and more variable, and winter brings occasional Kona storms with wind from the usually sheltered southwest.", "Sailors named them, and they are the reason Columbus went out on the northeast trades and home on the westerlies further north. The ITCZ where the two trade systems meet is a belt of calms, cloud and rain that shifts north in the northern summer and south in the northern winter.", "For a pilot the trades mean coastal soaring with a known direction and a moisture-laden sea breeze that stacks cloud on the windward mountains. The lee side of a Hawaiian island is dry, sunny and in rotor. Fly the windward cliffs, and respect the gap winds where the trades squeeze between peaks."], "rel": ["wx-trade-winds-quixada", "wx-convergence", "k-sea-breeze", "wx-rotor"], "src": [{"t": "Climate of Hawaii (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Climate_of_Hawaii"}, {"t": "Trade winds (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_winds"}]}, {"id": "wx-saharan-air-layer", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 14.93, "lon": -23.51, "name": "What is the Saharan dust layer?", "place": "Explained over Praia, Cape Verde", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": true, "hook": "A sheet of desert dust that crosses an ocean every few days and can switch hurricanes off.", "body": ["The Saharan Air Layer is a mass of very dry, dusty air that lifts off North Africa and travels west over the Atlantic. NOAA describes it as typically 2 to 2.5 miles thick, around 3 to 4 kilometres, with its base about a mile, 1.6 kilometres, above the surface. It forms from late spring to early autumn, with outbreaks every three to five days and a peak from late June to mid-August.", "Cape Verde, 600 kilometres off Senegal, is the first land it crosses. Clear tropical blue turns to a milky brown ceiling, the horizon vanishes and the sun sets red. From here the layer can reach the Caribbean, Florida, Texas and Central America, sometimes covering an area as large as the lower 48 states of the USA.", "NOAA tracks it because it matters for hurricanes. The layer's dry air, warmth and strong winds can suppress the formation and strengthening of tropical cyclones, so a summer with big dust outbreaks tends to be a quieter one for Atlantic storms. Its Harmattan cousin does the same job in winter at lower altitude over West Africa.", "For pilots in the Canaries, Cape Verde or the Caribbean, dust days mean poor visibility, a strong inversion at the base of the layer that caps thermals, and weak lift under the shade. The sea breeze still works on the coast, but the inland thermic flying mostly waits for the next clear slot between outbreaks."], "rel": ["wx-harmattan", "wx-haze-visibility", "wx-inversion", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "The Saharan Air Layer: what is it? Why does NOAA track it? (NOAA AOML)", "u": "https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/what-is-the-saharan-air-layer/"}]}, {"id": "wx-sounding-skew-t", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.22, "lon": -97.44, "name": "How do I read a skew-T sounding?", "place": "Explained over Norman, Oklahoma, USA", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "One chart shows the inversion, the cloudbase and the storm risk before you leave the house.", "body": ["A sounding is a profile of the atmosphere from the ground up, measured by a weather balloon or predicted by a model. The skew-T diagram is the standard way to draw it. Pressure runs up the side on a logarithmic scale, which stands in for height. Temperature lines are skewed at 45 degrees, which is where the name comes from. Two curves are plotted: the air temperature and the dew point.", "Norman, Oklahoma, home of the US Storm Prediction Center, is where soundings are read most carefully. The curved brown lines are dry adiabats, the path a dry rising parcel follows, cooling at 9.8 degrees per kilometre. The green curves are saturated adiabats for air inside cloud. Dashed green lines are mixing ratio, which carry the dew point upward.", "To find cloudbase, follow the mixing ratio line up from the surface dew point and the dry adiabat up from the surface temperature. Where they cross is the lifting condensation level. Where the temperature curve bends to the right with height, that is an inversion and the top of your thermals. Where the temperature and dew point curves touch, there is cloud.", "The number pilots should fear is CAPE, the energy available for convection. The NWS teaches that the square root of CAPE in joules per kilogram gives a theoretical maximum updraft in metres per second, so 2,500 gives about 50. A sounding with high CAPE and no cap is a thunderstorm forecast."], "rel": ["wx-lapse-rate", "wx-inversion", "wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "wx-forecast-models"], "src": [{"t": "The Skew-T log-P diagram (NWS)", "u": "https://www.weather.gov/media/ohx/PDF/TheskewTlogPdiagram.pdf"}, {"t": "Lapse rate (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}]}, {"id": "wx-forecast-models", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 50.1, "lon": 8.76, "name": "Which weather model should I trust?", "place": "Explained over Offenbach, Germany", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "GFS, ICON, ECMWF, AROME: the names on the app are the computers that guess your day.", "body": ["Every forecast app is a window onto a numerical model, a simulation of the atmosphere run on a supercomputer. The model divides the world into grid boxes and calculates what happens in each. The size of the box is the resolution. A box of 13 kilometres cannot see a valley 3 kilometres wide, so the valley wind it forecasts is an average over the mountains around it.", "Offenbach near Frankfurt is home to the German weather service, the DWD, which runs the ICON family. The global ICON runs at about 13 kilometres, ICON-EU over Europe at about 7, and ICON-D2 over Germany, the Alps and neighbours at 2.2, updated every three hours for two days ahead. The American GFS is a global model at roughly 13 to 25 kilometres depending on the product. The European ECMWF runs globally at 9 kilometres and is the default on Windy. France's AROME runs at 1.3 to 2.5 kilometres.", "Rule of thumb: use the global models for the pattern three to seven days out, and the finest local model for the day itself. Over the Alps, ICON-D2 or AROME. Over the Rockies, the 3-kilometre HRRR or NAM. Where no fine model exists, the global ones are what there is, and local knowledge matters more.", "What pilots get wrong is treating one model as truth. If GFS, ICON and ECMWF agree, trust the day. If they disagree, the atmosphere is on a knife edge and the forecast is a coin toss. Sites that show several models side by side exist for exactly this reason."], "rel": ["wx-meteoblue-windy-xcskies", "wx-sounding-skew-t", "wx-wind-gradient", "wx-high-pressure"], "src": [{"t": "What source of weather data does Windy use? (Windy community)", "u": "https://community.windy.com/topic/12/what-source-of-weather-data-windy-use/53"}, {"t": "XC Skies soaring forecasts", "u": "https://www.xcskies.com/"}, {"t": "DWD ICON-D2 (LuckGrib)", "u": "https://www.luckgrib.com/models/dwd_icon_d2"}]}, {"id": "wx-meteoblue-windy-xcskies", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.56, "lon": 7.59, "name": "What do Meteoblue, Windy and XC Skies each do best?", "place": "Explained over Basel, Switzerland", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Three apps, three different jobs: one shows the models, one shows the map, one shows the lift.", "body": ["Meteoblue, based in Basel, runs its own NEMS and NMM models at 4 kilometres over central Europe, 12 over Europe and 30 globally, and lays dozens of other models beside them. Its multimodel page lists ICON at 13, 7 and 2 kilometres, ECMWF IFS at 10, GFS at 12 to 40, AROME at 2, the Swiss ICON-CH at 1 and 2, and the American HRRR at 3. The value is the comparison: when the rows agree, the forecast is solid.", "Windy is a map. It animates wind, cloud, rain and CAPE from ECMWF at 9 kilometres, ICON-EU at 7, Meteoblue's NEMS at 4 and AROME at 1.3 to 2.5 over France, and lets you pick a point and read the wind at 10, 100 and 950 hectopascal heights. For a pilot the killer feature is wind at altitude drawn over the terrain you intend to fly.", "XC Skies is built for soaring. It takes GFS, ICON, ICON-EU, ICON-D2, AROME, RAP, NAM and HRRR and turns them into the numbers glider pilots want: lift potential, soaring height, cloud and thunderstorm threat, plus point forecasts, route analysis and skew-T soundings, on a paid subscription after a free trial.", "The habit worth building is the same with all three: look at the wind at ridge height, not the ground; look at the sounding for the inversion; and look at CAPE and cloud top for the afternoon. A single pretty icon of a sun is not a flying forecast."], "rel": ["wx-forecast-models", "wx-sounding-skew-t", "wx-wind-gradient", "wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck"], "src": [{"t": "Weather model comparison (Meteoblue multimodel)", "u": "https://www.meteoblue.com/en/weather/forecast/multimodel/46.787N8.997E3101_Europe%2FZurich"}, {"t": "What source of weather data does Windy use? (Windy community)", "u": "https://community.windy.com/topic/12/what-source-of-weather-data-windy-use/53"}, {"t": "Wie hoch reicht die Thermik (DHV magazine 241)", "u": "https://dhv.de/fileadmin/user_upload/files/2023/05/DHVmagazin_241/DHV241_Wie_hoch_reicht_die_Thermik.pdf"}]}, {"id": "wx-windsock", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.32, "lon": 5.82, "name": "How do I read a windsock?", "place": "Explained over Laragne, France", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "Straight out means 28 kilometres per hour, which for most paragliders means stay on the ground.", "body": ["A windsock is a cone of fabric that points downwind and lifts with wind speed. The international standard from ICAO Annex 14 is a 3.6 metre sock with a 0.9 metre throat on a 6 metre mast, built so that it flies fully horizontal at 28 kilometres per hour, which aviation quotes as 15 knots. The US FAA adds that a working sock should orient to a breeze of at least 5 kilometres per hour.", "The striped socks on many airfields carry five bands, and the old convention is that each band lifted adds about 5 or 6 kilometres per hour: one band 6, three bands 17, all five horizontal 28 or more. Above that the sock cannot tell you anything except the direction, and the direction is the most reliable thing it ever tells you.", "At Chabre above Laragne in the southern French Alps, one of the busiest launches in Europe, pilots watch the socks on both sides of the hill. A sock that lifts and drops rhythmically is thermal cycles. One that swings through 90 degrees is a convergence or a change coming. One that hangs limp while the trees on the ridge move is a sign the wind above is stronger than the launch.", "The mistake is reading a single moment. Watch the sock for five minutes, note the strongest gust and the direction of the swings, and remember the gradient: the sock sits a few metres up and the wing will be flying in air that is faster."], "rel": ["wx-wind-gradient", "wx-anabatic", "wx-dust-devils", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Windsock (Skybrary)", "u": "https://skybrary.aero/articles/windsock"}]}, {"id": "wx-anvil", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -12.46, "lon": 130.84, "name": "What does an anvil cloud mean?", "place": "Explained over Darwin, Australia", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The flat top is the storm hitting the roof of the troposphere, and lightning reaches out from under it.", "body": ["An anvil is the flattened, spreading top of a mature cumulonimbus. The cloud's updraft climbs until it reaches the tropopause, the stable lid at the top of the weather, normally around 12,000 metres but up to 20,000 in the tropics. Unable to rise further, the cloud spreads sideways downwind in a plume shaped like a blacksmith's anvil. NOAA's cloud guide describes the top as flattened in the shape of an anvil or vast plume.", "Darwin at the top of Australia builds some of the tallest storms on Earth every afternoon of the wet season. The humid monsoon air over the Tiwi Islands and the Top End towers up past 15,000 metres, the anvils spread for tens of kilometres and glow orange at sunset, and the lightning show runs for hours.", "The anvil tells you three things. The storm is mature, so downdrafts and a gust front are already happening. The high wind is blowing from the storm towards the long side of the anvil, so that is the way it is moving. And the shelf can extend many kilometres ahead of the rain, and lightning can strike out of the anvil far from the storm core. Sometimes a dome bulges above it, an overshooting top, which marks a very strong updraft and severe weather.", "If you can see an anvil, the storm is close enough to matter. Do not launch. If you are already flying, land at once and get away from the ridge before the outflow arrives. An anvil upwind of you is a deadline, and the gust front usually beats the rain."], "rel": ["wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "wx-gust-front", "wx-lightning-distance", "wx-virga"], "src": [{"t": "The ten basic clouds (NOAA JetStream)", "u": "https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/clouds/ten-basic-clouds"}, {"t": "Cumulonimbus cloud (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulonimbus_cloud"}]}, {"id": "wx-virga", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.08, "lon": -106.65, "name": "What is virga?", "place": "Explained over Albuquerque, New Mexico, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": true, "hook": "Rain that never reaches the ground, and the invisible downdraft it leaves behind.", "body": ["Virga is a streak of rain or snow falling from a cloud and evaporating before it reaches the ground. It looks like a grey curtain or a fringe hanging under the cloud base that fades to nothing part way down. It is common in deserts and dry climates: the American Southwest, the Canadian prairies, the Middle East, North Africa and Australia.", "Above Albuquerque the summer sky is full of it. High cloudbase, very dry air underneath, and cumulus that drop grey veils over the Sandia mountains that never wet the ground. The evening light through the curtains is one of the sights of New Mexico.", "The danger is what the evaporation does. Turning water back to vapour takes a great deal of heat, which it pulls from the surrounding air. That air gets cold, dense and heavy and falls, creating a downdraft. Under heavy virga the downdraft can reach the ground as a dry microburst, a sudden outward burst of wind that is extremely hazardous to aircraft. Airliners have crashed in them.", "For a paraglider, virga means sink and gusts underneath, often in air that still looks dry and benign. Treat a cloud trailing virga as a cloud to avoid, and land before its downdraft reaches the field. Virga under a growing congestus is also a sign the day is tipping into storms."], "rel": ["wx-gust-front", "wx-anvil", "wx-overdevelopment", "wx-blue-thermals"], "src": [{"t": "Virga (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virga"}]}, {"id": "wx-haze-visibility", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 1.35, "lon": 103.82, "name": "Why does haze matter to a pilot?", "place": "Explained over Singapore", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "You cannot judge a landing field you cannot see, and the murk also tells you the air is stable.", "body": ["Haze is dust, smoke and other dry particles suspended in the air, dimming the sky and shortening the view. Meteorologists distinguish it from mist and fog by moisture: haze forms in relatively dry air, mist in saturated air. Seen from a distance it looks brownish or bluish depending on the angle to the sun. Sources include stubble burning, forest fires, traffic, industry, dust storms and volcanoes.", "Singapore shows the extreme. Since 1991 smoke from fires on Sumatra and Borneo has repeatedly blanketed the region. In 2013 the three-hour pollution index hit a record 401 and Kuala Lumpur sat under smoke for more than a week. ASEAN countries signed an agreement on transboundary haze in 2002, and the problem has not gone away.", "For pilots haze is first a visibility problem. The horizon disappears, so judging height, glide and the position of other gliders becomes guesswork, and sun-side hills vanish into glare. In mountains a hazy day can hide the ridge you are heading for until you are close.", "Haze is also a message about the air. Particles collect under an inversion, so a thick haze layer with a sharp top usually marks a stable lid with the thermals capped beneath it. The clear days after rain or a cold front are the strong days. The murky days under a high are the weak ones."], "rel": ["wx-inversion", "wx-harmattan", "wx-saharan-air-layer", "wx-high-pressure"], "src": [{"t": "Haze (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haze"}]}, {"id": "wx-lightning-distance", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 9.7, "lon": -71.6, "name": "How far away is the lightning?", "place": "Explained over Lake Maracaibo, Venezuela", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "Three seconds per kilometre, and if you can hear it at all you are already in range.", "body": ["Light arrives instantly. Sound takes about three seconds to travel a kilometre. So count the seconds between the flash and the thunder and divide by three for kilometres, or divide by five for miles, as the US National Weather Service teaches: 5 seconds is a mile, 15 seconds is three miles. Thunder can be heard from only around 16 kilometres, 10 miles, away.", "Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela is the most lightning-struck place on Earth. Where the Catatumbo river enters the lake, storms build almost every night for much of the year, lit by flashes so continuous that sailors once used them as a lighthouse. It is the place to learn that a storm 15 kilometres off is not far.", "The NWS guidance is blunt: if you hear thunder, lightning is close enough to strike you, and no place outdoors is safe. Stay in shelter at least 30 minutes after the last thunder. Lightning can reach out from the anvil of a storm well ahead of the rain, striking from a sky that still looks partly blue.", "For a paraglider the count is already too late. A pilot in the air under a wet wing and carbon-reinforced risers is the highest conductive object around. The decision to land belongs at the moment the first cumulonimbus is seen building, not at the first rumble. He Zhongpin was killed by lightning in the Manilla storm of 2007 while still flying."], "rel": ["wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "wx-anvil", "wx-gust-front", "wx-overdevelopment"], "src": [{"t": "Understanding lightning: thunder (NWS)", "u": "https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-science-thunder"}, {"t": "Lightning safety tips (NWS)", "u": "https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-tips"}, {"t": "Ewa Wisnierska (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewa_Wi%C5%9Bnierska"}]}, {"id": "wx-hypoxia", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.81, "lon": 76.57, "name": "What does altitude do to a pilot's brain?", "place": "Explained over Broad Peak, Karakoram, Pakistan", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "The first symptom of hypoxia is feeling fine. One pilot flew to 8,407 metres here on purpose.", "body": ["Hypoxia is oxygen starvation. Air pressure falls with height, so each breath delivers less oxygen. Skybrary reports blood oxygen saturation falling from about 98 percent at sea level to 90 percent at 3,000 metres, with little effect except on night vision, which drops around 30 percent. Above 3,000 metres saturation falls fast, reaching about 65 percent at 6,000 metres, where normal function is seriously impaired.", "The symptoms are what make it dangerous: breathlessness, yawning, tiredness, euphoria, loss of recently learned skills, blue lips and fingertips, and finally unconsciousness. None of it hurts, and the euphoria means the pilot is the last person to notice. Aviation rules in the US require oxygen for more than 30 minutes above 3,800 metres and at all times above 4,300.", "Antoine Girard flew a paraglider over Broad Peak to 8,157 metres in 2016 and returned in 2021 to climb to 8,407 metres, launched from a valley 40 kilometres from base camp, on an expedition lasting two months. He was acclimatised for weeks and carried his life support. Ewa Wisnierska, pulled involuntarily to nearly 10,000 metres in Australia, lost consciousness.", "For ordinary cross-country pilots the practical ceiling is lower than the numbers suggest. Cold, dehydration and the mental load of flying all make hypoxia worse. Above 4,000 metres plan for short exposures, descend at the first odd feeling, and never judge your own state by how well you think you are doing."], "rel": ["wx-cold-altitude", "wx-cumulonimbus-cloud-suck", "bar-headed-goose", "himalayan-griffon"], "src": [{"t": "Hypoxia (Skybrary)", "u": "https://www.skybrary.aero/articles/hypoxia"}, {"t": "Don't hold your breath: oxygen rules and time of useful consciousness (AOPA)", "u": "https://www.aopa.org/news-and-media/all-news/2010/june/pilot/dont-hold-your-breath"}, {"t": "Flying up to 8407 m: interview with Antoine Girard (Alpine Mag)", "u": "https://alpinemag.com/flying-8407-interview-french-paraglider-antoine-girard-pakistan/"}]}, {"id": "wx-cold-altitude", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.92, "lon": 6.87, "name": "How cold does it get at cloudbase?", "place": "Explained over Chamonix, France", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 25 degree valley can mean minus 5 at cloudbase, and the wind chill makes it colder still.", "body": ["The air cools about 6.5 degrees for every 1,000 metres in the standard atmosphere, and the rising air inside a thermal cools faster, nearly 10 degrees per 1,000 metres until cloud forms. So a valley at 25 degrees can have a cloudbase 3,000 metres higher sitting near zero. Add the wind of flight across the body and the felt temperature drops further.", "Chamonix, at about 1,000 metres with Mont Blanc 3,800 metres above it, shows the full range on a single summer afternoon. Shorts in the town, down jacket at the Aiguille du Midi, and pilots launching from the Brevent on the opposite side climbing to 3,500 metres in gloves and a balaclava while tourists below are in the river.", "The US National Weather Service wind chill chart gives the scale of it: at minus 18 degrees with a 24 kilometre per hour wind, exposed skin can freeze in 30 minutes. Frostbite needs an actual air temperature below freezing, but hypothermia, a core temperature below 35 degrees, can set in well above it. Early signs are shivering, clumsiness, confusion and poor judgement, which is exactly what a pilot cannot afford.", "Dress for the top, not the launch. Hands go first, so mittens or heated gloves matter more than anything. Cold is also the quiet partner of hypoxia: both impair judgement, and a pilot who is cold at 3,500 metres is making worse decisions than they think."], "rel": ["wx-hypoxia", "wx-lapse-rate", "wx-dew-point-cloudbase", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Wind chill chart (NWS)", "u": "https://www.weather.gov/safety/cold-wind-chill-chart"}, {"t": "Hypothermia (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypothermia"}, {"t": "Lapse rate (Wikipedia)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}]}, {"id": "sf-five-point-check", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.59, "lon": -1.21, "name": "What is the 5-point pre-flight check?", "place": "Explained over Dune du Pilat, France", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Five things, checked in the same order every time, catch the mistakes that kill pilots on launch.", "body": ["The 5-point check is the last thing a paraglider pilot does before launching. The five points, in the order taught by the BHPA and most schools, are: helmet on and chin strap done up; harness buckles all closed, leg straps first, and carabiners locked; risers and lines clear, untwisted and the brakes free; wind checked for direction, strength and gusts; and airspace and traffic clear.", "The DHV rule is blunt: no launch without a start check, ever. Its 2010 analysis of a fatal fall from a harness at Tegelberg found that an open-strap error is almost impossible to miss if the buckles are checked by hand rather than by eye. Leg-sack harnesses hide the buckles inside the sack, which is exactly why touching them matters.", "What pilots get wrong is treating the check as done after an aborted launch. A 2025 DHV accident report from Werfenweng describes an experienced pilot who aborted once, relaunched without repeating the check, and fell out of his harness. The check is repeated in full every single time the glider is laid out again."], "rel": ["sf-hook-in", "sf-launch-abort", "sf-line-tangles", "sf-helmets"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble: Paragliding safety and the 5-point check", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/paragliding-safety"}, {"t": "DHV: 5-Punkte-Check (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/Spezialthemen/6_2010_165_5_punkte_check.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV accident report, Werfenweng, February 2025 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2025/UnfallberichtDHV28032025.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-reserve-parachute", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.76, "lon": 10.81, "name": "Which reserve parachute should you carry, and when do you throw it?", "place": "Explained over Malcesine, Lake Garda, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Half of Germany's fatal pilots in 2024 might have lived if they had thrown their reserve at once.", "body": ["A paragliding reserve is a second parachute packed into the harness and thrown by hand if the wing becomes unflyable. There are three families. Round reserves are the oldest and simplest. Square and multi-sided reserves open faster and swing less under the pilot. Rogallo-type reserves are steerable, with a glide, so a pilot can fly away from a cliff or river, but they need height and practice. Many instructors recommend a square for pilots who do not fly acro.", "Reserves sold in Europe are certified to EN 12491. Supair's manual states that the parachute must open in under 5 seconds and sink no faster than 5.5 metres per second at its maximum load, which it compares to jumping from a height of about 1.8 metres. Real landings vary: the same manual records two deployments at 5.2 and 9 metres per second. The buying mistake is ignoring the maximum load. Pilot, harness, wing and kit must all sit under the certified figure.", "The rule taught in every school is simple: if the wing is out of control and you are not sure you can fix it before you hit something, throw. Look at the handle, grip it, pull it free and throw the pod hard into clear air. Then pull in the paraglider by its rear risers so it cannot fight the reserve, and prepare for a parachute landing fall. A reserve needs roughly 60 metres to be useful, with 120 to 180 metres typical from throw to stable descent.", "The DHV's 2024 accident analysis counted 10 fatal accidents among German pilots and judged that five might have been prevented by an immediate reserve deployment. Its 2023 report recorded 35 deployments, mostly after collapses, cravats and riser twists, with five serious injuries. What pilots get wrong is waiting. A spinning wing can produce enough G-force to make reaching the handle impossible. Practising the throw in a hanging harness, and at an SIV course over water, turns a decision into a reflex."], "rel": ["sf-repacking", "sf-spiral-blackout", "sf-line-tangles", "sf-harness-protectors"], "src": [{"t": "Supair reserve parachute manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.supair.com/document/notices/reserve_en.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding, safety section", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "sf-repacking", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 19.19, "lon": -100.13, "name": "How often should a reserve parachute be repacked?", "place": "Explained over Valle de Bravo, Mexico", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A reserve that has sat compressed for years can open slowly, or not at all.", "body": ["Reserve manufacturers such as Supair state that the parachute must be repacked every six months. Folded nylon takes a set, the pod compresses, and humidity creeps in. Before repacking, the canopy is aired for at least 12 hours, the lines and fabric are checked for damage, and a hang test confirms the pilot can actually reach and pull the handle in the harness.", "Reserves also have a service life. Supair designs its reserves for at least ten years, after which a professional inspection is required, and the Canadian federation HPAC notes that most manufacturers advise against using a reserve older than ten years. HPAC's 2024 safety advisory found second-hand reserves that were expired, packed wrongly, and in one case packed with a vario inside.", "What pilots get wrong is treating a repack as a chore to postpone. Many clubs run repack evenings where you throw your own reserve in a hall, see how it opens, and repack it under supervision. Doing this once a year is the cheapest safety training in the sport."], "rel": ["sf-reserve-parachute", "sf-currency"], "src": [{"t": "Supair reserve parachute manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.supair.com/document/notices/reserve_en.pdf"}, {"t": "HPAC Safety Advisory, Spring 2024", "u": "https://hpac.ca/?p=4294"}]}, {"id": "sf-harness-protectors", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.45, "lon": 12.17, "name": "How much does a harness back protector actually protect?", "place": "Explained over Hohe Salve, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Certified protectors are dropped from 1.65 metres and must keep the impact under 50 g.", "body": ["Most paragliding harnesses carry a protector under and behind the seat: a block of foam, an inflatable airbag that fills with ram air, or a mix of both. Its job is to absorb the vertical impact of a hard landing or a collapse close to the ground, the most common way pilots break their backs and pelvises.", "In Europe harnesses are tested to EN 1651 and the German LTF rules. In the LTF shock test a dummy is dropped from 1.65 metres onto the protector. The peak must stay under 50 g, under 38 g for more than 7 milliseconds, and under 20 g for more than 25 milliseconds. Gin's test report for one airbag harness recorded peaks of about 21 g, well inside the limit.", "What pilots get wrong is assuming the protector works on launch. An airbag only inflates once there is airflow, so it is empty when a pilot stumbles on take-off. Foam works at once but adds bulk. In water, the protector's buoyancy can push a pilot face down, which is why the DHV treats it as a drowning hazard rather than a flotation aid."], "rel": ["sf-water-landing", "sf-landing-approach", "sf-hook-in"], "src": [{"t": "Gin Gliders Yeti Convertible LTF airbag test report (PDF)", "u": "https://www.gingliders.com/pdf/gr-yeti-convertible-airbag-test-ltf.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV: Todesfalle Wasser (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/StartLandetechnik/7_Todesfalle_Wasser.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-helmets", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.89, "lon": -117.25, "name": "What kind of helmet should a paraglider pilot wear?", "place": "Explained over Torrey Pines, California, USA", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Bike helmets are not tested for the sideways scrape of a dragged launch or a tree.", "body": ["The European standard written for paragliding and hang gliding helmets is EN 966, which also covers microlight flying. It sets requirements and tests for shock absorption, penetration resistance, the chin strap and its fastening, field of vision and head mobility, and puts paragliding and hang gliding helmets in its own category, HPG. Helmets certified only for cycling or climbing are tested for different impacts and are not a substitute.", "The helmet is point one of the 5-point check for a reason. Launches fail, pilots get dragged across rocks in strong wind, and landings end in trees. The 2019 USHPA hang gliding accident summary lists a fatal tandem tow in which nobody wore a helmet. Open-face helmets keep peripheral vision and hearing; full-face helmets add chin protection at the cost of both.", "What pilots get wrong is a loose strap. A helmet that comes off at the first impact does nothing for the second. Replace any helmet that has taken a real hit, even if the shell looks fine, because the foam inside crushes once."], "rel": ["sf-five-point-check", "sf-tree-landing", "sf-first-aid"], "src": [{"t": "EN 966: Helmets for airborne sports", "u": "https://genorma.com/en/standards/en-966-2012"}, {"t": "USHPA 2019 hang gliding accident summary", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/2019-hg-accident-summary.aspx"}]}, {"id": "sf-hook-in", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.56, "lon": 10.76, "name": "Why do pilots fall out of their harnesses?", "place": "Explained over Tegelberg, Bavaria, Germany", "icon": "skull", "hidden": true, "hook": "In 2010 a 19-year-old held on with his arms until he could not, then fell into a lake.", "body": ["On 11 July 2010 a young pilot launched from Tegelberg with the leg and chest straps of his leg-sack harness undone. He hung from the shoulder straps, gripped the risers, and fell from the harness over the Forggensee from an estimated 60 to 80 metres. He died. The DHV investigation found several other open-strap launches on the same harness type that had ended without injury, by luck.", "The problem has not gone away. A DHV safety notice issued in October 2023 counted five falls from leg-sack harnesses without a secondary retention strap in 2022 and 2023, two of them fatal, and a February 2025 report from Werfenweng describes another. In every case distraction during preparation, or a relaunch after an abort, led the pilot to skip closing the buckles. The German LTF airworthiness rules published in February 2025 now require leg-sack harnesses to carry a backup system that links the leg straps when the sack is closed.", "The fix is cheap: close the leg straps first, before anything else, touch every buckle by hand, and use a partner check where another pilot looks at your buckles and carabiners. Hang gliders have the same failure mode with a different name, the hang check. In both sports the rule is identical: nobody launches without being clipped in and checked."], "rel": ["sf-five-point-check", "sf-launch-abort", "sf-harness-protectors"], "src": [{"t": "DHV: 5-Punkte-Check and the Tegelberg accident (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/Spezialthemen/6_2010_165_5_punkte_check.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV: Herausfall-Schutz bei Beinsack-Gurtzeugen, update February 2025 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2025/news/02/Herausfall-Schutz_bei_Beinsack-Gurtzeugen__Liege-Gurtzeuge__-_Update_Februar_2025.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV accident report, Werfenweng, 2025 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2025/UnfallberichtDHV28032025.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-launch-abort", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.46, "lon": 13.26, "name": "When should a pilot abort a launch?", "place": "Explained over Bischling, Werfenweng, Austria", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Swiss data says the most common launch accident is a bad launch run that nobody stopped.", "body": ["A paraglider launch has a decision point: the moment after the wing comes up overhead and before the pilot commits to running off the slope. In those seconds the pilot looks up and checks that the wing is open, straight, free of knots and tangles, and flying in the right direction. If anything is wrong, the pilot stops and pulls the brakes or the rear risers to bring the wing down.", "The SHV accident analyses for 2023 and 2024 both name uncontrolled launch runs that were not aborted as the single most frequent cause of launch-phase accidents in Switzerland, followed by launching with line knots. In 2024, three of the six fatal accidents were launch failures in high alpine terrain. The DHV reports 112 launch and departure incidents in 2024 alone.", "What pilots get wrong is the sunk-cost feeling: the wing is up, people are watching, so they run anyway. The better habit is to decide the abort criteria before pulling up, abort early rather than late, and then do the full pre-flight check again from the start, because a second attempt after an abort is where buckles and lines get missed."], "rel": ["sf-five-point-check", "sf-line-tangles", "sf-peer-pressure", "sf-hook-in"], "src": [{"t": "SHV accident analysis 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2024.pdf"}, {"t": "SHV accident analysis 2023 (PDF)", "u": "https://shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2023.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-landing-approach", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.48, "lon": -111.9, "name": "How do pilots plan a landing approach?", "place": "Explained over Point of the Mountain, Utah, USA", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Landing is where paraglider pilots get hurt most: 146 German reports in 2024.", "body": ["A paraglider cannot go around, so the approach is planned from height. Pilots use two patterns. The aircraft circuit has a downwind leg, a base leg across the wind, and a final leg into wind. The figure-of-eight keeps the pilot facing the field on alternating beats and is used when space is tight. Both aim to arrive on final with enough height to correct, and never with a turn low to the ground.", "The DHV's 2024 statistics show why this matters. Landing produced 146 reports, 101 of them on approach and 45 at touchdown, with 74 serious injuries. Over the long term landing accounts for about 20 percent of all German accidents. Low turns, slow flight near the ground, and being dumped by a thermal on final appear again and again.", "What pilots get wrong is deciding late. The field, the wind direction, the obstacles and the escape options are chosen while still high, and the pilot flies the plan rather than improvising. Keeping some brake in reserve and some speed through the turbulence near the ground, then flaring fully in the last metre, lands a wing softly."], "rel": ["sf-downwind-landing", "sf-valley-wind-timing", "sf-rotor-buildings", "sf-power-lines"], "src": [{"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA safety resources", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/safety"}]}, {"id": "sf-tree-landing", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.93, "lon": 6.87, "name": "What should you do if you land in a tree?", "place": "Explained over Planpraz, Chamonix, France", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "Most pilots rescued from trees above Chamonix are uninjured. The danger comes after.", "body": ["A tree landing is often the safest bad option a pilot has, better than rocks, water or power lines. The technique is to fly into the centre of the thickest tree at minimum speed with the wing level, protect the face with crossed arms, and let the canopy and lines catch on the branches. The glider will usually fall through several branches before it holds.", "Cross Country magazine, drawing on the PGHM mountain rescue team in Chamonix, reports that most of the pilots pulled out of trees are uninjured. The danger comes afterwards, when pilots try to climb down. Once stopped, the pilot secures themselves to the trunk with a sling or the harness straps, checks the wing is holding, and only then uses a phone or radio to call for help.", "What pilots get wrong is impatience. A paraglider caught on a few twigs can let go without warning, and a fall of a few metres onto roots breaks legs and backs. Unless the ground is within reach, the rule is stay put, stay tied in, and wait for people with ropes. The German data for 2023 lists four tandem tree landings, including one from an illegal night flight."], "rel": ["sf-water-landing", "sf-rescue-plb", "sf-first-aid", "sf-tandem-risks"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: How to manage a tree landing", "u": "https://xcmag.com/fly-better/safety/how-to-manage-a-tree-landing"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2023 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/250_Unfallstatistik_Gleitschirm_2023.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-water-landing", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -12.12, "lon": -77.04, "name": "Why is landing in water so often fatal for paraglider pilots?", "place": "Explained over Miraflores, Lima, Peru", "icon": "wave", "hidden": true, "hook": "A wet 22 square metre wing can hold 4 tonnes of water and drag a pilot under.", "body": ["Water is the outlanding pilots underestimate most. The DHV recorded five drownings among German pilots in a single year, four on the coast and one in an alpine river, and its 2018 to 2022 fatality review lists five more water deaths. The reasons are physical. A harness protector floats and tips the pilot face down. Nearly 300 metres of line wrap around arms and legs with every swimming stroke.", "The wing is the worst part. The DHV estimates that a 22 square metre canopy can hold about 4 cubic metres of water, roughly 4 tonnes, and a half-filled wing can be reinflated by wind and drag the pilot through the surf. A deployed reserve pulls even harder. The FAI's water-landing guidance calls the pilot who believes they will just make the shore, and so never prepares for the water, one of the most dangerous situations in the sport.", "If a water landing is unavoidable: open the chest and leg buckles while still high, fly into wind, and slide out of the harness on contact so the wing lands a metre or two behind you. Then move as little as possible to avoid tangling, and cut lines with a hook knife. Pilots flying near large water carry a knife, a radio and an automatic life jacket of at least 200 newtons, which is more than a standard vest because the harness has to be lifted too."], "rel": ["sf-tree-landing", "sf-harness-protectors", "sf-rescue-plb", "sf-site-rules"], "src": [{"t": "DHV: Todesfalle Wasser (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/StartLandetechnik/7_Todesfalle_Wasser.pdf"}, {"t": "FAI: Water landings in paragliding", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/200298"}, {"t": "DHV: Fatal paragliding accidents 2018 to 2022 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallforschung/Toedliche_Gleitschirm-Unfaelle_2018-2022_Auswertung_der_DHV_Unfalldatenbank.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-power-lines", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.8, "lon": 11.73, "name": "How do pilots avoid power lines?", "place": "Explained over Bassano del Grappa, Italy", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "You will not see the wire. You will see the poles, the gap in the trees, or nothing.", "body": ["Power lines are nearly invisible from the air, and a paraglider at 35 kilometres per hour cannot stop or climb to avoid one spotted late. The avoidance rule from Cross Country magazine's safety guidance is to assume a line runs along every road and to every building, look for the shadows of poles, trace a logical route from pole to pole, and expect thinner wires above or below the main ones.", "A pilot who does end up in a line should not touch anything and should not try to climb down. Electricity distributors such as NIE Networks tell anyone caught in overhead lines to stay still and wait for the network to isolate the line. A 2014 USHPA fatality list records a pilot who launched in cloud and flew into power lines; another survivor cut himself free with a hook knife and fell two storeys.", "What pilots get wrong is choosing a field from directly above, where lines are at their least visible. Looking at a field from several angles while still high, and choosing the larger field with the obvious access track and no buildings, removes most of the risk. Local pilots know where the wires are, which is one more reason to ask."], "rel": ["sf-landing-approach", "sf-site-rules", "sf-tree-landing"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Avoiding power lines on landing", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/tuesday-tip-avoiding-power-lines-on-landing/"}, {"t": "NIE Networks: Aerial sports safety", "u": "https://www.nienetworks.co.uk/safety/home-leisure/aerial-sports-safety"}, {"t": "USHPA 2014 fatality list (PDF)", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/page/download.aspx?DocKey=751"}]}, {"id": "sf-strong-wind", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 53.35, "lon": -1.81, "name": "How strong is too strong for a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Mam Tor, Peak District, England", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "If the wind on the hill is faster than your wing, you are going backwards, like it or not.", "body": ["A paraglider flies at a fixed range of airspeeds, roughly 22 to 50 kilometres per hour depending on the wing and the speed bar. If the wind exceeds the wing's top speed the pilot cannot penetrate forward and drifts backwards into the rotor behind the hill. The practical limit on most ridges is lower than that, because gusts and the acceleration of wind over the crest add to the measured speed.", "Strong wind hurts pilots before they even fly. Being dragged across a launch by an inflating wing is a common injury, and the Swiss and German reports list turbulence, wind and thermals as leading causes of lost control on launch. Fatalities in strong conditions are over-represented in mini-wing and speed-flying accidents in the USHPA lists, where pilots chose small wings to cope with wind.", "What pilots get wrong is measuring the wind once. A sensible check is to watch for several minutes, note the gust range rather than the average, and compare the forecast with what the hill is doing. If the decision is marginal, the right answer is to wait. A wing packed in its bag has never been blown into a lee rotor."], "rel": ["sf-rotor-buildings", "sf-launch-abort", "k-window", "sf-mental-state"], "src": [{"t": "SHV accident analysis 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2024.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA 2014 fatality list (PDF)", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/page/download.aspx?DocKey=751"}]}, {"id": "sf-near-clouds", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -4.97, "lon": -39.02, "name": "How close to cloud is a paraglider allowed to fly?", "place": "Explained over Quixada, Ceara, Brazil", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The legal distance from cloud is 1,500 metres sideways and 300 metres below, in most of the world.", "body": ["Paragliders fly under visual flight rules, which means the pilot must be able to see and avoid other aircraft. ICAO Annex 2 sets the standard: above 3,000 feet (about 900 metres), or 1,000 feet above terrain if higher, an aircraft must stay 1,500 metres horizontally and 1,000 feet (about 300 metres) vertically from cloud, with at least 5 kilometres visibility. Lower down, the rule is clear of cloud and in sight of the surface.", "National rules vary and some countries relax the vertical distance for gliders in uncontrolled airspace, so pilots check their own air law. The reason for the rule is not bureaucratic. Light aircraft and gliders pop out of cloud at speed, and a paraglider skimming the base is invisible to them until it is too late.", "What pilots get wrong is treating cloudbase as a target. Lift strengthens as you approach the base of a growing cumulus, and a pilot who leaves the climb late can be pulled in with no view of the ground or other wings. The habit of leaving lift well before base, and flying away from the darkest part of the cloud, keeps the rule and keeps you alive."], "rel": ["sf-cloud-suck", "k-cloudbase", "sf-collision-rules"], "src": [{"t": "SKYbrary: Visual Meteorological Conditions", "u": "https://skybrary.aero/articles/visual-meteorological-conditions-vmc"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Visual flight rules", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visual_flight_rules"}]}, {"id": "sf-collision-rules", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.3, "lon": 5.88, "name": "Who has right of way between two paragliders?", "place": "Explained over Saint-Hilaire-du-Touvet, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Head on, both turn right. On a ridge, the pilot with the hill on their right keeps going.", "body": ["Free-flight collision rules are shared across Europe through the EHPU and published by the DHV. Head-on, both pilots turn right. On a ridge, the pilot with the ridge on their left gives way, because the other pilot cannot turn right without flying into the hill. In a thermal, the first pilot in sets the turn direction and everyone joining follows it. Overtaking is done on the right, at a safe distance, and the pilot being overtaken has priority.", "Near the ground the rule changes: the lower aircraft has priority, and nobody cuts in front of another pilot on final approach. Mid-air collisions are rare but rising. The DHV recorded 11 collisions in 2023, eight of them while thermalling, and 17 in 2024 with one death. A 2017 USHPA fatality was a mid-air that also injured the second pilot.", "What pilots get wrong is assuming the other pilot has seen them. Looking before every turn, keeping the head moving, and giving a thermal gaggle more room than feels necessary matter more than knowing the rule. The rule decides who was right. Looking decides who lives."], "rel": ["sf-radio-protocols", "sf-near-clouds", "sf-site-rules"], "src": [{"t": "DHV: Right of way rules for paragliders and hang gliders (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/08_en/GermanFlyingRules/DHV-Ausweichregeln-A2-engl.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA 2017 fatality list (PDF)", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/page/download.aspx?DocKey=754"}]}, {"id": "sf-radio-protocols", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 4.41, "lon": -76.15, "name": "How do paraglider pilots use radios safely?", "place": "Explained over Roldanillo, Colombia", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "A radio found Horacio Llorens in the Pacific after two and a half hours. A flat battery finds nobody.", "body": ["Radios link pilots to instructors, to each other and to the retrieve driver. Across most of Europe and the United Kingdom pilots use licence-free PMR446 handsets, limited to 500 milliwatts on 16 analogue channels between 446.0 and 446.2 megahertz. Elsewhere, pilots use 2-metre amateur or locally tolerated frequencies, which may need a licence. Wikipedia notes that frequencies vary by country and some are illegal but tolerated.", "The protocol is short messages, callsign first, then listen. Launch and landing are kept quiet so instructors can get through. Before a cross-country flight the group agrees a channel, a check-in interval and what to do if someone goes silent. Spanish acro pilot Horacio Llorens, who landed in the sea off Tahuata in 2014, told the DHV he waited about two and a half hours for rescue and credits his radio with being found.", "What pilots get wrong is treating the radio as entertainment. Chatter blocks an emergency call and drains the battery. A radio clipped where it can be reached with one hand, a spare battery, and a mobile phone as backup form the minimum kit for flying away from the hill."], "rel": ["sf-rescue-plb", "sf-retrieve-planning", "sf-flying-alone", "sf-collision-rules"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: PMR446", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PMR446"}, {"t": "DHV: Todesfalle Wasser, interview with Horacio Llorens (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/StartLandetechnik/7_Todesfalle_Wasser.pdf"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "sf-flying-alone", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.47, "lon": 44.48, "name": "Is it safe to fly a paraglider alone?", "place": "Explained over Gudauri, Georgia", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "The Werfenweng pilot was found the next day, below launch, because nobody saw him go.", "body": ["Flying alone removes two safety layers at once: the second pair of eyes on launch, and the person who notices you have not landed. The DHV's February 2025 accident report from Werfenweng describes an experienced pilot who launched unobserved with his harness buckles open. His body was found the following day just below the take-off. A partner check before launch would almost certainly have caught it.", "The same applies in the air. Several fatal accidents in the DHV and HPAC records show no sign of a reserve attempt, with pilots possibly unconscious or unable to reach the handle. Alone, nobody sees the spiral, nobody calls the helicopter, and nobody knows which valley to search.", "If you do fly alone, the standard precautions are to tell someone your site, your plan and your latest landing time, carry a phone and a satellite tracker or PLB, fly well inside your limits, and treat the pre-flight check as if an instructor were watching. Most hike-and-fly pilots who fly solo in remote mountains run a live tracker for exactly this reason."], "rel": ["sf-hook-in", "sf-rescue-plb", "sf-retrieve-planning", "sf-radio-protocols"], "src": [{"t": "DHV accident report, Werfenweng, 2025 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2025/UnfallberichtDHV28032025.pdf"}, {"t": "HPAC Safety Advisory, Spring 2024", "u": "https://hpac.ca/?p=4294"}]}, {"id": "sf-hypothermia", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.02, "lon": 7.74, "name": "How cold does it get on a paraglider at height?", "place": "Explained over Zermatt, Switzerland", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Climb 3,000 metres above a warm valley and the air is about 20 degrees colder, before the wind.", "body": ["Air cools with height. The standard atmosphere loses 6.5 degrees per 1,000 metres, and on a good thermal day the real rate is close to the dry adiabatic 9.8 degrees per 1,000 metres. A pilot climbing from a 25 degree valley to 3,500 metres can be sitting in air below zero, moving through it at 40 kilometres per hour, for an hour or more.", "Wind chill makes it worse. Environment Canada's index gives minus 20 degrees in a 5 kilometre per hour breeze as feeling like minus 24, and in a 30 kilometre per hour wind as minus 33. Hypothermia begins when core temperature drops below 35 degrees. The early signs are shivering and clumsy hands, then confusion and poor decisions, which in a paraglider means a pilot who can no longer feel the brakes or judge a landing.", "The fix is dressing for the top of the climb, not the launch: a windproof outer layer, gloves rated for the cold, a neck tube and insulated boots. A pod harness adds warmth. Pilots who start to shiver uncontrollably or feel muddled should descend and land while they can still fly accurately."], "rel": ["sf-altitude-sickness", "sf-dehydration", "sf-sun-exposure", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Hypothermia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypothermia"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Wind chill", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_chill"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Lapse rate", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}]}, {"id": "sf-dehydration", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.36, "lon": -118.4, "name": "Why does dehydration make pilots fly badly?", "place": "Explained over Owens Valley, California, USA", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Losing just 1 to 2 percent of your body water measurably impairs thinking. Flying is thinking.", "body": ["A paraglider pilot sits in dry air, often in sun, breathing hard with the effort of a long climb and the stress of turbulence. Fluid is lost through breath and skin without obvious sweat. Research summarised on Wikipedia shows that a loss of 1 to 2 percent of body water already impairs cognitive performance, and losses of 5 to 8 percent bring fatigue and dizziness.", "In the air this shows as slower decisions, poor tracking of other gliders and the kind of lapse that misses a wire on final. Owens Valley, with its 4,000-metre peaks and desert floor, is the classic example of a site where big flights and dry heat combine.", "The practice is to drink before launch, carry a hydration bladder with a hose reachable in flight, and sip regularly rather than waiting for thirst. Alcohol the night before and caffeine in the morning both make it worse. Pilots who find themselves with a headache and a dry mouth late in a flight should treat it as a reason to land early."], "rel": ["sf-sun-exposure", "sf-hypothermia", "sf-mental-state"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Dehydration", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dehydration"}, {"t": "FAA Risk Management Handbook, IMSAFE checklist (PDF)", "u": "https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-sun-exposure", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -20.21, "lon": -70.15, "name": "How much more UV does a pilot get at height?", "place": "Explained over Iquique, Chile", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "UV rises about 10 percent per 1,000 metres, and snow underneath you nearly doubles it.", "body": ["The World Health Organization puts the increase in ultraviolet radiation at about 10 percent for every 1,000 metres of altitude, because there is less atmosphere above to absorb it. A pilot at 3,000 metres is therefore taking roughly a third more UV than someone on the beach below, with nothing between them and the sky but a visor.", "Reflection adds to it. Fresh snow reflects up to 80 percent of UV and almost doubles exposure, which is why spring flying in the Alps burns the underside of the chin and nose. Dry sand reflects about 15 percent and sea foam about 25 percent, so coastal and desert sites such as Iquique add their own share.", "Long flights in the sun cause sunburn, snow blindness and, over the years, skin damage. The practical kit is a visor or sunglasses with UV protection, high-factor sunscreen applied before launch and again after landing, lip protection and a neck tube. Sunburn also dehydrates, and dehydration degrades judgement."], "rel": ["sf-dehydration", "sf-hypothermia", "sf-altitude-sickness"], "src": [{"t": "WHO: Radiation, ultraviolet (UV) questions and answers", "u": "https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/radiation-ultraviolet-(uv)"}]}, {"id": "sf-altitude-sickness", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -9.53, "lon": -77.53, "name": "Can paraglider pilots get altitude sickness?", "place": "Explained over Huaraz, Cordillera Blanca, Peru", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "About 20 percent of people get sick after a quick trip to 2,500 metres. Many launches are higher.", "body": ["Acute mountain sickness normally appears above 2,500 metres and can start as low as 1,500. Around 20 percent of people develop it after ascending rapidly to 2,500 metres and about 40 percent at 3,000 metres. The symptoms are headache, nausea, poor sleep, loss of appetite and tiredness, usually within ten hours of arrival.", "Paraglider pilots are exposed twice over. Mountain sites in the Andes, Himalaya and East Africa have launches and lodging above 2,500 metres, and the flight itself can add thousands of metres for an hour or two. Flying while sick with a headache and nausea is a poor idea in itself. The dangerous forms, high altitude pulmonary and cerebral oedema, can kill and are treated by immediate descent.", "The guidance from mountain medicine is to arrive early and acclimatise, limit the sleeping altitude gain to about 300 metres a day once above 2,500 metres, drink well and avoid alcohol. Anyone with worsening symptoms goes down, and anyone with mild symptoms does not go higher until they clear."], "rel": ["sf-hypothermia", "sf-dehydration", "sf-mental-state", "bar-headed-goose"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Altitude sickness", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altitude_sickness"}]}, {"id": "sf-accident-chain", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.75, "lon": 13.18, "name": "What is the accident chain, and how do you break it?", "place": "Explained over Greifenburg, Austria", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Accidents rarely have one cause. They have five small ones that happen to line up.", "body": ["In 1990 James Reason of the University of Manchester described accidents as holes lining up in slices of Swiss cheese. Each slice is a defence: training, equipment, weather judgement, the pre-flight check, a margin of height. Each has holes. Most days the holes do not align and a mistake is caught by the next layer. An accident is the day they do.", "Paragliding accident reports read exactly like this. The 2002 DHV report on a fatal spiral at Kossen lists a pilot who had just moved up two glider classes against advice, a crash days earlier, a deep racing harness that amplified weight shift, and finally passivity after a collapse. Remove any one link and she probably lands.", "The FAA's risk management handbook offers a tool pilots use before launch: PAVE, for Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment and External pressures, and IMSAFE, a self-check for Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue and Emotion. The point is not paperwork. It is noticing that today you are tired, on a new wing, at a new site, with friends waiting, and that three holes are already lined up."], "rel": ["sf-mental-state", "sf-peer-pressure", "sf-wing-above-level", "sf-incident-reporting"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Swiss cheese model", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swiss_cheese_model"}, {"t": "FAA Risk Management Handbook (PDF)", "u": "https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV fatal spiral accident report, Kössen 2002 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2002/Toedlicher_Unfall_Steilspirale_ausser_Kontrolle.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-currency", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.33, "lon": 5.75, "name": "How quickly do paragliding skills fade?", "place": "Explained over Chabre, Laragne, France", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "The BHPA rates an EN A wing as right for experienced pilots who fly under 25 hours a year.", "body": ["Paragliding is a motor skill, and like any motor skill it decays without practice. The BHPA's expanded glider class descriptions make this explicit: an EN A wing suits experienced pilots who fly fewer than about 25 hours a year, and an EN B suits those flying under 50. The implication is that a pilot's safe wing class depends on how much they fly now, not how much they once flew.", "The BHPA has published a Safety Matters article titled Maintaining Currency, and Flybubble's safety guidance tells low-airtime or out-of-practice pilots never to skip the full equipment inspection. The first flight after a winter lay-off is where launches go wrong, approaches are misjudged and checks are forgotten.", "The remedies are simple and most pilots skip them: ground handling in a field before the first flight of the season, a mellow evening flight before a strong thermic day, a reserve repack and a glider check over winter, and honest logging of hours. Six flights in a week teaches more than six flights spread over a year."], "rel": ["sf-en-classes", "sf-wing-above-level", "sf-repacking", "sf-accident-chain"], "src": [{"t": "BHPA expanded EN glider class descriptions (PDF)", "u": "https://dhpc.org.uk/assets/files/Exam%20Revision%20Material/Glider%20ratings%20-%20en926.pdf"}, {"t": "BHPA Safety Matters articles", "u": "https://www.bhpa.co.uk/safety/matters"}, {"t": "Flybubble: Paragliding safety", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/paragliding-safety"}]}, {"id": "sf-tandem-risks", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.68, "lon": 11.07, "name": "How risky is a tandem paragliding flight?", "place": "Explained over Hornle, Bad Kohlgrub, Germany", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "In Germany's 2023 tandem reports, passengers were hurt five times as often as pilots.", "body": ["A tandem flight puts a passenger under a larger wing with a licensed pilot. Tandem wings fly faster, resist collapse better and sink slightly faster than solo wings. The DHV's 2023 statistics list 21 tandem reports in which 10 passengers and 2 pilots were seriously injured. Nine incidents happened in the take-off area, eight of them during the launch run, and four ended in trees.", "The passenger is the weak point on launch. They must run until told to stop, not sit down early, and keep their legs up on landing. The pilot briefs this in a minute and then has to manage two bodies, a big wing and the wind. In 2024 the DHV recorded 18 tandem reports with nine seriously injured passengers.", "What customers get wrong is choosing on price. A tandem pilot should hold a tandem rating, carry a reserve sized for two, use a child-proof harness for children and refuse to fly in marginal wind. The 2019 USHPA summary describes a fatal tandem boat tow with an unrated pilot, substandard tow gear, no reserve and no helmets. Every one of those is a question a passenger can ask."], "rel": ["sf-launch-abort", "sf-tree-landing", "sf-accident-stats", "sf-helmets"], "src": [{"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2023 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/250_Unfallstatistik_Gleitschirm_2023.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA 2019 accident summary", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/2019-hg-accident-summary.aspx"}]}, {"id": "sf-incident-reporting", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 50.88, "lon": -0.21, "name": "Why should pilots report incidents that hurt nobody?", "place": "Explained over Devil's Dyke, Sussex, England", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "The BHPA database holds more than 3,300 reports. Most of the lessons in this encyclopedia come from them.", "body": ["Every national federation runs an incident reporting system. The BHPA's public database holds over 3,300 entries, searchable by wing type and date, each recording the pilot's rating and hours, the site, the wind, the wing and the outcome. The DHV publishes an annual analysis of around 385 reports, USHPA asks members to report anything they witness, and the SHV runs an online database which received 93 voluntary reports in 2024.", "Reports matter because fatal accidents are rare and near misses are common. The DHV's 2024 analysis found that incidents on EN C two-liner wings nearly doubled within a year, a trend only visible because pilots reported collapses and spirals that ended without injury. The SHV notes that its reports are a fraction of the real total: the Swiss accident prevention council BFU counts close to 1,000 injured free-flyers a year.", "What pilots get wrong is shame. A report of a blown launch or a near collision is not an admission of guilt, it is data. The BHPA publishes reports in anonymised summary form, and reading a page of them before a season does more for safety than any new piece of kit."], "rel": ["sf-accident-stats", "sf-accident-chain", "sf-en-classes"], "src": [{"t": "BHPA incident reports database", "u": "https://bhpa.co.uk/safety/incidents"}, {"t": "SHV accident analysis 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2024.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-rescue-plb", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.7, "lon": 8.85, "name": "How does a personal locator beacon get a pilot rescued?", "place": "Explained over Disentis, Switzerland", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": false, "hook": "One button, 406 megahertz, and a satellite network that has helped rescue more than 63,000 people.", "body": ["A personal locator beacon is a one-purpose device. Switched on, it transmits an SOS with its GPS position on 406 megahertz at 5 watts or more to the Cospas-Sarsat satellite system, which passes it to the national rescue centre. There is no subscription. Between 1982 and the end of 2023 the system helped rescue at least 63,745 people. Most beacons also carry a 121.5 megahertz homing signal for helicopters.", "Satellite messengers such as inReach and SPOT work differently. They use commercial networks, add tracking and two-way text, and need a subscription. Their SOS transmitters are weaker and more easily blocked by trees and terrain, which is why some pilots carry both. Either way the device must be registered to the owner with emergency contacts and a note that the activity is paragliding.", "What pilots get wrong is carrying the beacon in a rucksack strapped under the harness. If you are hanging in a tree with a broken arm it must be reachable with one hand, on the chest or shoulder strap. A phone is still the first call where there is signal, and a radio to other pilots is often faster than either."], "rel": ["sf-radio-protocols", "sf-flying-alone", "sf-tree-landing", "sf-retrieve-planning"], "src": [{"t": "Cumbria Soaring Club: Satellite beacons for pilots", "u": "https://cumbriasoaringclub.co.uk/kb/satellitebeacons.php"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Cospas-Sarsat", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cospas-Sarsat"}]}, {"id": "sf-retrieve-planning", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.84, "lon": -120.02, "name": "How do pilots plan a cross-country retrieve?", "place": "Explained over Chelan, Washington, USA", "icon": "train", "hidden": false, "hook": "The flight ends at the landing field. The day ends when someone knows exactly where you are.", "body": ["A cross-country flight finishes somewhere nobody chose, often hours from the car. Retrieve planning starts before launch: the group agrees a radio channel, a tracking page everyone can see, a driver or a deal on who drives, and a cut-off time after which silence means a search. Pilots carry a phone, a battery pack, water, cash and a card with local emergency numbers.", "The retrieve is also the safety net. Several fatal accidents in the DHV and USHPA records involved pilots who were not found for hours or until the next day. A live tracker on a phone or satellite messenger, updating every few minutes, turns a valley-wide search into a drive to a pin on a map.", "What pilots get wrong is landing out with 5 percent battery and no plan. Landing near a road, in sight of a village, before dusk, with a message sent while still in the air, costs a few kilometres of distance and saves a night in a field. Competitions in Chelan and elsewhere run organised retrieves for exactly this reason."], "rel": ["sf-radio-protocols", "sf-rescue-plb", "sf-flying-alone", "sf-downwind-landing"], "src": [{"t": "Cumbria Soaring Club: Satellite beacons for pilots", "u": "https://cumbriasoaringclub.co.uk/kb/satellitebeacons.php"}, {"t": "DHV accident report, Werfenweng, 2025 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2025/UnfallberichtDHV28032025.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-first-aid", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -18.85, "lon": -41.95, "name": "What first aid should pilots know for a flying accident?", "place": "Explained over Governador Valadares, Brazil", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Ankles and backs are what break. Moving a pilot with a spinal injury can mean a wheelchair.", "body": ["A study of 1,411 BHPA injury reports from 2000 to 2014 found that most paragliding injuries are to the lower limbs, with the ankle the most common site. Behind them come the spine and pelvis from hard vertical landings, the injuries harness protectors are designed to soften. Head injuries follow when helmets are missing or loose.", "For a witness the priorities are the standard ones. Make the scene safe: a wing still attached to a pilot in wind can drag them, so secure or disconnect it. Call for help with a location, ideally a grid reference or coordinates from a phone. Check breathing and bleeding. Do not move a pilot who complains of back or neck pain unless they are in immediate danger, and keep them warm while waiting.", "What pilots get wrong is leaving it to someone else. A one-day outdoor first aid course covers everything above, and the NIE Networks guidance for aerial sports asks clubs to train members in rescue and first aid. A small kit in the harness, with a foil blanket and a dressing, weighs less than a sandwich."], "rel": ["sf-helmets", "sf-harness-protectors", "sf-rescue-plb", "sf-tree-landing"], "src": [{"t": "Incidents and Injuries in Foot-Launched Flying Extreme Sports (Aerospace Medicine and Human Performance, 2017)", "u": "https://doi.org/10.3357/AMHP.4745.2017"}, {"t": "NIE Networks: Aerial sports safety", "u": "https://www.nienetworks.co.uk/safety/home-leisure/aerial-sports-safety"}]}, {"id": "sf-mental-state", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.47, "lon": -5.32, "name": "How do your mental state and the pressure to fly decide a flight?", "place": "Explained over Piedrahita, Spain", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "The flight you most want is the flight you should most carefully refuse.", "body": ["The FAA's risk management handbook lists five hazardous attitudes with their antidotes. Anti-authority (do not tell me) is answered with follow the rules. Impulsivity (do it quickly) with not so fast, think first. Invulnerability (it will not happen to me) with it could happen to me. Macho (I can do it) with taking chances is foolish. Resignation (what is the use) with I can make a difference. Most paragliding accident reports contain at least one of them.", "Before every flight the same handbook suggests the IMSAFE self-check: Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue and Emotion. It also names external pressure, the E in its PAVE checklist, as a hazard in its own right. For paraglider pilots that is the last day of a trip, the forecast that was better than the sky, the group already in the air, the camera, and the money spent getting there. None of these change the wind. All of them change the decision.", "Accident reports are full of it. The 2019 USHPA summary lists crossing a ridge with no landing option, cross-country from an urban site with few fields, and flying into sea fog as poor risk decisions. The DHV's 2002 Kossen report describes a pilot who moved up two wing classes against the advice of friends and an instructor. Swiss accident analyses list mental blockage as a cause on launch and in flight.", "What pilots get wrong is believing the decision is made on launch. It is made the night before, when the forecast is read, the sleep is had, and the conditions under which you will not fly are written down. Experienced pilots pack up in front of beginners without apology, say out loud why, and go for coffee. A pilot who sets limits in a calm moment only has to obey them in a tempting one."], "rel": ["sf-peer-pressure", "sf-accident-chain", "sf-strong-wind", "sf-dehydration"], "src": [{"t": "FAA Risk Management Handbook (PDF)", "u": "https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA 2019 accident summary", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/2019-hg-accident-summary.aspx"}, {"t": "SHV accident analysis 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2024.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-peer-pressure", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.52, "lon": 0.49, "name": "How does peer pressure get pilots hurt?", "place": "Explained over Castejon de Sos, Spain", "icon": "runner", "hidden": true, "hook": "Nobody says launch. They just launch, and you are the last one left on the hill.", "body": ["Peer pressure in free flight is rarely spoken. It is the sight of five wings in the air while you stand on launch, the group that moved to a harder site, or the friend who bought a hotter wing and says you would be fine on one. The FAA's macho attitude, I can do it, is answered with taking chances is foolish, but on a crowded launch that answer needs saying out loud.", "The DHV's October 2023 harness safety notice found that distraction during launch preparation, often from other people, was the common thread in pilots forgetting their leg straps. Its 2002 Kossen report shows the opposite failure: a pilot ignored peers who were right. The skill is telling the difference between a crowd's enthusiasm and a mentor's warning.", "Groups that fly together safely build habits that make refusal easy: a partner check that everyone does, a shared rule that anyone can call a no-fly for themselves without explanation, and a culture where the pilot who packs up gets a thumbs up rather than a joke. The wing that stays in the bag is never in the accident report."], "rel": ["sf-mental-state", "sf-wing-above-level", "sf-hook-in"], "src": [{"t": "DHV safety notice on leg-sack harnesses, October 2023 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2025/news/02/Sicherheitshinweis_Beinsack-Gurtzeuge_ohne_zus%C3%A4tzliche_Herausfall-Sicherungen_2025.pdf"}, {"t": "FAA Risk Management Handbook (PDF)", "u": "https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/2022-06/risk_management_handbook_2A.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-accident-stats", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.68, "lon": 11.53, "name": "What do paragliding accident statistics actually say?", "place": "Explained over Brauneck, Lenggries, Germany", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "Germany: 385 reports, 167 serious injuries and 10 deaths in 2024. Half of the fatal wings were EN B.", "body": ["The DHV runs the most detailed public dataset. In 2024 German pilots filed 385 accident and incident reports, 167 with serious injury and 10 fatal, up from 5 fatal in 2023. Landing produced the most reports (146), launch and departure 112. Collapses caused 63 reports and 4 deaths, and four fatalities came from lines tangling with the wing after a collapse. The DHV judged that five of the ten deaths might have been prevented by throwing the reserve at once.", "Its review of 48 fatal accidents between 2018 and 2022 undercuts a common belief. Half of the pilots who died were flying EN B wings, 10 percent EN A, 23 percent EN C and 4 percent EN D. Collapses, front and asymmetric, caused 19 of the 48; stalls and spins 7; water landings 5. Nearly half the dead were aged 51 to 60.", "Internationally the picture is similar. Switzerland recorded 6 deaths in 2024 and 10 in 2023. A study of BHPA reports found about 47 fatalities per 100,000 participants per year for paragliding, with the ankle the most common injury. Wikipedia cites French figures of two deaths and about sixty serious injuries per 10,000 licensed pilots in 2011. The common thread is not exotic failure. It is launch, landing, collapses close to terrain and reserves thrown too late."], "rel": ["sf-incident-reporting", "sf-reserve-parachute", "sf-en-classes", "sf-accident-chain"], "src": [{"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV: Fatal paragliding accidents 2018 to 2022 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallforschung/Toedliche_Gleitschirm-Unfaelle_2018-2022_Auswertung_der_DHV_Unfalldatenbank.pdf"}, {"t": "Incidents and Injuries in Foot-Launched Flying Extreme Sports (2017)", "u": "https://doi.org/10.3357/AMHP.4745.2017"}]}, {"id": "sf-en-classes", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.41, "lon": 8.14, "name": "What do the EN A, B, C and D wing classes mean for safety?", "place": "Explained over Fiesch, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The letter on the wing tells you how it misbehaves, not whether you will survive the misbehaviour.", "body": ["Paragliders sold in Europe are flight-tested to EN 926-2 and given a class from A to D based on how they react to collapses, stalls and spirals with the pilot doing nothing. EN A wings have maximum passive safety and recover on their own. EN B wings have good passive safety. EN C wings have moderate passive safety, with dynamic reactions and recovery that may need precise pilot input. EN D wings are demanding, with potentially violent reactions.", "The BHPA's expanded descriptions tie each class to hours. EN A suits any pilot including those in their first year or flying under 25 hours a year. Some EN B wings suit pilots with 30 or more hours including 10 in thermals. EN C is for pilots with several hundred hours and SIV training who fly regularly. EN D is for pilots flying 200 or more hours a year in strong conditions.", "What pilots get wrong is reading the letter as a safety guarantee. The DHV's fatality review found half of German pilots killed between 2018 and 2022 were on EN B wings, and in 2024 incidents on EN C two-liners nearly doubled in a year. The class describes the wing in a test over a lake. The pilot's reaction in turbulence over rocks is the rest of the story."], "rel": ["sf-wing-above-level", "sf-currency", "sf-accident-stats", "sf-spiral-blackout"], "src": [{"t": "BHPA expanded EN glider class descriptions (PDF)", "u": "https://dhpc.org.uk/assets/files/Exam%20Revision%20Material/Glider%20ratings%20-%20en926.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV: Fatal paragliding accidents 2018 to 2022 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallforschung/Toedliche_Gleitschirm-Unfaelle_2018-2022_Auswertung_der_DHV_Unfalldatenbank.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-wing-above-level", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.67, "lon": 12.4, "name": "What happens when you fly a wing above your level?", "place": "Explained over Kossen, Tyrol, Austria", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "She jumped two classes against everyone's advice. Days later the wing spiralled and she never reacted.", "body": ["In August 2002 a 47-year-old pilot died at Kossen on a high-performance wing she had just bought, having moved straight up from a beginner class against the advice of friends and an instructor. Days before she had walked away from a collapse-related crash. On the day a large collapse surged into a spiral dive, and witnesses saw her descend 100 to 200 metres with no visible input. The DHV called the class jump an unacceptable safety risk and said a small brake input would probably have ended the spiral.", "The mechanism is not that hot wings collapse more. It is that they recover less by themselves and demand a correct input within a second or two, while the pilot is being thrown sideways in the harness. The HPAC's 2024 advisory describes a P4 pilot's first flight on a new EN C wing: descent rates up to 19 metres per second, nearly 2,000 metres of height lost, no reserve thrown, and a fatal impact.", "The BHPA's class guidance is explicit: an EN C needs several hundred hours and SIV training, an EN D 200 hours a year in strong air. What pilots get wrong is believing the step up will make them better. It makes them busier. The pilots who progress safely spend a season on each wing, fly it in strong conditions, and take an SIV course before moving on."], "rel": ["sf-en-classes", "sf-spiral-blackout", "sf-currency", "sf-mental-state"], "src": [{"t": "DHV fatal spiral accident report, Kössen 2002 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2002/Toedlicher_Unfall_Steilspirale_ausser_Kontrolle.pdf"}, {"t": "HPAC Safety Advisory, Spring 2024", "u": "https://hpac.ca/?p=4294"}, {"t": "BHPA expanded EN glider class descriptions (PDF)", "u": "https://dhpc.org.uk/assets/files/Exam%20Revision%20Material/Glider%20ratings%20-%20en926.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-speed-system", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 43.36, "lon": 12.74, "name": "What happens when the speed system fails?", "place": "Explained over Monte Cucco, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A speed bar line that snaps on one side turns your glider into a wing that wants to turn.", "body": ["The speed system is a foot bar in the harness connected by two lines and pulleys to the front risers. Pushing it pulls the leading edge down, lowers the angle of attack and makes the wing fly faster, at the cost of stability. It is used to penetrate headwinds and escape sink. Advance's manuals, like every manufacturer's, require all equipment to be checked for damage and airworthiness before each flight, and the speed lines and pulleys are part of that.", "Failures come in three forms. A line or pulley breaks on one side, so the wing is accelerated asymmetrically and turns towards the slow side; the pilot releases the bar and flies on brakes. A line jams or a bar hooks on the harness, leaving the wing accelerated and less stable in turbulence. Or a riser is connected wrongly on the ground, which the pre-flight check should catch.", "What pilots get wrong is holding the bar in rough air. An accelerated wing collapses more readily and more dynamically, which is why Advance's flight manual tells pilots in severe turbulence to release the speed bar fully before applying any stabilising brake, and to ease off the bar whenever the wing pitches forward. SIV instructors teach the same reflex: feet off the bar first, then deal with the wing."], "rel": ["sf-five-point-check", "sf-line-tangles", "sf-en-classes"], "src": [{"t": "Advance Alpha 8 manual: general safety advice", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/alpha_8/4006"}, {"t": "Advance Alpha 8 manual: flight characteristics, accelerated flight", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/alpha_8/4009"}]}, {"id": "sf-line-tangles", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.65, "lon": 24.75, "name": "What is a cravat, and how do you clear one?", "place": "Explained over Sopot, Bulgaria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Four German pilots died in 2024 because lines wrapped the wing after a collapse.", "body": ["A cravat is a wingtip caught in its own lines. On the ground it usually happens when a tip curls over and falls through the A lines during inflation; in the air it follows a big asymmetric collapse, a dust devil, or heavy braking in a thermal. The trapped fabric creates drag on one side, the wing turns towards it, and if nothing is done the turn becomes a spiral dive. The DHV counted four deaths in 2024 from lines tangling with the wing after a collapse.", "The fixes, in the order Flybubble teaches them: shift weight away from the cravat and use a little opposite brake to keep flying straight and clear of terrain. Then pull a sharp big ear on the cravatted side to create slack so the fabric can escape, or pull the stabilo line on that side to draw the tip back out. A full stall also clears cravats but only for pilots with SIV training, because a failed stall makes everything worse.", "What pilots get wrong is spending too long on it. Low, or unsure the cravat will clear in time, the answer is the reserve. On the ground the answer is simpler: line checks before every launch, and looking up at the wing before committing to the run. The SHV lists launching with line knots as the second most common launch accident cause."], "rel": ["sf-reserve-parachute", "sf-five-point-check", "sf-launch-abort", "sf-spiral-blackout"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble: How to fix a cravat", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/fix-paraglider-cravatte"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}, {"t": "SHV accident analysis 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2024.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-downwind-landing", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -44.92, "lon": 168.73, "name": "Why is a downwind landing so much worse than it sounds?", "place": "Explained over Coronet Peak, Queenstown, New Zealand", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Impact energy grows with the square of ground speed. A 10 km/h tailwind is not 10 percent worse.", "body": ["A paraglider lands at its airspeed minus the wind. Into a 10 kilometre per hour breeze a wing trimming at 38 kilometres per hour touches down at 28 over the ground, a jog. With the same 10 kilometre per hour wind behind, the ground speed is 48. Kinetic energy rises with the square of speed, so the downwind landing carries roughly three times the energy of the into-wind one, and the pilot has to run at a sprint they cannot sustain.", "The Swiss federation's 2024 analysis lists tailwind landings among its recurring landing accident causes, alongside stalls from over-braking and poor approach choice. Broken ankles and wrists are the usual outcome, and a pilot who tries to turn into wind at the last second instead of accepting the fast landing risks a far worse low-turn crash.", "What pilots get wrong is deciding the wind direction from the launch windsock and never checking again. Smoke, dust, flags, crop movement and the drift of other gliders tell the truth over the landing field. If a downwind landing is unavoidable, the technique is to keep the wing flying straight, run hard, flare fully and fall forward into a parachute roll rather than planting the feet."], "rel": ["sf-landing-approach", "sf-valley-wind-timing", "sf-rotor-buildings", "sf-first-aid"], "src": [{"t": "SHV accident analysis 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Unfallmeldungen/Unfallanalysen/2024.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-valley-wind-timing", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.63, "lon": 13.76, "name": "Why does the valley wind change where and when you can land?", "place": "Explained over Gerlitzen, Carinthia, Austria", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The field that was calm at ten o'clock can have a 30 km/h wind blowing up it by two.", "body": ["In mountain valleys the wind runs on a daily cycle. As the sun heats the slopes the warmed air rises and draws air up the valley from the plains, a valley breeze that builds through the afternoon in calm, clear weather. After sunset the slopes cool, the air drains back down, and the flow reverses to a mountain breeze. Pilots in the Alps, the Himalaya and the Andes plan their landings around this clock.", "The consequences are concrete. A landing field that lies across the valley axis becomes a crosswind landing by early afternoon. One with trees or buildings on its up-valley side sits in rotor once the breeze strengthens. Thermals drifting with the valley wind lean downwind, and a pilot who descends through the layer where the breeze starts meets sudden shear. The DHV's long-term data puts landing at about 20 percent of all accidents, and valley wind timing is behind many of them.", "What pilots get wrong is landing on the morning plan. The standard approach is to look at the landing field's windsock from above before committing, to expect the strongest valley wind between early and late afternoon, and to choose fields with a clear upwind edge. Many sites have a stated rule such as landing before a certain hour on strong days, for exactly this reason."], "rel": ["k-valley-breeze", "sf-landing-approach", "sf-downwind-landing", "sf-rotor-buildings"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Mountain breeze and valley breeze", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_breeze_and_valley_breeze"}, {"t": "DHV Unfallstatistik Gleitschirm 2024 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_fliegen/Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallstatistik/Gleitschirm/2024_Unfallstatistik_GS.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-rotor-buildings", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.66, "lon": -122.49, "name": "What is rotor, and why do buildings and tree lines make it?", "place": "Explained over Mussel Rock, Pacifica, California, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": true, "hook": "The same wind that holds you up on the front of a hill rolls like surf behind it.", "body": ["Wind flowing over an obstacle does not simply go over and carry on. Behind a ridge, a building, a tree line or a hangar it curls back on itself in a horizontal vortex called rotor. In mountain lee waves the rotor sits roughly at the height of the peak and is described in meteorology as dangerously turbulent, a factor in airliner accidents as well as a known killer of paragliders and hang gliders.", "The small version behind a barn or a row of poplars is just as capable of collapsing a wing a few metres off the ground, where there is no height to recover. The DHV's fatality review lists three deaths from dust devils and the Swiss analyses list turbulence and difficult site conditions as common landing causes. USHPA's 2014 list includes a mini-wing deflated by mechanical turbulence in strong wind.", "What pilots get wrong is treating the windward side of an obstacle as the only hazard. The rule taught for landing is to stay upwind of anything taller than a car, to expect the turbulence to extend many times the obstacle's height downwind, and to carry extra speed through the last 30 metres. Coastal sites like Mussel Rock show the lesson on a large scale: the lift band in front of the cliff ends in rotor behind it."], "rel": ["sf-strong-wind", "sf-landing-approach", "sf-valley-wind-timing", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Rotor (meteorology)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rotor_(meteorology)"}, {"t": "DHV: Fatal paragliding accidents 2018 to 2022 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallforschung/Toedliche_Gleitschirm-Unfaelle_2018-2022_Auswertung_der_DHV_Unfalldatenbank.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA 2014 fatality list (PDF)", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/page/download.aspx?DocKey=751"}]}, {"id": "sf-site-rules", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -33.93, "lon": 18.4, "name": "Why do site rules and local knowledge matter so much?", "place": "Explained over Lion's Head, Cape Town, South Africa", "icon": "home", "hidden": false, "hook": "The locals know where the wire is, when the sea breeze arrives and which farmer shoots.", "body": ["Every established flying site has rules: where to launch and land, which hours or wind directions are off limits, how to share the ridge, and who to call. They exist because someone found out the hard way. Flybubble's safety guidance puts a site assessment with an experienced local pilot at the top of its list for any unfamiliar hill, covering wind on launch, hazards, traffic flow, landing fields and emergency options.", "Local knowledge is the kind of thing that never appears on a forecast: the gully that produces rotor in a southerly, the power line hidden by the tree line on the approach, the hour the sea breeze reaches the ridge, the tide table for the beach landing. The DHV's water-landing guidance tells coastal visitors to ask locals about hazards and to know the tide.", "What visiting pilots get wrong is assuming their home skills transfer. A good habit is to find the club website, read the site guide, call the number on it, watch the locals fly for an hour and launch after them, not before. Sites get closed by one visiting pilot landing in the wrong field. The rules protect the pilot and the site."], "rel": ["sf-power-lines", "sf-water-landing", "sf-collision-rules", "sf-valley-wind-timing"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble: Paragliding safety", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/paragliding-safety"}, {"t": "DHV: Todesfalle Wasser (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/StartLandetechnik/7_Todesfalle_Wasser.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-cloud-suck", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -30.75, "lon": 150.72, "name": "Can you survive being sucked into a thunderstorm?", "place": "Explained over Manilla, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": true, "hook": "Ewa Wisnierska was carried to nearly 10,000 metres in a 2007 storm. The pilot beside her died.", "body": ["On 14 February 2007, training for the World Championships near Manilla, German pilot Ewa Wisnierska was pulled into a cumulonimbus. Her GPS recorded a climb to around 10,000 metres, higher than Everest, in air far below freezing with hail and lightning. She lost consciousness, came to still flying, and landed more than three hours later about 60 kilometres away, covered in ice. Chinese pilot He Zhongpin, caught in the same storm, was killed by lightning.", "Cloud suck is the strong lift under a growing cumulus that gets stronger, not weaker, as you approach base. Under a storm cloud it can exceed any paraglider's descent rate. The DHV's guidance on rapid descents is to leave the lift first, then centre sinking air, then descend, using big ears with speed bar or a B-line stall. A spiral dive inside a strong updraft is discouraged because the lift pushes you off course and the G-force can black you out.", "What pilots get wrong is leaving late. The survival answer is to leave while leaving is still possible: the moment a cloud shows vertical growth, a dark base, or lift that keeps increasing, fly away from it upwind at full speed and get down. Wisnierska's survival is called a miracle for a reason. The right lesson is He Zhongpin's."], "rel": ["sf-near-clouds", "k-cloudbase", "sf-spiral-blackout", "sf-hypothermia"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Ewa Wisnierska", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewa_Wi%C5%9Bnierska"}, {"t": "Cloud Appreciation Society: Paraglider in a cumulonimbus", "u": "https://cloudappreciationsociety.org/?p=1081"}, {"t": "DHV: Steilspirale and rapid descent techniques (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/FlugpraxisFlugtechnik/22_2015_195_steilspirale.pdf"}]}, {"id": "sf-spiral-blackout", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.41, "lon": 9.86, "name": "Can a spiral dive make a pilot black out?", "place": "Explained over Andelsbuch, Vorarlberg, Austria", "icon": "skull", "hidden": true, "hook": "Untrained people black out between 4 and 6 g. A hard spiral on a paraglider gets there.", "body": ["A spiral dive is the fastest way down a paraglider has, with sink rates well over 5 metres per second and much more in an aggressive one. The cost is G-force. The pilot's weight pulls blood from the head, vision greys and narrows, and at 4 to 6 g an untrained person loses vision entirely and then consciousness. After G-induced loss of consciousness, average incapacitation is about 12 seconds unconscious and another 15 confused.", "Settled into a spiral, those seconds are enough to reach the ground. On 11 August 2011 at Andelsbuch a pilot licensed since 1991 entered a deliberate spiral on an EN B wing from a few hundred metres. It accelerated until the canopy was almost level with him, sink reached about 20 metres per second, and witnesses saw no input before impact. The DHV could not tell whether he blacked out, but noted that inner brake alone speeds a spiral up, so outer brake is essential above 14 metres per second.", "A DHV training article on fast descents teaches controlled spirals only to about 6 metres per second, treats extreme spirals as aerobatics for freestyle courses, and favours G-free descents: big ears with speed bar, or a B-line stall. What pilots get wrong is entering hard and exiting late. The exit is begun early, with outside brake and weight, and released gradually so the wing does not surge. Any pilot feeling their vision narrow has already gone too far."], "rel": ["sf-wing-above-level", "sf-reserve-parachute", "sf-cloud-suck", "sf-en-classes"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: G-LOC", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G-LOC"}, {"t": "DHV accident report, Steilspirale Andelsbuch, 11 August 2011 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/02_Sicherheit/Unfaelle/Dateien/Unfallberichte/2011/Unfallbericht_Steilspirale_Andelsbuch_11_8_11.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV: Steilspirale and fast descent techniques (PDF)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/jahre/2024/02_fliegen/Lehrmaterial/Artikel/Flugtechnik/FlugpraxisFlugtechnik/22_2015_195_steilspirale.pdf"}]}, {"id": "eq-cloth", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.45, "lon": 5.33, "name": "How is a paraglider made?", "place": "Explained over Badinieres, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wing is a few kilos of coated nylon, cut by laser and sewn by hand in Vietnam.", "body": ["A paraglider is two sheets of ripstop nylon, an upper and a lower surface, joined by vertical ribs into a row of cells that fill with air through openings near the leading edge. Most of the world's wing cloth comes from one mill, Porcher Sport in Badinieres near Grenoble, whose Skytex fabrics are woven from high tenacity polyamide 6.6 yarn and then coated. The coating, not the weave, is what makes the cloth nearly airtight.", "Weight is quoted in grams per square metre. Standard wings use cloth around 38 to 40 g/m2, while race and hike and fly wings use Skytex 27 at 27 g/m2, and a 21 g/m2 prototype was announced in 2019. Lighter cloth packs smaller and launches easier, but its thinner coating wears faster. Nylon or nitinol rods in the leading edge hold the profile shape, which is why a wing must never be dragged across the ground.", "Design happens in small offices, often near good flying: Ozone near Nice, Advance in Thun, Gin in Yongin, Nova in Terfens. Sewing happens elsewhere. Ozone's factory in Vietnam grew from about 20 staff to around 900, and Nova has sewn in Pecs, Hungary since 1990 and in Vietnam since 2015. Panels are laser cut from computer files, sewn, lined, then every finished wing is measured and inspected before it ships."], "rel": ["eq-lines", "eq-porosity-testing", "eq-uv-damage", "eq-packing"], "src": [{"t": "Innovation in Textiles: weightless to the max (Porcher Skytex)", "u": "https://www.innovationintextiles.com/weightless-to-the-max"}, {"t": "Ozone: how paragliders are made, inside the factory", "u": "https://dev.flyozone.com/paramotor/news/how-paragliders-are-made-inside-ozone-s-factory"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Nova Performance Paragliders", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nova_Performance_Paragliders"}]}, {"id": "eq-lines", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.69, "lon": 10.04, "name": "What are paraglider lines made of?", "place": "Explained over Isny im Allgau, Germany", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The thinnest line on your wing is 0.66 mm across and holds 56 kilograms.", "body": ["Paraglider lines are made from two families of fibre. Dyneema is ultra high molecular weight polyethylene, made by the Dutch firm DSM, with a strength to weight ratio about eight times that of high strength steel. Aramid, sold as Kevlar or Twaron, was invented at DuPont in 1965 and is about five times stronger than steel by weight. Line makers such as Edelrid in Isny and Liros in Germany braid these fibres into cores, with or without a protective sheath.", "Each has a weakness. Dyneema shrinks when heated, beginning around 70 degrees, so never leave a wing in a hot car, and it creeps slowly under load. Aramid does not shrink but degrades in sunlight and loses strength when flexed repeatedly, so it is usually sheathed. Unsheathed lines are stronger for their diameter and have less drag, which is why competition wings use them, but they are easier to damage and harder to inspect.", "Lines are arranged in cascades: a few thick main lines from the risers split into middle and then upper lines, so that total line length on a tandem can reach 450 metres. A common thin upper line of 0.66 mm has a breaking strength of 56 kgf, or 550 N. Because brake and rear lines shrink while front lines stretch, trim slowly drifts, which is why a line length check belongs in every service."], "rel": ["eq-cloth", "eq-line-length", "eq-trim-check", "eq-risers-speed-system"], "src": [{"t": "UP Paragliders: paraglider line materials and why they matter", "u": "https://up-paragliders.com/en/content/item/502-paraglider-line-materials,-and-why-they-matter-to-you"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: ultra-high-molecular-weight polyethylene (Dyneema)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultra-high-molecular-weight_polyethylene"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (equipment)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "eq-risers-speed-system", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.89, "lon": 6.03, "name": "What do the risers and speed system do?", "place": "Explained over Chavanod, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Four short straps of webbing are the only thing between you and 300 metres of line.", "body": ["Risers are the short webbing straps, about 40 cm long, that gather the lines into groups and clip to the harness with carabiners. Most wings have A, B and C risers, sometimes D, named for the line rows from front to back. The A risers pull the leading edge and are used to launch, the rear risers can be used to steer on bar, and the brakes run through pulleys on the rear risers to the trailing edge.", "The speed system is a foot bar in the harness connected by cord through pulleys to the front risers. Pushing it shortens the A and B risers, lowers the angle of attack and speeds the wing up. Trim speed on a typical wing is 32 to 40 km/h and the full range is roughly 22 to 55 km/h. Bruce Goldsmith's rule of thumb is about 1 km/h per centimetre of travel, with pulley routing giving 10 to 13 cm in total.", "The brakes can only slow the wing from trim speed, so the bar is the only way to go faster, essential for getting through sink and headwind. Safety first: on bar the wing is more prone to collapse and reacts harder, so ease off in turbulence and never fly accelerated close to terrain. Check the Brummel hooks every flight, since they can separate if misaligned. Supair, near Annecy, makes many of the harnesses these systems live in."], "rel": ["eq-speed-bar-setup", "eq-trimmers", "eq-lines", "eq-carabiners"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (speed control)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "Cross Country: speedbar range, Bruce Goldsmith", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=48826"}, {"t": "Flybubble: connecting the speed system", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/connecting-the-speed-system"}]}, {"id": "eq-trimmers", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.68, "lon": 7.86, "name": "What are trimmers on a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Interlaken, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Tandem pilots change their wing's speed with a buckle, not their feet.", "body": ["Trimmers are adjustable buckles on the rear risers that let the pilot lengthen or shorten them without touching a foot bar. Letting the trimmers out lowers the trailing edge relative to the leading edge, reducing the angle of attack and increasing speed. Pulling them in does the opposite, giving a slower, more lifting wing for launch and landing. Unlike a speed bar, the setting stays where you leave it.", "You find trimmers on tandem wings and paramotor wings, where the pilot's feet are busy or the loads are heavy. On a tandem such as the Ozone Magnum 3, certified EN B for 130 to 220 kg in flight, the slow trim setting gives a shorter, slower take off run, and the fast setting improves glide into a headwind or suits a light passenger. The BGD Dual quotes 42 km/h at trim and 52 km/h at full speed.", "The safety lesson is that trimmers change the wing's certified behaviour. Asymmetric trimmers, one in and one out, make the wing turn constantly and are a classic cause of tandem launch accidents. Many manuals say to land with trimmers closed, because a fast trimmed wing has less flare. Set both to the same mark, check them in the pre flight, and only release them once safely clear of the hill."], "rel": ["eq-tandem-wings", "eq-risers-speed-system", "eq-paramotors", "eq-speed-bar-setup"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Magnum 3 tandem", "u": "https://flyozone.com/paragliders/products/gliders/magnum-3"}, {"t": "BGD Dual tandem specifications", "u": "https://www.flybgd.com/en/paragliders/dual---2021-1283-0.html"}]}, {"id": "eq-en-ltf-certification", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.75, "lon": 11.74, "name": "What does EN or LTF certification mean?", "place": "Explained over Gmund am Tegernsee, Germany", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Every certified wing has been deliberately collapsed over a lake by a professional.", "body": ["Before a paraglider can be sold in most of Europe it is tested to EN 926. Part 1 is a load test, in which the wing is dragged behind a vehicle to prove the lines and cloth survive far more than flying loads. Part 2 is a flight test, in which a test pilot flies a set of manoeuvres, including collapses, stalls and spirals, and the wing's recovery is graded A, B, C or D. The worst single grade becomes the wing's class.", "Two test houses dominate. Air Turquoise in Villeneuve, at the eastern end of Lake Geneva, flies most of the industry's wings with lead test pilot Alain Zoller. The DHV type inspection centre in Gmund am Tegernsee, accredited to ISO 17020, tests to the German LTF rules. LTF is Germany's legal airworthiness standard and now largely mirrors EN, so most wings carry both labels. Harnesses are tested to EN 1651 and reserves to EN 12491.", "Certification is a comparison in smooth air with an expert pilot, not a promise. The DHV's own safety tests found that many LTF A and B wings, pushed to bigger collapses than the minimum, behaved far more dynamically than their label suggests. Read the class as the minimum skill needed to recover from a collapse, then add a margin for turbulence, tiredness and the fact that you are not Alain Zoller."], "rel": ["eq-en-classes", "eq-ccc-wings", "eq-harnesses", "eq-reserves"], "src": [{"t": "DHV type inspection centre", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/en/type-inspection/"}, {"t": "Cross Country: how paragliders are tested and certified (Air Turquoise)", "u": "https://xcmag.com/magazine-articles/video-how-paragliders-are-tested-and-certified/"}, {"t": "DHV safety tests of LTF A and B paragliders, part 6", "u": "https://dhv.de/en/safety/safety-tests/safety-test-ltf-a-and-b-part-6"}]}, {"id": "eq-en-classes", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.4, "lon": 6.93, "name": "What do EN A, B, C and D mean?", "place": "Explained over Villeneuve, Switzerland", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Most pilots who get hurt were flying a wing one letter above their skill.", "body": ["The EN class describes how a wing behaves when something goes wrong. An EN A wing has maximum passive safety: after a collapse it turns less than 90 degrees and reopens on its own. A low EN B behaves much the same, while a high B may turn up to 180 degrees with dynamics that surprise a new pilot. An EN C may turn up to 360 degrees after a 60 percent collapse and needs an immediate, precise pilot input. EN D includes competition wings whose reactions can be violent.", "The letters do not describe performance directly, but higher classes trade passive safety for glide and speed. Flybubble's guidance is that an A suits first year pilots and anyone who flies rarely, a B suits licensed pilots with around 10 hours, a C needs roughly 75 hours of thermic flying plus an SIV course, and a D belongs to pilots flying 100 hours a year in rough air.", "The test is done by an expert over Lake Geneva in smooth air, with the pilot deliberately passive. Real collapses happen in turbulence with a tired pilot close to a ridge. The Swiss federation puts it bluntly: never choose a wing by its EN class alone. The safe answer is to fly one class below your ability, stay within the certified weight range, and move up only when the current wing feels boring in strong conditions."], "rel": ["eq-en-ltf-certification", "eq-ccc-wings", "eq-buying-second-hand", "eq-ballast"], "src": [{"t": "SHV/FSVL: description of EN classes (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Sicherheit/EN-Klassen_Beschreibung_FR.pdf"}, {"t": "Flybubble: reducing risk, your wing", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/reducing-risk-wing"}, {"t": "Dales HGPC: glider ratings EN 926 (PDF)", "u": "https://dhpc.org.uk/assets/files/Exam Revision Material/Glider ratings - en926.pdf"}]}, {"id": "eq-ccc-wings", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 4.41, "lon": -76.15, "name": "What is a CCC competition wing?", "place": "Explained over Roldanillo, Colombia", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": true, "hook": "After pilots died on open class wings, the sport wrote its own rule book.", "body": ["CCC stands for CIVL Competition Class, a specification written by the FAI's hang gliding and paragliding commission for the wings flown in world championships and the Paragliding World Cup. It came into force on 1 January 2015, in time for the World Championship in Roldanillo, Colombia, after a period in which uncertified open class wings were banned from competition following fatal accidents in 2011.", "The rules cap flat aspect ratio at 7.9, limit speed bar travel to 18 cm, and forbid concave sections across the middle of the wing. The flight tests resemble EN D but are flown only at maximum weight, and the pilot may intervene after 3 seconds rather than 5. Low speed spin tests are dropped. In practice these are two line wings, with only A and B rows and the rear risers used for control on bar.", "A CCC wing is not a faster EN D with the same safety; it is a racing machine that needs daily currency and an SIV habit. The class exists so that the top of the sport has a known ceiling instead of an arms race. For almost every pilot the lesson is the opposite one: the best glide you can use is the glide of a wing you still feel relaxed on when the air turns rough."], "rel": ["eq-en-classes", "eq-en-ltf-certification", "eq-ballast", "eq-speed-bar-setup"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble: CCC, the new competition class explained", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/ccc"}, {"t": "FAI: what's up with the new CCC paragliders?", "u": "https://fai.org/node/21590"}]}, {"id": "eq-single-skin", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.92, "lon": 6.87, "name": "What is a single skin paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Chamonix, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wing weighing less than a loaf of bread can fly you off Mont Blanc.", "body": ["A normal paraglider has an upper and a lower surface with air trapped between them. A single skin wing drops most of the lower surface, keeping only an inflated tube along the leading edge and a handful of ram air cells, so the profile is held by lines and a few ribs instead of internal pressure. Ozone's XXLite 2 weighs 1.3 kg in the 16 m2 size and 1.4 kg in the 18 m2, and the Niviuk Skin P 18 weighs 1.8 kg.", "The appeal is weight and launch behaviour. These wings inflate with almost no wind, pop up without A riser input and lift off in a few steps, which is why alpinists and hike and fly racers use them. They are forgiving in gusts because they store little energy, and they can be slowed to a walking pace for landing. A full kit with harness and reserve can weigh under 5 kg.", "The cost is performance and feel. Turns are slow, a thermal circle takes around 20 seconds instead of 12 to 14, glide is modest and the brakes chatter in turbulence. Ozone describes the XXLite as a specialist mountain wing for experienced pilots, and that is the safe reading: a tool for a descent from a summit in calm evening air, not a first wing, and not a cross country machine."], "rel": ["eq-hike-and-fly", "eq-cloth", "eq-mini-wings-speed-flying", "eq-storage-lifespan"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Ozone XXLite 2 review", "u": "https://xcmag.com/gear-guide/paraglider-reviews/ozone-xxlite-2-review/"}, {"t": "Flybubble: Niviuk Skin P single skin review", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blog/niviuk-skin-p-single-skin-paraglider-review"}]}, {"id": "eq-hike-and-fly", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.8, "lon": 13.11, "name": "What is a hike and fly wing?", "place": "Explained over Salzburg, Austria", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "A whole aircraft that fits in a 30 litre rucksack and weighs less than your lunch.", "body": ["A hike and fly wing is a normal double surface paraglider built from the lightest cloth and thinnest lines the maker dares to use. Ozone's Ultralite 5 weighs 1.76 kg in the 17 m2 size and 2.41 kg in the 25 m2, with 7 mm Dyneema risers instead of 20 mm webbing. Advance, Nova, Niviuk, Gin and Skywalk all sell similar wings, often with the word light or ULS in the name.", "The weight comes from Skytex 27 class cloth at 27 g/m2 and unsheathed lines. The cost is durability: thin coatings lose porosity faster, thin lines wear faster, and Advance halves the service interval for its light models to 100 flights or 100 hours. Treat a light wing as a tool for walking up and flying down, and keep a heavier wing for days of ground handling on gravel.", "The Red Bull X-Alps, which starts in Salzburg, drove this whole category. Athletes who crossed the Alps with 12 kg kits in the 2000s now carry under 7 kg. The safety lesson is in sizing: small light wings fly fast and sink fast, and many are certified EN C or EN B in the small sizes, so check the class for your size, not the brand name."], "rel": ["eq-single-skin", "eq-cloth", "eq-trim-check", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "Ozone Ultralite 5 specifications", "u": "https://flyozone.com/paragliders/products/gliders/ultralite-5"}, {"t": "Advance: glider checks and intervals", "u": "https://cdn.advance.swiss/en/services/maintenance/checks/glider"}, {"t": "Innovation in Textiles: weightless to the max", "u": "https://www.innovationintextiles.com/weightless-to-the-max"}]}, {"id": "eq-tandem-wings", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.24, "lon": 127.18, "name": "How is a tandem wing different?", "place": "Explained over Yongin, South Korea", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The wing that carries two people has 450 metres of line and a 220 kg limit.", "body": ["A tandem paraglider is simply bigger and stronger. The BGD Dual has 40 m2 of flat area, 53 cells, 450 m of line and weighs 7.5 kg, certified EN B for 120 to 220 kg in flight. Ozone's Magnum 3 in the 41 size covers 130 to 220 kg. Pilot and passenger hang from spreader bars that separate the two harnesses, and the carabiners between risers and spreaders must have a breaking strain of at least 24 kN.", "Because the pilot's feet are not free, tandems use trimmers on the rear risers to change speed, and the best glide is around 9. The reserve must be rated for the whole flight weight, which is why tandem rescues are 55 m2 or more and weigh nearly 3 kg. Gin Gliders in Yongin, founded by competition pilot Gin Seok Song in 1998, is one of the big suppliers of tandem wings and spreaders.", "Commercial tandem flying has its own rulebook. New Zealand's federation limits training pilots to winds of 12 knots and mild thermals, requires a reserve on every flight and a hard shell helmet for both people, and will not recommend passengers under five. The safe answer for anyone buying a flight is to ask for the pilot's licence and the wing's certification sticker, and to accept that a cancelled flight is good judgement."], "rel": ["eq-trimmers", "eq-reserves", "eq-carabiners", "eq-en-ltf-certification"], "src": [{"t": "BGD Dual tandem specifications", "u": "https://www.flybgd.com/en/paragliders/dual---2021-1283-0.html"}, {"t": "NZHGPA Tandem Technical Manual TM28 (PDF)", "u": "https://nzhgpa.org.nz/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Tandem-Technical-Manual-TM28-6.5.24.pdf"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Gin Gliders", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gin_Gliders"}]}, {"id": "eq-mini-wings-speed-flying", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.57, "lon": 6.83, "name": "What are mini wings and speed flying?", "place": "Explained over Les Arcs, France", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": false, "hook": "Speed wings fly at 70 km/h just above the snow, and kill about five pilots a year.", "body": ["Speed wings are small ram air wings of roughly 7 to 18 m2, compared with 20 to 35 m2 for a paraglider. Flown on skis the sport is called speed riding, on foot it is speed flying. Cruising speed is around 70 km/h with a glide of only 3 to 7, so flights are short, steep and close to the slope. Mini wings sit between the two, around 14 to 19 m2, flown in strong wind on coastal sites or for fast descents.", "The sport was born in the French Alps. Frederic Fugen had the idea of launching a parachute while skiing at Valfrejus in 2001, Francois Bon helped develop the first dedicated speed wing, the Gin Nano, between 2005 and 2007, and the first annual competition was held at Les Arcs in 2007. The French federation recognised it as a separate sport in 2006. Today more than 30 makers build speed wings.", "The danger is proximity to terrain at speed. A record of 93 pilot deaths worldwide between 2006 and March 2024 works out at about five a year. Speed wings cannot arrest a fall and landings use parachute technique. A small wing is not an easier paraglider: wing loading is far higher, reactions are instant, and helmet, back protector and professional instruction are the minimum."], "rel": ["eq-single-skin", "eq-hike-and-fly", "eq-ballast", "eq-helmets"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Speed flying and speed riding", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speed_flying_and_speed_riding"}, {"t": "Ozone: the new Arc speedride wing", "u": "https://flyozone.com/speed/news/the-new-arc-a-speedride-specific-big-mountain-wing"}]}, {"id": "eq-paramotors", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.18, "lon": -3.6, "name": "What is a paramotor?", "place": "Explained over Granada, Spain", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "A motor on your back turns a paraglider into an aircraft that has flown 1,105 km in one go.", "body": ["A paramotor is a two stroke engine and propeller in a cage, worn like a rucksack, that lets a paraglider take off from flat ground. Units weigh 20 to 41 kg, engines are typically 80 to 350 cc, and they burn roughly 3.7 litres of petrol and oil mix an hour. Take off runs are short, often around 3 m in a light wind, and cruising speeds are 24 to 80 km/h depending on the wing.", "Paramotor wings are reflex profiled and carry trimmers, because the thrust line sits below the wing and the pilot's feet are not free. Regulation is light. In the UK a foot launched paramotor under 70 kg with a stall speed of 35 knots or less needs no registration or licence, and in the USA Part 103 ultralights need no licence but may not fly over built up areas. Spain's Ramon Morillas holds the distance record, 1,105 km in 2007, and the altitude record, 7,589 m in 2009.", "The accident record is sobering. Germany recorded 232 accidents, 109 serious injuries and 9 deaths among about 36,000 pilots in 2018. Propeller strikes on launch, wing and throttle mishandling, and low turns are the usual causes. No licence does not mean no training: learn with a school, start on a wing certified for motor use, and keep hands and lines away from a running propeller."], "rel": ["eq-trimmers", "eq-mini-wings-speed-flying", "eq-radios", "eq-airspace-instruments"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Powered paragliding", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Powered_paragliding"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paramotor", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paramotor"}]}, {"id": "eq-harnesses", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.75, "lon": 7.63, "name": "Pod, open, airbag or foam: which harness?", "place": "Explained over Thun, Switzerland", "icon": "home", "hidden": false, "hook": "A dummy is dropped 1.65 metres onto every certified harness to see how hard it hits.", "body": ["The harness is the seat you fly in. An open harness has a simple seat and back, is easy to get in and out of, and is what schools use. A pod harness closes around the legs like a sleeping bag, cutting drag and keeping you warm on long cross country flights, but it is harder to exit before landing. Reversible harnesses zip into a rucksack for hike and fly. All are tested to EN 1651 for strength, holding loads up to 15 g for several seconds.", "Under the seat sits a protector. Foam protectors work immediately on launch and in any attitude. Airbag protectors are lighter and pack smaller but need airflow to inflate, so they offer little on a failed launch. Both are tested by dropping a dummy from 1.65 m, about 5.3 m/s, and recording deceleration; the limit is 50 g peak, and good protectors of 12 to 30 cm thickness record 20 to 40 g. A harness with no protector records over 250 g.", "Advance in Thun and Supair near Annecy build the harnesses most pilots recognise. The choice is about skill and mission rather than fashion: a new pilot in a pod is a common cause of launch accidents, because the leg strap gets forgotten or the pilot cannot get upright to land. Whatever you buy, do a hang check, and check the leg loops are closed before every single launch."], "rel": ["eq-reserves", "eq-carabiners", "eq-en-ltf-certification", "eq-gloves-suits"], "src": [{"t": "Air Turquoise EN 1651 test report, Supair VIP Lite (PDF)", "u": "https://supair.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/en-1651_2013_air-turquoise_vip-lite_ph-095.2013.pdf"}, {"t": "DHV protector tests: Koroyd 3 (PDF)", "u": "https://dhv.de/fileadmin/user_upload/files/2024/Sicherheit_2024/Protektortests_Koroyd_3_en.pdf"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (harness)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "eq-reserves", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.7, "lon": 10.72, "name": "Round, square or Rogallo: how do reserves differ?", "place": "Explained over Lake Garda, Italy", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Your reserve must open in under 5 seconds and land you no faster than a jump from 1.8 metres.", "body": ["A reserve is the parachute packed into your harness for the day the wing cannot be recovered. The European standard EN 12491 requires it to open in less than 5 seconds, sink at no more than 5.5 m/s at maximum load, and survive a 40 m/s airflow without damage. Supair's manual notes that 5.5 m/s is about the impact of a 1.8 m jump, so landing under a reserve still needs a parachute landing fall.", "Round reserves are cheapest and easiest to pack but swing the most. Square and cruciform designs resist pendulum swing and have shorter lines, so they tangle less with the main wing. Rogallo reserves, such as the Beamer 3 from High Adventure in Switzerland, are steerable and glide forward, with tested sink rates of 3.6 to 3.8 m/s and a 2 second opening, but they can carry you into obstacles if you do not fly them. Light models weigh around 1 kg for small pilots.", "Minimum useful height is about 60 m, and deployment to a stable descent typically uses 120 to 180 m. Pilots get three things wrong: they wait too long, they throw without looking and the bridle fouls, and they never practise. Repack every six months, air the canopy for 12 hours first, retire it at ten to twelve years, and throw it hard towards clear air the moment you decide the wing is not coming back."], "rel": ["eq-rogallo-reserve", "eq-harnesses", "eq-en-ltf-certification", "eq-tandem-wings"], "src": [{"t": "Supair reserve parachute manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.supair.com/document/notices/reserve_en.pdf"}, {"t": "Air Turquoise EN 12491 test, Gin Yeti Rescue 60 (PDF)", "u": "https://www.gingliders.com/pdf/rs-yeti-rescue-test-60.pdf"}, {"t": "Freedom Parapente: rescue parachute types", "u": "https://www.freedom-parapente.fr/en/shop/rescue"}]}, {"id": "eq-rogallo-reserve", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.09, "lon": -76.38, "name": "What is a Rogallo reserve?", "place": "Explained over Hampton, Virginia, USA", "icon": "kite", "hidden": true, "hook": "A NASA wind tunnel engineer's 1948 kite now hangs under every steerable reserve.", "body": ["Francis Rogallo, an engineer at the NACA wind tunnels in Hampton, Virginia, patented a flexible delta wing with his wife Gertrude in 1948. NASA tested it as the Parawing for recovering spacecraft, and in the 1970s it became the first hang glider. The same triangular, two lobed shape is now used for steerable reserve parachutes, because unlike a round canopy it flies forward and can be turned.", "High Adventure's Beamer 3 is the best known. Its EN tests show sink rates of 3.6 to 3.8 m/s and a 2 second opening, against the 5.5 m/s and 5 second limits for ordinary reserves. With the paraglider still attached and braked, the sink rate drops below 3 m/s. The pilot steers with toggles, and if the main wing is tangled it can be cut away, which doubles the glide.", "The catch is that a Rogallo keeps moving. If the pilot is unconscious, or too shaken to steer, it can carry them into a cliff, power lines or a tree that a round reserve would have dropped them short of. Makers recommend them for experienced pilots and acro flyers, and they are bulkier to pack. For most pilots a square reserve, correctly sized and repacked, is the sensible choice."], "rel": ["eq-reserves", "eq-harnesses", "eq-rescue-ropes", "kitty-hawk"], "src": [{"t": "High Adventure: Beamer 3", "u": "https://www.highadventure.ch/en/beamer-3-en.html"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Francis Rogallo", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Rogallo"}]}, {"id": "eq-instruments", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.92, "lon": 8.2, "name": "What instruments does a paraglider pilot carry?", "place": "Explained over Titisee-Neustadt, Germany", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 200 gram box in the Black Forest tells you where the lift is and where the airspace isn't.", "body": ["The core instrument is the variometer, which beeps faster as you climb and groans as you sink, letting you feel lift of a few centimetres a second. Almost every modern vario also carries a GNSS receiver for position, speed over ground and a tracklog, a barometric altimeter and an airspace database. Skytraxx, based in Titisee-Neustadt in the Black Forest, and Naviter in Kranj, Slovenia, build the instruments most European pilots fly with.", "A Skytraxx 5 weighs 200 g, runs up to 30 hours on a 2700 mAh battery, shows a sunlight readable 4.4 inch screen and logs to 32 GB of memory. It receives GPS, Galileo, GLONASS and BeiDou, and includes FANET and a FLARM beacon so that nearby pilots and gliders can see each other. Phones running free apps such as XCTrack do much of the same job, at the cost of battery and sunlight readability.", "Instruments are a help, not a pilot. The safe habit is to set everything up on the ground, mount the vario where a glance covers it, and fly with your eyes outside. Pilots get hurt staring at a screen in a crowded thermal. Before a flight in new country, load the current airspace file, set the QNH, and switch on the tracklog, since a tracklog is what lets a rescue team find you."], "rel": ["eq-how-vario-works", "eq-gps-igc", "eq-airspace-instruments", "eq-flarm-fanet"], "src": [{"t": "Skytraxx GmbH", "u": "https://www.skytraxx.eu/en/"}, {"t": "Flybubble: Skytraxx 5 with FANET, FLARM and ADS-L", "u": "https://flybubble.com/products/skytraxx-5-including-fanet-flarm-and-ads-l"}, {"t": "Naviter: flight instruments and SeeYou software", "u": "https://naviter.com/"}]}, {"id": "eq-gps-igc", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.52, "lon": 6.63, "name": "What is an IGC tracklog?", "place": "Explained over Lausanne, Switzerland", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "A plain text file, signed with a secret key, is the only proof a 300 km flight ever happened.", "body": ["An IGC file is a text file written by a flight instrument that records your position, GPS altitude and barometric altitude at regular intervals, typically every second. The format is defined by the International Gliding Commission of the FAI, the world air sports federation headquartered in Lausanne, and the same file is used by sailplanes, hang gliders and paragliders. Each line of position data begins with the letter B.", "What makes it evidence is the signature at the end, called the G record. A certified instrument signs the file with public key cryptography, so a contest server can check it was recorded by that device and not edited. Files from phones are not signed, which is why some competitions and record claims require a dedicated logger. Online contests such as XContest score IGC files by distance, with bonuses for triangles.", "The practical lessons are simple. Start the log before launch and stop it after landing, because a file that starts in the air looks like a fake. Keep the barometric sensor uncovered so altitude is valid. Upload promptly, since contests have deadlines. And remember the file is also a safety record: friends and rescuers can see your last fix, as long as the instrument was actually switched on."], "rel": ["eq-instruments", "eq-live-tracking", "eq-airspace-instruments", "eq-how-vario-works"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: IGC (file format)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IGC_(file_format)"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Hang gliding (instruments)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hang_gliding#Instruments"}]}, {"id": "eq-live-tracking", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.24, "lon": 14.36, "name": "How does live tracking work?", "place": "Explained over Kranj, Slovenia", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "Your family can watch your dot cross the Alps, second by second, from the sofa.", "body": ["Live tracking sends your position from the air to a server, usually over the mobile phone network, so that friends, retrieve drivers and race organisers can see where you are. Flymaster's dedicated tracker sends one position a second over GSM for more than 20 hours on a charge, with a built in SOS button, and its system is the standard for the Paragliding World Cup and the Red Bull X-Alps. Phone apps do the same job when there is signal.", "Where there is no phone signal, pilots use FANET, a short range radio network built into instruments such as the Skytraxx 5, or satellite messengers. Naviter in Kranj and Skytraxx in Germany both feed live positions to public web maps, and DHV and XContest accept the same data. In a competition, live tracking also lets safety staff see who has landed and who has stopped moving on a hillside.", "Tracking is not a rescue device. A phone in a pocket with no signal, or a tracker left in the car, has caused slow searches for pilots who could have been found in an hour. Set the tracker going before launch, tell someone the link, and agree what happens if the dot stops. For remote flying, pair it with a satellite device that works without any network."], "rel": ["eq-trackers-sos", "eq-flarm-fanet", "eq-gps-igc", "eq-instruments"], "src": [{"t": "Flymaster Tracker", "u": "https://www.flymaster.net/tracker"}, {"t": "Flybubble: Skytraxx 5 with FANET, FLARM and ADS-L", "u": "https://flybubble.com/products/skytraxx-5-including-fanet-flarm-and-ads-l"}]}, {"id": "eq-flarm-fanet", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.2, "lon": 8.53, "name": "What are FLARM and FANET?", "place": "Explained over Baar, Switzerland", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "Thirty thousand gliders broadcast where they will be in twenty seconds, and your vario can listen.", "body": ["FLARM is a collision warning system for light aircraft, designed in Switzerland by Urs Rothacher and Andrea Schlapbach, with first sales in 2004. Each unit uses GPS and a barometric sensor to broadcast its position and a prediction of its path for the next seconds, listens for others, and warns only when the paths actually conflict. Nearly 30,000 devices are in use, and gliders must carry it in several countries including France.", "FANET, the Flying Ad hoc Network, is the free flying answer. It is a low power, open protocol that lets paragliders and hang gliders exchange position, name and messages directly between instruments without a phone network, and ground stations relay it to the internet. Instruments such as the Skytraxx 5 include FANET plus a FLARM beacon, so a sailplane at 150 km/h can see a paraglider that is otherwise nearly invisible.", "These systems reduce risk, they do not remove it. A FLARM beacon lets gliders see you; it does not warn you about them unless your instrument also receives. Many pilots fly with nothing. The safe rule remains to look out, especially in the Alps where sailplanes share the same ridges and thermals, and to treat an alarm as a reason to turn away now, not to look for the traffic first."], "rel": ["eq-live-tracking", "eq-instruments", "eq-airspace-instruments", "eq-trackers-sos"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: FLARM", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FLARM"}, {"t": "Flybubble: Skytraxx 5 with FANET, FLARM and ADS-L", "u": "https://flybubble.com/products/skytraxx-5-including-fanet-flarm-and-ads-l"}]}, {"id": "eq-radios", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 52.63, "lon": -1.13, "name": "Do I need a licence to use a radio while flying?", "place": "Explained over Leicester, England", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "Most of Europe's free flyers talk on frequencies they are not strictly allowed to use.", "body": ["Pilots use radios for two things: talking to each other, and talking to retrieve drivers or instructors. Across Europe the common choice is a handheld two metre band VHF set with a helmet speaker, a microphone set back on the jaw to beat wind noise and a push to talk button on a finger. Ireland's federation recommends 143.950 MHz as a calling frequency and warns pilots never to stray below 143.750 MHz or into the 144 to 146 MHz amateur band.", "The legal position is messy. In many countries no frequency is formally assigned to free flight, so pilots squat on allocations, a grey area the federations are trying to fix. Transmitting on amateur frequencies without an amateur licence is illegal, and airband radios, which allow talking to aircraft and the emergency frequency, require a radiotelephony licence in almost every country. Check with your national body, such as the BHPA in Leicester, before you buy.", "The licence free option is PMR446, a set of 16 analogue channels around 446 MHz limited to 500 mW, legal without paperwork across most of Europe. Range is a few kilometres in open country and far more from a hill, which is plenty for a flying site. Whatever the set, keep calls short, since the channel is shared, and never let a conversation replace looking out."], "rel": ["eq-live-tracking", "eq-trackers-sos", "eq-helmets", "eq-instruments"], "src": [{"t": "IHPA: radio frequencies", "u": "https://ihpa.ie/index.php/flight-safety/radio-frequencies"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: PMR446", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PMR446"}, {"t": "BHPA", "u": "https://www.bhpa.co.uk/"}]}, {"id": "eq-helmets", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.33, "lon": 11.65, "name": "What helmet standard applies to paragliding?", "place": "Explained over Terfens, Austria", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "A bicycle helmet is designed for one kind of fall; a flying helmet for several.", "body": ["The European standard for airborne sports helmets is EN 966, last revised in 2012, with a category HPG for hang gliding and paragliding and UL for ultralight aircraft. It sets requirements for shock absorption, penetration resistance, the retention system that keeps the helmet on your head, field of vision and head mobility, so that you can still look up at the wing. A helmet carrying the EN 966 mark has passed all of these.", "Pilots fall in unusual ways: a stumble on launch, a swing into a slope, a tree landing, a blown flare. Open face helmets give better hearing and vision, which matters for judging wind and traffic. Full face helmets protect the chin and are common in acro, speed flying and competition. Some federations accept a hard shell bicycle helmet for tandem passengers, but recommend air sports certification.", "Helmets are single use after a real impact, and the foam ages: a cracked shell or compressed liner means replacement, not repair. Fit matters more than price. Fasten it before you clip in, since many launch accidents happen before the pilot is ready, and attach nothing to it that cannot break away, because a camera mount can catch a line or lever the helmet off in a fall."], "rel": ["eq-gloves-suits", "eq-cameras-drones", "eq-harnesses", "eq-mini-wings-speed-flying"], "src": [{"t": "EN 966:2012 Helmets for airborne sports", "u": "https://genorma.com/en/standards/en-966-2012"}, {"t": "NZHGPA Tandem Technical Manual TM28 (PDF)", "u": "https://nzhgpa.org.nz/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Tandem-Technical-Manual-TM28-6.5.24.pdf"}]}, {"id": "eq-gloves-suits", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.84, "lon": -120.02, "name": "Why do pilots wear flying suits and heavy gloves in summer?", "place": "Explained over Chelan, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Climb 3,000 metres above a 30 degree valley and you are flying in sub zero air.", "body": ["Air cools with height. Dry air cools at 9.8 degrees per 1,000 m, and the standard atmosphere assumes 6.5 degrees per 1,000 m, so a summer thermal from a valley at 30 degrees reaches 3,000 m above it at around 10 degrees or less, with cloudbase colder still. Add 40 km/h of wind over bare skin and hands lose dexterity long before anyone feels in danger. Chelan in Washington State, with its big desert thermals, is a classic place to get caught out.", "A flying suit is a windproof one piece that stops the draught at waist and ankles, and a pod harness does the same for the legs. Gloves are the critical item: cold fingers cannot feel the brakes, cannot work a speed bar hook or a reserve handle, and cannot operate a phone after landing. Many cross country pilots carry a thin pair for launch and a thick or heated pair for height.", "Frostbite is linked to prolonged exposure below about minus 0.5 degrees, and fingers, toes, nose and ears go first. Hypothermia is the quieter risk: a cold pilot makes slow decisions. Dress for the top of the climb, not the launch. If your hands stop working, lose height and land while you can still steer, rather than staying high in the hope of warming up."], "rel": ["eq-oxygen", "eq-harnesses", "eq-helmets", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Lapse rate", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lapse_rate"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Frostbite", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frostbite"}]}, {"id": "eq-oxygen", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.24, "lon": 77.19, "name": "When do pilots need oxygen?", "place": "Explained over Manali, India", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": true, "hook": "Hypoxia feels good right up until you cannot remember how to land.", "body": ["Above about 3,000 m the body starts to run short of oxygen, and at 3,700 m a moderate level of hypoxia can already dull colour vision. Symptoms creep in as mild euphoria, slower thinking and poor judgement, which is why pilots do not notice. Glider pilots who studied themselves on long high flights found periodic breathing in everyone above 2,400 m and oxygen saturation swinging by up to 12 percent.", "Rules written for aircraft are a rough guide. European regulations require oxygen above 3,050 m after 30 minutes, and United States rules above about 3,800 m. Experienced American pilots carry oxygen whenever the forecast top of lift is above roughly 3,650 m and have flown four hours above that with a pulse demand system, which gives a puff on each breath and uses far less gas than constant flow. Time of useful consciousness falls to 20 to 30 minutes at about 5,500 m.", "In the Himalaya, where Manali launches already sit around 2,000 m and thermals reach 5,000 m or more, oxygen is normal kit for serious cross country. The safe answer is to decide a ceiling on the ground, use oxygen from around 3,000 m on long flights, and treat any confusion or strange cheerfulness as a reason to descend. Oxygen also keeps you warmer, which is a second reason to carry it."], "rel": ["eq-gloves-suits", "eq-instruments", "bar-headed-goose", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "British Gliding Association: hypoxia and mountain flying (PDF)", "u": "https://www.gliding.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2018/01/Hypoxia-and-Mountain-Flying.pdf"}, {"t": "Mountain High Oxygen: oxygen is only for wimps, right?", "u": "https://mhoxygen.com/?p=194"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Time of useful consciousness", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_of_useful_consciousness"}]}, {"id": "eq-trim-check", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.07, "lon": 18.23, "name": "What happens at a wing inspection and trim check?", "place": "Explained over Pecs, Hungary", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Every two years a workshop snaps three of your lines on purpose.", "body": ["A full service checks the things you cannot see. The workshop measures porosity at several points on the top surface, tests tear strength at the leading edge with a Bettsometer, and pulls sample lines from several levels to breaking point on a machine. If any group fails the manufacturer's minimum, the whole set of that type is replaced. Every line is then laser measured, under a standard 5 kg load, and compared with the maker's trim sheet.", "Advance asks for this every 24 months or 150 flights or hours, whichever comes first, and every 100 for its light models. Many workshops also offer a cheaper annual trim check, because lines change most in a wing's first year. Rear and brake lines shrink while front lines stretch, raising the angle of attack, so an old wing flies slower, launches badly and sits closer to a parachutal stall. The fix is relengthening or new lines.", "A completed check gets a stamp on the type plate with date, examiner and expiry, and a written report. That report is what a buyer should ask for. Nova's wings are sewn in Pecs, Hungary, where 95 of the firm's 110 employees worked in 2016, and like every maker it says a repaired, checked wing is airworthy and an unchecked one is a guess. Skipping the check voids the warranty."], "rel": ["eq-line-length", "eq-porosity-testing", "eq-buying-second-hand", "eq-wing-repairs"], "src": [{"t": "Advance: glider checks", "u": "https://cdn.advance.swiss/en/services/maintenance/checks/glider"}, {"t": "High Adventure: paraglider checks", "u": "https://www.highadventure.ch/en/gleitschirmchecks-en.html"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Nova Performance Paragliders", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nova_Performance_Paragliders"}]}, {"id": "eq-line-length", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.41, "lon": 10.28, "name": "How are line lengths measured?", "place": "Explained over Oberstdorf, Germany", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 15 millimetre error in one line is enough to fail a wing's inspection.", "body": ["Line length is measured from the riser maillon to the attachment tab on the canopy, with each line held under a standard load, usually 5 kg, so that the braid is straightened the same way each time. Workshops use a laser distance meter on a long bench and record every line to the millimetre, then compare the result to the manufacturer's line plan. Lines are measured in pairs, left and right, since asymmetry matters as much as absolute length.", "Apco's control manual gives the typical tolerances: a line may be 15 mm either side of nominal, and the total spread must not exceed 30 mm. Shortened C or D lines can be restretched symmetrically with a 20 daN pull and measured again. A quick field check compares mirror lines: more than 1 cm difference means the trim has drifted and the wing should go to a workshop.", "Why it matters is angle of attack. Shrinking rear lines tilt the wing nose up, which steals speed and brings the stall closer, particularly on big ears or in a wet wing. Stretched A lines do the reverse. The DHV in Germany and the national federations all expect line measurements in a service report, and a test flight after any adjustment, so that the wing is flown as certified."], "rel": ["eq-trim-check", "eq-lines", "eq-buying-second-hand", "eq-en-ltf-certification"], "src": [{"t": "Apco Aviation: check and control manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.apcoaviation.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/manual_check_and_control_manual.pdf"}, {"t": "High Adventure: paraglider checks", "u": "https://www.highadventure.ch/en/gleitschirmchecks-en.html"}, {"t": "Skynomad: buying second hand paragliding gear", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/buying-second-hand-paragliding-gear"}]}, {"id": "eq-porosity-testing", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.7, "lon": 23.32, "name": "What is a porosity test?", "place": "Explained over Sofia, Bulgaria", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Suck on the top of your wing: if air comes through, it is nearly finished.", "body": ["Porosity is how much air leaks through the cloth. A porosimeter clamps a small area of the top surface and times how long a fixed volume of air takes to pass through at a set pressure. The common JDC MK1 pushes 250 ml through 38.5 cm2 at 10 mbar. A longer time means a tighter coating. New cloth reads several hundred seconds, often 1,000 or more, and the number falls as UV, heat, dragging and folding crack the coating.", "Workshops test three to five clean spots on the upper surface, 10 to 30 cm behind the leading edge, because the coating ages unevenly and different colours fade differently. Readings over 100 seconds are fine, 25 seconds is the point to take notice, and 10 to 20 seconds is the end: the wing launches badly, stalls early and is no longer airworthy. Converted to litres per square metre per minute, 20 seconds is about 270 on the JDC scale.", "Porosity is the single best guide to remaining life, and the decline is not linear: in the last 50 hours it accelerates sharply. Heat above about 60 degrees in a closed car can shorten a coating's life by a third. Spray on restorers help only briefly. The Bulgarian school Skynomad's advice to buyers is simple: a seller who refuses a porosity test has told you the result."], "rel": ["eq-trim-check", "eq-uv-damage", "eq-storage-lifespan", "eq-buying-second-hand"], "src": [{"t": "Skynomad: porosity", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/porosity"}, {"t": "406 Paragliding: porosity testing", "u": "https://406paragliding.com/porosity-testing/"}, {"t": "Apco Aviation: check and control manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.apcoaviation.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/manual_check_and_control_manual.pdf"}]}, {"id": "eq-uv-damage", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -33.93, "lon": 18.42, "name": "How much damage does sunlight do to a wing?", "place": "Explained over Cape Town, South Africa", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "The hours your wing spends waiting on launch count as much as the hours it flies.", "body": ["Ultraviolet light is the biggest single cause of paraglider ageing. It bleaches the nylon, makes the fibres more prone to tearing and breaks down the coating that keeps the cloth airtight. Manufacturers add UV inhibitors to the coating, but these weaken with exposure. Lines suffer too: Dyneema copes well, while aramid degrades fast when unsheathed, and a sheath protects it for roughly 1,000 hours.", "The arithmetic surprises people. Operating hours include every minute the wing is unpacked in daylight, so a pilot who lays out at 11 and launches at 2 has used three hours of life before leaving the ground. Some wings are sound at 500 to 800 hours, others look worn at 150 or less, and the difference is mostly how long they lay in the sun. Wings ten years old should be professionally checked before they fly again.", "Cape Town's Lion's Head, where pilots wait for the wind for hours under a hard southern sun, is the perfect place to learn the habit: keep the wing in its bag until you are ready, pack it the moment you land, and never dry it in direct sun. A UV protective cover bag for layouts on launch costs less than a new set of lines."], "rel": ["eq-porosity-testing", "eq-storage-lifespan", "eq-cloth", "eq-lines"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: how to pack and take care of your paraglider", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=68075"}, {"t": "UP Paragliders: how can I make my paraglider last?", "u": "https://www.up-paragliders.com/service/faq/products/how-can-i-make-my-paraglider-last-for-as-long-as-possible"}]}, {"id": "eq-packing", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 43.71, "lon": 7.26, "name": "What is the right way to pack a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Nice, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The plastic rods in your leading edge remember every tight fold.", "body": ["Modern wings carry nylon or nitinol rods along the leading edge to hold the profile. Packing is mostly about not bending them. Concertina packing, cell by cell, stacks the leading edge openings on top of each other and keeps the rods straight, which is why Ozone, Gin, Nova and the rest sell concertina bags. A quicker mushroom fold is acceptable for a short break if the leading edge is gathered and not creased.", "Carry the wing, never drag it. Pulling a bunched wing across stones or tarmac grinds the rods against the cloth from inside and scuffs the coating, and repairs to a damaged leading edge are costly. Do not spread the wing on a hard surface to pack it, since stiff seams and ribs wear quickly on grit. Fold loosely: very tight packing crazes the coating, and tight straps around a damp wing make it worse.", "Ozone's design office sits near Nice, and its advice matches every other maker: pack loosely, pack dry, pack out of the sun. Leave the inner bag only lightly compressed in the rucksack. Before you close it, run the lines through your fingers for knots and sticks, so that the next launch starts clean rather than with a cravat you packed in yourself."], "rel": ["eq-storage-lifespan", "eq-uv-damage", "eq-washing", "eq-cloth"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: how to pack and take care of your paraglider", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=68075"}, {"t": "Advance Alpha 8 manual: care and storage", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/alpha_8/4011"}]}, {"id": "eq-storage-lifespan", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.83, "lon": 13.21, "name": "How long does a paraglider last, and how should I store it?", "place": "Explained over Castelluccio, Italy", "icon": "home", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wing stored damp in a garage dies faster than one flown every weekend.", "body": ["There is no fixed lifespan. Manufacturers decline to quote years or hours because use varies so much: a wing flown in sun and sand may be tired at 150 hours, while a carefully kept one is sound at 500 to 800. What ends a wing is porosity, measured by a workshop, and line strength, not the calendar. Advance requires a check every 24 months or 150 flights or hours, and the stamp on the type plate shows when it is due.", "Storage rules are the same in every manual. Keep the wing dry, dark, cool and loosely packed, ideally hanging or in an open bag rather than compressed. Never store it damp, since mould eats the coating, and never leave it in a car in summer, where temperatures can pass 60 degrees and shrink Dyneema lines. Avoid a shed that freezes. Rinse salt water out with fresh water and dry it in shade.", "Reserve parachutes have a clearer limit: makers design them for about ten years with proper care, and buyers are told not to take one older than twelve years from manufacture. Castelluccio's high plateau, with its summer flying and long cold winters, is where many Italian wings spend the off season badly. A dry cupboard indoors is better than any garage."], "rel": ["eq-uv-damage", "eq-porosity-testing", "eq-packing", "eq-washing"], "src": [{"t": "Advance Alpha 8 manual: care and storage", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/alpha_8/4011"}, {"t": "Cross Country: how to pack and take care of your paraglider", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=68075"}, {"t": "Supair reserve parachute manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.supair.com/document/notices/reserve_en.pdf"}]}, {"id": "eq-buying-second-hand", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 36.88, "lon": -5.4, "name": "What should I check when buying a second hand wing?", "place": "Explained over Algodonales, Spain", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A new beginner kit costs 2,500 to 4,000 euros; a bad used one can cost far more.", "body": ["Start with the paperwork. The wing needs a type plate with model, size and serial number, an inspection stamp or service report, and ideally a porosity reading. A wing without a label may be a prototype or stolen, and its size cannot be confirmed. Buy through pilots' forums or a dealer rather than general auction sites, and ask your instructor which classes and models suit you before you look.", "Then the cloth and lines. Look for white star shaped cracks in folds or a powdery surface, which mean a dead coating, and for fading, which hints at UV. Patches should have rounded corners and lie flat, and tears over 10 to 15 cm should have been stitched. Dark, dirty lines near the risers mean many hours. Mirror lines should match within 1 cm; a 1 to 2 cm difference means trim drift. Upper A risers are the best wear indicator.", "Harness and reserve are different. Replace any aluminium carabiners. Do not buy a reserve older than twelve years, and avoid any that has been repaired, wet or mouldy; many instructors say buy the reserve new. Algodonales, Spain's winter training hub, sees dozens of school wings sold on each spring; the sensible ones come with a fresh check and a test flight, and the price should reflect the remaining porosity, not the age."], "rel": ["eq-porosity-testing", "eq-trim-check", "eq-en-classes", "eq-carabiners"], "src": [{"t": "Skynomad: buying second hand paragliding gear", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/buying-second-hand-paragliding-gear"}, {"t": "Flybubble: reducing risk, your wing", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/reducing-risk-wing"}]}, {"id": "eq-ballast", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.47, "lon": -5.33, "name": "Why do pilots carry ballast?", "place": "Explained over Piedrahita, Spain", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": true, "hook": "Competition pilots fly with up to ten litres of water strapped under the seat.", "body": ["Every wing has a certified weight range, the total of pilot, harness, wing and everything else in flight. Within it, a heavier load makes the wing faster, more pressurised and more resistant to collapse, at the cost of a higher sink rate. A lighter load climbs better in weak lift but flies slower and gets pushed around. Ballast, usually water in a bag under the seat, lets a pilot move up the range on a windy day.", "Flybubble's guidance is that being under the range is worse than being over it: more collapses, more shaking and less forward speed. On strong days at sites like Piedrahita, where the Gredos thermals can be rough and the valley wind strong, pilots near the bottom of their range either add ballast or fly earlier and later in lighter air. Competition pilots carry water they can dump before landing.", "Ballast does not change the EN class, but flying outside the certified range does: recovery behaviour is only tested within it. Weigh yourself with the complete kit, including instruments, water and clothing, and compare it with the sticker. If you are habitually at the bottom, the honest answer is a smaller wing rather than permanent ballast, since you are also carrying it up the hill."], "rel": ["eq-en-classes", "eq-speed-bar-setup", "eq-harnesses", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Flybubble: reducing risk, your wing", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/reducing-risk-wing"}, {"t": "SHV/FSVL: description of EN classes (PDF)", "u": "https://www.shv-fsvl.ch/fileadmin/files/redakteure/Allgemein/Sicherheit/Sicherheit/EN-Klassen_Beschreibung_FR.pdf"}]}, {"id": "eq-speed-bar-setup", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.77, "lon": 11.73, "name": "How should I set up my speed bar?", "place": "Explained over Bassano del Grappa, Italy", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Set too long, the bar does nothing; set too short, you are flying accelerated without knowing.", "body": ["The speed bar is a cord from a foot stirrup in the harness, through pulleys, to Brummel hooks on the A and B risers. Length is the whole setup. Hang in a simulator or from a beam and adjust the cord so that with legs relaxed there is a little slack and the risers are untouched, and with legs straight the riser pulleys meet. If the pulleys never meet, you cannot reach the certified speed; if the cord is tight at rest, the wing is permanently a little accelerated.", "Routing affects range. Threading the cord in a figure of eight rather than an oval adds about 3 cm of travel, and typical full travel is 10 to 13 cm, roughly 1 km/h per centimetre. Brummel hooks only release in one orientation, so keep them aligned, and consider covers. Some pilots remove the hooks altogether and tie the cord directly, which is allowed as long as the speed range on the certification label is not exceeded.", "Check it every flight, especially after unclipping to wait on launch, because a hook left dangling becomes a line snag or a one sided bar. On Bassano's long autumn glides pilots live on the bar; the safe habits are to release it smoothly in turbulence rather than stamping on and off, keep hands up and light while accelerated, and never use full bar close to the ridge."], "rel": ["eq-risers-speed-system", "eq-harnesses", "eq-ballast", "eq-ccc-wings"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: speedbar range, Bruce Goldsmith", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=48826"}, {"t": "Flybubble: connecting the speed system", "u": "https://flybubble.com/blogs/blog/connecting-the-speed-system"}]}, {"id": "eq-how-vario-works", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.36, "lon": -118.4, "name": "How does a variometer work?", "place": "Explained over Owens Valley, USA", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "A pressure sensor the size of a grain of rice hears lift of one centimetre a second.", "body": ["A variometer measures vertical speed, not altitude. Air pressure drops as you climb, so the instrument samples the pressure many times a second and works out the rate of change, which it turns into a climb or sink figure and a tone. Older mechanical varios used a flask of air leaking through a tiny hole; modern ones use a solid state barometric sensor and a microprocessor, which is why they are small and respond fast.", "The sound is the point. A rising, chopping beep means lift, and the chop quickens as the climb improves, while a low groan means sink. The vario is usually silent in still air, so the pilot can keep eyes outside and still feel the thermal. Good units resolve changes of about 1 cm/s, and most also show an averager over 10 to 30 seconds, which is the number to trust when centring a climb.", "The first portable audio vario, built by Frank Colver in the early 1970s for hang gliders, is what made thermal cross country possible, and the Owens Valley in California was where those early long flights were flown. The traps are chasing the instant beep instead of the average, and flying with the volume off. A vario reports where the lift was half a second ago; your job is to guess where it will be next."], "rel": ["eq-instruments", "eq-gps-igc", "k-thermal", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Variometer", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Variometer"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Hang gliding (instruments)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hang_gliding#Instruments"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (instruments)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "eq-airspace-instruments", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.26, "lon": 11.39, "name": "How do I see airspace on my instrument?", "place": "Explained over Innsbruck, Austria", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "The line between a good flight and a police letter is a shaded polygon on a 4 inch screen.", "body": ["Airspace is divided into classes from A to G. Classes A to E are controlled, where air traffic control has authority, and F and G are uncontrolled, where paragliders normally fly. Around airports, and in the lower Alpine valleys near Innsbruck, controlled zones reach down to the ground, and restricted and danger areas sit on top. Flight instruments draw these as polygons with floors and ceilings, and warn when you approach one horizontally or vertically.", "The data comes as a file, usually in OpenAir format, published by national federations such as the DHV for Germany and loaded onto the instrument over WiFi or USB. It goes out of date: airspace changes with each aeronautical cycle, and temporary restrictions for air shows, military exercises and fire fighting never appear in last year's file. Instruments such as the Skytraxx 5 and Naviter's Oudie show distance to the nearest airspace and a vertical profile.", "The pilot is responsible, not the instrument. Set the altimeter to the day's QNH, because airspace limits are defined by pressure altitude and flight level, not GPS height. Check NOTAMs before an unfamiliar site. And decide on a margin: a wing drifting in a thermal gains 100 m a minute, so a limit should be treated as a wall that starts 200 m below it."], "rel": ["eq-instruments", "eq-gps-igc", "eq-flarm-fanet", "eq-paramotors"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Airspace class", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Airspace_class"}, {"t": "Flybubble: Skytraxx 5 with FANET, FLARM and ADS-L", "u": "https://flybubble.com/products/skytraxx-5-including-fanet-flarm-and-ads-l"}, {"t": "DHV: airspace Germany", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/en/sites-nature/airspace-germany/"}]}, {"id": "eq-trackers-sos", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -4.97, "lon": -39.02, "name": "What is a satellite tracker or SOS device?", "place": "Explained over Quixada, Brazil", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "Four hundred kilometres into the Brazilian outback, there is no phone signal and no road.", "body": ["A satellite messenger such as the Garmin inReach talks to the Iridium satellite network rather than a phone mast, so it works anywhere with a clear view of the sky. It sends position points at a set interval to a web map, allows two way text messages, and has a guarded SOS button that alerts a 24 hour emergency monitoring centre, which can message you back and call out local rescue. All of this needs a paid subscription.", "Pilots flying the record routes out of Quixada in Brazil's Ceara, where flights of 400 km or more end in scrub far from any town, treat one as essential kit, as do pilots in the Himalaya, Kenya and the Alps. Flymaster's cellular tracker also has an SOS button but depends on GSM coverage, which is exactly what is missing where you most need help. Many competitions now require a satellite device on remote tasks.", "The device must be on you, not in the harness pocket you may be separated from, and switched on before launch. Register the emergency contacts, tell them the plan, and send a check in after landing, because an unanswered dot in the hills triggers a search. An SOS is for injury or real danger; a long walk out is a message, not an emergency."], "rel": ["eq-live-tracking", "eq-radios", "eq-rescue-ropes", "kerio-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Garmin: inReach everyday aviation uses", "u": "https://www.garmin.com/en-US/blog/aviation/inreach-everyday-aviation-uses/"}, {"t": "Flymaster Tracker", "u": "https://www.flymaster.net/tracker"}]}, {"id": "eq-cameras-drones", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.59, "lon": -1.21, "name": "What are the risks of cameras and drones?", "place": "Explained over Dune du Pilat, France", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "A camera on a pole has snagged brake lines, and a drone has no idea you are coming.", "body": ["Action cameras are everywhere in paragliding, and the risks are mechanical and mental. A camera on a helmet or a selfie pole can catch a brake line during a launch or a collapse, and a rigid mount on the helmet can lever it off or transfer impact in a fall. Anything attached to a pilot should break away under load. The mental risk is bigger: a pilot framing a shot is not watching the wing, the ridge or the traffic.", "Drones add a second aircraft. In the UK the Drone Code limits drones to 120 m above the surface and keeps them away from people and other aircraft, and most countries have similar rules, but drone pilots on a popular site rarely know a paraglider is about to top land through their shot. The Dune du Pilat, Europe's tallest dune, packs tourists, drones and soaring pilots into the same 100 m of lift on a summer afternoon.", "The safe answers are dull. Mount cameras low on the harness or chest, use breakaway mounts, press record on the ground and forget it. If you fly a drone for your own footage, do it when nobody is flying, keep it well below ridge height and have a spotter. Many sites and clubs ban drones outright, and federations ask pilots to report near misses so that rules can follow the evidence."], "rel": ["eq-helmets", "eq-flarm-fanet", "eq-lines", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "UK CAA: the Drone Code", "u": "https://www.caa.co.uk/drones/open-category/drone-code/"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "eq-wing-repairs", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 21.03, "lon": 105.85, "name": "Can I repair my own wing?", "place": "Explained over Hanoi, Vietnam", "icon": "pot", "hidden": false, "hook": "A tear under 5 centimetres is a sticker job; anything near a rib goes to a workshop.", "body": ["Small damage is normal. Apco's control manual, typical of the industry, allows a tear up to 5 cm long to be patched with self adhesive ripstop tape, provided it is at least 5 cm from a rib or seam. Clean the cloth, cut the patch with rounded corners so it does not peel, lay it flat on both sides of the tear and press it hard. Patches that wrinkle or lift in the corners are what buyers see as warning signs.", "Anything bigger needs a sewing workshop, and tears over 10 to 15 cm should be stitched rather than taped. Damage at the leading edge, around the rod pockets or through a rib changes the wing's shape and must be repaired to the original pattern. Lines are replaced, never knotted: a field knot cuts the strength of the line by about a third and is a red flag at inspection. Replacement cells and line sets come from the factories in Vietnam that sew most of the world's wings.", "After any repair, check the line lengths around it, because stitching pulls the cloth, and test fly in calm air. Keep a small roll of tape, a few spare maillons and a spare brake line in the car. Hanoi and the factories around it supply Ozone, Nova, Niviuk and others; the same hands that built the wing are the right ones to rebuild it when the damage is serious."], "rel": ["eq-trim-check", "eq-line-length", "eq-cloth", "eq-buying-second-hand"], "src": [{"t": "Apco Aviation: check and control manual (PDF)", "u": "https://www.apcoaviation.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/12/manual_check_and_control_manual.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad: buying second hand paragliding gear", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/buying-second-hand-paragliding-gear"}, {"t": "Cross Country: inside the Niviuk factory in Vietnam", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/watch-niviuks-factory-tour-by-drone/"}]}, {"id": "eq-washing", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -12.12, "lon": -77.03, "name": "How do I wash a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Lima, Peru", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Water only, in the shade, and never in a washing machine.", "body": ["Every manufacturer gives the same answer. Remove dirt with a soft sponge or cloth and lukewarm fresh water, as soon as possible after it happens. For a stubborn mark use very diluted soap at most. Never use solvents, cleaning agents, brushes or a pressure washer, because they strip the coating that keeps the cloth airtight, and a washing machine does the same while tangling 300 m of line.", "Salt is the common problem. Pilots soaring the sea cliffs at Miraflores in Lima, or any coastal site, get spray and sand on the wing, and dried sea salt works like an abrasive inside the folds. Rinse the whole wing and lines thoroughly in fresh water after any contact with the sea. Sand and dust should be shaken out, not rubbed. Acids, silage, cow dung, grease and fuel should be rinsed off immediately.", "Drying matters as much as washing. Hang or spread the wing in shade at room temperature until it is completely dry, including the inside of the cells and the line sheaths, before it goes in the bag. A wing packed damp grows mould that destroys the coating. If you are unsure whether a stain is harmful, ask the maker, since a workshop can replace a panel but not a coating."], "rel": ["eq-storage-lifespan", "eq-packing", "eq-uv-damage", "eq-porosity-testing"], "src": [{"t": "Advance Alpha 8 manual: care and storage", "u": "https://manual.advance.ch/en/alpha_8/4011"}, {"t": "UP Paragliders: how can I make my paraglider last?", "u": "https://www.up-paragliders.com/service/faq/products/how-can-i-make-my-paraglider-last-for-as-long-as-possible"}]}, {"id": "eq-rescue-ropes", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.49, "lon": -121.95, "name": "What do I do after landing in a tree?", "place": "Explained over Tiger Mountain, USA", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": true, "hook": "The wing that caught you in the canopy is now the only thing holding you up.", "body": ["First, do nothing sudden. Most tree landing injuries happen after the landing, when the pilot thrashes, the wing tears free and they fall. Stay in the harness, check for injuries, and radio or phone your position and condition so that nobody launches a search for someone who is fine. If a solid branch is within reach, clip a sling to it with a locking carabiner before anything else, so that the tree holds you rather than the cloth.", "Then wait for help unless you are sure you can climb down. Rescue teams and tree climbers prefer a pilot still hanging than one on the ground with a broken back. If self rescue is the only option, pilots in forested country such as the Pacific Northwest, where Tiger Mountain is ringed by tall firs, carry a kit: 35 to 40 m of 6 mm cord rated well above body weight, a rappel device or Munter hitch, two locking carabiners, Kevlar slings and a whistle.", "Abseiling off the wing's own risers is a last resort, because once you weight the lines there is no going back if the canopy slips. A knot cuts a cord's strength by about a third, so 6 mm beats 5 mm for heavier pilots. None of this works untrained: practise with the kit from a low beam. And accept the real lesson, which is that the glide to the landing field was shorter than it looked."], "rel": ["eq-trackers-sos", "eq-radios", "eq-reserves", "eq-carabiners"], "src": [{"t": "Cascade Paragliding Club: tree rescue for paraglider pilots", "u": "https://cascadeparaglidingclub.org/reference-tree-rescue-for-paraglider-pilots/"}]}, {"id": "eq-carabiners", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 43.72, "lon": 6.98, "name": "How strong are the carabiners that hold me up?", "place": "Explained over Gourdon, France", "icon": "home", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two small steel or aluminium links carry everything, and they are the only part pilots replace on a schedule.", "body": ["The wing's risers clip to the harness with two carabiners, and the lines join the risers with small maillons. Flying loads are a few hundred newtons per side, but certification demands much more: harnesses are load tested to 15 g, and tandem connections must have a minimum breaking strain of 24 kN. Paragliding carabiners are purpose built, with gates designed to be loaded along the spine and often with a locking sleeve.", "Aluminium carabiners are light but suffer fatigue from the constant small load cycles of flight, and most makers recommend replacing them after a set number of hours or years, which is why advice to used gear buyers is to fit new ones. Steel lasts far longer at a weight penalty. Soft links of Dyneema are used on hike and fly kit, and they must be checked for abrasion because there is no metal to inspect.", "Pilots get hurt by the simple errors: a gate not closed, a riser clipped outside the carabiner, a maillon left loose after a line change so that it unscrews in flight. Gourdon, on the hills above the Cote d'Azur, is a busy site where a daily check routine matters. Look at both carabiners and all maillons before every launch, tighten maillons with a tool, and replace anything with a visible crack or a stiff gate."], "rel": ["eq-harnesses", "eq-risers-speed-system", "eq-tandem-wings", "eq-buying-second-hand"], "src": [{"t": "NZHGPA Tandem Technical Manual TM28 (PDF)", "u": "https://nzhgpa.org.nz/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/Tandem-Technical-Manual-TM28-6.5.24.pdf"}, {"t": "Air Turquoise EN 1651 test report, Supair VIP Lite (PDF)", "u": "https://supair.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/en-1651_2013_air-turquoise_vip-lite_ph-095.2013.pdf"}, {"t": "Skynomad: buying second hand paragliding gear", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/buying-second-hand-paragliding-gear"}]}, {"id": "st-chamonix-planpraz", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": 45.939, "lon": 6.853, "name": "Chamonix Planpraz", "place": "Planpraz, Chamonix-Mont-Blanc, France", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A cable car ride, a short walk, and you launch facing the highest mountain in western Europe.", "body": ["Planpraz sits at 2,032 metres on the Brévent side of the Chamonix valley, reached by the Planpraz gondola from the town centre. The launch faces south and north-east and looks straight across at Mont Blanc. The landing is the Bois du Bouchet field on the valley floor, about 1,000 metres lower, near the town at roughly 1,035 metres. You cannot see the landing from launch, so new visitors walk it first.", "The site is open all year and the best flying is from about 8 am to early afternoon, before the valley wind and thermals get strong. West or north winds above 20 km/h at 2,000 metres mean you would be flying downwind in rough air, and locals stay on the ground. In winter, landing at Le Bouchet and Le Savoy is banned while ski trails are open, which leaves only the small, turbulent delta field.", "Hazards are specific and well signed. The Brévent cable car cables run south-west of the launch, so pilots move right to pass under them. Horizontal cables cross the Lachenal combe at about 1,700 metres on the way to La Flégère. West and north-west of the summit ridge lies the Aiguilles Rouges nature reserve, where flying below 1,000 metres above ground is prohibited. The town says the site suits beginners to cross-country pilots, but thermic afternoons need real experience, so a current club pilot rating is the sensible minimum."], "rel": ["st-zermatt-rothorn", "st-st-hilaire-du-touvet", "annecy", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Chamonix tourist office: take-off from Planpraz, Brévent", "u": "https://en.chamonix.com/activities/summer/paragliding/take-off-from-planpraz-brevent"}, {"t": "Chamonix, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamonix"}]}, {"id": "st-malcesine-monte-baldo", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": 45.764, "lon": 10.845, "name": "Malcesine Monte Baldo", "place": "Monte Baldo, Malcesine, Italy", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "A postage-stamp landing beside a lake that has swallowed many pilots who misjudged the wind.", "body": ["The Monte Baldo cable car lifts pilots from the shore of Lake Garda to a launch at 1,760 metres. The south take-off, a ten minute walk from the top station, works in west winds. The north take-off at the far end of the ridge is steeper and used in east winds. The landing is a man-made field at 60 metres beside the lake at Malcesine, which gives a height difference of 1,700 metres.", "The lake runs its own wind machine. The Pelèr blows from the north in the morning, then after about 10 am the Ora arrives from the south and can be very strong around midday in summer, dying to nothing by evening. Around 5 pm the launch often becomes soarable again. April to October is the main season. November to March still gives wind but with more cloud and cold.", "Garda is one of the best known SIV and acro training sites in the world because a water landing is survivable where a ground landing is not. That same water is the main hazard: wear a lifejacket every flight, use a safety boat for manoeuvres, and never launch south or west in a north or east wind. The small landing and gusty midday Ora make this a site for pilots with a solid club rating and good landing accuracy."], "rel": ["st-bassano-del-grappa", "st-feltre-monte-avena", "k-valley-breeze", "st-ager"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Lake Garda", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-lake-garda-italy/"}, {"t": "Funivia Malcesine Monte Baldo: paragliding", "u": "https://funiviedelbaldo.it/en/paragliding/"}]}, {"id": "st-bassano-del-grappa", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": 45.81, "lon": 11.77, "name": "Bassano del Grappa", "place": "Semonzo, Borso del Grappa, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Carry a green smoke flare here, or a tree landing can cost you a 500 euro helicopter.", "body": ["Bassano is the southern edge of the Alps dropping onto the Venetian plain. Three road-access launches sit between 700 and 1,550 metres on south-facing slopes above the villages of Semonzo and Borso del Grappa, with the main landing at 160 metres in the grounds of a hotel on the valley floor. Most visiting pilots stay within a few minutes of the landing, which makes it one of the easiest sites in Europe to fly independently.", "Spring and autumn are the seasons to come. March to May gives the strongest conditions but more bad weather, September to November is more stable, and high summer is hot and flyable but tame. The afternoon heat low over the Alps swings the wind to the west, so the classic out-and-return goes west first and comes home with a tailwind later in the day.", "Rules are firm. Every visiting pilot joins the local club, Vol Libero Monte Grappa, and carries a FlyCard bought online or in Borso. Pilots are asked to carry a green smoke flare: after a tree landing it tells the rescue helicopter you are fine, and without it the launch costs a minimum of 500 euros. The real hazard is the string of unlandable valleys along the ridge, so get high before crossing them. Suitable for club pilots upward, with schools running courses for newer pilots."], "rel": ["st-feltre-monte-avena", "st-malcesine-monte-baldo", "st-meduno-monte-valinis"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Bassano", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-bassano-italy/"}]}, {"id": "st-fiesch-kuhboden", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": 46.4146, "lon": 8.108, "name": "Fiesch Kühboden", "place": "Fiescheralp, Fiesch, Switzerland", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Launch above the longest glacier in the Alps and fly the valley where the world's best XC pilots train.", "body": ["Fiesch in the upper Rhône valley is the Swiss cross-country capital. A cable car climbs from the village at 1,049 metres to Fiescheralp, the area long known as Kühboden, where several launches sit between 2,170 and 2,250 metres. A second lift continues to the Eggishorn at 2,879 metres, with the Great Aletsch Glacier spread out below it, part of the Jungfrau-Aletsch UNESCO World Heritage site.", "The valley wind is the character of the place. By early afternoon it regularly blows over 20 km/h up the Rhône, and the site guide warns that it can be turbulent even in a north wind. The Galfera launch has thermals pulling through the launch itself, so pilots keep the brakes in hand from the moment the wing comes up. The Chalet launch is reserved for tandems and the Kühboden 2 meadow is steep and short.", "This is a site for current pilots with mountain experience. Less experienced pilots are told to fly only in calm morning conditions, and the long glides across the valley to the south side need a plan for landing in strong wind. The reward is a valley system that runs for more than 100 kilometres, with routes east into the Goms and west toward Brig, and some of the most consistent alpine thermals in Switzerland."], "rel": ["st-zermatt-rothorn", "st-chamonix-planpraz", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding Map: Fiesch region sites", "u": "https://www.paraglidingmap.com/regions/fiesch"}, {"t": "Fiesch, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiesch"}]}, {"id": "st-st-hilaire-du-touvet", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": 45.303, "lon": 5.886, "name": "Saint-Hilaire du Touvet", "place": "Saint-Hilaire du Touvet, Chartreuse, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Every September 100,000 people come to watch pilots launch in fancy dress off this cliff.", "body": ["Saint-Hilaire is a plateau village on the edge of the Chartreuse massif, with a launch at about 900 metres on the lip of the cliff above the Isère valley. The landing is at Lumbin on the valley floor at 230 metres, so a plain top-to-bottom is 670 metres. The Dent de Crolles rises to 2,040 metres behind launch. There are north and south launches, which between them cover most wind directions.", "April, May and June are the cross-country months, with the long cliff keeping intermediate pilots entertained for hours in ridge and thermal lift. September brings the Coupe Icare, held on the last weekend of the month, where manufacturers show new wings and pilots fly in costume in front of about 100,000 visitors. A special bus runs up from Grenoble during the festival.", "The air close to the cliff face is bumpy in spring, and a southerly puts the top-landing area near the tourist office into turbulence and the hang glider launch in the lee. Watch the crowded gaggles below launch, where pilots of mixed ability share the same lift. Airspace for Grenoble and Chambéry airports caps the altitude over the valley. A club pilot rating is the realistic minimum for an unsupervised visit."], "rel": ["st-chamonix-planpraz", "st-laragne-chabre", "annecy", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to St Hilaire du Touvet", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-st-hilaire-du-touvet-france/"}]}, {"id": "st-roldanillo", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": 4.4, "lon": -76.19, "name": "Roldanillo", "place": "Los Tanques, Roldanillo, Colombia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A valley where 100 kilometre flights are routine in January and the sea breeze arrives from 100 kilometres away.", "body": ["Roldanillo lies in the Cauca valley of western Colombia, with the launch at Los Tanques at 1,800 metres on the western range and the landing at 950 metres on the flat valley floor around the town. Two lower launches, La Tulia and Pico, are reached by public transport and a walk. The site has hosted the Paragliding World Cup Superfinal and the FAI World Championships.", "The season runs December to March, with a second window from July to September. Meteo wind is usually light from north or south. The catch is the Pacific sea breeze that pushes in from the west in the afternoon with gusts of 25 to 30 km/h, reaching two or three kilometres into the valley. Pilots on the western ridges feel its turbulence first.", "The most common accident is launching too early into a westerly backwind on the hill, so wait for the upslope breeze to establish. Power lines run below launch, sugarcane fires throw rough thermals, and airspace is capped at 9,000 feet to the south-east and 10,000 feet to the north. Easy for new pilots in the morning, serious for competition pilots after noon."], "rel": ["st-governador-valadares", "st-castelo-brazil", "k-sea-breeze", "st-iquique"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Roldanillo", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-roldanillo-colombia/"}]}, {"id": "st-iquique", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": -20.25, "lon": -70.1, "name": "Iquique", "place": "Alto Hospicio, Iquique, Chile", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Flyable about 360 days a year, with a 220 metre sand dune between launch and the beach.", "body": ["Iquique sits on a narrow strip of Atacama coast with a ridge rising straight behind the city. The launch at Alto Hospicio is on that ridge, about 500 metres above the town, and the bus from the city goes almost to the top. Pilots soar the coastal ridge in a mix of dynamic and thermal lift, then glide out over the city to land on Playa Brava beach.", "The climate is the point. Rain is almost unknown and the site is flyable around 360 days a year, with the peak season from October to February and cross-country still possible in June and July. Mornings are gentle enough for students; by about 1 pm the wind and thermals build and the site belongs to the experienced XC and acro pilots.", "The hazard in between is Cerro Dragón, a 220 metre sand dune sitting between the ridge and the city. In strong conditions it is easy to end up low behind it and unable to reach the beach, and walking back up through sand in 30 degree heat with a glider is punishing. Early launches for beginners, later ones only with a current intermediate rating."], "rel": ["st-roldanillo", "st-torrey-pines", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: paragliding and hang gliding in Chile", "u": "https://xcmag.com/magazine-articles/red-hot-paragliding-and-hang-gliding-in-chile/"}]}, {"id": "st-queenstown-coronet-peak", "t": 2, "dom": "site", "lat": -44.92, "lon": 168.73, "name": "Queenstown Coronet Peak", "place": "Coronet Peak, Queenstown, New Zealand", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "In winter you launch from the top of a ski field with 1,600 metres of air beneath you.", "body": ["Coronet Peak is the ski mountain above Queenstown, a 25 minute drive from town and seven kilometres west of Arrowtown. Its summit is at 1,649 metres. There are two launches: a spring and summer launch with about 700 metres of height above the landing, and a winter launch from the very top of the ski field giving around 1,600 metres of descent over the Wakatipu basin.", "It is an afternoon site. Spring is good for cross-country, autumn and winter are more stable, and the evenings can get windy but usually stay flyable. The Queenstown Lakes area runs a complicated mix of sea breeze and valley wind, and the lakes themselves give useful warning: a change in the water surface shows a change in the wind before it reaches you.", "Visiting pilots need an international licence and NZHGPA visitor membership to fly legally in New Zealand. The guide describes the region as intermediate to advanced mountain flying, with limited phone coverage in side valleys, so file a plan with someone before you fly out."], "rel": ["st-wanaka-treble-cone", "st-bright-mystic", "st-stanwell-park", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Queenstown", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-queenstown-new-zealand-3/"}, {"t": "Coronet Peak, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coronet_Peak"}]}, {"id": "st-feltre-monte-avena", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 46.0305, "lon": 11.816, "name": "Feltre Monte Avena", "place": "Monte Avena, Feltre, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wide pasture on a hilltop where the 2017 World Championships were decided.", "body": ["Monte Avena is a grassy summit south-west of the Dolomites near Feltre, with launches at 1,400 metres facing west, south and east, so there is nearly always a slope into wind. The launch is open pasture with no obstacles and room for more than 20 gliders laid out. The landing is the Boscherai sports ground in Pedavena at 350 metres, a field of about 20,000 square metres.", "The main wind is from the south and south-east. The best season is May to August, with cloudbase typically at 2,000 to 2,500 metres and temperatures of 20 to 30 degrees. The road is paved except for the last two kilometres, and the Malga Campet hut sits beside launch. The 2017 FAI Paragliding World Championships flew here from 1 to 15 July.", "The ridge chains link west toward Bassano and the Monte Grappa massif, so pilots who want to go far need the same discipline as at Bassano: cross valleys high. For a local flight, the gentle launch and large landing make it one of the kinder competition venues in the Alps, suitable for a confident club pilot."], "rel": ["st-bassano-del-grappa", "st-malcesine-monte-baldo", "st-meduno-monte-valinis", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Airtribune: 15th FAI Paragliding World Championship, Monte Avena 2017", "u": "https://airtribune.com/worlds2017/"}]}, {"id": "st-gourdon", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 43.72, "lon": 6.98, "name": "Gourdon", "place": "Gourdon, Alpes-Maritimes, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Launch from a perched medieval village with the Mediterranean on the horizon and a 2,000 metre ceiling.", "body": ["Gourdon is a hilltop village above the Loup gorge behind Grasse, a few minutes from the Côte d'Azur. The L'Embarnier launches sit between 1,094 and 1,124 metres on a south-east facing ridge, with the lower car park launch and an upper launch that less experienced pilots are told to use. Landings on the valley floor near Le Bar-sur-Loup are tolerated rather than formally agreed, so pilots keep them tidy.", "The sea breeze from the south-east is the engine, and the Paragliding World Cup came here in 2024. The constraint is the Nice airport terminal area: flights are capped at flight level 65, roughly 2,000 metres, so this is a site for ridge and short XC rather than big climbs. Mini wings may not land in the valley fields, and landing on snow-covered ground is forbidden.", "North and north-west winds are the danger at the nearby Kennedy and Cavillore launches and make the whole area rough, and the Cavillore cliff launch has loose stones that snag lines. Expect a site briefing and a club rating as the minimum, keep an eye on the ceiling on your instrument, and remember that the valley landings are tolerated by the farmers rather than owed to you."], "rel": ["st-laragne-chabre", "st-st-hilaire-du-touvet", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding Map: Gourdon region sites", "u": "https://www.paraglidingmap.com/regions/gourdon"}, {"t": "PWCA: 2024 Paragliding World Cup Gourdon press release", "u": "https://pwca.events/2024/08/24/2024-paragliding-world-cup-gourdon-france-press-release"}]}, {"id": "st-laragne-chabre", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 44.3, "lon": 5.68, "name": "Laragne Chabre", "place": "Montagne de Chabre, Laragne, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The sky above Laragne hums with sailplanes, and the parachutists at Gap drop through it.", "body": ["The Montagne de Chabre is a long ridge in the Hautes-Alpes above Laragne-Montéglin, with launches at 1,304 metres facing both south and north. The south launch is an easy scrub slope. The north launch is a short, steep cliff launch that needs clean ground handling. The usual landing is the campsite field at Laragne at 735 metres.", "April to October is the season, and the site has hosted the Chabre Open for years. It is built for cross-country pilots of all levels: wide valleys, many landing fields and a ridge system that links toward Gap and the Durance. Air drawn in from the western plains arrives in the afternoon, so experienced pilots launch and get away before it does.", "The traffic is the hazard. The region is full of gliding airfields, and sailplanes share the thermals at closing speeds you will not expect, so know where the airfields are. The Gap drop zone is very active and must be avoided entirely. A club rating and a map of the airspace are the minimum."], "rel": ["st-gourdon", "st-st-hilaire-du-touvet", "st-piedrahita"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Laragne and Chabre", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=37963"}, {"t": "Sisteron Buëch tourism: Chabre free flight site", "u": "https://www.sisteron-alpesprovencales.fr/en/?p=7286"}]}, {"id": "st-piedrahita", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 40.42, "lon": -5.37, "name": "Piedrahita", "place": "Peña Negra, Piedrahita, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "New XC pilots regularly fly 50 kilometres to Avila here, and the best days give 200.", "body": ["Piedrahita sits at the foot of the Sierra de Gredos west of Avila. The launch at Peña Negra is a shallow north-west facing slope at 1,900 metres, with the landing on the flatland near the town at 1,000 metres. The gentle slope means heating builds from late morning rather than first thing, and pilots are usually airborne well before 1.30 pm, when the thermal breeze on launch becomes very strong.", "Classic cross-country runs from April to the end of September, with the strongest conditions from mid-June to late July. The 50 kilometre run to Avila is a standard first XC, 100 kilometre out-and-returns are common and 200 kilometre open distance flights are achievable on the best days. Airspace beyond Avila is avoided by routing further north.", "Strong easterlies and westerlies are the danger. A westerly accelerates at the pass and in the Ambles valley; an easterly leaves launch sheltered but turbulent further out, and strengthens beyond the col at Santiago de Collado. Bombing out in the Gredos means long retrieves. A club rating with some XC ambition is the right level."], "rel": ["st-ager", "st-algodonales", "k-thermal", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Piedrahita", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-piedrahita-spain/"}]}, {"id": "st-algodonales", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 36.898, "lon": -5.393, "name": "Algodonales", "place": "Sierra de Líjar, Algodonales, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two launches on one hill, one for each of Andalucia's two winds.", "body": ["Algodonales is a white village below the Sierra de Líjar in Cadiz province, and the hill has launches for both of the region's winds. Levante launch at 1,020 metres faces south-east to south-west and gives about 640 metres over its landing. Poniente launch at 970 metres faces west to south-west and drops about 495 metres. The Paragliding World Cup opened its 2025 season here.", "Spring and autumn give the strongest cross-country days, and winter is the stable, airtime-building season that fills the local schools. Summer thermals are strong enough that most guided weeks do not run in July and August. The Levante launch is shallow, so commit to the run and control the wing early.", "Levante is rated for pilots just past their first qualification, Poniente for club pilots with around 50 hours. The Poniente landing cannot be seen from launch and has electricity cables on its north-east side, so walk it before flying. The gully right of the Levante launch and the main landing can be strongly thermic by late morning, so beginners fly early."], "rel": ["st-piedrahita", "st-ager", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding Guide: paragliding sites in Algodonales", "u": "https://paraglidingguide.com/paragliding-sites-in-algodonales/"}, {"t": "PWCA: Paragliding World Cup kicks off in Algodonales 2025", "u": "https://pwca.events/2025/05/05/new-season-new-skies-paragliding-world-cup-kicks-off-in-algodonales-spain/"}]}, {"id": "st-ager", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 42.06, "lon": 0.8, "name": "Ager", "place": "Montsec, Ager, Catalonia, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The site that fines you for not wearing a seatbelt on the drive to launch.", "body": ["Ager lies in a valley below the Montsec ridge in the Catalan Pre-Pyrenees. The main launch at Coll d'Ares is at 1,586 metres and the campsite landing in the valley is at 606 metres, a height difference of nearly 1,000 metres. The ridge faces south and takes winds from south-west to south-east, and in a light north wind a thermic breeze up the face often keeps it working.", "It flies all year, with thermic conditions from early February to the end of October. Cross-country peaks in late spring and early summer and again in September and October. Novices can fly XC inside the valley while competition pilots have used the same air for decades, which is why schools and world champions share the launch.", "Easterlies put Coll d'Ares in rotor. Do not cross east over the reservoir, and watch the tow-boat line used for SIV courses on the route to the Chiringuito. The local traffic police fine pilots on the launch road, so belt up. A club rating is enough for valley flying; the ridge runs need more."], "rel": ["st-piedrahita", "st-algodonales", "st-malcesine-monte-baldo"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Ager", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-ager-spain/"}]}, {"id": "st-castelo-brazil", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -20.6, "lon": -41.19, "name": "Castelo", "place": "Ubá take-off, Castelo, Espírito Santo, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 110 metre grass launch that works in a crosswind and even a light tailwind.", "body": ["Castelo is a small town in the hills of Espírito Santo, inland from the Brazilian coast. The Ubá take-off sits about 850 metres above the valley on private land with public-interest status, so pilots use it without fees or permissions. The launch is a grass slope about 110 metres long and 50 metres wide, facing north to north-east, which makes it a natural sun collector from early morning.", "Because the slope is so wide and gentle, it can be used in light to moderate crosswinds or tailwinds without moving to another launch, which is rare for a mountain site. The FAI chose it for the 2026 Pan-American Paragliding XC Championship from 13 to 21 March, the end of summer and the start of the dry months.", "The organisers describe the launch as free of power lines, cables and other obstacles, with room for a helicopter and buses. The hazards are the usual ones of Brazilian inland flying: strong midday thermals, overdevelopment in the wet season and long retrieves. Suitable for club pilots upward."], "rel": ["st-governador-valadares", "st-rio-pedra-bonita", "st-roldanillo", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: PG XC Pan American 2026 hosted by Castelo, Brazil", "u": "https://www.fai.org/news/pg-xc-pan-american-2026-hosted-castelo-brazil"}]}, {"id": "st-governador-valadares", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -18.88, "lon": -41.95, "name": "Governador Valadares", "place": "Pico do Ibituruna, Governador Valadares, Brazil", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "One mountain, one launch, and climbs of 8 metres per second by September.", "body": ["Governador Valadares in Minas Gerais is built around a single rock, Pico do Ibituruna, with the only launch at 911 metres and the landing on the valley floor at 150 metres. The road goes to the top. Pilots aim to be on launch around 11 am and in the air between noon and 1 pm. The wind is usually light and thermic, and the heat is severe.", "January to April gives smooth, reliable thermals that suit a first Brazilian trip. Conditions strengthen through the year and peak in August and September, when the Paragliding World Cup has visited and climbs can reach 8 metres per second. Overdevelopment is common, so a growing cumulus deserves attention.", "The rules are about the town and the river. Do not fly over the town, and only cross the river to land in the official Via Dapas field, staying below 300 metres above it. An airport lies west-south-west. Do not scratch low on the cliff or in the steep valleys leading to it, and if flushed after launch, climb before working the fields."], "rel": ["st-castelo-brazil", "st-rio-pedra-bonita", "k-thermal", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Governador Valadares", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=37936"}, {"t": "PWCA: Paragliding World Cup Brazil, Governador Valadares 2026", "u": "https://pwca.events/paragliding-world-cup-brazil-governador-valadares-2026-2025-season"}]}, {"id": "st-sopot", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 42.68, "lon": 24.74, "name": "Sopot", "place": "Sopot, Stara Planina, Bulgaria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": true, "hook": "A chairlift launch on a 400 kilometre mountain wall with the Black Sea as a goal.", "body": ["Sopot sits in the Karlovo valley directly under the southern slopes of the Balkan range, the Stara Planina, which runs about 400 kilometres across Bulgaria and rises to around 2,300 metres. A chairlift carries pilots to a grassy launch at about 1,400 metres, with the landing in the monastery meadow at about 550 metres. Local house thermals sit just below launch.", "The warm, sunny south face is the attraction. Cross-country routes follow the range east and west, crossing gorges and passes, and stated goals include open distance to the Black Sea at 205 kilometres, out-and-returns of 140 kilometres and FAI triangles of 200 kilometres. The site hosted the 2012 pre-Worlds and the Bulgarian rounds of many international competitions.", "A strong north wind spills over the range and makes Sopot unflyable, and the guides move to alternative sites within an hour or two. Turbulence in the lee of the passes is the hazard to respect. Guided weeks take club pilots; independent XC here needs mountain experience and a retrieve plan for the long valley."], "rel": ["st-krushevo", "st-kossen-unterberg", "foehn", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "SkyNomad: cross-country paragliding in the Sopot region", "u": "https://www.skynomad.com/xc-guiding-sopot-region"}, {"t": "Sopot, Bulgaria, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sopot,_Bulgaria"}]}, {"id": "st-krushevo", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 41.37, "lon": 21.24, "name": "Krushevo", "place": "Krushevo, North Macedonia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A town in a bowl on a ridge top, with gentle thermals one side and rocky boomers the other.", "body": ["Krushevo is the highest town in North Macedonia, sitting in a natural bowl on a ridge above the Pelagonia plain. The grassy launch is on the ridge top above the town at 1,400 metres and the landing is at 650 metres in the flatland. Two further launches on the east side near Prilep are reached by tarmac roads.", "June to September has the best conditions and the site is generally flyable from April to October. There are no strong valley winds and no airspace issues, which is why the Paragliding World Cup and many international hang gliding and paragliding events have come here. Pilots follow the ridge north or south, or push out over the plain.", "The wooded west side gives milder air for newer pilots; the rocky east side produces the strong thermals that experienced XC pilots want. The usual problem is a walk-out from the fields, where drainage ditches make the ground awkward, so land near roads or villages. Suitable for all levels, with the Ozone Krushevo Open aimed at first-time competitors."], "rel": ["st-sopot", "st-castelluccio", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Krusevo", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-krusevo-macedonia/"}, {"t": "PWCA: Paragliding World Cup Macedonia, Krushevo 2025", "u": "https://pwca.events/paragliding-world-cup-macedonia-krushevo-2025"}]}, {"id": "st-kamshet", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 18.76, "lon": 73.55, "name": "Kamshet", "place": "Kamshet, Maharashtra, India", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Airliners from Mumbai to Pune pass 1,800 metres above India's busiest training hill.", "body": ["Kamshet is a cluster of flat-topped Western Ghats hills between Mumbai and Pune, and the home of most of India's paragliding schools. Tower Hill launch at 857 metres faces east with its landing at 617 metres, and flies from November to February on the morning east wind. Shelar launch at 720 metres faces west, lands at 640 metres and takes over from January to May when the wind swings west.", "The flying is smooth and the height differences are small, which makes it ideal for new and intermediate pilots building hours. The monsoon shuts everything from June to September. The sites lie under the Mumbai to Pune flight path, with airliners passing at about 1,800 metres, which puts a hard lid on the climbs.", "Each hill has a trap. On Tower Hill a south-west wind puts the west face in rotor. At Shelar, strong wind forms a venturi to the left of launch that catches pilots every season, and summer mornings are often too strong, so most flying happens later in the day. Students fly under instruction; qualified pilots find it easy."], "rel": ["st-vagamon", "bir-billing", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: paragliding in Kamshet, India", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/asia/paragliding-in-kamshet-india/"}]}, {"id": "st-vagamon", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 9.688, "lon": 76.907, "name": "Vagamon", "place": "Vagamon, Kerala, India", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "Rolling green meadows at 1,100 metres where Kerala stages its paragliding festivals.", "body": ["Vagamon is a hill station on the Kottayam-Idukki border in Kerala, about 1,100 metres above sea level, with bare grass hills, pine plantations and tea estates. The flying areas are within five kilometres of the town and the launches are grass meadows, so take-off and landing need only a short run. Kerala Tourism promotes the site and has hosted international festivals and competitions here.", "The season is October to March, with December to February the most reliable, after the south-west and north-east monsoons have both passed. Flights run between about 9 am and 4 pm depending on wind, and nothing flies in heavy rain or strong wind. The hills are low, so this is soaring and short thermal flying rather than big cross-country.", "For a visiting pilot the attraction is a mild, scenic site to keep current in the Indian winter, and a counterpoint to the mountain flying of the Himalaya. Treat the gentle appearance with respect: the meadows are rounded and rotor forms behind the crests in stronger wind. A basic qualification is sufficient under local guidance."], "rel": ["st-kamshet", "bir-billing", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Kerala Tourism: paragliding in Vagamon", "u": "https://www.keralatourism.org/adventure-tourism/paragliding/vagamon"}, {"t": "Vagamon, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vagamon"}]}, {"id": "st-bright-mystic", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -36.759, "lon": 146.966, "name": "Bright Mystic", "place": "Mystic Hill, Bright, Victoria, Australia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Wedge-tailed eagles defend this hill and will attack your wing from October to March.", "body": ["Mystic is the launch above the town of Bright in north-east Victoria, at 797 metres above sea level and 477 metres above the landing. The valley breeze blows most of the time from the north-west and the launch works on it. The Mystic landing ground is beside the road to Wandiligong, with a newer field on the right of that road and power lines running along it.", "In summer the lift between noon and 3.30 pm is strong and rough, so the site guide says to launch before or after. Other seasons fly almost any time. The Bright Open and the Australian Open have used the site for decades, and the routes run along the Ovens valley or south into the high country.", "The site rating is PG4, with PG2 and PG3 pilots flying only under PG4 supervision. The south and the gap between Pyramid Hill and the Kiewa valley are tiger country with no landings. Wedge-tailed eagles are territorial, especially near Little Buffalo, and attacks are more frequent from October to March: turn away, do not stall, and expect holes in the fabric."], "rel": ["st-stanwell-park", "st-queenstown-coronet-peak", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Australian site guide: Mystic, Bright", "u": "https://siteguide.org.au/Sites/Mystic.html"}, {"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Bright", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-bright-australia"}]}, {"id": "st-wanaka-treble-cone", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -44.63, "lon": 168.89, "name": "Wanaka Treble Cone", "place": "Treble Cone, Wanaka, New Zealand", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "The gate code to launch belongs to the club, and joining the national association is the law.", "body": ["Treble Cone is the ski field west of Wanaka, and the main paragliding launch sits just below it at 1,100 metres on the access road, with the landing at 300 metres near the lake. The height difference of 800 metres and the view over Lake Wanaka make it the most used site in the district for both tandems and visiting pilots.", "October and November bring the most unstable, thermic skies, with more chance of rain and wind. January to March is more consistent. The lakes act as a wind gauge, showing a change on the water surface before it reaches the hill, and the sea breeze and valley breeze system across the region is complicated enough to need a local briefing.", "Visiting pilots must join the NZHGPA, which the guide states plainly is the law, and the local club holds the gate combination for the road. Some valleys beyond have no roads and little phone coverage, so check the map and plan retrieves before flying far. The landing is tight for hang gliders. Qualified club pilots are welcome."], "rel": ["st-queenstown-coronet-peak", "st-bright-mystic", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Wanaka", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-wanaka-new-zealand/"}]}, {"id": "st-torrey-pines", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 32.89, "lon": -117.251, "name": "Torrey Pines", "place": "Torrey Pines Gliderport, San Diego, USA", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 98 metre cliff with about 300 flying days a year, and a whistle is compulsory.", "body": ["The Torrey Pines Gliderport sits on a sandstone cliff north of San Diego, with the launch at 98 metres above Black's Beach. Sea breeze hits the cliff and gives smooth ridge lift along the coast, and most flights end with a top landing back at the gliderport. Sinking out to the beach is easy; the climb back up is a steep hike.", "April to October is most consistent, but winter is also good and the site counts about 300 flying days a year. The gliderport is one of the most historic flying sites in the United States and is tightly run: check in at the office, show your rating and logbook, sign waivers, watch the orientation video, pay the day fee and carry a whistle and a radio on the site frequency.", "Visiting paraglider pilots need a USHPA P3 with 50 hours, or the foreign equivalent shown on an IPPI card with 150 flights and 50 hours, plus a ground handling check. Other aircraft are the main hazard: hang gliders, tandems and radio-controlled models share the ridge, so follow the ridge rules and whistle to signal. Rotor when top landing outside the marked areas catches people out."], "rel": ["st-stanwell-park", "st-point-of-the-mountain", "st-iquique", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Torrey Pines Gliderport: visiting pilot requirements", "u": "https://www.flytorrey.com/?p=149"}, {"t": "Cross Country magazine: Torrey Pines site guide", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=38834"}]}, {"id": "st-point-of-the-mountain", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 40.478, "lon": -111.906, "name": "Point of the Mountain", "place": "Flight Park State Park, Draper, Utah, USA", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A gravel ridge where the south side flies at dawn and the north side at dusk, almost every day.", "body": ["Point of the Mountain is a low ridge between Salt Lake City and Provo, protected as Flight Park State Park. It is the most used training hill in the United States. Mornings bring a consistent south wind and smooth soaring on the south side; evenings flip to the north side at the county park. After about 10 am in summer the thermals get strong and the smooth soaring ends.", "The park asks for a current USHPA P2 or H2 rating as the minimum, with students and P1 pilots flying only with an approved instructor. Paragliders launch and land on the west side, hang gliders on the east. Everyone flies the standard pattern, passes on the right, keeps the counter-clockwise rotation and thermals outside the ridge pattern. Paramotors are banned, as is freestyle in a crowd.", "The listed hazards are precise. An irrigation canal south of the landing zone is described as extremely dangerous. Power lines cross the gravel pit and run behind launch. Gliders flying streamers are students, so give them room. The park warns that congestion and a wind shift can turn a P2 day into a P4 day in minutes."], "rel": ["st-torrey-pines", "st-chelan-butte", "st-woodrat-mountain"], "src": [{"t": "Utah State Parks: Flight Park brochure", "u": "https://stateparks.utah.gov/wp-content/uploads/sites/13/2018/02/Flight_Parkbrochure.pdf"}]}, {"id": "st-chelan-butte", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 47.82, "lon": -120.06, "name": "Chelan Butte", "place": "Chelan Butte, Washington, USA", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Launch between a lake and a river, then fly 160 kilometres of flatland with nowhere you cannot land.", "body": ["Chelan Butte stands between Lake Chelan and the Columbia River in central Washington. The main south-facing launch is at 1,158 metres and starts working around 11 am. The main landing is Chelan Falls Park at 244 metres on the river, with rolling fields all the way down. Thermals from the south face funnel up to the Between the Rocks launch.", "The season runs March to October and the cross-country months are June to August, when the US Open and the Chelan Open are flown. Surface flow usually carries pilots north-east across the river onto the flats toward Mansfield, and the flatlands run south and east to the state line with landing options almost everywhere. Controlled airspace only begins near Spokane, about 160 kilometres east.", "Dust devils on launch are the first hazard: watch the bushes and pick your cycle, and stay alert from the moment you are hooked in. Military jets fly low and fast through the area. Much of the land is public and the farmers are welcoming if crops are respected. An intermediate rating with thermal experience is the practical minimum."], "rel": ["st-golden-mount-7", "st-woodrat-mountain", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Chelan", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=37804"}, {"t": "Airtribune: US Open of Paragliding 2026, Chelan", "u": "https://airtribune.com/us-open-paragliding-2026/"}]}, {"id": "st-golden-mount-7", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 51.27, "lon": -116.93, "name": "Golden Mount 7", "place": "Mount 7, Golden, British Columbia, Canada", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Land out in the wrong valley here and the walk home can take two days.", "body": ["Mount 7 rises above the town of Golden in the Rocky Mountain Trench, and a 14 kilometre forestry road reaches the main launch at 1,960 metres. The landing is at Nicholson at 800 metres, ten minutes south of town. Two lower launches are used in spring. Most flights head south along the trench, and the Willi Classic held here is the longest running cross-country competition in Canada.", "The season is May to October with July, August and September the peak. Summer thermals of 8 to 12 metres per second are normal, and the route east across the Rockies to Canmore has been completed by only four pilots. Early summer is kind enough for all abilities; high summer is for pilots who are current and strong.", "Thunderstorm gust fronts are the named hazard, reaching 70 km/h and travelling far along the valley: the advice is to fly downwind as far as possible and land. Strong south winds can stop paragliders reaching the normal landing. The back country is remote, and a landing in the next valley can mean a two-day walk out, so carry a tracker."], "rel": ["st-chelan-butte", "st-woodrat-mountain", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Golden, Canada", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-golden-canada/"}, {"t": "Airtribune: 2026 Canadian Paragliding Nationals, Golden", "u": "https://airtribune.com/2026-canadian-paragliding-nationals-golden/info"}]}, {"id": "st-wilderness-map-of-africa", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -33.992, "lon": 22.56, "name": "Wilderness Map of Africa", "place": "Map of Africa, Wilderness, South Africa", "icon": "wave", "hidden": true, "hook": "A grass launch 120 metres above the surf, named for a river bend shaped like the continent.", "body": ["The Map of Africa is a viewpoint on the Garden Route above Wilderness, where the Kaaimans river loops into the shape of the continent. The launch is a gently sloping grass field, steepening toward the edge, about 120 metres above the waves. Pilots soar the sea cliffs and land anywhere on the beach away from sunbathers; in the busy season, east of the rocks on the first section. A forest path leads back up from the car park.", "The site works in a south to south-east sea breeze. A south-westerly curls up the face and feels fine on launch but turns into heavy sink and turbulence once you fly out, and an easterly makes launching risky. Airspace is capped at 450 metres and George Airport lies to the north-west, so pilots phone George air traffic control before flying.", "Read the water. Cat's paws spreading across the sea mean a sudden wind increase that can blow you over the back; white caps mean it is usually too strong. Powerlines run to the west, the surf is powerful and the sea is never a landing option. Site fees go to the Map Jump Club, which posts the rules on the board at launch."], "rel": ["st-cape-town-lions-head", "st-porterville-dasklip", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "SAHPA site guide: Map of Africa", "u": "https://www.sahpa.co.za/site-guide/map-of-africa"}]}, {"id": "st-porterville-dasklip", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -32.89, "lon": 19.06, "name": "Porterville Dasklip Pass", "place": "Dasklip Pass, Porterville, South Africa", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 100 kilometre escarpment facing the afternoon sun, in the country with more XC records than any other.", "body": ["Porterville is a farming town below the Olifants River mountains, 150 kilometres north of Cape Town. The Dasklip Pass launch is at 713 metres on a west-facing escarpment that runs for about 100 kilometres, and a second launch at Pampoenfontein sits at 900 metres. The landing is on the farmland outside Porterville at 233 metres.", "November to March is the cross-country season, when the summer sun heats the wheat plains and the sea breeze from the Atlantic feeds the escarpment through the afternoon. The site has hosted several large competitions and is a regular base for European pilots escaping winter. The classic run goes north along the ridge toward the Cederberg.", "The named risks are bombing out somewhere remote, encounters with wildlife on the walk out, and an air force training area to the west. On the Renosterhoek route, getting home becomes tricky if the wind swings too far south. A club rating with thermalling experience is the realistic minimum, and the local schools run guided weeks."], "rel": ["st-cape-town-lions-head", "st-wilderness-map-of-africa", "kerio-valley", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Porterville", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=38707"}]}, {"id": "st-tenerife-izana", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 28.3, "lon": -16.5, "name": "Tenerife Izaña", "place": "Izaña, Teide National Park, Tenerife, Spain", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Launch at 2,300 metres beside the Teide observatory and land on a beach, north or south, 2,000 metres below.", "body": ["Izaña is a launch at about 2,300 metres on the TF-24 road between kilometres 33 and 34, inside Teide National Park on Tenerife. Pilots take off facing the volcano and then choose a side of the island. Northbound flights land at the Faro or the El Peñón football ground esplanade in Puerto de la Cruz. Southbound flights land at the Güímar-La Ladera esplanade or on Puertito de Güímar beach.", "The beach landing is allowed only in winter, when the sand is empty, and never in summer with people on it. The island's weather changes fast: the tourism board rates the site intermediate on a normal day but says it can become expert territory within hours. Pilots are asked to contact the Canary Islands federation shortly before flying for updates on launch and landing conditions.", "The trade winds from the north-east that make Lanzarote difficult are what Tenerife's sheltered sites avoid, but at 2,300 metres the upper wind can be nothing like the coast. Cloud forms on the north side below the launch, so a northbound flight can mean descending through the inversion into a different climate. Launch only with a current local forecast."], "rel": ["st-chamonix-planpraz", "st-torrey-pines", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Tenerife tourism: Izaña, either north or southbound", "u": "https://www.webtenerife.co.uk/what-to-do/sports/air/paragliding/izana-norte-sur"}, {"t": "Cross Country magazine: paragliding in Lanzarote", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/destination-lanzarote/"}]}, {"id": "st-cape-town-lions-head", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -33.935, "lon": 18.39, "name": "Cape Town Lion's Head", "place": "Lion's Head, Table Mountain National Park, Cape Town, South Africa, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two launches on a city peak, a 55 flight minimum, and the Atlantic waiting if the wind shadow breaks.", "body": ["Lion's Head is the 669 metre peak between Table Mountain and the Atlantic suburbs of Cape Town, inside Table Mountain National Park, part of the Cape Floral Region World Heritage site. The lower launch is at 400 metres and the upper at 450 metres. The primary landing is the Edgar Lipsett Oval at the Glen Country Club, with the Camps Bay High School field as an alternative. Airspace is capped at 1,200 metres.", "The lower launch takes a south-westerly of 5 to 20 km/h and the upper a south to west-south-westerly in the same range. The best thermic flying is often from 3 pm in the wind shadow of a south-easter. In a south-westerly the wind splits around the peak and strengthens at both launches, crossing from the left above and from the right below.", "Access rules are strict: SAHPA membership, a basic licence with at least 55 flights, the first four flights supervised and co-signed, a SANParks activity permit and, for visitors, a foreign pilot permit. The hazards are being blown over the back, strong venturi at both launches, steel fence poles 20 metres below the upper mat, and rotor from Table Mountain when the south-easter breaks down and pushes pilots out to sea."], "rel": ["st-wilderness-map-of-africa", "st-porterville-dasklip", "st-rio-pedra-bonita"], "src": [{"t": "SAHPA site guide: Lion's Head", "u": "https://sahpa.co.za/site-guide/lions-head"}]}, {"id": "st-rio-pedra-bonita", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -22.995, "lon": -43.28, "name": "Rio Pedra Bonita", "place": "Pedra Bonita, São Conrado, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "A ramp 507 metres above a city beach, and about 30 days a year when the Christ flight works.", "body": ["Pedra Bonita is the flat-topped mountain above São Conrado in Rio, with a launch at about 507 metres and the landing on Pepino beach at sea level. A buggy takes pilots up the forest road, marshals lay out gliders and control the queue, and paperwork is checked at both the clubhouse beside the landing and on launch. Licences are verified and visitors pay a day fee for temporary membership.", "The site is flyable all year. Spring and summer give the best chance of the famous flight to the Christ statue, a technical cross-country of about 30 kilometres out and back that needs a cloudbase around 800 metres and an east wind, conditions that come perhaps 30 times a year, often behind a cold front.", "Hazards are urban: tandem pilots jumping the launch queue, low cloud sitting on launch, limited landing options and wires if you fly low over the city, and rotor behind the apartment blocks along the beach. Tandem hang gliding and paragliding here is a large industry, and the club rules exist because of past accidents. A club rating and a briefing are the minimum."], "rel": ["st-governador-valadares", "st-castelo-brazil", "st-cape-town-lions-head", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Rio de Janeiro", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-rio-de-janeiro-brazil/"}]}, {"id": "st-stanwell-park", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": -34.224, "lon": 150.997, "name": "Stanwell Park Bald Hill", "place": "Bald Hill, Stanwell Park, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "If no one has set out the cones on the beach, nobody flies.", "body": ["Bald Hill is a 180 metre headland above Stanwell Park on the coast south of Sydney, the hill where Lawrence Hargrave flew his box kites and the birthplace of Australian hang gliding. The Point launch takes winds from east-south-east through south-south-west; the East launch takes a straight easterly. Hang gliders want 10 to 20 knots, paragliders 5 to 15 knots, roughly 9 to 28 km/h.", "The main landing is beside the lagoon in the area called the chute, or in the coned-off area on the beach, with an alternative at the south end. The cones or markers must be in place before anyone flies, and the site may not be flown without them. Beachgoers wander into the landing area constantly, and the sea is never a landing.", "The site rating is PG4 or hang gliding intermediate, and only endorsed pilots may top land; the hill itself is a no-fly zone. The sneaky hazard is winter katabatic flow, which shows as glass-off on the water and can switch off a strong south-easterly in minutes. Shadows crossing the landing area are the warning. Rotor pours off the spurs north and south of the chute in strong south or north-east winds."], "rel": ["st-bright-mystic", "st-torrey-pines", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Australian site guide: Stanwell Park", "u": "https://siteguide.org.au/Sites/Details/Stanwell"}]}, {"id": "st-asagiri-kogen", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 35.4, "lon": 138.57, "name": "Asagiri Kogen", "place": "Asagiri Plateau, Fujinomiya, Japan", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": true, "hook": "A dairy plateau at 800 metres where Mount Fuji fills the background of every photo, when the haze allows.", "body": ["The Asagiri Plateau lies on the south-western flank of Mount Fuji in Shizuoka Prefecture, at about 800 to 900 metres, a landscape of dairy farms and grassland. It is one of Japan's top hang gliding and paragliding sites and draws pilots from across the country and abroad, with two schools based along Route 139 at Fumoto, in Fujinomiya.", "The flying is gentle soaring and thermals over wide fields, with a 3,776 metre volcano as the backdrop, which is why the schools sell tandems to visitors all year. Late autumn and winter give the clearest air and the best chance of seeing Fuji; in summer the haze often hides it entirely, and the schools say so plainly in their booking notes.", "A mountain that size shapes the wind for a long way around it, and the plateau is not the place to guess at what the upper air is doing, so visiting pilots fly with the schools, which run tandems and instruction every day the weather permits. A basic rating with a local briefing is enough for the training slopes; anything more needs a local pilot alongside."], "rel": ["st-kamshet", "st-queenstown-coronet-peak", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Asagiri Plateau, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asagiri_Plateau"}, {"t": "Explore Shizuoka: Mt Fuji paragliding experience", "u": "https://exploreshizuoka.jp/activities/places/mt-fuji-paragliding-experience/"}]}, {"id": "st-kossen-unterberg", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 47.64, "lon": 12.43, "name": "Kössen Unterberg", "place": "Unterberghorn, Kössen, Tyrol, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Wide grass launches, a cable car, and a landing-free valley the locals call the Bermuda Triangle.", "body": ["Kössen is a village in the Kaiserwinkl of Tyrol, on the German border, and the Unterberghorn above it is one of the most civilised sites in the Alps. A cable car reaches launches between 1,500 and 1,700 metres with take-offs facing east, north and west, so some slope always faces the wind. The landing is at 650 metres near the village, with the Fliegerbar beside it where the landing rules are posted.", "May to the end of September is the season. Regular afternoon alpine breezes set up but rarely become unmanageable, the valleys are open and friendly for landing, and the guide calls it as safe a place to fly as you can find in the Alps. Cross-country routes run along the Chiemgau and Kaiser ranges for all levels of pilot.", "The one hazard with a name is the Bermuda Triangle, a valley west of launch with no landing options at all. Kössen has hosted FAI world championships in both hang gliding and paragliding, and the first paragliding worlds flew here in 1989. A club rating is enough for the house flights."], "rel": ["st-greifenburg-emberger-alm", "st-zermatt-rothorn", "st-sopot", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Koessen", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-koessen-austria"}]}, {"id": "st-greifenburg-emberger-alm", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 46.78, "lon": 13.18, "name": "Greifenburg Emberger Alm", "place": "Emberger Alm, Greifenburg, Carinthia, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A campsite built for pilots, with the landing field at the tent door and launches 900 metres above it.", "body": ["Greifenburg in the Drau valley of Carinthia is organised around the Fliegercamp, a campsite with the landing field alongside it at about 1,068 metres. The launches on the Emberger Alm above sit between 1,741 metres at the Thermiktreff and about 2,005 metres at the upper take-offs, reached by a toll road. The Drau valley runs east to west and gives long valley wind routes toward Lienz and the Dolomites.", "The site guide notes that the launches should only be used with a headwind, and the Thermiktreff in particular is not entirely safe for paragliders without wind from the front. West wind brings turbulence in the lee of the trees, and the landing area has power lines nearby, so the approach is briefed before the first flight.", "Greifenburg hosts the Austrian league and a steady stream of club trips. The combination of a huge landing, a valley with options and a reliable thermal source makes it a good first alpine XC site for club pilots who have done a mountain briefing. The afternoon valley wind is strong enough that the campsite landing is an exercise in wind-gradient awareness."], "rel": ["st-kossen-unterberg", "st-meduno-monte-valinis", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding Map: Greifenburg region sites", "u": "https://www.paraglidingmap.com/regions/greifenburg"}]}, {"id": "st-meduno-monte-valinis", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 46.23, "lon": 12.81, "name": "Meduno Monte Valinis", "place": "Monte Valinis, Meduno, Friuli, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Close the gate behind you: the cows share the launch at this World Cup site.", "body": ["Monte Valinis is the launch above the village of Meduno in Friuli, where the Carnic Pre-Alps meet the plain. The take-off is at 975 metres on a grassy pasture with a large landing in the fields below the village. The wind at launch usually comes from the south, off the plain, and the site has hosted Paragliding World Cup rounds and Italian championships.", "The ridge behind links to Piancavallo and the Cansiglio to the west and to Gemona and the Julian Alps to the east, with the flatland of the Tagliamento to the south for long glides home. Spring and early summer are the competition months, with afternoon valley winds that need managing once a pilot heads into the mountains.", "The guide warns about easterly winds and says the air becomes turbulent quickly once the wind moves away from the south. Do not approach the landing from too far back, and keep the pasture gate closed because animals run free near launch. Club pilots with thermal experience will find it an easy introduction to Italian flying."], "rel": ["st-feltre-monte-avena", "st-bassano-del-grappa", "st-greifenburg-emberger-alm", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding Map: Meduno region sites", "u": "https://www.paraglidingmap.com/regions/meduno"}]}, {"id": "st-castelluccio", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 42.808, "lon": 13.19, "name": "Castelluccio di Norcia", "place": "Castelluccio di Norcia, Umbria, Italy", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": true, "hook": "A flowering plateau at 1,452 metres, a ruined village, and dust devils that hunt the landing field.", "body": ["Castelluccio is a hilltop village at 1,452 metres above the Piano Grande, a high karst plain about 64 kilometres around, under Monte Vettore in the Sibillini mountains. The earthquakes of August and October 2016 destroyed about 60 per cent of the historic village, and much of it was still in ruins in 2025, but a national free flight school has operated here for more than 25 years and the flying continues.", "The Fontanile launch above the village gives soaring on the slopes and thermals off the plain, with cross-country possible along the Sibillini. June brings the fioritura, when the plain turns to stripes of poppies and lentils in flower, and the shuttle to launch runs daily when conditions are good in season.", "The site notes are blunt: dust devils, especially low over the landing site, thunderstorms that build quickly in May and June, and strong wind at altitude when there is none on launch, particularly when soaring over Monte Vettore. No low passes or figure eights over the horses near the landing. A club rating with thermal experience is appropriate."], "rel": ["st-monte-cucco", "st-krushevo", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Castelluccio (Norcia), Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castelluccio_(Norcia)"}, {"t": "Flyappi: Castelluccio Fontanile site", "u": "https://flyappi.org/flysite/6552/"}, {"t": "Landscape atlas: paragliding at Castelluccio", "u": "https://atlas.landscapefor.eu/category/luoghi-rurali/poi/6648-castelluccio-di-norcia/5594-paragliding-at-castelluccio/"}]}, {"id": "st-monte-cucco", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 43.37, "lon": 12.74, "name": "Monte Cucco", "place": "Monte Cucco, Sigillo, Umbria, Italy", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Four launches on one mountain, so a slope always faces the wind, with rigging space for 125 gliders.", "body": ["Monte Cucco is a 1,566 metre limestone mountain in the Apennines above the village of Sigillo in Umbria, and one of the historic homes of Italian hang gliding. The main take-off is at 1,250 metres, about 700 metres above the valley, launched straight from the grass hillside with no ramps. The rigging area holds more than 125 pilots and cars park beside it.", "Four launches, Val di Ranco, Pian di Monte, La Pianaccia and the Sella di Culumeo, cover the compass, and the organisers say one is always usable whatever the wind. Flying works in both north and south winds. Top landings are possible at Pian di Monte and the Sella di Culumeo, which keeps the training traffic off the valley.", "The Monte Cucco team was awarded the 2020 European Hang Gliding Championship and has run the Piero Alberini international trophy here in August. The air is described as the meeting of Atlantic and Balkan flows with no serious obstacles, which also means a strong day switches from one to the other without warning. Suitable for club pilots upward, with a school on the hill."], "rel": ["st-castelluccio", "st-meduno-monte-valinis", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: Monte Cucco to host 21st European Hang Gliding Championship", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/183047"}, {"t": "Airtribune: Monte Cucco International Trophy 2018", "u": "https://airtribune.com/cucco2018/"}]}, {"id": "st-woodrat-mountain", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 42.231, "lon": -123.004, "name": "Woodrat Mountain", "place": "Woodrat Mountain, Ruch, Oregon, USA", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Home of the Rat Race, where two inversions and a convergence line decide who gets high.", "body": ["Woodrat Mountain stands above the Applegate valley near Ruch in southern Oregon, on public land managed by the Bureau of Land Management. Top launch is at 1,162 metres and mid launch at 880 metres, with the main landing at 500 metres in the valley. The site is best in west, north-west or north winds, and the best days come after a cold front.", "April to June is the prime season, though it flies every month: summer brings steady high pressure, early autumn is reliable, winter gives sled rides. Woodrat hosts the Rat Race, one of the best known paragliding competitions in the United States. RVHPA membership is required to fly, with day membership sold at the Ruch country store a mile from the landing.", "Do not fly in strong south or east winds because of large-scale rotor, and a southerly can hide rotor at top launch, so check the winds aloft. The valley sits under a convergence of Applegate and Rogue valley winds, summer inversions at 1,250 and 1,850 metres cause turbulence, and Medford airspace 8 kilometres east means staying under 1,850 metres. Some landing fields are sensitive; ask locals first."], "rel": ["st-chelan-butte", "st-golden-mount-7", "st-point-of-the-mountain"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country magazine: guide to Woodrat", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-woodrat-usa/"}, {"t": "Bureau of Land Management: Woodrat Mountain", "u": "https://www.blm.gov/visit/woodrat-mountain-paragliding"}]}, {"id": "st-zermatt-rothorn", "t": 3, "dom": "site", "lat": 46.021, "lon": 7.796, "name": "Zermatt Rothorn", "place": "Rothorn, Zermatt, Switzerland", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": true, "hook": "Launch at 3,100 metres with the Matterhorn in front of you and stones under the wing.", "body": ["Zermatt has three lift-served launches stacked on the Sunnegga side of the valley: Sunnegga at about 2,200 metres, Blauherd at about 2,600 metres facing west, and the Rothorn at about 3,100 metres with take-offs facing north and south. The village landing is at roughly 1,620 metres, so a flight from the Rothorn starts with 1,500 metres of height and the 4,478 metre Matterhorn across the valley.", "The site notes are short and consistent: wind starts moderate at Sunnegga and becomes too strong to launch by early afternoon, thermic and valley winds build at Blauherd through the afternoon, and strong winds are to be expected on the Rothorn after midday. In summer the Rothorn launch is rocky, which punishes a dragged wing and a poor inflation.", "This is a high-alpine site, not a beginner's one: thin air at 3,100 metres means a faster ground run, the valley is narrow and the lee of the big ridges is severe. Tandem operators fly here daily in good weather; solo visitors should have a solid mountain record and launch in the morning."], "rel": ["st-fiesch-kuhboden", "st-chamonix-planpraz", "st-kossen-unterberg", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Flyappi: Zermatt Rothorn, Blauherd, Sunnegga site", "u": "https://flyappi.org/take_off/9126/"}, {"t": "Zermatt, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zermatt"}]}, {"id": "hx-domina-jalbert", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 26.37, "lon": -80.1, "name": "Domina Jalbert and the ram-air wing", "place": "Boca Raton, Florida, United States", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "A kite maker in Florida drew the cell-and-rib wing that every paraglider still uses.", "body": ["Domina Jalbert was born in Quebec in 1904, held a pilot licence from 1927, and spent the 1930s flying large advertising kites. During the war he worked on blimps and barrage balloons, and in 1944 he filed a patent for a balloon with a stiffened wing, the kytoon. He settled in Boca Raton, Florida, in 1950 and tested a square parachute there in 1956.", "His key idea came in the early 1960s: a wing of two fabric surfaces joined by airfoil-shaped ribs, open at the front so that air rammed in and inflated it. He filed the patent on 10 January 1963 (US 3131894), the wing first flew in March 1964, and he called it the Para-Foil. The ram-air cell is the direct ancestor of the modern paraglider.", "Early Para-Foils opened so hard that they could injure skydivers, and later designers added the slider that slows the opening. Paragliders went the other way: with no opening shock to tame, they dropped the slider and refined the wing for foot launching. Jalbert first heard of the sport in 1988, when European journalists came to interview him. He died in Boca Raton on 26 June 1991; the FAI awarded him its Gold Air Medal in 2019."], "rel": ["hx-david-barish", "hx-francis-rogallo", "hx-kalbermatten-ailes-de-k", "mieussy"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: Domina Jalbert awarded Gold Air Medal", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/183413"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Domina Jalbert", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domina_Jalbert"}, {"t": "International Skydiving Museum: Domina C. Jalbert", "u": "https://skydivingmuseum.org/member/domina-c-jalbert/"}]}, {"id": "hx-david-barish", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 42.18, "lon": -74.23, "name": "David Barish and slope soaring, 1965", "place": "Hunter Mountain, Catskills, New York, United States", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Thirteen years before Mieussy, a NASA parachute man ran off a Catskills ski slope under a wing.", "body": ["David Barish, born in 1921, was a New Yorker who flew for TWA, trained on P-51 fighters and took a master's degree in aerodynamics at Caltech. After leaving the air force in 1953 he consulted for NASA on parachutes, and in 1964 he designed the Sail Wing, a single-surface canopy meant to bring space capsules back to Earth. NASA studied it and chose round parachutes instead.", "In September 1965 he took a slope-soaring version, with the aspect ratio raised from 3 to 5, to the Catskills ski country north of New York. With his son and the skydiver Jacques Istel he ran down a grass ski slope and flew. Accounts name Hunter Mountain and the Bel Air slope near Woodstock. He called it slope soaring. The wing's glide was later measured at 4.2 to 1, below a modern school paraglider.", "In summer 1966 Barish and his son toured ski resorts from Vermont to California, prompted by Ski magazine, to show that slope soaring could keep lifts busy in summer. Flights were short and low, and the resorts did not take it up. Barish later said it was probably too soon. He died on 15 December 2009, aged 88, having been called the forgotten father of paragliding."], "rel": ["hx-domina-jalbert", "hx-francis-rogallo", "hx-first-paragliding-schools", "mieussy"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: David Barish 1921-2009, the forgotten father of paragliding", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/21810"}, {"t": "USHPA: David Barish, the probable inventor of the paraglider", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/david-barish-the-probable-inventor-of-the-paraglider.aspx"}, {"t": "Cross Country: David Barish, the forgotten father of paragliding", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/david-barish-the-forgotten-father-of-paragliding/"}]}, {"id": "hx-francis-rogallo", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 35.96, "lon": -75.63, "name": "Francis Rogallo and the flexible wing", "place": "Jockey's Ridge, Nags Head, North Carolina, United States", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "A kitchen-table kite built by a husband and wife became the first hang glider wing.", "body": ["Francis Rogallo was born in Sanger, California, on 27 January 1912 and joined the US government's aeronautics committee, NACA, in 1936 to work in its wind tunnels at Langley. At home he and his wife Gertrude experimented with kites, and in 1948 they tested a fully flexible, self-inflating wing in a home-made wind tunnel. They patented it in 1951 (US 2546078), and it became known simply as the Rogallo wing.", "After Sputnik in 1957 NASA tested the wing as a steerable recovery system for Gemini capsules and called it the Parawing. The space agency chose conventional parachutes in the end, but the research was public, and amateurs noticed a wing that was cheap to build, flew slowly and landed gently. From 1963 it was turned into the first practical hang gliders.", "Rogallo retired to the Outer Banks and flew his own hang glider on Jockey's Ridge, a sand dune about eight kilometres from the site of the Wright brothers' first powered flight, through the 1970s and 1980s. Thousands of people have since taken their first hang gliding lesson there. He died at home in Southern Shores on 1 September 2009, aged 97."], "rel": ["hx-john-dickenson-ski-wing", "hx-bill-moyes", "hx-bill-bennett", "kitty-hawk"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Francis Rogallo", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Rogallo"}, {"t": "Lemelson-MIT: Francis and Gertrude Rogallo", "u": "https://lemelson.mit.edu/resources/francis-and-gertrude-rogallo"}]}, {"id": "hx-kalbermatten-ailes-de-k", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 46.13, "lon": 6.17, "name": "Laurent de Kalbermatten and Ailes de K", "place": "Salève, Haute-Savoie, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The first wing built to fly from a hillside, not jump from a plane, was Swiss, 1985.", "body": ["The pilots who launched above Mieussy in 1978 were flying skydiving canopies. They worked, but a jump canopy is built to open at speed, with porous fabric and stretchy lines, and it glides poorly. A Swiss designer, Laurent de Kalbermatten, began supplying them with canopies cut from coated spinnaker cloth, the slippery sailcloth used on racing yachts, which inflated more easily on a steep slope.", "In 1985 he produced La Randonneuse, a nine-cell wing designed from the start for paragliding rather than parachuting, sold by his company Ailes de K, named from his initials. Non-porous fabric and non-elastic lines added roughly one point of glide ratio over the converted parachutes. French and Swiss histories call it the first purpose-built paraglider, and the history of the Delta Club Genève, which flies the Salève above Geneva, credits a hang glider pilot, Didier Favre, with putting the idea to him.", "That one-point gain sounds small, but it changed what a flight could be: pilots began to reach the valley floor with height to spare and to look for lift on the way. Kalbermatten later designed the Woopy, a hang glider with an inflatable wing. His name is less known than the Mieussy pioneers, yet most pilots today fly a descendant of his design choices."], "rel": ["hx-domina-jalbert", "hx-first-paragliding-schools", "hx-kossen-1989", "mieussy"], "src": [{"t": "Delta Club Genève: L'historique du club", "u": "https://www.paradelta.ch/club/lhistorique-du-club/"}, {"t": "Freedom Parapente: The history of paragliding", "u": "https://www.freedom-parapente.fr/en/blog/the-history-of-paragliding"}, {"t": "Skywing: history of paragliding", "u": "https://skywing.de/01_site_en/history.html"}]}, {"id": "hx-kossen-1989", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 47.67, "lon": 12.4, "name": "First Paragliding World Championship, Kössen 1989", "place": "Kössen, Tyrol, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The sport's first world title was contested in 1989 and, officially, nobody won it.", "body": ["Kössen sits in the Kaiserwinkl, where the Unterberg offers wide grass launches between 1,500 and 1,700 metres facing east, north and west, reached by a cable car ride of about 15 minutes. The landing at the Fliegerbar is at 650 metres. The village had already hosted the first FAI hang gliding world championship in 1976 and hosted it again in 1985.", "An unofficial paragliding world championship was flown at Verbier, Switzerland, in 1987. Two years later the FAI sanctioned the first official one, held at Kössen in 1989. The FAI's own list of champions records the 1989 results as not validated, so the first official individual world champion in the books is Robbie Whittall of Great Britain, at Digne in 1991, with Switzerland taking the team title.", "The championships have been held every two years since, from Verbier in 1993 through Kitakyushu, Governador Valadares, Manilla and Roldanillo to Castelo in Brazil in 2025. Switzerland won the team title at the first four validated editions. Kössen remains a working flying site with a flight school and a yearly test festival where manufacturers let pilots try new wings."], "rel": ["hx-kalbermatten-ailes-de-k", "hx-first-paragliding-schools", "hx-dhv-founding", "hx-chrigel-maurer"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: Paragliding XC world champions", "u": "https://www.fai.org/page/paragliding-xc-world-champions"}, {"t": "FAI CIVL: Hang gliding world championships", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/110442"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Guide to Kössen, Austria", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-koessen-austria/"}]}, {"id": "hx-copperton-2008-record", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -29.93, "lon": 22.29, "name": "Nevil Hulett's 502.9 km, Copperton 2008", "place": "Copperton, Northern Cape, South Africa", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The first paraglider flight past 500 km started with a tow launch in the Karoo in December 2008.", "body": ["Copperton is a small former mining town in the Northern Cape of South Africa. The land is flat, dry and almost empty, so pilots launch by tow behind a vehicle or winch and then fly downwind across the Karoo. Summer days bring cloudbase of several thousand metres and strong thermals from early in the morning.", "On 14 December 2008 the South African pilot Nevil Hulett tow-launched at Copperton and flew 502.9 km, the first paraglider flight beyond 500 km and a new FAI world record for free distance. Flights of this length last most of the day, and dehydration, cold and fatigue are the real limits on them, not the wing.", "The record stood for almost seven years, until a Brazilian team flew 513 km from Tacima on 9 October 2015. Copperton is still used for big flights, and Cross Country magazine has written up a 300 km day there. Flatland record flying demands careful planning for retrieve, water and airspace, and a mistake far from any road is hard to recover from."], "rel": ["hx-tacima-record-chain", "hx-texas-2021-record", "hx-womens-distance-records", "kerio-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Brazilians set new 513 km paragliding world record", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/brazilians-set-new-513km-paragliding-world-record/"}, {"t": "Cross Country: 300k day paragliding Copperton", "u": "https://xcmag.com/magazine-articles/300k-day-paragliding-copperton/"}]}, {"id": "hx-tacima-record-chain", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -6.49, "lon": -35.64, "name": "The Tacima record flights, 2015 to 2020", "place": "Tacima, Paraíba, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A team of Brazilian friends flew together for eleven hours and moved the world record three times.", "body": ["Tacima is a small town in the dry north-east of Brazil, the sertão. From October the trade wind blows steadily from the east, and pilots launch early, from the hill at nearby Araruna or by tow, and run downwind across the interior for the whole day. Quixadá, roughly 400 km to the north-west, was the earlier record ground; Tacima took over from the mid-2010s.", "On 9 October 2015 Frank Brown, Donizete Lemos and Marcelo Prieto flew 513 km together, taking the world record from South Africa. On 13 October 2016 Lemos, Rafael Saladini and Samuel Nascimento flew 564 km in 11 hours 48 minutes after a foot launch. On 10 October 2019 Prieto, Saladini and Rafael de Moraes Barros pushed it to 588 km, averaging 53.5 km/h over about 11 hours.", "The team style matters: three pilots sharing thermals and scouting cloud lines find lift faster than one. On 15 October 2020 Saladini took off at about 6:40 and flew alone for more than 11 hours to a declared goal 530 km away, a separate FAI category. These flights were on competition wings such as the Ozone Enzo and Gin Boomerang, and they ended on dirt roads deep in the interior, hours from the nearest retrieve."], "rel": ["hx-copperton-2008-record", "hx-texas-2021-record", "hx-womens-distance-records", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Ojovolador: 564.3 km, Donizete Lemos tells the story", "u": "https://ojovolador.com/en/564-3-km-donizete-lemos-tells-story-longest-paraglider-flight/"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Record-breaking week in Brazil, October 2019", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=59833"}, {"t": "FAI: Rafael Saladini, 530 km to a declared goal", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/186984"}]}, {"id": "hx-texas-2021-record", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 29.36, "lon": -100.9, "name": "Sebastien Kayrouz's 610 km, Texas 2021", "place": "Del Rio, Texas, United States", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The first 600 km paraglider flight was flown solo across Texas, along the hang gliding record route.", "body": ["On 19 June 2021 Sebastien Kayrouz tow-launched from ranch land near Del Rio and Brackettville, a few kilometres from the Mexican border, shortly before 10 in the morning. Nearly eleven hours later he landed near Claude, south-east of Amarillo. The FAI ratified the flight at 609.9 km as the world record for free distance, the first paraglider flight beyond 600 km, taking the title from Brazil. He flew an Ozone Enzo 3, alone.", "The same south-to-north corridor had already produced the hang gliding world record: Jonny Durand and Dustin Martin raced each other more than 750 km along it from Zapata on 3 July 2012. The weather pattern is a strong southerly wind under cumulus for the whole day, with moist Gulf of Mexico air feeding the clouds.", "Kayrouz had flown a long flight the year before from a foot launch at Caprock Mountain. The FAI also ratified his 2021 flight as a declared-goal record of 556.8 km, to a road junction near Silverton that he had named before take-off. Record flying here means ten or more hours in a harness, oxygen on the big days, and a landing at dusk on someone else's land. Water and a satellite tracker are not optional."], "rel": ["hx-tacima-record-chain", "hx-copperton-2008-record", "hx-womens-distance-records", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: Sebastien Kayrouz 610 km world record interview", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/189835"}, {"t": "Flybubble: New paragliding records summer 2021", "u": "https://www.flybubble.com/blog/new-paragliding-records-summer-2021"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (world records)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "hx-womens-distance-records", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -6.46, "lon": -37.1, "name": "Women's paragliding distance records", "place": "Caicó, Rio Grande do Norte, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "In one week of October 2019 the women's world record changed hands twice and passed 500 km.", "body": ["Caicó lies in the same dry north-east of Brazil as Tacima, and the same trade wind carries pilots west for the whole day. Tow launches start soon after sunrise, and the flying is a race against the light: the record flights all land close to sunset, after ten or eleven hours in the air.", "October 2019 was the decisive week. Yael Margelisch of Switzerland broke the women's world record early in the month, Seiko Fukuoka Naville of Japan answered with about 460 km, and on 12 October Margelisch tow-launched from Caicó on an Ozone Enzo 3 and flew 531.7 km in a straight line, the first woman past 500 km and the FAI record that still stands. The men's record that same week, 588 km from Tacima, was flown by a team of three.", "On 14 October 2022 Marcella Pomarico Uchoa of Brazil added the women's declared-goal record from Caicó, 457.3 km. The women's distance is within about 15 per cent of the men's. Ewa Wisnierska holds a different kind of mark: on 14 February 2007 she was sucked to almost 10,000 metres inside a storm cloud in Australia and survived, which no record book counts."], "rel": ["hx-tacima-record-chain", "hx-texas-2021-record", "hx-copperton-2008-record", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Record-breaking week in Brazil, October 2019", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=59833"}, {"t": "Freedom Parapente: Men's and women's records beaten in two days", "u": "https://www.freedom-parapente.fr/en/blog/mens-and-womens-paragliding-world-records-beat-in-2-days"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (world records)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "hx-chrigel-maurer", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 46.59, "lon": 7.65, "name": "Chrigel Maurer", "place": "Frutigen, Bernese Oberland, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A bricklayer from the Bernese Oberland has won every Red Bull X-Alps he has entered, eight in a row.", "body": ["Christian Maurer, known as Chrigel, was born in 1982 and grew up in Adelboden, in the Bernese Oberland. He worked as a bricklayer for two years before flying full time from 2004. He won the Paragliding World Cup overall title three years running, 2005 to 2007, and European titles, before he turned from racing round turnpoints to racing across mountains.", "His record in the Red Bull X-Alps, the hike-and-fly race from Salzburg towards Monaco, is unmatched: eight wins in every edition from 2009 to 2023. He also won every X-Pyr across the Pyrenees from 2014 to 2022. His speed comes less from flying faster than others than from choosing lines where he rarely has to walk far, and from reading the weather hours ahead.", "He set world records for consecutive infinity tumbles, 210 in 2009 and 612 in 2018, since overtaken, which says something about his tolerance for G forces. Today he works as a test pilot, coach, instructor and speaker, and the nickname Eagle of Adelboden has stuck. The lesson he repeats to students is unglamorous: take off early, stay in lift, land before the sky turns on you."], "rel": ["red-bull-x-alps", "hx-antoine-girard-broad-peak", "hx-kossen-1989", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Chrigel Maurer", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrigel_Maurer"}, {"t": "Red Bull X-Alps: Christian Maurer", "u": "https://www.redbullxalps.com/int-en/athlete/christian-maurer"}]}, {"id": "hx-antoine-girard-broad-peak", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 35.81, "lon": 76.57, "name": "Antoine Girard over Broad Peak", "place": "Broad Peak, Karakoram, Pakistan", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A Frenchman left a thermal at 8,100 metres only because his fingers had frozen.", "body": ["Broad Peak is an 8,051 metre mountain on the Pakistan-China border, next door to K2. On 23 July 2016 the French pilot Antoine Girard flew up the Baltoro Glacier past Trango Tower to Concordia, found thermals on the west face and climbed over the summit to 8,157 metres. The flight lasted about seven hours and was part of a 19-day solo trip across the Karakoram covering 260 km.", "He said afterwards that he left the thermal at 8,100 metres because all his fingers were frozen. At that altitude the air holds about a third of the oxygen at sea level, temperatures are far below freezing, and a wing flies much faster through thin air, so every stall and every landing comes at a higher true speed. He flew without a climbing team or oxygen bottles.", "Girard returned in 2021 and on 18 July, on his sixth attempt in 18 days on the Baltoro, reached 8,407 metres above the same mountain, since ratified by the FAI as the paragliding altitude world record. He also holds the FAI gain-of-height record, 5,854 metres over Aconcagua on 15 February 2019. These flights sit at the edge of what a body can take; hypoxia makes pilots feel fine while their judgement goes."], "rel": ["hx-chrigel-maurer", "hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "bar-headed-goose", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "PlanetMountain: Antoine Girard soars to new record above Broad Peak", "u": "https://planetmountain.com/en/news/alpinism/antoine-girard-soars-to-new-paragliding-record-above-broad-peak-karakorum.html"}, {"t": "FAI: Antoine Girard claims gain of height record on Aconcagua", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/182341"}, {"t": "Flybubble: New paragliding records summer 2021", "u": "https://www.flybubble.com/blog/new-paragliding-records-summer-2021"}]}, {"id": "hx-bill-moyes", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -33.9, "lon": 151.27, "name": "Bill Moyes", "place": "Bronte, Sydney, Australia", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "An Australian water-skier towed a kite seven hours towards Brisbane and then founded a glider dynasty.", "body": ["Bill Moyes was born on 12 July 1932 in Bronte, a beach suburb of Sydney. He learned to fly a ski-kite, a Rogallo wing towed behind a speedboat, in 1967, and on 17 May that year set an altitude record of about 320 metres under tow. The kites of the day were built for water-skiing shows, not for free flight.", "In 1969 he tried to fly roughly 1,000 km from Sydney to Brisbane behind a boat. The boat was swamped by big seas after about 300 km and almost seven hours, but the attempt gave him the endurance record, and the same year he demonstrated the wing at the world water-ski championship in Copenhagen. In 1970 he released from tow at the rim of the Grand Canyon and glided about 1,460 metres down to land at Phantom Ranch, a flight that made hang gliding news worldwide.", "By 1972 his company had become Moyes Delta Gliders, still family-run in Sydney and one of the oldest hang glider makers in the world. The FAI gave him its Gold Air Medal in 2014. He died at home in Sydney on 24 September 2024, aged 92. His family and the test pilots he hired built much of modern hang gliding."], "rel": ["hx-bill-bennett", "hx-john-dickenson-ski-wing", "hx-francis-rogallo", "hx-fai-founding"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: Bill Moyes awarded the FAI Gold Air Medal", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/21653"}, {"t": "FAI: Tribute to Bill Moyes, 2024", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/200541"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Bill Moyes", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Moyes"}]}, {"id": "hx-bill-bennett", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 40.69, "lon": -74.04, "name": "Bill Bennett", "place": "Liberty Island, New York, United States", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "On 4 July 1969 an Australian glided round the Statue of Liberty and brought hang gliding to America.", "body": ["Bill Bennett was born in Korumburra, Victoria, Australia. Like Bill Moyes he came from water-ski kiting, and in 1969 he carried the Dickenson-style Rogallo wing to the United States. On 4 July 1969 he released from tow in New York Harbour, circled the Statue of Liberty and landed on Liberty Island. The stunt put the new wing on television and in the newspapers.", "He settled in California and ran Delta Wing Kites and Gliders in Van Nuys through the 1970s and 1980s. For a time it was among the largest hang glider manufacturers in the world, and one of its Phoenix gliders is held in the Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum collection. Bennett also helped make hang gliders a familiar sight in films and advertising.", "He died on 7 October 2004, aged 73, during a recertification flight in a powered hang glider at Lake Havasu City Airport, Arizona. The aircraft lost power on take-off and dived into the ground; his instructor was badly hurt. Engine failure just after take-off remains one of the most dangerous moments in any form of flying, including paramotoring."], "rel": ["hx-bill-moyes", "hx-john-dickenson-ski-wing", "hx-francis-rogallo", "lilienthal"], "src": [{"t": "Seattle Times: Hang-gliding pioneer Bill Bennett, 73", "u": "https://archive.seattletimes.com/archive/20041121/bennettobit21/hang-gliding-pioneer-bill-bennett-73"}, {"t": "Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum: Delta Wing Phoenix Viper 175", "u": "https://www.airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/delta-wing-phoenix-viper-175/nasm_A19840713000"}]}, {"id": "hx-john-dickenson-ski-wing", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -29.69, "lon": 152.93, "name": "John Dickenson's Ski Wing, Grafton 1963", "place": "Grafton, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "kite", "hidden": true, "hook": "The modern hang glider first flew behind a speedboat at a flower festival in September 1963.", "body": ["John Dickenson, born on 22 January 1934, was an Australian inventor. The Grafton Water Ski Club asked him to build a kite for its display at the Jacaranda Festival. Starting from a magazine picture of the Rogallo wing, he built a flexible wing with an A-frame the pilot could hold, so that shifting body weight steered it. He called it the Ski Wing.", "The wing was tried at the club on 7 September 1963. Dickenson and then Norm Stamford could not get it airborne, and on the third attempt Bob Clements climbed, dived and hit the river hard. On the fourth try Rod Fuller flew it, towed by Pat Crowe's boat, and it worked. Dickenson filed a provisional patent on 11 October 1963 but never had the money to complete it.", "Dickenson never foot-launched a glider, yet his A-frame and weight-shift layout is the one every hang glider still uses. The FAI gave him its Hang Gliding Diploma in 2006 for inventing the modern hang glider, and its Gold Air Medal in 2012. He was made a Member of the Order of Australia in 1996 and died on 5 July 2023, aged 89."], "rel": ["hx-francis-rogallo", "hx-bill-moyes", "hx-bill-bennett", "hx-bhpa-founding"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: John W. Dickenson", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_W._Dickenson"}, {"t": "BHPA history: The Jacaranda Festival 1963", "u": "https://bhpahistory.com/first-flight.html"}, {"t": "FAI: 55 years of hang gliding, Pat Crowe honoured", "u": "https://fai.org/node/179956"}]}, {"id": "hx-percy-pilcher", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 52.42, "lon": -1.2, "name": "Percy Pilcher", "place": "Stanford Hall, Leicestershire, England", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Britain's glider pioneer died in 1899 flying a wet wing he should have left in the shed.", "body": ["Percy Pilcher was born in Bath in 1867, served seven years in the Royal Navy from the age of 13 and then trained as an engineer in Glasgow. He met Otto Lilienthal in Germany in 1895 and built his own hang gliders: the Bat, first flown in 1895, and the Hawk, built in 1895 and 1896. The Hawk was launched by a towline, and in 1897 it carried him about 250 metres at Stanford Hall, a world distance record for a glider.", "By 1899 he had designed a powered triplane with a small petrol engine and had gone into debt building it. On 30 September 1899 he was to show it to backers at Stanford Hall, but the engine's crankshaft had broken. He flew the Hawk instead, in rain. The soaked fabric overloaded the frame, the tail snapped in flight and he fell about 10 metres. He died two days later, aged 32.", "Cranfield University built and flew a replica of his triplane for a BBC programme in 2003 and found the design workable, so he might have made a powered, controlled flight years before the Wright brothers. The lesson pilots still draw is plain: a glider that has been flown in rain is a different, heavier aircraft, and pressure from spectators is a poor reason to launch."], "rel": ["lilienthal", "hx-octave-chanute", "kitty-hawk", "hx-eilmer-of-malmesbury"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Percy Pilcher", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Percy_Pilcher"}, {"t": "The Engineer: Percy Pilcher, unsung hero of early aviation", "u": "https://theengineer.co.uk/content/in-depth/late-great-engineers-percy-pilcher-unsung-hero-of-early-aviation"}, {"t": "Linda Hall Library: Percy Pilcher", "u": "https://www.lindahall.org/about/news/scientist-of-the-day/percy-pilcher/"}]}, {"id": "hx-octave-chanute", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 41.62, "lon": -87.27, "name": "Octave Chanute at the Indiana Dunes", "place": "Miller Beach, Indiana, United States", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A retired railway engineer in his sixties ran glider camps on Lake Michigan and taught the Wrights.", "body": ["Octave Chanute was born in Paris on 18 February 1832 and became one of America's leading civil engineers, building railways and bridges. In retirement he collected every serious account of heavier-than-air flight he could find and published them as Progress in Flying Machines in 1894, the first systematic survey of the field. Wilbur Wright read it and wrote to him in May 1900.", "In 1896 Chanute, Augustus Herring and William Avery camped in the sand dunes at Miller Beach on Lake Michigan, east of Chicago, from June, and made more than 700 glides. They found that stacking wings gave extra lift without much extra weight, and Chanute braced the pair with struts and wires, the Pratt truss he knew from bridges. An improved biplane glider flew there in 1897.", "The Wright brothers built their gliders on Chanute's double-decker, and he visited their camp at Kitty Hawk in 1901, 1902 and 1903. The two sides exchanged hundreds of letters until his death in Chicago on 23 November 1910, though they fell out over patents and credit. The dunes are now part of Indiana Dunes National Park, and a lake breeze still gives soarable lift on their faces."], "rel": ["lilienthal", "kitty-hawk", "hx-percy-pilcher", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Octave Chanute", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Octave_Chanute"}, {"t": "National Soaring Museum: Landmark 8, Miller Beach", "u": "https://soaringmuseum.org/landmark_more.php?id=8"}]}, {"id": "hx-igor-sikorsky", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 41.18, "lon": -73.13, "name": "Igor Sikorsky and the first practical helicopter", "place": "Stratford, Connecticut, United States", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "He failed at helicopters in 1909, built the first four-engine aircraft, and returned to helicopters thirty years later.", "body": ["Igor Sikorsky was born in Kyiv on 25 May 1889. His first two helicopters, in 1909 and 1910, could not lift a pilot, so he turned to aeroplanes and earned FAI pilot licence number 64 in 1911. In 1913 his Russky Vityaz became the first successful four-engine aircraft, and its successor, the Ilya Muromets, served as a bomber in the First World War.", "He left Russia after the 1917 revolution and arrived in New York on 30 March 1919. He founded his aircraft company in 1923, moved it to Stratford, Connecticut, in 1929 and built flying boats for Pan American. Then he returned to the problem he had abandoned. The VS-300 made its first tethered flight on 14 September 1939 and its first free flight on 24 May 1940.", "The VS-300 settled the layout most helicopters still use: a single main rotor with a small tail rotor to cancel its torque. It led directly to the R-4, delivered from 1942 and the first helicopter built in quantity. Sikorsky died at home in Easton, Connecticut, on 26 October 1972. Helicopters are also the usual rescue tool when free flight goes wrong in the mountains, which gives pilots a reason to know how they land."], "rel": ["hx-hanna-reitsch", "hx-fai-founding", "hx-chuck-yeager", "hx-wiley-post"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Igor Sikorsky", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Igor_Sikorsky"}, {"t": "Sikorsky Archives: Timeline of aviation firsts", "u": "https://sikorskyarchives.com/home/igor-sikorsky/timeline-of-aviation-firsts/"}]}, {"id": "hx-hanna-reitsch", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 52.5, "lon": 13.27, "name": "Hanna Reitsch", "place": "Deutschlandhalle, Berlin, Germany", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "A gifted glider pilot flew a helicopter inside a Berlin hall in 1938 and never disowned the regime.", "body": ["Hanna Reitsch was born on 29 March 1912 in Hirschberg, Silesia, now Jelenia Góra in Poland. She learned to glide at Grunau from 1932 and in 1934 became the first woman to earn the Silver C soaring badge. She joined the DFS gliding research institute in 1934, became a test pilot there in 1935 and moved to the Luftwaffe test centre at Rechlin in 1937, flying dive bombers, bombers and the Me 163 rocket fighter.", "In February 1938 she flew the Focke-Achgelis Fa 61 helicopter on successive evenings inside the Deutschlandhalle in Berlin, the first helicopter flights by a woman. She was a willing face of Nazi propaganda, received the Iron Cross, and on 26 April 1945 flew into besieged Berlin to visit Hitler in his bunker days before his suicide. Late in life she said she was not ashamed of having believed in National Socialism.", "After the war she went back to gliding: third place at the 1952 world championships, German champion in 1955, a women's altitude record of 6,848 metres in 1957. From 1962 to 1966 she ran a national gliding school in Ghana under Kwame Nkrumah. She died in Frankfurt on 24 August 1979. Her skill in soaring flight is real; so is the record she chose to stand behind."], "rel": ["hx-igor-sikorsky", "hx-amy-johnson", "hx-dhv-founding", "lilienthal"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Hanna Reitsch", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanna_Reitsch"}, {"t": "Britannica: Hanna Reitsch", "u": "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Hanna-Reitsch"}, {"t": "Migflug: The Focke-Wulf Fw 61, the helicopter they flew indoors", "u": "https://migflug.com/afterburner/focke-wulf-fw-61-first-helicopter/"}]}, {"id": "hx-bessie-coleman", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 33.11, "lon": -94.16, "name": "Bessie Coleman", "place": "Atlanta, Texas, United States", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "No American school would teach her, so she learned French and got her licence in France in 1921.", "body": ["Bessie Coleman was born on 26 January 1892 in Atlanta, Texas, to a family of sharecroppers, and moved to Chicago in 1915. Flying schools in the United States would not take a Black woman. She took French classes, saved money, and trained near Paris. On 15 June 1921 she received an international licence from the FAI, the first Black woman and the first Native American to hold one.", "Back home she became a barnstormer, flying loops and figure eights at air shows and earning the name Queen Bess. She refused to perform at events that barred Black spectators and hoped to open a flying school for Black students. She survived a serious crash in February 1923 and went back to the circuit.", "On 30 April 1926, on a practice flight before a show in Jacksonville, Florida, she was in the passenger seat without her seat belt fastened, looking over the side for a parachute landing site, while her mechanic William Wills flew. A wrench left loose jammed the controls, the aircraft dived and spun, and she was thrown out. She was 34. The United States put her on a stamp in 1995 and in its National Aviation Hall of Fame in 2006."], "rel": ["hx-amy-johnson", "hx-jean-batten", "hx-fai-founding", "hx-valentina-tereshkova"], "src": [{"t": "Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum: Bessie Coleman", "u": "https://airandspace.si.edu/explore/stories/bessie-coleman"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Bessie Coleman", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessie_Coleman"}]}, {"id": "hx-jean-batten", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -36.98, "lon": 174.79, "name": "Jean Batten", "place": "Māngere, Auckland, New Zealand", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "She flew alone from England to Auckland in 1936, and the record stood for 44 years.", "body": ["Jean Batten was born in Rotorua on 15 September 1909 and went to England to learn to fly against her father's wishes. After two failed attempts she flew a Gipsy Moth solo from England to Darwin in May 1934, and became the first woman to fly England to Australia and back. In November 1935 she crossed the South Atlantic alone to Brazil.", "In October 1936 she flew a Percival Gull from England to New Zealand, crossing the Tasman Sea alone, and landed at Māngere aerodrome in Auckland on 16 October after 11 days and 45 minutes. The England to New Zealand record lasted 44 years. The Tasman crossing was the dangerous part: around 2,000 km of open water with no alternate and no radio beacon.", "Her last flight as a pilot was in August 1939, days before the Second World War began, and she faded from view. She died in Palma, Mallorca, on 22 November 1982 after refusing treatment for a dog bite, and was buried in a paupers' grave; nobody in New Zealand knew until a journalist traced her in 1987. Auckland Airport's international terminal, on the site of her landing, carries her name."], "rel": ["hx-amy-johnson", "hx-bessie-coleman", "hx-wiley-post", "lindbergh"], "src": [{"t": "NZHistory: Jean Batten", "u": "https://nzhistory.govt.nz/people/jean-batten"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Jean Batten", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_Batten"}]}, {"id": "hx-amy-johnson", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 51.36, "lon": -0.12, "name": "Amy Johnson", "place": "Croydon Airport, London, England", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "A Hull typist with barely a year's flying behind her flew a second-hand Moth to Australia in 1930.", "body": ["Amy Johnson was born in Kingston upon Hull on 1 July 1903. She learned to fly at the London Aeroplane Club and in 1929 qualified as a ground engineer, the first British woman to do so. On 5 May 1930 she took off from Croydon in a Gipsy Moth named Jason, bought with her father's help, and landed at Darwin on 24 May, 19 days and about 17,700 km later, the first woman to fly solo from England to Australia.", "The route crossed deserts, monsoon and jungle in an open cockpit with no radio, in 18 legs. She set further records to Japan with a co-pilot in 1931 and solo to Cape Town in 1932, and with her husband Jim Mollison attempted the Atlantic in 1933, landing short of New York. In Britain she was a celebrity on the scale of a film star, and a song was written about her.", "In the war she flew for the Air Transport Auxiliary, ferrying aircraft between factories and airfields. On 5 January 1941, in an Airspeed Oxford in bad weather, she bailed out over the Thames Estuary off Herne Bay and was lost; her body was never found. She was 37. The cause is still argued over, from running out of fuel above cloud to friendly fire. Jason hangs in the Science Museum in London."], "rel": ["hx-jean-batten", "hx-bessie-coleman", "hx-hanna-reitsch", "lindbergh"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Amy Johnson", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amy_Johnson"}, {"t": "This Day in Aviation: Amy Johnson", "u": "https://www.thisdayinaviation.com/tag/amy-johnson/"}]}, {"id": "hx-wiley-post", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 71.29, "lon": -156.79, "name": "Wiley Post", "place": "Point Barrow, Alaska, United States", "icon": "plane", "hidden": true, "hook": "A one-eyed oilfield worker flew round the world alone in 1933 and found the jet stream.", "body": ["Wiley Post was born in Texas on 22 November 1898 and worked on oil rigs until an accident in October 1926 cost him the sight of his left eye. He used the compensation to buy an aeroplane. Flying a Lockheed Vega called Winnie Mae, he and navigator Harold Gatty went round the world in 8 days 15 hours 51 minutes in 1931.", "In 1933 he did it again alone, with an early autopilot, in 7 days 18 hours 49 minutes from Floyd Bennett Field, New York. In 1934 he and the B.F. Goodrich company built one of the first practical pressure suits, and he flew above 12,000 metres in it, where ground speeds of up to 550 km/h told him he was riding a wind nobody had mapped. He had found the jet stream.", "On 15 August 1935 he and the humorist Will Rogers took off from a lagoon near Point Barrow, Alaska, in a float-plane assembled from parts of different aircraft. The engine failed just after take-off and both men died. Winnie Mae is in the Smithsonian, and the pressure suit he wore is the ancestor of the suits worn in rocket planes and spacecraft."], "rel": ["hx-chuck-yeager", "hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "hx-amy-johnson", "lindbergh"], "src": [{"t": "Smithsonian: Lockheed Vega Winnie Mae", "u": "https://si.edu/object/lockheed-vega-winnie-mae:nasm_A19360030000"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Wiley Post", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiley_Post"}]}, {"id": "hx-chuck-yeager", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 34.93, "lon": -117.87, "name": "Chuck Yeager and the sound barrier", "place": "Rogers Dry Lake, Edwards, California, United States", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": false, "hook": "He flew faster than sound in 1947 with two broken ribs and a broom handle to shut the hatch.", "body": ["Charles Yeager was born on 13 February 1923 in Myra, West Virginia, enlisted as a mechanic in 1941 and became a fighter pilot in the Second World War. In 1947 he was chosen to fly the Bell X-1, a rocket aircraft shaped like a bullet, dropped from the bomb bay of a B-29 at about 7,000 metres over the Mojave Desert and landed without power on the dry lake bed at Muroc, later Edwards Air Force Base.", "Two nights before the flight he broke two ribs falling from a horse and told only his wife and a friend. On 14 October 1947 his colleague Jack Ridley cut down a broom handle so he could lever the hatch shut, and Yeager reached about Mach 1.06, roughly 1,100 km/h, at around 13,000 metres in level flight. The aircraft, painted with his wife's name, Glamorous Glennis, is in the Smithsonian. The flight was kept secret until June 1948.", "On 12 December 1953 he reached Mach 2.44 in the X-1A, lost control at about 24,000 metres and recovered near 9,000 metres. He died on 7 December 2020, aged 97. Paragliders never approach these speeds, but the same question applies: a wing behaves differently at the edge of its envelope, and the only safe place to find that edge is with height to spare."], "rel": ["hx-wiley-post", "hx-roswell-stratosphere-jumps", "hx-igor-sikorsky", "hx-steve-fossett"], "src": [{"t": "Smithsonian National Air and Space Museum: Bell X-1 Glamorous Glennis", "u": "https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/bell-x-1-glamorous-glennis/nasm_A19510007000"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Chuck Yeager", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chuck_Yeager"}]}, {"id": "hx-valentina-tereshkova", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 53.27, "lon": 80.77, "name": "Valentina Tereshkova, Vostok 6", "place": "Bayevsky District, Altai Krai, Russia", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": false, "hook": "A textile worker chosen for her parachute jumps became the first woman in space in 1963.", "body": ["Valentina Tereshkova was born on 6 March 1937 in Bolshoye Maslennikovo, a village in Yaroslavl Oblast, and worked in a tyre factory and then a textile mill. Her way into the space programme was parachuting: she made her first jump on 21 May 1959 and was an experienced amateur jumper when the Soviet Union picked women candidates. Vostok cosmonauts ejected from the capsule and landed separately by parachute, so jumpers were wanted.", "She launched on 16 June 1963 in Vostok 6, aged 26, and flew 48 orbits in 2 days 22 hours 50 minutes, longer than all the American astronauts to that date combined. On 19 June she ejected several kilometres above the ground and came down under her parachute in the Altai region, around 620 km north-east of Karaganda. A monument stands near the landing site in Bayevsky District.", "She remains the only woman to have flown a solo space mission and the youngest woman to orbit Earth. It took the Soviet Union 19 years to fly another woman, and the United States did not fly one until 1983. Tereshkova later became a politician. Her flight is a reminder that parachuting, not piloting, was the first route for women into space."], "rel": ["hx-bessie-coleman", "hx-roswell-stratosphere-jumps", "hx-domina-jalbert", "hx-fai-founding"], "src": [{"t": "NASA StarChild: Valentina Tereshkova", "u": "https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/whos_who_level2/tereshkova.html"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Valentina Tereshkova", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentina_Tereshkova"}]}, {"id": "hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 48.37, "lon": 10.9, "name": "Auguste Piccard enters the stratosphere, 1931", "place": "Augsburg, Bavaria, Germany", "icon": "balloon", "hidden": false, "hook": "The model for Tintin's Professor Calculus rode a sealed aluminium ball to 15,781 metres in 1931.", "body": ["Auguste Piccard was a Swiss physicist, born in Basel on 28 January 1884, who wanted to measure cosmic rays above most of the atmosphere. Open balloon baskets had killed and injured crews at great height from lack of oxygen and cold. His answer, designed in 1930, was a pressurised spherical aluminium gondola, airtight and about two metres across, hung beneath a hydrogen balloon.", "On 27 May 1931 he and his assistant Paul Kipfer lifted off from Augsburg and rose to 15,781 metres, the first people in the stratosphere. The flight did not go to plan: they stayed up far longer than intended and made an emergency landing on the Gurgler Ferner, a glacier above Obergurgl in the Ötztal Alps of Austria. Piccard flew higher again in 1932 with Max Cosyns.", "The pressurised cabin he proved there is the reason airliners and spacecraft can carry people above the breathable sky. Piccard then turned the same thinking upside down and designed the bathyscaphe, a pressure sphere for the deep sea, which he and his son Jacques dived in to 3,150 metres in 1953. His grandson Bertrand flew round the world by balloon and by solar aeroplane. Hergé said Calculus was Piccard at reduced scale."], "rel": ["hx-breitling-orbiter-3", "hx-solar-impulse", "hx-roswell-stratosphere-jumps", "montgolfier"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Auguste Piccard", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Auguste_Piccard"}, {"t": "Gurgl: In grandpa's footsteps, Piccard's 1931 landing", "u": "https://www.gurgl.com/en/-/in-grandpa-s-footsteps.om-211343"}]}, {"id": "hx-roswell-stratosphere-jumps", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 33.3, "lon": -104.53, "name": "Stratosphere jumps at Roswell, 2012 and 2014", "place": "Roswell, New Mexico, United States", "icon": "balloon", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two years after Baumgartner fell faster than sound, a Google engineer quietly jumped from higher still.", "body": ["On 14 October 2012 the Austrian skydiver Felix Baumgartner rose in a capsule under a helium balloon from Roswell to 38,969 metres, stepped off and fell for about 4 minutes 19 seconds before opening his parachute. He reached 1,357.6 km/h, Mach 1.25, the first person to pass the speed of sound without an engine, and went into an uncontrolled spin in the first minute before recovering.", "His mentor and capsule communicator was Joseph Kittinger, who had set the previous record of 31,300 metres in 1960. On 22 February 2013 the FAI ratified three records from the jump: exit altitude, vertical speed without a drogue, and a freefall of 36,402.6 metres. The project was a large, televised production with a pressurised capsule and a ground team of hundreds.", "On 24 October 2014 Alan Eustace, a Google senior vice-president and computer scientist, went higher with almost no publicity. He hung beneath a balloon in a pressure suit alone, with no capsule, released at 41,419 metres and fell for about 4 minutes 27 seconds at speeds over 1,320 km/h. Because he used a stabilising drogue, his fall sits in a different FAI category from Baumgartner's. Both landed by parachute in the New Mexico desert."], "rel": ["hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "hx-chuck-yeager", "hx-valentina-tereshkova", "hx-domina-jalbert"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: FAI ratifies three Baumgartner world records", "u": "https://fai.org/node/53039"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Red Bull Stratos", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red_Bull_Stratos"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Alan Eustace", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Eustace"}]}, {"id": "hx-santos-dumont", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 48.87, "lon": 2.25, "name": "Alberto Santos-Dumont", "place": "Bagatelle, Bois de Boulogne, Paris, France", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "A Brazilian flew an airship round the Eiffel Tower in 1901, then made Europe's first public aeroplane flight.", "body": ["Alberto Santos-Dumont was born on 20 July 1873 near Palmira, Minas Gerais, to a wealthy coffee-growing family, and moved to Paris as a young man. He built a series of small airships and on 19 October 1901 flew his No. 6 from Saint-Cloud round the Eiffel Tower and back, about 11 km, in under half an hour to win the Deutsch prize. The judges first refused him because landing took a minute too long, then gave way to public opinion.", "He then turned to heavier-than-air flight. On 23 October 1906 his 14-bis, a canard biplane with box-kite cells, took off under its own power on the lawn at Bagatelle and flew about 60 metres. On 12 November it flew 220 metres, the first heavier-than-air flights certified by the Aéro-Club de France. The Wrights had flown three years earlier but in near secrecy; this one was watched by a crowd.", "His Demoiselle of 1909 was a tiny bamboo monoplane whose plans were published for anyone to build, and about 300 were made; it is often called the first ultralight. In Brazil he is regarded as the father of aviation. He fell ill, was distressed by the military use of aircraft, and took his own life in Guarujá on 23 July 1932."], "rel": ["kitty-hawk", "hx-fai-founding", "hx-bartolomeu-de-gusmao", "hx-octave-chanute"], "src": [{"t": "Britannica: Alberto Santos-Dumont", "u": "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Alberto-Santos-Dumont"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Alberto Santos-Dumont", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alberto_Santos-Dumont"}]}, {"id": "hx-abbas-ibn-firnas", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 37.88, "lon": -4.78, "name": "Abbas ibn Firnas", "place": "Córdoba, Andalusia, Spain", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "A ninth-century polymath of Córdoba is said to have glided in feathered wings and landed badly, lacking a tail.", "body": ["Abbas ibn Firnas was born around 810 in Ronda and worked in Córdoba, capital of Muslim Spain, until his death in 887. He was an astronomer, physician, chemist, musician, poet and engineer. He is credited with a method for making colourless glass, magnifying reading stones, a water clock and a device showing the motion of the planets and stars.", "The flight story comes from the historian al-Maqqari, writing in the 17th century, about 700 years after the event, and he cites no source. He says Ibn Firnas covered himself in feathers, fitted wings, jumped from a height and flew a considerable distance like a bird, but was badly hurt on landing. A poem by a court contemporary appears to allude to the attempt. No other early source mentions it, so popular retellings with dates, tower names and silk wings go beyond the evidence.", "Whatever happened, the account names the same failing that the monk Eilmer of Malmesbury blamed a century and a half later: no tail, so no way to control pitch or slow down for landing. A lunar crater was named after him in 1976, a bridge over the Guadalquivir in Córdoba in 2011, and a statue of him stands at Baghdad airport."], "rel": ["hx-eilmer-of-malmesbury", "hx-chinese-man-lifting-kites", "hx-percy-pilcher", "lilienthal"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Abbas ibn Firnas", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbas_ibn_Firnas"}, {"t": "Wikipedia (ES): Abbás Ibn Firnás, the popular version", "u": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abb%C3%A1s_Ibn_Firn%C3%A1s"}]}, {"id": "hx-eilmer-of-malmesbury", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 51.58, "lon": -2.1, "name": "Eilmer of Malmesbury", "place": "Malmesbury Abbey, Wiltshire, England", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": true, "hook": "Around 1010 a monk jumped off an abbey tower with wings and glided 200 metres before breaking both legs.", "body": ["Eilmer was a Benedictine monk at Malmesbury Abbey in Wiltshire. Almost everything known about him comes from the historian William of Malmesbury, writing in the same abbey around 1125, who had known old monks who remembered Eilmer. Inspired by the Greek story of Daedalus, Eilmer fixed wings to his hands and feet and leapt from a tower.", "William says he flew more than a furlong, about 200 metres, before a gust and his own nerve failed him. He fell, broke both legs and was lame for the rest of his life. His own verdict was that he had forgotten to give himself a tail. The flight is usually dated to the first decade of the 11th century; he was still alive in 1066, when he saw Halley's comet and called it an omen.", "A furlong from a tower of perhaps 25 metres would be a glide ratio around 8 to 1 if the figures are taken literally, generous for cloth wings, and the distance may be rounded. Even so, it is one of the earliest credible accounts of a glide in Europe. A stained-glass window in the abbey shows him with his wings. Every paraglider pilot who has had a hard landing understands the tail remark."], "rel": ["hx-abbas-ibn-firnas", "hx-percy-pilcher", "hx-daedalus-88", "lilienthal"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Eilmer of Malmesbury", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eilmer_of_Malmesbury"}, {"t": "Mysteries of Mercia: Flying monks at Malmesbury Abbey", "u": "https://www.mysteriesofmercia.com/post/tigers-flying-monks-at-malmesbury-abbey"}]}, {"id": "hx-bartolomeu-de-gusmao", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 38.71, "lon": -9.14, "name": "Bartolomeu de Gusmão's balloon, 1709", "place": "Lisbon, Portugal", "icon": "balloon", "hidden": true, "hook": "Seventy-four years before the Montgolfiers, a Brazilian priest floated a hot-air balloon to a palace ceiling in Lisbon.", "body": ["Bartolomeu Lourenço de Gusmão was born in December 1685 in Santos, in Portuguese Brazil, and became a priest and scholar in Portugal. In 1709 he petitioned King João V for support for a flying machine. Prints across Europe showed it as a bird-shaped boat with a sail, the Passarola, drawn deliberately wrong, with the lift credited to magnets, to hide the real principle. A public test announced for 24 June 1709 never took place.", "What did happen was smaller and more important. On 8 August 1709, in the audience hall of the royal palace in Lisbon, he lit a flame beneath a small paper balloon in front of the court, and it rose to the ceiling before sinking back. It is among the first recorded demonstrations that hot air can lift a craft. The king rewarded him with a professorship at Coimbra and a church post.", "Gusmão never scaled it up. Fearing that he had been denounced to the Inquisition, he burned his papers, fled to Spain in 1724 and died in Toledo on 18 November that year, aged 38. The Montgolfier brothers flew their first balloon in 1783 without knowing of him. Brazil, which also claims Santos-Dumont, remembers Gusmão as the first of its aviation pioneers."], "rel": ["montgolfier", "hx-santos-dumont", "hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "hx-breitling-orbiter-3"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Bartolomeu de Gusmão", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bartolomeu_de_Gusm%C3%A3o"}, {"t": "Wikipedia (PT): Bartolomeu de Gusmão", "u": "https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bartolomeu_de_Gusm%C3%A3o"}]}, {"id": "hx-chinese-man-lifting-kites", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 36.33, "lon": 114.63, "name": "Chinese man-lifting kites", "place": "Ye (Linzhang), Hebei, China", "icon": "kite", "hidden": true, "hook": "In 559 a condemned prince flew from a tower on a paper kite and landed alive 2.5 km away.", "body": ["The earliest accounts of people carried by kites come from China, and they are not happy ones. The Book of Sui, compiled in 636, says that Emperor Wenxuan of the Northern Qi, who reigned from 550 to 559, had prisoners fly from a tower on bamboo mats. His capital was Ye, near modern Linzhang in Hebei.", "The Zizhi Tongjian of 1044 adds a survivor. In 559 the prince Yuan Huangtou and other prisoners were launched from the Tower of the Golden Phoenix, about 33 metres high, attached to paper owls. Only Yuan Huangtou flew clear; he came down at the Purple Way, a road about 2.5 km from the tower, and lived, though he was later starved to death in prison.", "Marco Polo, in 1282, described the Chinese strapping a man to a large kite and flying him before a voyage to read the omens for the ship, and he noted how dangerous it was. A kite with a person under it is a tethered glider; the pull of the line and the wind decide everything, which is why towing a paraglider today is done only with trained operators and weak links."], "rel": ["hx-abbas-ibn-firnas", "hx-francis-rogallo", "hx-john-dickenson-ski-wing", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Man-lifting kite", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man-lifting_kite"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Yuan Huangtou", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuan_Huangtou"}]}, {"id": "hx-daedalus-88", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 36.35, "lon": 25.47, "name": "Daedalus 88, human-powered flight across the Aegean", "place": "Perissa, Santorini, Greece", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "An Olympic cyclist pedalled a 31 kg aeroplane 115 km from Crete and ditched seven metres from the beach.", "body": ["The Daedalus project at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology set out to retrace the myth: a human-powered flight from Crete. The aircraft, Daedalus 88, had a frame of carbon-fibre tubes and a Mylar skin about 8 microns thick, and weighed 31 kg. The pilot pedalled a propeller through a drivetrain, producing roughly the power of a bright light bulb for hours on end.", "On 23 April 1988 the Greek cyclist Kanellos Kanellopoulos, who had ridden in the 1984 Olympics, took off from Heraklion airport on Crete at 7 in the morning and flew north over the sea towards Santorini. After 3 hours 54 minutes and 115.11 km, gusts off the island broke the tail boom and then the wing spar, and the aircraft dropped into the water about 7 metres from Perissa beach. He swam ashore unhurt.", "The flight set FAI world records for distance and duration by a human-powered aircraft that still stand. It needed a windless morning, a pilot with a huge aerobic capacity and a chase boat. The landing is the lesson: the air over a sun-warmed island is rough even on a calm day, and the lightest aircraft are the ones that suffer most."], "rel": ["hx-gossamer-albatross", "hx-solar-impulse", "hx-eilmer-of-malmesbury", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "FAI: Thirty years since the longest human-powered flight", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/179063"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: MIT Daedalus", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIT_Daedalus"}]}, {"id": "hx-gossamer-albatross", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 51.08, "lon": 1.17, "name": "Gossamer Albatross crosses the Channel, 1979", "place": "Folkestone, Kent, England", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A cyclist pedalled a 32 kg aircraft across the Channel in 1979 for a prize unclaimed for twenty years.", "body": ["The Kremer prize offered 100,000 pounds for the first human-powered flight across the English Channel. Paul MacCready, an American engineer and former gliding champion, had already won the earlier Kremer prize in 1977 with the Gossamer Condor. His company AeroVironment built the Gossamer Albatross from a carbon-fibre frame, expanded polystyrene ribs and clear Mylar film: wingspan 29.77 metres, empty weight 32 kg.", "On 12 June 1979 the cyclist Bryan Allen took off from near Folkestone and pedalled across the Channel to Cap Gris-Nez in France in 2 hours 49 minutes, covering about 35.7 km a few metres above the water. Headwinds nearly stopped him and his water ran out; a boat moved in to hook the aircraft, but he climbed a little, found smoother air and carried on.", "The aircraft is at the Smithsonian's Udvar-Hazy Center near Washington; its back-up, which later flew inside the Houston Astrodome, is at the Museum of Flight in Seattle. MacCready went on to solar aircraft and electric cars. His approach, make it absurdly light and fly it slowly, is also why a paraglider weighs about 5 kg and sinks at only about a metre a second."], "rel": ["hx-daedalus-88", "hx-solar-impulse", "hx-percy-pilcher", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Museum of Flight: MacCready Gossamer Albatross II", "u": "https://www.museumofflight.org/aircraft/maccready-gossamer-albatross-ii"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: MacCready Gossamer Albatross", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacCready_Gossamer_Albatross"}]}, {"id": "hx-solar-impulse", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 24.43, "lon": 54.46, "name": "Solar Impulse round the world, 2015 to 2016", "place": "Al Bateen Airport, Abu Dhabi, United Arab Emirates", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "An aeroplane with a jumbo jet's wingspan and a car's weight flew round the world on sunlight alone.", "body": ["Solar Impulse 2 was a Swiss project led by Bertrand Piccard and André Borschberg. The aircraft had a wingspan of about 72 metres, comparable to a Boeing 747, yet weighed about 2,300 kg. Its wings and tail carried 17,248 solar cells that charged batteries by day so that four electric motors could keep it aloft through the night, cruising at roughly the speed of a car on a country road.", "It left Abu Dhabi on 9 March 2015 and returned on 26 July 2016, after 17 legs and about 40,000 km. The two pilots took turns alone in an unheated, unpressurised cockpit. The longest leg, Nagoya to Hawaii, took Borschberg 117 hours 52 minutes, almost five days without landing, the longest solo flight by any pilot. Battery damage on that leg grounded the project in Hawaii for more than nine months.", "It was the first circumnavigation by a piloted fixed-wing aircraft on solar power alone. The lesson for free flight is familiar: the slow, light aircraft is at the mercy of weather, and the team spent far longer waiting for the right wind than flying in it. The aircraft was sold in 2019 for autonomous-flight research and was lost in the Gulf of Mexico on 4 May 2026, with nobody on board."], "rel": ["hx-breitling-orbiter-3", "hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "hx-gossamer-albatross", "hx-daedalus-88"], "src": [{"t": "Solar Impulse: The adventure", "u": "https://aroundtheworld.solarimpulse.com/adventure"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Solar Impulse", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solar_Impulse"}]}, {"id": "hx-breitling-orbiter-3", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 46.47, "lon": 7.13, "name": "Breitling Orbiter 3, first non-stop balloon circumnavigation", "place": "Château-d'Oex, Vaud, Switzerland", "icon": "balloon", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two men left a Swiss valley by balloon on 1 March 1999 and landed in Egypt twenty days later.", "body": ["Château-d'Oex, a village in the Swiss Pre-Alps, hosts an international balloon festival every January and was the launch point for Breitling Orbiter 3. The balloon was a Rozière, a design combining a helium cell with a hot-air section beneath it, built by Cameron Balloons in Bristol as the R-650. The crew lived in a pressurised capsule the size of a small van.", "Bertrand Piccard, a Swiss psychiatrist and grandson of the stratosphere pioneer Auguste Piccard, and the British balloonist Brian Jones lifted off at 8:05 GMT on 1 March 1999. They rode the jet streams east across North Africa, Asia and the Pacific, and landed in the Egyptian desert about 80 km north of Mut, near the Dakhla Oasis, on 21 March after 19 days 21 hours 55 minutes and 40,814 km, the first non-stop round-the-world balloon flight.", "The gondola is in the Smithsonian's Udvar-Hazy Center near Washington. A balloon has no steering; the crew chose altitude to find a wind going the right way, which is the same decision a paraglider pilot makes when picking which layer to fly in. Piccard went on to lead the Solar Impulse flights."], "rel": ["hx-auguste-piccard-1931", "hx-solar-impulse", "hx-steve-fossett", "montgolfier"], "src": [{"t": "Cameron Balloons: Breitling Orbiter 3", "u": "https://cfe.cameronballoons.co.uk/work/breitling-orbiter-3"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Breitling Orbiter", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breitling_Orbiter"}]}, {"id": "hx-steve-fossett", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -31.65, "lon": 116.67, "name": "Steve Fossett", "place": "Northam, Western Australia, Australia", "icon": "balloon", "hidden": false, "hook": "He circled the world alone by balloon and by aeroplane, then vanished on a short flight in Nevada.", "body": ["Steve Fossett was born in Jackson, Tennessee, on 22 April 1944 and made a fortune in the Chicago financial markets. From the 1990s he spent it on records in balloons, sailing boats, gliders and aircraft. His first five attempts at a solo round-the-world balloon flight failed; one, in 1998, ended with a fall into the Coral Sea.", "On the sixth try, in 2002, he launched the Spirit of Freedom from Northam, Western Australia, on 19 June and crossed his starting longitude on 2 July, the first solo non-stop balloon flight round the world, landing in the Queensland outback. In 2005 he flew the Virgin Atlantic GlobalFlyer from Salina, Kansas, round the world alone without refuelling in about 67 hours. On 29 August 2006 he and Einar Enevoldson rode mountain wave over El Calafate, Argentina, to 15,460 metres in a glider, a world record.", "On 3 September 2007 he took off alone in a small Super Decathlon from the Flying M Ranch in Nevada and did not return. The wreckage was found in October 2008 high in the Sierra Nevada of California. Investigators blamed downdraughts stronger than the aircraft could out-climb, the same mountain lee-side air that a paraglider pilot is taught to stay clear of."], "rel": ["hx-breitling-orbiter-3", "hx-chuck-yeager", "foehn", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Britannica: Steve Fossett", "u": "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Steve-Fossett"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Steve Fossett", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Fossett"}]}, {"id": "hx-fai-founding", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 46.52, "lon": 6.63, "name": "The FAI, founded 1905", "place": "Lausanne, Vaud, Switzerland", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Every world record here exists because eight countries met in Paris in 1905 and agreed to keep score.", "body": ["The Fédération Aéronautique Internationale was founded in Paris on 14 October 1905, at the end of a three-day conference attended by Belgium, France, Germany, Great Britain, Italy, Spain, Switzerland and the United States. Ballooning was the main sport then; the Wrights had flown only two years earlier. The FAI now has its headquarters in Lausanne, Switzerland.", "Its job is to govern air sports and to be the single judge of records, from balloons and gliders to spaceflight. A claimed record must meet the FAI's rules for evidence, observers and instruments before it is ratified, which is why a flight can be famous for months before it is official. Felix Baumgartner's jump and Sebastien Kayrouz's 610 km both waited for this process.", "Hang gliding and paragliding sit under the Commission Internationale de Vol Libre, the CIVL, which runs the world championships and keeps the distance and height records. National bodies such as the BHPA, DHV, FFVL and USHPA are its members. When pilots fly for a record they carry a certified tracker and submit the file within days, or the flight simply counts as a nice day out."], "rel": ["hx-kossen-1989", "hx-bhpa-founding", "hx-dhv-founding", "hx-bessie-coleman"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Fédération Aéronautique Internationale", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A9d%C3%A9ration_A%C3%A9ronautique_Internationale"}, {"t": "FAI: Paragliding XC world champions", "u": "https://www.fai.org/page/paragliding-xc-world-champions"}]}, {"id": "hx-first-paragliding-schools", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 46.1, "lon": 7.23, "name": "The first paragliding schools and tandems", "place": "Verbier, Valais, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Within six years of the first hillside launch, people were being taught to fly, then carried as passengers.", "body": ["The first people to teach paragliding were the Mieussy pioneers themselves. The club Les Choucas, founded on 5 May 1979 by Gérard Bosson, Georges Perret and Michel Didriche, ran the first courses on the slopes above Mieussy, led by Michel Sarthe, Didriche, Bosson and Jean-Claude Bétemps. At first only qualified parachutists were admitted; the first two students who were not, Marc Buffet and Jean-François Baudet, came from the village.", "Across the border the sport took root in the Valais. Swiss press histories date the first paragliding school to Verbier in 1984, and the resort has been a recognised centre since 1985, the year the first purpose-built wing appeared. Verbier then hosted the unofficial world championship of 1987 and the official one in 1993, and the first flight manual, published in 1985 by Patrick Gilligan and Bertrand Dubuis, coined the word paragliding.", "Tandem wings, larger canopies rated for two people, followed as schools grew, and by the 1990s tandem flights were a commercial product in Alpine resorts. Today they are the usual first contact with the sport, and tandem records have their own FAI category: Julien and Emilie Irilli flew 363 km from Quixadá, Brazil, on 26 October 2015. A tandem pilot carries a professional licence and a reserve big enough for two."], "rel": ["mieussy", "hx-kalbermatten-ailes-de-k", "hx-kossen-1989", "hx-bhpa-founding"], "src": [{"t": "L'Avenir: Le premier vol de parapente au monde", "u": "https://www.lavenir.net/lifestyle/2014/10/30/le-premier-vol-de-parapente-au-monde-TIKTNUW7Y5E6HJFATWTLXBLKMA/"}, {"t": "Cross Country: 363 km tandem paraglider record", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/363km-tandem-paraglider-record/"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "hx-dhv-founding", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 47.75, "lon": 11.74, "name": "The DHV, Germany's free-flight federation", "place": "Gmund am Tegernsee, Bavaria, Germany", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A hang gliding body founded in 1979 became the world's largest paragliding association, with powers delegated by the state.", "body": ["The Deutscher Hängegleiterverband was founded by hang glider pilots on 24 November 1979 in Donzdorf, and Peter Janssen chaired it for its first 18 years. Paragliding was officially permitted in Germany on 15 April 1987 and paraglider pilots now make up most of the membership. The association is based in Gmund am Tegernsee in Bavaria, took its 40,000th member in 2021, and calls itself the largest such association in the world.", "What makes the DHV unusual is that the German state has delegated real authority to it. Under a federal ordinance of 16 December 1993 it issues pilot licences, approves training, and grants permits for launching and landing outside airfields. It also runs an accredited test centre for wings and harnesses and offers accident investigation and safety notices to pilots, though it does not write the certification standards themselves.", "The DHV selects the German national teams, runs the DHV-XC online contest where pilots upload and compare flights, and publishes DHVmagazin every two months. For pilots abroad the practical point is the DHV test reports and accident statistics, which are among the most detailed anywhere and shape how the EN rating on the wing you fly is understood."], "rel": ["hx-bhpa-founding", "hx-fai-founding", "hx-kossen-1989", "hx-hanna-reitsch"], "src": [{"t": "DHV: Historie des Verbands", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/verband/historie/"}, {"t": "Wikipedia (DE): Deutscher Hängegleiterverband", "u": "https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deutscher_H%C3%A4ngegleiterverband"}]}, {"id": "hx-bhpa-founding", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 52.63, "lon": -1.13, "name": "The BHPA, Britain's free-flight association", "place": "Leicester, England", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "British hang gliding and parascending ran as separate tribes until 1992, when they merged in Leicester.", "body": ["British free flight has two roots. Parascending, being towed up under a round canopy and released, grew out of parachuting; in 1973 Walter Neumark and others left the British Parachute Association to form the British Association of Parascending Clubs. Neumark had predicted in Flight magazine in 1954 that pilots would one day run off a slope under a canopy, and the Royal Aero Club gave him its gold medal in 1997.", "Hang gliding arrived from Australia and the United States in the early 1970s and had its own body, the British Hang Gliding Association. When paragliders appeared in the 1980s they fell awkwardly between the two. In 1992 the associations merged to form the British Hang Gliding and Paragliding Association, based in Leicester.", "The BHPA has more than 60 clubs and about 7,000 members and exists mainly to keep the sport safe. Training runs through a badge system: Elementary Pilot, Club Pilot, Pilot and Advanced Pilot, with written exams in air law and meteorology along the way. Members receive the monthly magazine Skywings, and most British sites are run by clubs under BHPA rules."], "rel": ["hx-dhv-founding", "hx-fai-founding", "hx-john-dickenson-ski-wing", "hx-first-paragliding-schools"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: British Hang Gliding and Paragliding Association", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/British_Hang_Gliding_and_Paragliding_Association"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding (history)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "BHPA: British Hang Gliding History", "u": "https://bhpahistory.com/"}]}, {"id": "na-golden-eagle", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": 57.08, "lon": -3.67, "name": "Golden eagle", "place": "Cairngorms, Scotland, United Kingdom", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "A bird that soars at the pace of a paraglider, then dives at four times that speed.", "body": ["The golden eagle is the soaring bird most pilots in the northern hemisphere will actually share a thermal with. Cornell Lab gives a wingspan of 185 to 220 centimetres and a weight of 3 to 6 kilograms, with females the larger. It soars with its wings held in a shallow V and the tip feathers spread like fingers, which cuts wingtip drag in the same way a glider's winglets do.", "Its relaxed soaring speed is about 45 to 52 km/h, close to the trim speed of a paraglider, which is why eagles and wings so often end up circling together. When it folds into a dive after prey it reaches roughly 240 to 320 km/h. One bird was recorded circling at 6,190 metres over the Khumbu region of Nepal in 1975.", "In Britain the species now survives only in the Scottish Highlands and islands and a few pairs in Northern Ireland, and the RSPB logged 22 confirmed persecution incidents between 2007 and 2022. Worldwide the population is estimated at 170,000 to 250,000 birds. Home ranges run from 20 to 200 square kilometres, so a pair you meet above a Highland glen is probably the only pair for a long way.", "If an eagle closes on you in a thermal it is usually curious rather than hostile, but keep turning smoothly and never try to out-turn it. Pilots get this wrong by changing direction suddenly, which is exactly what makes a big raptor react."], "rel": ["na-steppe-eagle", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "na-wind-turbines-raptors", "himalayan-griffon"], "src": [{"t": "Golden Eagle identification, Cornell Lab All About Birds", "u": "https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Golden_Eagle/id"}, {"t": "Golden eagle, RSPB", "u": "https://www.rspb.org.uk/birds-and-wildlife/golden-eagle"}, {"t": "Golden eagle, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_eagle"}]}, {"id": "na-steppe-eagle", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 50.43, "lon": 69.2, "name": "Steppe eagle", "place": "Korgalzhyn, Kazakhstan, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "An endangered eagle that commutes from Kazakhstan to India and Africa, and dies on power lines on the way.", "body": ["The steppe eagle breeds on the open grasslands of Kazakhstan, Russia, Mongolia and China, and Kazakhstan is its main stronghold. It is a large, dark, broad-winged eagle with a wingspan quoted between about 165 and 262 centimetres depending on the source, and a weight of roughly 2.3 to 5.5 kilograms.", "It is a true soaring migrant. Birds from Central Asia winter across the Indian subcontinent, Arabia and East Africa, and they funnel through the same bottlenecks paraglider pilots know. At Eilat in Israel an average of about 28,000 pass each spring, with a peak around 10 March, and up to 75,000 were counted there in 1985. In Nepal about 7,850 were tallied over two and a half weeks one autumn.", "The species is listed as Endangered by the IUCN after a decline thought to be well over 50 percent. Electrocution on power pylons is a major killer, especially in Kazakhstan, along with steppe fires, nest disturbance and livestock trampling ground nests. For a pilot flying the Kazakh or Indian plains in autumn, a loose line of big dark eagles drifting south is a free map of the lift."], "rel": ["na-golden-eagle", "na-eilat-flyway", "na-vulture-diclofenac", "almaty-site"], "src": [{"t": "Steppe eagle, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steppe_eagle"}, {"t": "Raptor migration at Eilat, British Birds", "u": "https://britishbirds.co.uk/journal/article/raptor-migration-eilat"}]}, {"id": "na-white-stork", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": 36.01, "lon": -5.6, "name": "White stork", "place": "Tarifa, Strait of Gibraltar, Spain", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Half a million storks a year detour thousands of kilometres rather than flap across the sea.", "body": ["The white stork is the classic thermal migrant. With a wingspan of 155 to 215 centimetres and a weight of 2.3 to 4.5 kilograms it is too heavy to flap for long, so it climbs in thermals and glides between them exactly as a paraglider does. Thermals do not form over open water, so storks avoid the Mediterranean and squeeze through two land crossings: the Strait of Gibraltar in the west and the Bosphorus in the east.", "Flapping flight is estimated to burn about 23 times more body fat per kilometre than soaring, which is why the detour pays. About 530,000 storks use the eastern route each year, following the Levant and the Nile. It is roughly twice as long as the western route, yet birds arrive in Africa in about the same time. The autumn trip takes about 26 days; the spring return averages 49.", "Flocks spiral up until they top out at about 1,200 to 1,500 metres, then set off on glide. One bird over western Sudan was recorded at 3,300 metres. Migrating columns of storks, raptors and pelicans can stretch 200 kilometres. For a pilot at Tarifa or Algodonales in September, a stork kettle is the best thermal marker there is.", "Poland holds about a quarter of the world's white storks, with 52,500 pairs counted in 2004 and 2005. In Alsace fewer than nine pairs were left in the early 1980s; by 2008 there were 270 pairs after a reintroduction programme."], "rel": ["na-how-birds-find-thermals", "na-via-pontica", "na-east-atlantic-flyway", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "White stork, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_stork"}, {"t": "Flack et al. 2018, From local collective behavior to global migratory patterns in white storks, Science", "u": "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aap7781"}]}, {"id": "na-griffon-vulture", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 41.05, "lon": -6.75, "name": "Eurasian griffon vulture", "place": "Douro International Natural Park, Portugal", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "A ten kilogram bird that soars on barely more energy than it uses sitting still.", "body": ["The Eurasian griffon is the vulture of Spain, Portugal, the Balkans, Crete and the Caucasus, and the one most European pilots thermal with. Its wingspan is 2.3 to 2.8 metres and a typical adult weighs 6 to 10 kilograms. It nests on crags, cave mouths and sheltered ledges, sometimes only 10 metres above the ground at Beli on the island of Cres in Croatia.", "Researchers use the griffon as a model for soaring physiology. A soaring griffon burns about 1.43 times its basal metabolic rate, while flapping birds can reach 16 times, and its heart rate returns to resting level within ten minutes of landing. That efficiency is the whole point of soaring flight, for birds and for us.", "Colonies worth knowing as a pilot include the Douro gorge on the Portugal-Spain border, which holds more than half of Portugal's birds, the Trešnjica canyon in Serbia, and Crete, where groups of up to 20 soar together. Armenia had only 46 to 54 pairs at the last count, and Cyprus fewer than 30 birds in 2006.", "Griffons will join your thermal and often climb straight through you. Hold your circle, do not flare or wave at them, and never fly along a cliff colony in the breeding season. Pilots get it wrong by chasing birds into the lee of the ridge where the rotor lives."], "rel": ["na-bearded-vulture", "na-egyptian-vulture", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "ruppells-vulture"], "src": [{"t": "Griffon vulture, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Griffon_vulture"}, {"t": "Griffon vulture, Vulture Conservation Foundation", "u": "https://4vultures.org/vultures/griffon-vulture/"}]}, {"id": "na-bearded-vulture", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 47.12, "lon": 12.7, "name": "Bearded vulture", "place": "Hohe Tauern, Austria", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Shot out of the Alps by 1913, it came back on a diet of bones dropped from 100 metres.", "body": ["The bearded vulture, or lammergeier, is the only bird that lives mostly on bone. Adults get around 85 to 90 percent of their diet from it. Bones too big to swallow are carried up 50 to 150 metres and dropped onto rock slabs to crack them for the marrow; the bird can lift pieces more than 4 kilograms and 10 centimetres thick, and young birds take up to seven years to perfect the trick.", "Its wingspan is 2.3 to 2.8 metres and it weighs 4.5 to 7.8 kilograms. It is a bird of the high country, usually above the tree line, up to 2,000 metres in Europe and 5,000 metres in Central Asia, and has been seen at 7,500 metres in the Himalaya. Narrow, pointed wings and a long wedge tail let it work weak ridge lift along cliffs for hours.", "The Alpine population was shot to extinction by the 1930s. A second reintroduction began in the 1980s, placing zoo-bred chicks in protected eyries such as the Rauris valley in Hohe Tauern and feeding them until they fledged. More than 200 birds were released between 1987 and 2015 and the species now breeds across the Alps again, with more than 60 pairs there and about 465 pairs in Europe as a whole.", "When you see one quartering a cliff, it is riding exactly the slope lift you would use, and it will often hold station in air too weak for a paraglider. Give it the ridge and never fly along a nesting cliff between December and July, when a single disturbance can cost the pair their one chick of the year. The Alps' bearded vultures have had a hard enough century already."], "rel": ["na-griffon-vulture", "na-alps-formation", "na-egyptian-vulture", "himalayan-griffon"], "src": [{"t": "Bearded vulture, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bearded_vulture"}, {"t": "Bearded vulture, Vulture Conservation Foundation", "u": "https://4vultures.org/vultures/bearded-vulture/"}]}, {"id": "na-egyptian-vulture", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 28.4, "lon": -14, "name": "Egyptian vulture", "place": "Fuerteventura, Canary Islands, Spain", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "The only vulture that throws stones, and one of the few that can fly 500 kilometres in a day.", "body": ["The Egyptian vulture is the small white vulture of the Mediterranean, Africa and India, weighing about 1.9 kilograms in Spain and about 2.4 kilograms in the Canary Islands subspecies. African birds crack ostrich and bustard eggs by picking up a pebble and hurling it down with a swing of the neck, repeating until the shell breaks. Tests suggest the behaviour is innate, and it has never been recorded in the Indian subspecies.", "European birds migrate to the southern edge of the Sahara, a journey of 3,500 to 5,500 kilometres. Iberian birds cross at the Strait of Gibraltar, Italian birds hop via Sicily and Pantelleria to Tunisia, and Balkan birds go through the Levant. On a good day of thermals a migrant can cover 500 kilometres, which is a respectable paragliding cross-country distance.", "The species is listed as Endangered, with roughly 12,400 to 36,000 mature birds left worldwide and only about 2,700 to 2,900 pairs in Europe, four fifths of them in Iberia. Europe's population has halved in 40 years and the Balkan population has fallen by about 80 percent. Poisoned baits, electrocution on power lines and the vulture poisoning crisis in South Asia drive the decline, and more than half of young birds die on their first migration. Fuerteventura holds a closely studied resident subspecies."], "rel": ["na-griffon-vulture", "na-vulture-diclofenac", "na-parahawking", "na-volcanic-cone-thermals"], "src": [{"t": "Egyptian vulture, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_vulture"}, {"t": "Egyptian vulture, Vulture Conservation Foundation", "u": "https://4vultures.org/vultures/egyptian-vulture/"}]}, {"id": "na-black-kite", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 28.61, "lon": 77.21, "name": "Black kite", "place": "Old Delhi, India", "icon": "bird", "hidden": true, "hook": "The world's most numerous large raptor, and in Australia it is accused of starting fires.", "body": ["The black kite is thought to be the most abundant member of the hawk and eagle family on Earth, with estimates of up to 6.7 million birds. It is a light, buoyant soarer of about 735 grams with a wingspan around 150 centimetres and a forked tail it twists like a rudder. In the thermals over Delhi it is the bird you see most.", "Delhi is the densest kite city anywhere. A 1967 survey of about 150 square kilometres estimated 2,200 pairs, about 15 pairs per square kilometre, and a 2013 survey found the same density. The birds live on offal, rubbish and the meat scraps tossed to them by residents, and they ride the hot air rising off the city.", "In northern Australia kites have been reported picking up burning twigs from grass fires and dropping them in unburnt grass, apparently to flush prey, which has earned them the name firehawks. Kites everywhere are drawn to smoke, and before a water crossing they gather into far larger flocks than other migrating raptors.", "Kites are harmless to a paraglider but tell you something: a dense kettle over a town in the afternoon is urban thermal lift, and it is turbulent, low and full of birds. Stay high and well clear of the rooftops."], "rel": ["na-red-kite", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "na-vulture-diclofenac", "bir-billing"], "src": [{"t": "Black kite, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_kite"}, {"t": "Bonta et al. 2017, Intentional fire-spreading by firehawk raptors in northern Australia, Journal of Ethnobiology", "u": "https://doi.org/10.2993/0278-0771-37.4.700"}]}, {"id": "na-red-kite", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 51.64, "lon": -0.98, "name": "Red kite", "place": "Watlington Hill, Chiltern Hills, England, United Kingdom", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Thirteen Spanish chicks released in 1990 became one of Europe's great wildlife comebacks.", "body": ["By the end of the nineteenth century the red kite was extinct in England, Scotland and Ireland, with only a handful of pairs hanging on in mid Wales. The first releases came in 1989 in the Chilterns and the north of Scotland, and in July 1990, 13 young kites from Navarra in Spain were set free in the Chiltern Hills, the start of a programme led by the RSPB and what is now Natural England. By 1996 at least 37 pairs were breeding in southern England.", "Today Britain holds around 15 percent of the world's red kites, close to 6,000 breeding pairs by BirdLife's 2021 estimate, and the species is Green listed in the UK and was downgraded to Least Concern on the global Red List in 2020. On the Chiltern escarpment, a ridge-soaring site in a northwest wind, a pilot may share the slope with twenty or thirty of them.", "The red kite is a slow, elegant soarer that flies with its wings flat or slightly bowed, unlike the buzzard's raised V, and steers constantly with its long forked tail. It is a scavenger rather than a hunter and is no threat to anyone, but it is a protected Schedule 1 species, and buzzing a nest tree in spring is both illegal and poor form."], "rel": ["na-black-kite", "na-common-buzzard", "na-escarpment", "na-slope-soaring-gulls"], "src": [{"t": "Red kite, RSPB", "u": "https://www.rspb.org.uk/birds-and-wildlife/red-kite"}, {"t": "30-year anniversary of landmark release of red kites in the Chiltern Hills, UK Government", "u": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/30-year-anniversary-of-landmark-release-of-red-kites-in-the-chiltern-hills"}, {"t": "Red List update: how we brought the Red Kite home to the UK, BirdLife International", "u": "https://www.birdlife.org/news/2021/03/05/red-list-update-how-we-brought-the-red-kite-home-to-the-uk/"}]}, {"id": "na-common-buzzard", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 55.39, "lon": 12.83, "name": "Common buzzard", "place": "Falsterbo, Sweden", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Europe's commonest raptor, and the best free thermal marker a pilot will ever get.", "body": ["The common buzzard is probably the most numerous daytime raptor in Europe, with a global population in the millions. Its wingspan is 109 to 140 centimetres and British birds weigh roughly 430 to 1,370 grams, with females heavier. It looks heavy and laboured in level flapping flight but is a gifted soarer once it is up, holding its wings in a shallow V with the tail fanned.", "Northern buzzards migrate, and they pile up at the same coastal bottlenecks as other soaring birds. At Falsterbo, the sandy tip of southern Sweden where birds wait for the shortest crossing to Denmark, a maximum of 41,000 common buzzards has been recorded in a season. The steppe buzzard subspecies pours through Israel each spring in numbers between 150,000 and nearly 466,000.", "Watch where a buzzard circles and how fast it climbs. A buzzard going up steadily without flapping is sitting in a thermal strong enough for a paraglider; a buzzard flapping and drifting is telling you the lift has died. Tracked migrants over Israel mostly stay below 1,000 metres, half of them under 400, because the flat desert gives few triggers and they rely on tailwind instead. Pilots get it wrong by assuming every circling bird is in lift."], "rel": ["na-red-kite", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "na-eilat-flyway", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Common buzzard, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_buzzard"}, {"t": "Buzzard, RSPB", "u": "https://www.rspb.org.uk/birds-and-wildlife/buzzard"}]}, {"id": "na-frigatebird", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -22.36, "lon": 40.37, "name": "Great frigatebird", "place": "Europa Island, Mozambique Channel, France", "icon": "bird", "hidden": true, "hook": "A seabird that cannot land on the sea, so it stays aloft for months and sleeps mid-climb.", "body": ["The frigatebird has the lowest wing loading of any bird: a wingspan of 205 to 230 centimetres carrying only 1 to 1.6 kilograms. It cannot settle on the water because its feathers are not waterproof, so it has evolved to do what a paraglider pilot dreams of, staying airborne by soaring alone. It usually takes off in the late afternoon, when sea breezes and thermals are strongest.", "In 2016 a French team led by Henri Weimerskirch fitted about fifty adult and juvenile great frigatebirds on Europa Island with GPS, heart rate and wingbeat loggers. Juveniles on their first ocean crossings stayed aloft for more than two months without landing, travelling between Africa and Indonesia on the trade winds and circling the Indian Ocean.", "The birds climb in the updrafts under cumulus clouds to about 600 to 700 metres and glide off. When they need distance across clearer air they climb inside the clouds to 3,000 to 4,000 metres, where temperatures are below freezing, then glide for tens of kilometres. Brief spells of total inactivity during these climbs appear to be sleep lasting a few minutes.", "That is the paragliding game played to perfection: climb under cumulus, glide, repeat, for weeks on end. The difference is that the bird is doing it over an ocean with no landing option at all, on a wing loading no human aircraft can match. The next time a cross-country day feels long, remember a juvenile frigatebird on its first crossing has not touched anything solid since it left the island."], "rel": ["na-swift", "na-dynamic-soaring", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "albatross"], "src": [{"t": "Frigate birds: never touching down, CNRS", "u": "https://www.cnrs.fr/en/press/frigate-birds-never-touching-down"}, {"t": "Magnificent Frigatebird overview, Cornell Lab All About Birds", "u": "https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Magnificent_Frigatebird/overview"}, {"t": "Great frigatebird, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_frigatebird"}]}, {"id": "na-swift", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 55.7, "lon": 13.19, "name": "Common swift", "place": "Lund, Sweden", "icon": "bird", "hidden": true, "hook": "Some swifts do not touch anything solid for ten months of the year.", "body": ["The common swift weighs about as much as a small bar of chocolate, has a wingspan of 38 to 40 centimetres, and spends more of its life in the air than any other bird. It eats, drinks, mates and sleeps on the wing, landing only to nest. Its maximum level flight speed has been measured at 111.6 km/h, which makes it one of the fastest birds in level flight.", "In 2016 Anders Hedenström's team at Lund University published data loggers from 19 swifts tracked through their non-breeding season, from leaving Europe in August to returning the next summer. The birds were flying for more than 99 percent of that ten month period, and some individuals never landed at all. Each dawn and dusk they climb to high altitude, apparently to sleep and to read the wind.", "Swifts average about 5.5 years of life, but one Oxford bird reached at least 18 and was estimated to have flown about 6.4 million kilometres. In the UK the species has declined by 62 percent between 1995 and 2021 and is on the Red List, mostly through loss of nest sites in old buildings.", "Pilots see swifts most on summer evenings, screaming low over launch, but they are also useful in the middle of the day. A flock feeding high and spread out means insects are being carried up on lift, so the thermals are still working. When the swifts drop to tree height and hunt in tight groups the air has gone stable, and it is usually time to think about landing."], "rel": ["na-frigatebird", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "na-v-formation", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Common swifts stay airborne for ten months, Science Daily", "u": "https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2016/10/161027123227.htm"}, {"t": "Swift, RSPB", "u": "https://www.rspb.org.uk/birds-and-wildlife/swift"}, {"t": "Common swift, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_swift"}]}, {"id": "na-peregrine-stoop", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.52, "lon": -122.68, "name": "How fast does a peregrine falcon really dive?", "place": "Explained over Portland, Oregon, United States", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "The famous 389 km/h figure has never been confirmed by radar, and the truth is still astonishing.", "body": ["The peregrine is the fastest animal on Earth in a dive, or stoop. Cornell Lab states that from heights of more than a kilometre it may reach about 320 km/h as it drops on a bird. In 2005 a falconer's bird named Frightful was filmed for National Geographic diving at a reported 389 km/h, but other sources point out that radar tracking has never confirmed such speeds, and the highest reliably measured stoop is 184 km/h.", "The bird is built for it. Weighing 330 to 1,500 grams on a wingspan of 74 to 120 centimetres, it has a high wing loading that suits speed rather than soaring. Small bony tubercles in its nostrils are thought to tame the airflow so it can breathe in the dive, and a third eyelid wipes the eyes clear without blinking. Level cruise is a modest 40 to 55 km/h, and direct pursuit up to 112 km/h.", "DDT thinned peregrine eggshells and crashed the population; after the early 1970s ban it recovered and was taken off the US endangered list in 1999. It has since moved into cities, nesting on cathedrals and tower blocks that mimic cliff ledges. Portland, Oregon holds a tenth of its state's peregrine nests on about 0.1 percent of its land.", "For a pilot the lesson is wing loading. The peregrine trades sink rate for speed; a paraglider does the opposite, which is why we go up and it goes down. A city peregrine will sometimes stoop at a wing out of curiosity or to defend a nest ledge, and the right response is simply to keep flying straight. It will pull out long before contact, and a sudden turn is more likely to cause a collapse than the bird is."], "rel": ["na-golden-eagle", "na-swift", "na-sea-cliffs", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Peregrine Falcon overview, Cornell Lab All About Birds", "u": "https://www.allaboutbirds.org/guide/Peregrine_Falcon/overview"}, {"t": "Peregrine falcon, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peregrine_falcon"}]}, {"id": "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.73, "lon": 8.97, "name": "How do birds find thermals?", "place": "Explained over Lake Constance, Germany", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Birds have no variometer, yet they centre a thermal in two turns. Here is what they are reading.", "body": ["A soaring bird uses the same tools as a pilot, only better. It feels the surge of lift through its body and through the airflow over its feathers, and it turns towards the wing that was lifted. It reads the ground, favouring sun-facing slopes, dark fields and dry ground that heat fastest, and it reads the sky, heading for the shaded underside of a growing cumulus where the thermal is feeding the cloud.", "The other great tool is other birds. Storks, vultures and eagles watch each other across kilometres, and a bird climbing without flapping is an invitation. In 2018 researchers from Konstanz tracked 27 young white storks from Lake Constance on their first migration and found that a few leader birds searched out the thermals while the rest followed, flapped less, and flew further south as a result. That is exactly what a gaggle of paragliders does on a cross-country task.", "Birds climb with tighter circles than we can. A vulture may bank steeply at low speed, keeping its circle inside the strong core, while a paraglider needs a wider radius. Pilots get thermalling wrong by following a bird's exact track; copy its position relative to the core instead. And remember the bird has priority. It was here first, and it can out-turn you."], "rel": ["na-white-stork", "na-common-buzzard", "na-griffon-vulture", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Flack et al. 2018, From local collective behavior to global migratory patterns in white storks, Science", "u": "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aap7781"}, {"t": "Bird flight, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird_flight"}]}, {"id": "na-v-formation", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.58, "lon": 21.15, "name": "Why do geese fly in a V?", "place": "Explained over Hortobágy National Park, Hungary, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "goose", "hidden": false, "hook": "Each bird surfs the upwash off the wingtip of the one in front, and times its flaps to match.", "body": ["A flapping wing leaves a pair of spinning vortices behind its tips. Inside the span the air is pushed down; outside the tips it rolls up. A bird that sits just behind and to the side of the one ahead flies in that rising air and gets some of its weight carried for free, which is why skeins of geese, cranes and pelicans spread into a V or a long diagonal line.", "In 2014 Steven Portugal and colleagues fitted GPS and accelerometer loggers to a flock of northern bald ibis migrating behind a microlight. The birds not only held the upwash position but phased their wingbeats to the bird in front so that their wings rose through the upwash trail, and when directly behind another bird they reversed the phasing to avoid the downwash. An earlier study of pelicans found lower heart rates and fewer flaps in formation than flying alone at the same speed.", "Theory predicts big savings: a 1970 model suggested a 25-bird formation could extend range by up to 71 percent. Airbus tests with two helicopters flying in formation measured 5 to 10 percent fuel saving for the trailing aircraft. In autumn over Hortobágy, Europe's great crane staging ground, you can watch thousands of birds doing the sums at dusk."], "rel": ["na-white-stork", "na-east-atlantic-flyway", "na-swift", "bar-headed-goose"], "src": [{"t": "V formation, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_formation"}, {"t": "Portugal et al. 2014, Upwash exploitation and downwash avoidance by flap phasing in ibis formation flight, Nature", "u": "https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12939"}]}, {"id": "na-dynamic-soaring", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -54.01, "lon": -38.05, "name": "What is dynamic soaring?", "place": "Explained over Bird Island, South Georgia, United Kingdom", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Albatrosses cross oceans by stealing energy from the wind gradient; model gliders have hit 882 km/h doing it.", "body": ["Thermal soaring and ridge soaring use rising air. Dynamic soaring uses something else: a change in wind speed with height, called a wind gradient or shear layer. Close to the sea surface the wind is slowed by friction; a few metres up it blows much harder. A bird that climbs into the faster layer gains airspeed, turns downwind, dives back into the slow layer with that speed in hand, and repeats.", "Lord Rayleigh first described the idea in Nature in 1883, arguing that a bird could not stay up indefinitely in uniform wind, so the wind must be non-uniform. Albatrosses do it as a zigzag of half-turns, climbing into wind, turning, and dropping downwind across the gradient, and they can cover thousands of kilometres with hardly a wingbeat. South Georgia's Bird Island is one of the great albatross study colonies.", "Radio-controlled gliders flown across the sharp shear on the lee side of ridges have pushed the method to a record 882 km/h, set by Spencer Lisenby in California in January 2021, roughly ten times the wind speed. For a paraglider dynamic soaring is not an option, and the same wind gradient is a hazard: on final approach the wind drops off in the last few metres, your airspeed falls with it, and that is where pilots get surprised."], "rel": ["na-frigatebird", "na-slope-soaring-gulls", "na-sea-cliffs", "albatross"], "src": [{"t": "Dynamic soaring, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_soaring"}, {"t": "Richardson 2012, High-speed dynamic soaring, RC Soaring Digest (WHOI)", "u": "https://www2.whoi.edu/staff/prichardson/wp-content/uploads/sites/75/2018/11/Richardson-2012-RCSD-High-speed-Dynamic-Soaring.pdf"}, {"t": "RC glider hits 548 mph in record flight, AVweb", "u": "https://avweb.com/aviation-news/rc-glider-hits-548-mph-in-record-flight"}]}, {"id": "na-slope-soaring-gulls", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 52.97, "lon": -9.43, "name": "How do gulls hang motionless over a cliff?", "place": "Explained over the Cliffs of Moher, Ireland", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "The gull is not working at all. It is parked in rising air you can use too.", "body": ["When wind meets a cliff or a steep hill it cannot go through, so it goes up and over. In that rising band, roughly as deep as the cliff is high and strongest just in front of the crest, a gull with its wings spread sinks through the air at about the same rate the air is rising, so it stays level without a flap. This is ridge lift, also called slope lift, and it is the first kind of lift most paraglider pilots ever use.", "Gulls are the perfect demonstrators because their wings are long and narrow for their weight, which gives a low sink rate, and they live where sea wind hits land. At the Cliffs of Moher on the Atlantic coast of County Clare the wall rises around 200 metres and the onshore wind is nearly constant, so the air in front of it is a lift conveyor. Watch where the birds hang: that is the lift band, and where they drift back it is the top of it.", "The safety lesson is on the back side. Behind the crest the air tumbles down in a rotor, and a gull folds its wings and drops through it in a second. A paraglider blown over the back into that rotor can collapse. Fly in front of the ridge, never over or behind it in strong wind, and leave well before the wind picks up."], "rel": ["na-sea-cliffs", "na-dynamic-soaring", "na-escarpment", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "Ridge lift, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge_lift"}, {"t": "Cliffs of Moher, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliffs_of_Moher"}]}, {"id": "na-east-atlantic-flyway", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 53.5, "lon": 8.55, "name": "East Atlantic Flyway", "place": "Wadden Sea, Germany, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "goose", "hidden": false, "hook": "Ninety million birds a year commute along one coast from the Arctic to Africa.", "body": ["A flyway is a bird motorway: a broad corridor that migrants follow between breeding and wintering grounds. The East Atlantic Flyway runs down the western edge of Europe and Africa, from breeding areas in Canada, Greenland, Iceland, Scandinavia and Siberia to wintering grounds in western Europe and as far as southern Africa. About 90 million birds use it every year.", "Its key stopovers are the great tidal flats. The Wadden Sea, shared by the Netherlands, Germany and Denmark and a UNESCO site, is the biggest refuelling station in Europe for waders and geese. Further south the chain runs through French and Iberian estuaries, the Moroccan coast, Banc d'Arguin in Mauritania, and the deltas of Senegal and Guinea-Bissau.", "Soaring birds use the same corridor in their own way. Storks and raptors from western Europe funnel down to the Strait of Gibraltar, the only short sea crossing, while honey buzzards take the long way round rather than face the Sahara and the Atlas. For pilots the flyway means that a September day at Tarifa, or a spring day on the Dutch coast, comes with a sky full of birds showing where the lift is."], "rel": ["na-white-stork", "na-via-pontica", "na-v-formation", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "East Atlantic Flyway, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Atlantic_Flyway"}, {"t": "Wadden Sea, UNESCO World Heritage Centre", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1314"}]}, {"id": "na-via-pontica", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 42.5, "lon": 27.47, "name": "Via Pontica", "place": "Burgas Lakes, Bulgaria", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "A Roman road's name now belongs to Europe's second biggest bird migration route.", "body": ["Via Pontica was a Roman road along the western shore of the Black Sea, from Byzantium north through what is now Burgas and Varna to the Danube delta. Bulgarian ornithologist Nikolay Boev borrowed the name for the migration route that follows the same coast, and it is now considered the second largest bird migration corridor in Europe.", "Soaring birds from eastern and central Europe avoid the open Black Sea and track its western edge southwards, resting on the chain of coastal lakes and lagoons: the Danube delta, the Burgas lakes, and the marshes between. The route feeds into the Bosphorus crossing at Istanbul, the eastern gateway for storks, pelicans and raptors heading for the Levant and Africa. The eastern stork route alone carries about 530,000 white storks a year.", "For a pilot the geography is a lesson. Birds that need thermals hug the coast because the land heats and the sea does not, and they stack up wherever a water crossing is shortest. Where you find a migration bottleneck, you have usually found a soaring site, and the Bulgarian coast in September is one long demonstration."], "rel": ["na-white-stork", "na-east-atlantic-flyway", "na-eilat-flyway", "na-egyptian-vulture"], "src": [{"t": "Via Pontica, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Via_Pontica"}, {"t": "White stork, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_stork"}]}, {"id": "na-eilat-flyway", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": 29.55, "lon": 34.95, "name": "Eilat raptor migration", "place": "Eilat, Israel", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Up to 850,000 honey buzzards pass this one desert town in a single spring.", "body": ["Eilat sits at the top of the Gulf of Aqaba, where Africa, Asia and the Red Sea meet, and soaring birds returning north from Africa in spring squeeze through the land bridge beside it. It is one of the best places on Earth to watch Palearctic raptor migration. Up to 850,000 honey buzzards and up to 465,000 steppe buzzards have been counted passing through the Middle East in a single season.", "Steppe eagles average about 28,000 a spring at Eilat, with a peak around 10 March; 75,000 were logged in 1985. The birds cross the Red Sea at Bab-el-Mandeb, climb up the Rift, and follow the desert ridges north. Because the terrain has few mountain ridges, tracked migrants over Israel mostly stay under 1,000 metres, half of them below 400, and rely heavily on tailwinds to make distance.", "Pilots know the same sky from a different angle. The Eilat mountains give ridge lift in the sea breeze and the desert gives rough thermals; in migration season both are full of birds. Rules of the air put the bird first: give way, never dive at a kettle, and remember that a hundred eagles climbing in one thermal are doing it more efficiently than you."], "rel": ["na-steppe-eagle", "na-common-buzzard", "na-via-pontica", "na-rift-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Raptor migration at Eilat, British Birds", "u": "https://britishbirds.co.uk/journal/article/raptor-migration-eilat"}, {"t": "Steppe eagle, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steppe_eagle"}]}, {"id": "na-veracruz-river-of-raptors", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": 19.37, "lon": -96.37, "name": "Veracruz River of Raptors", "place": "Cardel, Veracruz, Mexico", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Four to six million raptors a season through one coastal gap, with half a million on a big day.", "body": ["In central Veracruz the Sierra Madre Oriental and the eastern end of Mexico's volcanic belt come down almost to the Gulf, leaving a narrow strip of lowland. Nearly every soaring raptor that breeds in North America and winters in Central or South America is squeezed through it each autumn. The count project run by Pronatura Veracruz with Hawk Mountain Sanctuary, from two lookouts at Cardel and Chichicaxtle, has tallied the largest concentration of migrating raptors in the world since its first full season in 1992.", "Season totals run to 4 to 6 million birds, with an average of about 4.5 million each autumn. Turkey vultures, broad-winged hawks, Swainson's hawks and Mississippi kites are the most numerous. On a typical early October day half a million raptors can stream past the lookouts, and one observer logged about 640,000 in a single day, kettling up in thermals over the coastal plain and gliding south in rivers of birds.", "The spectacle is a soaring lesson at industrial scale: thermals over sun-baked lowland, a glide line set by the coast, and an entire population choosing the route with the best lift and the shortest water. For a pilot the Veracruz coast in October is also a warning. That many birds at paraglider height means you are the hazard, not them."], "rel": ["na-panama-flyway", "na-hawk-mountain", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "The Veracruz River of Raptors success story, Hawk Mountain Sanctuary", "u": "https://hawkmountain.org/news/special-projects/the-veracruz-river-of-raptors-success-story"}, {"t": "River of Raptors, Audubon Magazine", "u": "https://www.audubon.org/magazine/river-raptors"}]}, {"id": "na-panama-flyway", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 8.96, "lon": -79.55, "name": "Panama raptor flyway", "place": "Cerro Ancón, Panama City, Panama", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Where the Americas narrow to a thread, two million raptors have passed in a single day.", "body": ["The Isthmus of Panama is the narrowest land in the Americas, and every soaring raptor migrating from North to South America by land must cross it. The same species that flood Veracruz, turkey vultures, broad-winged hawks and Swainson's hawks, arrive over Panama City a few weeks later, between October and November, and again on the way north in spring. The Audubon Society of Panama has run a count from Ancón Hill, the landmark above the Pacific entrance to the canal, since 2004.", "Soaring birds will not cross open sea if they can help it, so the isthmus concentrates them into a corridor over the canal zone. On 2 November 2014 the counters on Ancón Hill logged 2,105,060 birds in one day, mostly turkey vultures and Swainson's hawks, more than double the previous record of almost 900,000. That season's total passed three million. Columns of vultures take hours to pass, climbing in thermals over the forest and gliding down the land bridge in long lines.", "The lesson is the same as at Gibraltar, the Bosphorus and Eilat. Soaring migration is governed by thermals and by the fear of water, and wherever land narrows between two seas the birds pile up. Pilots in Panama fly mostly on the Pacific coast in the dry season, and a migrating kettle in the wet season is a sign that the rains have not killed the thermals yet."], "rel": ["na-veracruz-river-of-raptors", "na-hawk-mountain", "na-white-stork", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Panama shatters its own raptor migration record, Smithsonian Insider", "u": "https://insider.si.edu/2014/11/panama-shatters-raptor-migration-record"}, {"t": "Raptor migration watchsites of Central America, Hawk Mountain Sanctuary", "u": "https://hawkmountain.org/conservation-science/active-research/raptor-conservation-studies/conserve-the-corridor/corridor-watchsites"}]}, {"id": "na-hawk-mountain", "t": 4, "dom": "nature", "lat": 40.64, "lon": -75.99, "name": "Hawk Mountain Sanctuary", "place": "Kempton, Pennsylvania, United States", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "A woman bought a hill in 1934 to stop the shooting, and created the world's first raptor refuge.", "body": ["Hawk Mountain is a ridge on the Kittatinny, the long Appalachian rampart in eastern Pennsylvania. In northwest winds after a cold front, migrating raptors ride the ridge lift along its crest for hundreds of kilometres, and in the early twentieth century gunners lined the lookouts to shoot them by the hundred. In 1934 the conservationist Rosalie Edge leased 1,400 acres of the mountain and hired wardens, Maurice and Irma Broun, to keep the shooters out.", "She later bought the land and deeded it to the Hawk Mountain Sanctuary Association, incorporated in 1938, the first refuge in the world created for birds of prey. Its autumn counts, running continuously since the 1930s, are the longest raptor migration record anywhere, and about 20,000 hawks, eagles and falcons pass the lookouts each season.", "The mountain is also a textbook on ridge soaring. The birds use exactly the slope lift a paraglider would, hugging the windward side of the ridge in a good breeze and switching to thermals on calm days. Hawk Mountain's scientists also work on the Veracruz count, linking the Appalachian ridge to the great Mexican bottleneck."], "rel": ["na-veracruz-river-of-raptors", "na-slope-soaring-gulls", "na-escarpment", "na-golden-eagle"], "src": [{"t": "Hawk Mountain Sanctuary, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hawk_Mountain_Sanctuary"}, {"t": "Ridge lift, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge_lift"}]}, {"id": "na-parahawking", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 28.24, "lon": 83.95, "name": "What was parahawking?", "place": "Explained over Sarangkot, Pokhara, Nepal", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Trained vultures led tandem paragliders to thermals, until the Nepalese government stepped in.", "body": ["Parahawking was the practice of flying a trained bird of prey alongside a paraglider so that the bird would find thermals and guide the pilot into them, landing on a gloved hand in flight to take a scrap of meat as a reward. The word was coined by the British falconer Scott Mason, who ran it from his Himalayan Raptor Rescue centre at Pokhara in Nepal, below the Sarangkot launch.", "The birds used were rescued raptors, including Egyptian vultures, and the flights doubled as a conservation message about Asia's collapsing vulture populations. For the tandem passenger it was a close encounter with a soaring bird doing what it does best; for the pilot it was a thermal finder with far better instruments than any variometer.", "It did not last. In 2010 the Nepalese government announced the centre would close over allegations that it held endangered birds illegally and that the activity amounted to cruelty. Flights continued for some years, and in 2017 the authorities confiscated some of the birds. Whatever the verdict on parahawking, the vultures of Pokhara still mark the thermals every morning, and you can follow them for free."], "rel": ["na-egyptian-vulture", "na-vulture-diclofenac", "na-how-birds-find-thermals", "pokhara"], "src": [{"t": "Parahawking, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parahawking"}, {"t": "Egyptian vulture, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_vulture"}]}, {"id": "na-vulture-diclofenac", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 30.8, "lon": 76.92, "name": "Why did India's vultures vanish?", "place": "Explained over Pinjore, Haryana, India", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "A cattle painkiller killed 99.7 percent of one vulture species in a decade.", "body": ["Until the 1990s the skies over Indian villages were full of vultures, tens of millions of them, clearing cattle carcasses within hours. Between 1993 and 2002 the white-rumped vulture fell by 99.7 percent, and the Indian vulture and slender-billed vulture by 97.4 percent. It was the fastest decline of any bird population ever recorded.", "In 2003 Lindsay Oaks and a team from The Peregrine Fund, working in Pakistan, identified the cause: diclofenac, a cheap anti-inflammatory given to sick cattle. Vultures that fed on a treated carcass died of kidney failure within days. India banned veterinary diclofenac on 11 March 2006, Nepal and Pakistan followed the same year, and Bangladesh in 2010. Meloxicam, which is harmless to vultures, became the replacement, and by 2017 it made up about 90 percent of veterinary painkiller use in Nepal.", "The human cost was enormous. Without vultures, carcasses rotted and fed feral dogs, whose numbers rose by at least 5 million. Dog bites rose by more than 38 million and rabies deaths by more than 47,000, with an estimated economic impact of 34 billion dollars. The Jatayu conservation breeding centre at Pinjore, north of Chandigarh, now rears birds for release, and India's 2020 to 2025 Vulture Action Plan aims to keep diclofenac out of the food chain.", "Pilots in Bir Billing thermal with Himalayan griffons every day, and it is easy to assume Indian vultures are fine. Those are mountain birds that feed mostly away from treated cattle. Their lowland cousins, the white-rumped, Indian and slender-billed vultures, are the ones that disappeared, and the first captive-bred birds were only released from Pinjore in 2016. Diclofenac is still sold for human use, and vials still find their way into veterinary hands."], "rel": ["na-egyptian-vulture", "na-black-kite", "na-parahawking", "himalayan-griffon"], "src": [{"t": "Indian vulture crisis, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_vulture_crisis"}, {"t": "Oaks et al. 2004, Diclofenac residues as the cause of vulture population decline in Pakistan, Nature", "u": "https://doi.org/10.1038/nature02317"}]}, {"id": "na-wind-turbines-raptors", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.73, "lon": -121.65, "name": "Do wind turbines kill soaring birds?", "place": "Explained over Altamont Pass, California, United States", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "One Californian pass killed about 70 golden eagles a year, until the turbines were replaced.", "body": ["Wind turbines sit where the wind is, which is on ridges and passes where soaring birds also fly. Altamont Pass, east of San Francisco Bay, was one of the first big wind farms, with turbines going up from 1981 and nearly 5,000 small machines by the 2000s. A 2007 estimate put the toll at about 1,300 raptors a year, including around 70 federally protected golden eagles.", "The old Altamont turbines were small, fast-spinning and set on lattice towers at exactly the height eagles hunt ground squirrels. Repowering replaced them: NextEra agreed in 2015 to swap its 1980s machines for 48 modern ones, and one farm replaced 569 small turbines with 23 large ones in 2021. Fewer, taller, slower blades on tubular towers kill far fewer birds.", "Visibility is the other lever. At Smøla in Norway, a 68-turbine wind farm on an island full of white-tailed eagles, researchers painted a single blade of four turbines black so the rotor flickered rather than blurred. Bird deaths at those turbines fell by about 70 percent, a result published in 2020 in Ecology and Evolution.", "For a paraglider, a turbine is a hazard twice over: the blades, which at the tip of a modern machine move at well over 200 km/h, and the turbulent wake that stretches many rotor diameters downwind. Most national federations and site guides treat a wind farm as a no-fly zone. Cross well upwind or far above it, never in its lee, and remember that the wind that makes the farm worthwhile is usually too strong for you anyway."], "rel": ["na-golden-eagle", "na-escarpment", "na-slope-soaring-gulls", "na-hawk-mountain"], "src": [{"t": "Altamont Pass Wind Farm, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altamont_Pass_Wind_Farm"}, {"t": "Smøla Wind Farm, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sm%C3%B8la_Wind_Farm"}, {"t": "May et al. 2020, Paint it black: Efficacy of increased wind turbine rotor blade visibility to reduce avian fatalities, Ecology and Evolution", "u": "https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.6592"}]}, {"id": "na-escarpment", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -28.68, "lon": 28.9, "name": "What is an escarpment, and why do pilots love them?", "place": "Explained over the Drakensberg Amphitheatre, South Africa, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A thousand kilometre wall of basalt, facing the morning sun: the perfect soaring machine.", "body": ["An escarpment is a long, steep slope or cliff that separates two levels of land, a high plateau from a low plain. Some are fault scarps, where the ground on one side has dropped along a crack in the crust, as along the Rift Valley. Others, like the Drakensberg, are erosion scarps: the edge of a hard rock layer that is slowly being eaten back from the lowlands.", "The Drakensberg is the eastern rim of southern Africa's interior plateau, running more than 1,000 kilometres with its crest above 2,000 metres and a top of 3,482 metres at Thabana Ntlenyana in Lesotho. It is capped by a thick layer of basalt that erupted about 180 million years ago over sandstone laid down 20 million years earlier. The uKhahlamba Drakensberg Park was listed by UNESCO in 2000.", "For a soaring pilot an escarpment is a gift. Wind blowing from the lowland towards the plateau is forced up the whole face as ridge lift, and the sun-facing wall heats fast and sheds thermals along its entire length. You can fly for 100 kilometres along one without ever leaving the lift band.", "The catch is the top. Air that is smooth on the face tumbles over the plateau edge into rotor, and an escarpment that makes its own wind in the afternoon can trap you below the rim. Launch early, land early, and never drift over the back."], "rel": ["na-rift-valley", "na-slope-soaring-gulls", "na-hawk-mountain", "kerio-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Drakensberg, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drakensberg"}, {"t": "Maloti-Drakensberg Park, UNESCO World Heritage Centre", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/985"}, {"t": "Elgeyo Escarpment, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elgeyo_Escarpment"}]}, {"id": "na-rift-valley", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -0.91, "lon": 36.45, "name": "How did the Great Rift Valley form?", "place": "Explained over Mount Longonot, Kenya", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Africa is tearing in two at the speed your fingernails grow, and the tear is a soaring site.", "body": ["The East African Rift is a crack in a continent. About 22 to 25 million years ago, hot material rising under the crust began to stretch it, and the surface broke along parallel faults. The land between the faults dropped to form a valley floor, and the land outside stayed high as escarpments. The African plate is splitting into a Somali plate and a Nubian plate at about 8 to 9 millimetres a year, roughly the rate your fingernails grow.", "The eastern branch, the Gregory Rift, runs south from the Afar triangle through Ethiopia and down the length of Kenya into northern Tanzania. Stretching thins the crust and lets magma through, which is why the rift is lined with volcanoes: Longonot, a dormant cone beside Lake Naivasha, Kilimanjaro, and dozens more. Most of them sit on the rift shoulders rather than on the floor.", "For pilots the rift is the reason Kenya has world-class flying. The Elgeyo escarpment above the Kerio Valley is a fault scarp on the western wall, dropping from a plateau that rises to 3,350 metres in the Cherangani Hills. Hot valley floor, high cool rim and a wall facing the morning sun are the three ingredients of big, reliable thermals.", "The hazard is the same geometry. The Turkana channel in northern Kenya funnels strong easterly winds between the Ethiopian and Kenyan highlands, and every rift wall makes a rotor behind it when the wind blows across the top. The valley floor also heats fast and the afternoon can turn violent, with dust devils and strong gusts. Launch early, respect the rim, and treat any drift over the back of the escarpment as the start of an emergency."], "rel": ["na-escarpment", "na-volcanic-cone-thermals", "na-eilat-flyway", "kerio-valley"], "src": [{"t": "East African Rift, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_African_Rift"}, {"t": "Understanding plate motions, USGS", "u": "https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/understanding.html"}, {"t": "Elgeyo Escarpment, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elgeyo_Escarpment"}]}, {"id": "na-alps-formation", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.98, "lon": 7.66, "name": "How were the Alps formed?", "place": "Explained over the Matterhorn, Zermatt, Switzerland", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "An ocean closed, Africa shoved into Europe, and the wreckage is still rising 2.5 millimetres a year.", "body": ["About 180 million years ago, as the supercontinent Pangaea split, a narrow ocean opened between what would become Europe and Africa. Geologists call it the Alpine Tethys. Around 100 million years ago the Atlantic began to open and Africa swung northeast, and the Adriatic plate, a promontory of Africa, started closing that ocean. Its floor was dragged down, subducted, beneath the Adriatic plate.", "By the Paleocene and Eocene, roughly 60 to 40 million years ago, the ocean was gone and continent met continent. Slivers of crust were pushed down to about 70 kilometres and squeezed, then thrust back up and over each other in great sheets called nappes. Most of the shortening happened in the Oligocene and Miocene, when the stack of nappes was shoved north and the range took shape. The last two million years of ice ages carved it into the valleys we fly.", "The crystalline cores of the Matterhorn, Mont Blanc and the other giants are exposed where erosion has stripped the cover. Parts of the western and central Alps still rise by up to 2.5 millimetres a year, mostly rebound from the weight of ice that melted after the last glaciation, while the Adriatic plate keeps rotating and pushing up the eastern Alps.", "For a pilot all of this explains why the big valleys run the way they do: they follow the grain of the nappes, and that grain decides where the valley wind blows. The long east-west trenches of the Rhône, the Valais and the Inn are structural lines, not accidents, and the limestone pre-Alps in front of the crystalline core give the sunny, lower launches where most Alpine flying is learned."], "rel": ["na-himalaya-collision", "na-aosta-valley", "na-glacier-retreat", "na-rhone-mistral"], "src": [{"t": "Geology of the Alps, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geology_of_the_Alps"}, {"t": "Aletsch Glacier, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aletsch_Glacier"}]}, {"id": "na-himalaya-collision", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.24, "lon": 74.59, "name": "Why are the Himalaya still rising?", "place": "Explained over Nanga Parbat, Pakistan", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "India hit Asia at 18 centimetres a year and has not stopped pushing.", "body": ["Before about 55 million years ago the Indian plate was racing north across the Tethys ocean at 18 to 19.5 centimetres a year, extraordinarily fast for a continent. Then it ran into Asia. Its speed dropped to about 4.5 centimetres a year, and the sea floor and sediments caught between the two were folded, stacked and lifted into the highest mountain range on Earth. Contact in the central Himalaya may have begun as early as 65 million years ago.", "The collision has never ended. India and Eurasia still converge at about 17 millimetres a year, and the crust takes up the squeeze by thickening and rising. At Nanga Parbat, the western anchor of the range above the Indus, uplift reaches nearly 10 millimetres a year, the fastest anywhere in the belt, which is why the mountain stands 8,126 metres tall above a gorge far below.", "For paragliding the consequence is the terrain of Bir Billing, Pokhara and the Karakoram: ranges that rise in a single step from the plains, with valleys deep enough to make their own winds and summits high enough to reach the jet stream. The collision also makes the region earthquake country, and the loose, steep ground of the Himalayan foothills is the reason landing fields are small and rivers change course."], "rel": ["na-alps-formation", "na-andes-formation", "na-glacier-retreat", "bir-billing"], "src": [{"t": "Geology of the Himalayas, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geology_of_the_Himalayas"}, {"t": "The Himalayas: Two continents collide, USGS", "u": "https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/himalaya.html"}]}, {"id": "na-andes-formation", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -32.65, "lon": -70.01, "name": "How did the Andes form?", "place": "Explained over Aconcagua, Mendoza, Argentina", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Not a collision but a conveyor belt: ocean floor diving under a continent built the longest range on Earth.", "body": ["The Andes are different from the Alps and the Himalaya. No continent crashed into South America. Instead the floor of the Pacific, the Nazca plate, slides beneath the South American plate along a trench just off the coast. As it sinks it melts, feeding a chain of volcanoes, and the overriding continent is crumpled and thickened into a range about 7,000 kilometres long.", "The process began with the breakup of Pangaea in the Triassic and the range took its present form in the Cretaceous, through uplift, faulting and folding. Aconcagua, at 6,961 metres the highest point outside Asia, is an old volcanic complex lifted on the back of this crumpling. Further north the crust has thickened into the Altiplano, a plateau around 4,000 metres high where La Paz sits.", "The dry Atacama coast matters too: with almost no rain to erode the mountains, the load stayed put and deformation shifted east. For pilots the Andes mean enormous dry thermals, high launches and strong valley winds. The Sacred Valley in Peru, Iquique's coastal dunes in Chile and the Patagonian wave used by the Perlan Project are all products of the same subduction."], "rel": ["na-himalaya-collision", "na-volcanic-cone-thermals", "na-perlan-wave", "sacred-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Andes, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andes"}, {"t": "Understanding plate motions, USGS", "u": "https://pubs.usgs.gov/gip/dynamic/understanding.html"}, {"t": "Perlan Project, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perlan_Project"}]}, {"id": "na-volcanic-cone-thermals", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 28.27, "lon": -16.64, "name": "Why are volcanic cones such good thermal triggers?", "place": "Explained over Mount Teide, Tenerife, Spain, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Black rock, a lone peak and a lid of trade wind cloud: a thermal engine with a ceiling.", "body": ["A volcanic cone is an isolated, symmetrical hill of dark rock standing on a plain. Each of those features helps lift. Dark lava and ash absorb sunlight and heat faster than the surrounding land. An isolated peak is a focus: warm air drains up its slopes from every side and meets at the summit, which pins the thermal in one place. And a cone has a sun-facing slope at every hour of the day.", "Teide on Tenerife is the extreme case. It rises 3,715 metres above the sea, the highest point in Spain, and about 7,500 metres from the ocean floor, inside a caldera roughly 16 kilometres across. Its last eruption was in 1909 and the park was listed by UNESCO in 2007. The northeast trade winds pile moist cloud against the island between about 240 and 490 metres, and an inversion near 1,000 metres stops it rising further, so the upper mountain sits in dry, clear air above a white sea of cloud.", "That inversion is the ceiling. Thermals from the dark upper slopes can be strong but they die at the lid, and a pilot who glides out over the cloud sea to the coast must descend through it. The summit also makes its own lenticular cap in strong wind, which locals call Teide's hat and which means wave and rotor. Elsewhere, Longonot in Kenya and the cones of the Andes work the same way, without the sea."], "rel": ["na-rift-valley", "na-andes-formation", "na-egyptian-vulture", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Teide, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teide"}, {"t": "Teide National Park, UNESCO World Heritage Centre", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1258"}]}, {"id": "na-dune-du-pilat", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 44.59, "lon": -1.21, "name": "Dune du Pilat", "place": "Arcachon, France", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Europe's tallest sand dune is a soaring ridge that moves inland on its own.", "body": ["The Dune du Pilat on the Atlantic coast south of Bordeaux is the tallest sand dune in Europe, measured at 106.6 metres above sea level in 2018. It runs about 2.7 kilometres along the shore, is about 500 metres wide, and holds around 60 million cubic metres of sand. Onshore wind keeps pushing it slowly inland, burying the pine forest behind it a little further each decade.", "It is also one of the most famous paragliding spots in France. The sea breeze arrives straight off the Atlantic, meets the steep seaward face and is forced up it as smooth ridge lift, strongest just in front of the crest. Because there are no rocks, trees or thermals to speak of, the dune is where many pilots learn to ridge soar, and it is forgiving of the inevitable sand landings.", "The lesson a dune teaches is pure slope lift: no thermals, just wind and shape. The same rules apply as on any ridge. Stay in front of the crest, come down before the wind strengthens in the afternoon, and never let yourself be pushed over the back where the air tumbles into the trees. Landing on the beach is fine; landing in the forest is not."], "rel": ["na-sea-cliffs", "na-slope-soaring-gulls", "na-east-atlantic-flyway", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Dune of Pilat, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dune_of_Pilat"}, {"t": "Ridge lift, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge_lift"}]}, {"id": "na-sea-cliffs", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.89, "lon": -117.25, "name": "Why do sea cliffs give such smooth lift?", "place": "Explained over Torrey Pines, San Diego, United States", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "Lindbergh soared these cliffs in 1930, and the same sea breeze still runs like clockwork.", "body": ["A sea cliff facing the prevailing wind is the most dependable lift on Earth. Air coming off the ocean is smooth because water is flat and does not produce thermals, and the sea breeze that builds every sunny afternoon arrives at the cliff at a steady strength from a steady direction. The cliff turns that horizontal wind into a vertical band of ridge lift, and a glider sits in it for as long as the breeze blows.", "Torrey Pines, on the cliffs north of San Diego, has been proving this since the 1930s. On 24 February 1930 Charles Lindbergh made the first soaring flight above the cliffs in a Bowlus sailplane, from Mount Soledad to Del Mar, and in 1936 Woody Brown was the first to launch from the clifftop and land back on it, which opened the gliderport. The site is listed on the US National Register of Historic Places and is still flown daily by paragliders and hang gliders.", "Smooth is not the same as safe. The lift band is thin, the landing is on the clifftop in the wind, and a lull or a shift leaves you on the beach below with no way back up. Pilots get it wrong by flying too low behind the lip or by ignoring the tide, and by forgetting that a sea breeze strengthens through the afternoon."], "rel": ["na-slope-soaring-gulls", "na-dune-du-pilat", "na-dynamic-soaring", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Torrey Pines Gliderport, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torrey_Pines_Gliderport"}, {"t": "Ridge lift, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge_lift"}]}, {"id": "na-karst", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.66, "lon": 13.99, "name": "What is karst?", "place": "Explained over Škocjan Caves, Slovenia, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": true, "hook": "Rain plus limestone makes a landscape of towers, sinkholes and rivers that vanish underground.", "body": ["Karst is country made of limestone or dolomite that rainwater has dissolved. Rain picks up carbon dioxide from the air and soil to become weak carbonic acid, and over millions of years that acid eats along cracks in the rock, widening them into sinkholes, dry valleys, cave systems and underground rivers. The surface is often bare, stony and dry even in wet climates because the water goes straight down.", "The word comes from the Kras plateau behind Trieste, on the border of Slovenia and Italy, where Johann Weikhard von Valvasor first described underground river flows to European science in 1689. At Škocjan the river Reka drops into a collapsed sinkhole, runs 34 kilometres underground and resurfaces near the Adriatic. Its underground gorge is 3.5 kilometres long and more than 140 metres high, and Martel's Chamber, at 2.2 million cubic metres, is the largest known cave chamber in Europe. UNESCO listed the caves in 1986.", "For pilots karst means two things. Mature karst makes dramatic soaring terrain, the limestone towers of southern China and the grey walls of the Dinaric Alps and the Picos de Europa, usually facing the sun and shedding strong thermals from bare rock. It also means almost no safe landing: fields are small, stony and ringed with sinkholes, so pick your outlanding before you need it."], "rel": ["na-alps-formation", "na-escarpment", "na-glacier-retreat", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Karst, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karst"}, {"t": "Škocjan Caves, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C5%A0kocjan_Caves"}]}, {"id": "na-glacier-retreat", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.4, "lon": 8.08, "name": "What does glacier retreat mean for flying sites?", "place": "Explained over the Aletsch Glacier, Fiesch, Switzerland, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "The Alps' biggest glacier has lost 3.2 kilometres since 1870, and the air above it is changing with it.", "body": ["The Aletsch is the largest glacier in the Alps, a river of ice 22.6 kilometres long as measured in 2014 that bends down from the Jungfrau towards the Rhône valley. It has retreated 3.2 kilometres since 1870 and thinned by more than 300 metres. Models suggest that by 2100 it may hold only a tenth of its 2018 ice mass. The Jungfrau-Aletsch region was listed by UNESCO in 2001.", "Pilots launching from Fiesch and the Bettmeralp balconies fly over this ice, and the ice shapes the air. A glacier is a cold floor: air above it is stable and sinking, and in the afternoon a cold glacier wind flows down its surface into the valley. As the ice thins and the bare rock and moraine around it spread, those areas heat up and start producing thermals where there used to be none, while the glacier wind weakens.", "Retreat also changes the ground. Moraines slump, new lakes form behind them, rock faces that the ice held together begin to collapse, and landing fields near a glacier snout can be cut by new streams. Treat a glacier valley as unstable terrain: check the landing from the air, expect sink and cold downslope wind over the ice, and expect the thermals to be found on the rock, not the white."], "rel": ["na-alps-formation", "na-aosta-valley", "na-himalaya-collision", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Aletsch Glacier, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aletsch_Glacier"}, {"t": "Geology of the Alps, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geology_of_the_Alps"}]}, {"id": "na-rhone-mistral", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 44.93, "lon": 4.89, "name": "Why is the Rhône Valley a wind channel?", "place": "Explained over Valence, France", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The Mistral blows down one valley at 50 km/h for days, and it is not a simple funnel.", "body": ["The Rhône runs almost straight south from Lyon to the Mediterranean between the Alps on the east and the Cévennes and Massif Central on the west. When cold air builds over France behind a cold front and low pressure sits over the Gulf of Genoa, that air pours south down the valley as the Mistral. Daytime averages are about 50 km/h, easing at night, with gusts past 90 km/h in the valley and sometimes 100 km/h at the coast.", "The usual explanation is a Venturi effect: the valley narrows near Valence and the wind accelerates through the gap. The real picture is more complex. The Mistral is strongest well south of the narrows, where the valley has opened out onto the plain of La Crau and the Rhône delta, and the wind reaches up to about 3 kilometres into the atmosphere. Cold air draining down from the mountains adds to it, and unlike a foehn it does not warm much on the way down.", "It is most frequent in winter and spring, lasts a day or two at least and sometimes more than a week; spells above 30 km/h for over 65 hours have been recorded. For pilots in Provence and the southern Alps the Mistral is a stand-down. The sky is brilliantly clear and the temptation is real, but 50 km/h on the ground is beyond any paraglider, and the lee of every hill is rotor."], "rel": ["na-aosta-valley", "na-alps-formation", "foehn", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Mistral (wind), Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mistral_(wind)"}, {"t": "Geology of the Alps, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geology_of_the_Alps"}]}, {"id": "na-aosta-valley", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 45.74, "lon": 7.32, "name": "Why does the Aosta Valley breathe?", "place": "Explained over Aosta, Italy", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Ringed by Mont Blanc, Monte Rosa, the Matterhorn and Gran Paradiso, this valley pumps air uphill every afternoon.", "body": ["The Aosta Valley is a deep trench in the north-western corner of Italy, walled by four of the highest massifs in the Alps: Mont Blanc at 4,810 metres to the west, Monte Rosa and the Matterhorn to the north, Gran Paradiso to the south. The river Dora Baltea drains it eastwards into the Po plain, and side valleys run up to the glaciers from both flanks. The high country, mostly above 1,600 metres, is cold and continental, with snow lying eight or nine months on the peaks.", "That shape makes a textbook valley wind system. By day the sunlit slopes heat the air, which rises up the sides as slope breeze, and the whole valley draws replacement air in from the plain: a valley breeze blowing up the trench and up every side valley towards the glaciers. At night it reverses, with cold air draining down the ice and the slopes as a mountain breeze. The valley breathes in by day and out by night.", "For a pilot the valley breeze is both engine and trap. It strengthens through the afternoon and can reach speeds a paraglider cannot penetrate, so a cross-country flight along the valley is usually flown downwind with the breeze. Pilots get caught by flying up a side valley late in the day and finding the wind at the head of it stronger than at the mouth. Fly early, know where the breeze meets the glacier wind, and pick a landing before the valley starts to blow."], "rel": ["na-alps-formation", "na-glacier-retreat", "na-rhone-mistral", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Aosta Valley, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aosta_Valley"}, {"t": "Geology of the Alps, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geology_of_the_Alps"}]}, {"id": "na-owens-valley-rotor", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 37.36, "lon": -118.4, "name": "Owens Valley rotor and wave", "place": "Bishop, California, United States", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The deepest valley in the United States, famous for both record flights and the most violent rotor in gliding.", "body": ["The Owens Valley lies between the Sierra Nevada and the White Mountains in eastern California. Its floor sits at about 1,200 to 1,300 metres while the Sierra crest to the west rises above 4,300 metres, which makes it the deepest valley in the United States. The Sierra casts it in a rain shadow, the Land of Little Rain, so the ground is dry, bare and hot: a thermal factory in summer and a wave machine in spring.", "When a strong westerly crosses the Sierra crest and the atmosphere is stable, the air plunges down the eastern face and bounces back up in a series of standing lee waves that can reach the stratosphere. Beneath the first wave crest a rotor forms, a horizontal vortex as wide as the valley with its axis along the range, often marked by a ragged roll cloud. Rotor turbulence here has torn gliders apart, and in 2006 the Terrain-induced Rotor Experiment brought research aircraft to the valley to measure it.", "Pilots at Bishop and Lone Pine fly the valley's thermals on calm summer days and watch the sky for the signs of wave: lenticular clouds standing still over the peaks and a roll cloud in the valley. When those appear the paragliders stay on the ground. Wave lift is a sailplane game; the rotor beneath it is nobody's."], "rel": ["na-sierra-wave", "na-perlan-wave", "na-andes-formation", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Owens Valley, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Owens_Valley"}, {"t": "Grubišić et al. 2008, The Terrain-Induced Rotor Experiment, Bulletin of the American Meteorological Society", "u": "https://doi.org/10.1175/2008BAMS2487.1"}, {"t": "Lee wave, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_wave"}]}, {"id": "na-sierra-wave", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 36.58, "lon": -118.29, "name": "What is the Sierra Wave?", "place": "Explained over Mount Whitney, Lone Pine, United States", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "Standing waves off the Sierra Nevada took a glider to 14,102 metres in 1961 without an engine.", "body": ["A lee wave forms when stable air is pushed over a mountain range and overshoots. Buoyancy pulls each parcel back down past its level, it overshoots again, and the air oscillates up and down for tens of kilometres downwind, like water over a weir. The waves stand still relative to the mountain while the wind blows through them. Where the air rises it cools and can condense into smooth lens-shaped lenticular clouds; where it sinks the cloud evaporates.", "The Sierra Nevada is one of the great wave generators on Earth because it is a single steep wall 4,000 metres high facing the prevailing westerlies. The Sierra Wave was first studied systematically around 1950 from the Owens Valley, and on 25 February 1961 Paul Bikle rode wave lift in a Schweizer 1-23E sailplane to 14,102 metres near Lancaster, a record that stood for decades. Later altitude and distance records were set in the lee of the Sierra, the Alps, the Patagonian Andes and New Zealand's Southern Alps.", "A paraglider has no business in wave. The lift is above the rotor, the wind at wave height is far beyond a wing's speed, and the air is below freezing and short of oxygen. The practical use of wave knowledge for a paraglider pilot is recognition: lenticular clouds stacked over a ridge mean strong wind aloft and rotor below, and that is a day to walk."], "rel": ["na-owens-valley-rotor", "na-perlan-wave", "na-volcanic-cone-thermals", "foehn"], "src": [{"t": "Lee wave, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_wave"}, {"t": "Paul Bikle, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Bikle"}, {"t": "Sierra Wave, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierra_Wave"}]}, {"id": "na-perlan-wave", "t": 4, "dom": "nature", "lat": -50.34, "lon": -72.26, "name": "Perlan stratospheric wave", "place": "El Calafate, Patagonia, Argentina", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": true, "hook": "A glider with no engine reached 22,657 metres over Patagonia, higher than a U-2 spy plane cruises.", "body": ["The Perlan Project set out to prove that mountain waves do not stop at the tropopause. In the far south, when the polar vortex strengthens in late winter, lee waves off the Andes couple with stratospheric winds and keep propagating upwards. On 29 August 2006 Steve Fossett and Einar Enevoldson rode such a wave from El Calafate in a modified sailplane to 15,460 metres, the first glider flight into the stratosphere.", "The purpose-built Perlan 2, a pressurised glider with a 25 metre wing, followed. On 2 September 2018 Jim Payne and Tim Gardner took it to 22,657 metres above El Calafate, the highest flight ever by a winged aircraft in level, sustained flight without an engine, above the service ceiling of most jets. The air up there is about two percent of sea-level density, so the glider's true airspeed was several hundred km/h while it crawled through the thin air.", "The project is not a stunt. The same waves carry energy and moisture into the stratosphere and affect ozone and climate models, and the Patagonian Andes are one of the few places on Earth where they can be sampled by a quiet, unpowered aircraft. It is also the Andes subduction story taken to its limit: a wall of mountains, a westerly gale, and air that keeps bouncing all the way to the edge of space."], "rel": ["na-sierra-wave", "na-owens-valley-rotor", "na-andes-formation", "sacred-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Perlan Project, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perlan_Project"}, {"t": "Lee wave, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lee_wave"}]}, {"id": "ru-fai", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.52, "lon": 6.63, "name": "What is the FAI?", "place": "Explained over Lausanne, Switzerland", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "The body that ratifies every paragliding world record was founded before most people had seen an aeroplane.", "body": ["The Fédération Aéronautique Internationale, the FAI, is the world governing body for air sports. It was founded in Paris on 14 October 1905, two years after the Wright brothers first flew, and is now based in Lausanne, Switzerland, the city that also hosts the International Olympic Committee. More than 100 national aero clubs and federations are members, and the IOC has recognised the FAI as an Olympic federation since 1986.", "For a paraglider pilot the FAI matters in three ways. It ratifies world records, so a 600 km flight in Texas only becomes a record once FAI observers have checked the tracklog. It sanctions world and continental championships. And it sets the common standards, such as the IPPI card, that let a licence from one country be understood in another.", "The FAI does not run the sport day to day. It works through commissions for each discipline, and the hang gliding and paragliding commission, CIVL, writes the rules that national federations and competition organisers follow. Your own national federation, whether that is the BHPA, USHPA, DHV or FFVL, is the FAI member that represents you."], "rel": ["ru-civl", "ru-world-champs", "ru-xc-explained", "k-ippi"], "src": [{"t": "Fédération Aéronautique Internationale, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F%C3%A9d%C3%A9ration_A%C3%A9ronautique_Internationale"}, {"t": "About FAI, letter from the Secretary General, 2020", "u": "https://fai.org/sites/default/files/about-fai_to-whom-it-may-concern_2020_secgen.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-civl", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.67, "lon": 12.4, "name": "What is CIVL?", "place": "Explained over Kössen, Austria", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "A committee of volunteer pilots decides how every paragliding world championship is scored.", "body": ["CIVL is the FAI's Commission Internationale de Vol Libre, the hang gliding and paragliding commission. Each FAI member country sends a delegate, and the commission meets in a plenary each year to vote on rules. It is the body that decides which events count as world championships, how tasks are scored, which glider classes are allowed and what a record claim must contain.", "Kössen in the Tyrol is where CIVL's paragliding story began. The first officially sanctioned FAI World Paragliding Championship was flown here in 1989, two years after an unofficial contest in Verbier. Since then the championships have run every two years, with 19 editions by 2025, and CIVL has added continental championships, accuracy and acro titles.", "CIVL also owns two things that touch ordinary pilots. The IPPI card and its Safe Pro Para levels 1 to 5 are a CIVL scheme, used to compare national ratings when you fly abroad. And the record categories, from straight distance to gain of height, are CIVL definitions, so a national record and a world record are measured the same way."], "rel": ["ru-fai", "ru-world-champs", "ru-comp-task", "k-ippi"], "src": [{"t": "FAI Hang Gliding and Paragliding Commission (CIVL)", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/24489"}, {"t": "World Paragliding Championships, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Paragliding_Championships"}]}, {"id": "ru-ratings-compared", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.75, "lon": 11.74, "name": "How do pilot ratings compare between countries?", "place": "Explained over Gmund am Tegernsee, Germany", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A British Club Pilot, an American P2 and a German A-Schein holder are the same pilot with different paperwork.", "body": ["Every federation splits training into a stage where you can fly alone near a school site, and a stage where you are trusted to fly anywhere and go cross country. In Britain the BHPA issues the Elementary Pilot award, then Club Pilot, which ends formal instruction, then Pilot, which the BHPA says you should hold before flying cross country, and finally Advanced Pilot.", "In the United States USHPA uses P1 to P5: Beginner, Novice, Intermediate, Advanced and Master. P2 lets you fly without an instructor within set limits, P3 adds weather, micrometeorology and airspace knowledge, and P4 pilots can act as site monitors and apply for instructor ratings. Germany's DHV, based at Gmund am Tegernsee, issues the A-Schein after at least 25 supervised height flights and 55 launches in total, and the B-Schein after proving a 10 km cross country with 400 m of thermal climb.", "France's FFVL runs the Brevet Initial, the Brevet de Pilote with a 60 question theory exam, and the Brevet de Pilote Confirmé, which is the gateway to tandem and instructor training. To translate between them, federations use the FAI's IPPI card: Club Pilot is Safe Pro Para level 4, and Pilot or Advanced Pilot is level 5. Switzerland and Germany both ask visiting pilots for IPPI 4 or 5, and Germany does not allow cross country on IPPI 4."], "rel": ["k-ippi", "ru-fai", "ru-flying-abroad", "ru-instructor"], "src": [{"t": "BHPA Pilot Rating Scheme", "u": "https://www.bhpa.co.uk/safety/prs"}, {"t": "USHPA Ratings and Skills", "u": "https://ushpa.org/USHPA/Pilot_Resources/Public/PilotResources/ratings-and-skills.aspx"}, {"t": "DHV, Zusammenfassung Änderung APO 2019", "u": "https://service.dhv.de/dbfiles/aus/Arbeitsordner%20Seli/Zusammenfassung_nderung_APO_2019.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-airspace-classes", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.1, "lon": 7.23, "name": "What do airspace classes A to G mean for a paraglider?", "place": "Explained over Verbier, Switzerland", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "The letters on an airspace map say whether you may climb there, and you need only remember three.", "body": ["ICAO divides the sky into classes A to G. Class A is for instrument flights only and is closed to us. Classes B, C and D are controlled: you need a clearance from air traffic control to enter, which a paraglider without a radio and a transponder will almost never get. Class E is controlled for airliners but open to visual flights without a clearance. Classes F and G are uncontrolled, and G is where most free flying happens.", "The same letter can mean different things in different countries. In Britain class C sits above flight level 195, around 5,900 m, so it rarely troubles a paraglider, while a class D control zone around a regional airport can start at the ground. Switzerland and Germany use class E widely, which is why Alpine pilots can climb to cloudbase over Verbier but must still watch the ceiling where class C begins.", "The practical rules are simple. Load the current airspace file into your instrument and set a warning. Know the floor and ceiling of every piece of controlled airspace within gliding range before you launch. And treat an infringement as a serious safety event, not a paperwork problem: it is how sites get closed and how an airliner ends up sharing a thermal with you."], "rel": ["ru-ctr-tma", "ru-openair", "ru-notam", "ru-near-airports"], "src": [{"t": "UK CAA, Controlled Airspace tutorial", "u": "https://infringements.caa.co.uk/tutorial/controlled-airspace/"}, {"t": "British Gliding Association, What is airspace?", "u": "https://members.gliding.co.uk/?p=58300"}]}, {"id": "ru-ctr-tma", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.23, "lon": 6.11, "name": "What are a CTR and a TMA?", "place": "Explained over Geneva, Switzerland", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "One starts at the grass under your feet, the other is a lid several hundred metres above it.", "body": ["A CTR, or control zone, is the controlled airspace wrapped around an airport. ICAO Annex 11 defines it as extending upwards from the surface of the earth to a specified upper limit, and it reaches at least 5 nautical miles, about 9 km, from the aerodrome in the directions aircraft approach from. Because it starts at the ground, you cannot fly under it. A paraglider inside a CTR without a clearance is an infringement from the moment of launch.", "A TMA, or terminal control area, sits above and around one or more busy airports where arriving and departing traffic is funnelled together. Its lower limit is a defined level above the ground, not the ground itself, so you can fly beneath it as long as you stay under the floor. In Britain the London, Manchester and Scottish TMAs are often class A; a control area that overlies a CTR typically starts around 1,500 ft, roughly 450 m.", "Geneva is the textbook case for Alpine pilots. Its airport airspace reaches out across the border, and the floors step down toward the city, so a cross country pilot heading west from the Savoie mountains needs to know the exact altitude at which the lid begins over each ridge. Instruments show it, but only if the airspace file is current."], "rel": ["ru-airspace-classes", "ru-near-airports", "ru-openair", "annecy"], "src": [{"t": "Skybrary, Control Zone (CTR)", "u": "https://www.skybrary.aero/articles/control-zone-ctr"}, {"t": "UK CAA, Controlled Airspace tutorial", "u": "https://infringements.caa.co.uk/tutorial/controlled-airspace/"}]}, {"id": "ru-notam", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 43.72, "lon": 6.98, "name": "What is a NOTAM?", "place": "Explained over Gourdon, France", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "Your airspace file is weeks old; the jets rehearsing over the ridge today were announced yesterday.", "body": ["A NOTAM is a Notice to Airmen, a short official message about a temporary or short notice change that affects flying. Air displays, military exercises, parachuting, temporary restricted areas around a royal or presidential flight, a crane near a runway or a danger area switched on for the day all arrive as NOTAMs. They are written in a fixed ICAO format that looks like code until you learn the fields.", "The letters matter. Field A is the location, B the start time and C the end time, both normally in UTC, so a 0600 start is 8 am in a French summer. Field E is the plain text description, and F and G are the lower and upper limits, for example SFC to 5000 ft AMSL within a 5 nautical mile radius. If your planned flight passes through that circle during those hours, it is closed to you.", "National aeronautical information services publish NOTAMs, and most flight planning apps and some paragliding instruments can filter them by area and date. Check them on the morning you fly, not the week before, because they change. Club site rules in Britain tell pilots to check NOTAMs before every flight, and a NOTAM you did not read is no defence after an infringement."], "rel": ["ru-airspace-classes", "ru-openair", "ru-near-airports", "ru-clubs-site-guides"], "src": [{"t": "Learn ATC, How to read NOTAMs", "u": "https://www.learn-atc.com/blog/how-to-read-notams"}, {"t": "Derbyshire Soaring Club, Pilot information", "u": "https://derbyshiresoaringclub.org.uk/pilot-information/"}]}, {"id": "ru-openair", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.75, "lon": 13.18, "name": "How do I read an OpenAir airspace file?", "place": "Explained over Greifenburg, Austria", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "The airspace on your vario is a plain text file, and every line starts with two letters.", "body": ["OpenAir is the text format almost every free flight instrument uses to draw airspace. It was created by the WinPilot software in 1998 and later extended by Naviter, the makers of SeeYou. A file is a list of airspace blocks, and each block is a handful of short lines that a human can read. Open one and you will see why pilots are able to fix errors themselves.", "Each block begins with AC, the class, such as AC D or AC R for a restricted area. AN gives the name. AH is the ceiling and AL the floor, written like AH FL100, AL 2500 ft AMSL or AL GND. Then come the shape lines: DP adds a polygon corner in latitude and longitude, V X= sets a centre, and DC 2.00 draws a circle of that radius in nautical miles. DA and DB draw arcs, and a line starting with an asterisk is a comment.", "Three habits keep you safe. Download a fresh file each season from your federation or a maintained source, because boundaries and floors change. Check the units your instrument assumes, since a floor given in feet above ground is not the same as feet above sea level. And when the file and the official chart disagree, the chart wins."], "rel": ["ru-airspace-classes", "ru-ctr-tma", "ru-notam", "ru-flarm"], "src": [{"t": "Naviter, OpenAir File Format Support", "u": "https://github.com/naviter/seeyou_file_formats/blob/main/OpenAir_File_Format_Support.md"}]}, {"id": "ru-flarm", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.2, "lon": 8.53, "name": "What is FLARM?", "place": "Explained over Baar, Switzerland", "icon": "plane", "hidden": true, "hook": "A small Swiss box tells sailplanes where you are before their pilot can see a paraglider against the hillside.", "body": ["FLARM is a collision warning system built for gliders. The company behind it was founded in Switzerland in 2003 by Urs Rothacher and Andrea Schlapbach, with first sales in early 2004, and it is still based in Baar near Zurich. Each unit takes its position from GPS, broadcasts it on a short range radio link, 868 MHz in Europe, together with its predicted track, and listens for other units. When two tracks are about to cross, both pilots get a warning.", "Tens of thousands of units are in use, mostly in sailplanes, tow planes and helicopters working in the lower Alpine airspace, and newer PowerFLARM models reach over 10 km. France has required it in gliders. The protocol is encrypted and licensed, so only approved manufacturers can build compatible devices.", "For paraglider pilots FLARM is mainly about being seen. Some modern free flight instruments now include a FLARM transmitter, so a sailplane at 150 km/h sees you on its display long before its pilot can pick a wing out against the rock. It does not replace looking out: a transmitter only helps if the other aircraft is also carrying one, and plenty are not."], "rel": ["ru-near-airports", "ru-airspace-classes", "ru-openair", "ru-thermal-etiquette"], "src": [{"t": "FLARM, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FLARM"}, {"t": "FLARM Technology, Who we are", "u": "https://www.flarm.com/en/about-us/"}]}, {"id": "ru-near-airports", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -45.03, "lon": 168.66, "name": "Can I fly a paraglider near an airport?", "place": "Explained over Queenstown, New Zealand", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "Airliners land a few kilometres from Queenstown's busiest tandem launch, and it works because of lines on a map.", "body": ["Usually not without an arrangement, because an airport sits inside a control zone that starts at the ground. But many famous sites are inside or beside airport airspace, and they work through agreements between the local club and air traffic control. Queenstown is the clearest example. The control zone contains four general aviation areas that become uncontrolled class G airspace during daylight, each with its own ceiling.", "The Coronet Peak area, G755, runs from the surface to 5,500 ft, about 1,680 m, and is active every day in daylight. Skyline above the town, G756, goes to 4,500 ft but may only be entered with specific approval from ATC, which is why visiting pilots need accreditation from a local instructor before flying it. The CAA guide warns aircraft that paragliders are unlikely to make radio calls, so pilots must stay inside their box and never cross the boundary road without a clearance.", "The lesson travels. Find out whether a site has a letter of agreement, what its ceiling is and whether it needs a phone call or a radio call before launch. Follow it exactly, because one visiting group straying outside the area is enough to lose the arrangement for everyone."], "rel": ["ru-ctr-tma", "ru-airspace-classes", "ru-notam", "ru-clubs-site-guides"], "src": [{"t": "CAA New Zealand, In, Out and Around Queenstown", "u": "https://www.aviation.govt.nz/assets/publications/gaps/In_Out_and_Around_Queenstown.pdf"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Queenstown travel guide", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-queenstown-new-zealand-3/"}]}, {"id": "ru-xc-explained", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 29.36, "lon": -100.9, "name": "What is cross country flying?", "place": "Explained over Del Rio, Texas, United States", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Leave the hill, link thermal to thermal, and the record now stands above 600 km in one day.", "body": ["Cross country, or XC, means leaving the home site and flying across the landscape by climbing in thermals and gliding downwind or along a planned route to the next one. The paraglider has no engine, so every kilometre is bought with height. On a good day the climbs go to cloudbase, and the pilot spends each glide searching for the next column of rising air.", "The distances have grown with the wings. On 20 June 2021 Sebastien Kayrouz flew 609.9 km from Del Rio to Claude in Texas, the current FAI free distance record, on an Ozone Enzo 3. The women's straight distance record is 531.7 km, set by Yael Margelisch from Caicó in Brazil in 2019. These flights last ten hours or more and depend on flat land, strong sun and a steady wind.", "For most pilots XC starts with a 10 km flight to the next village. Before the first one, learn the airspace, carry a tracker, tell someone your route and plan the retrieve. The BHPA expects pilots to hold the Pilot rating before going cross country, and the usual mistakes are flying low over unlandable ground and chasing distance into airspace or a deteriorating sky."], "rel": ["ru-xc-leagues", "k-thermal", "k-cloudbase", "ru-airspace-classes"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia (FAI records table)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}, {"t": "FAI, Rafael Saladini: 530 km to a declared goal", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/186984"}]}, {"id": "ru-xc-leagues", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 50.41, "lon": 13.78, "name": "What are XContest, Leonardo and the XC League?", "place": "Explained over Raná, Czech Republic", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Upload a tracklog from your phone and a Czech server scores your flight against every pilot on Earth.", "body": ["An XC league is an online contest where pilots upload their GPS tracklogs and the server works out the best distance and awards points. XContest, run from the Czech Republic, is the biggest. Its world contest scores free distance at 1 point per km, a free triangle at 1.2 and an FAI triangle at 1.4, counts your best six flights, and runs a season from 1 October to 30 September. It also hosts national and club contests for dozens of countries.", "Leonardo is an older open flight database hosted on the Paragliding Forum. Clubs and some national bodies, Ireland among them, use it as their league engine and logbook, and pilots can upload straight from apps such as XCSoar or FlySkyHy. Britain runs its own XC League on a separate site, and BHPA competition rules require a Pilot or Advanced Pilot rating, or IPPI level 4 or 5, to score in national events.", "Leagues are the best free coaching in the sport. Open any flight and you can replay the track, see where the pilot climbed and where they ran out of height, and compare it with your own. They also create pressure: the usual error is pushing on for points when the day, the terrain or the airspace is telling you to land."], "rel": ["ru-xc-explained", "ru-comp-task", "ru-community-online", "ru-flying-abroad"], "src": [{"t": "XContest World, Rules", "u": "https://www.xcontest.org/world/en/rules/"}, {"t": "IHPA, What is Leonardo?", "u": "https://ihpa.ie/index.php/competitions/how-to-use-and-manage-the-comps-leagues"}, {"t": "BHPA, British XC Paragliding Competition Rules 2026", "u": "https://pgcomps.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/British-XC-Paragliding-Competition-Rules-v2026.01.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-pwc", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -19.52, "lon": -41.02, "name": "What is the Paragliding World Cup?", "place": "Explained over Baixo Guandu, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The pilots own the series: they founded it in Verbier in 1991 so the best could race without quotas.", "body": ["The Paragliding World Cup is the professional racing circuit of the sport. It is run by the Paragliding World Cup Association, a non-profit founded on 9 August 1991 in Verbier when the first pilots' committee was elected. The idea was a series where pilots qualify on their own results rather than through limited national team places. The first season was 1992, won by Uli Wiesmeier of Germany.", "Each year the tour visits five or six venues on different continents, and at each one the pilots fly a race task a day for about a week. Results across the season decide who is invited to the Superfinal, a single event that crowns the overall champion. The association is based in Marlens near Annecy and had 384 pilot members in October 2026. Honorin Hamard won the overall title in 2022 and 2023, Maxime Pinot in 2024 and Andy Tallia in 2025.", "Baixo Guandu in Espírito Santo is a regular stop and hosted a World Cup round in October 2026, as well as the Superfinals of 2018 and 2023. Watching a World Cup task on live tracking is the fastest way to learn how the best pilots choose lines, and the gaggle flying it demands is a skill in itself."], "rel": ["ru-superfinal", "ru-comp-task", "ru-world-champs", "ru-xc-explained"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding World Cup Association, About us", "u": "http://pwca.org/about-us"}, {"t": "Paragliding World Cup, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding_World_Cup"}, {"t": "PWCA events", "u": "https://pwca.org/events"}]}, {"id": "ru-world-champs", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 45.55, "lon": 6.17, "name": "FAI Paragliding World Championships", "place": "Chamoux-sur-Gelon, France", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Every two years national teams race for a week, and France has won most of the recent titles.", "body": ["The World Championships are the FAI's official title event, held every two years since the first sanctioned edition at Kössen, Austria, in 1989. Unlike the World Cup, pilots fly for national teams selected by their federations, and there is a team result as well as individual men's and women's titles. By 2025 there had been 19 editions on four continents, from Kitakyushu in Japan to Manilla in Australia and Roldanillo in Colombia.", "The 2023 championships were flown from Chamoux-sur-Gelon in the Savoie Alps, where Maxime Pinot and Meryl Delferriere took the titles for France. In 2025 at Castelo in Brazil, Baptiste Lambert and Constance Mettetal kept both titles French. Earlier champions include Robbie Whittall of Britain in 1991, Judy Leden in 1995 and Bruce Goldsmith in 2007, and Switzerland's Chrigel Maurer never won one despite dominating the X-Alps.", "Championships are flown under CIVL rules, with a daily task scored to 1,000 points. The 2011 edition in Piedrahita was the one that pushed CIVL to rethink competition wing safety after accidents on uncertified open class gliders, and championships have been flown on certified wings since."], "rel": ["ru-civl", "ru-pwc", "ru-comp-task", "ru-women"], "src": [{"t": "World Paragliding Championships, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Paragliding_Championships"}, {"t": "FAI CIVL", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/24489"}]}, {"id": "ru-superfinal", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -18.85, "lon": -41.95, "name": "What is the PWC Superfinal?", "place": "Explained over Governador Valadares, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "One week, one venue, the season's best pilots, and the world number one decided on the last task.", "body": ["Until 2008 the World Cup champion was the pilot with the best total across the season's events. In 2009 the association replaced that with a Superfinal, first flown at Poggio Bustone in Italy. The pilots who score best at the year's selective events are invited to one closing competition, and whoever wins that week is the overall champion. It gives the season a final that can turn on a single task.", "Brazil has hosted it more than anywhere. Governador Valadares in Minas Gerais, with its Ibituruna launch above the Rio Doce, staged the Superfinals of 2013, won by Francisco Javier Reina, and 2016, won by Aaron Durogati. Baixo Guandu followed in 2018 and 2023. The 2019 Superfinal at Castelo and the whole 2020 season were cancelled by the pandemic. The 2025 season's Superfinal was flown at Pegalajar in Spain in May 2026 and won by Andy Tallia and Constance Mettetal.", "Because the field is the strongest of the year, Superfinal tasks are long, fast and flown on the highest performance certified wings. Organisers set a minimum safety standard for venues, and a task can still be stopped mid-air if the weather turns, which is one reason live tracking is now standard."], "rel": ["ru-pwc", "ru-comp-task", "ru-world-champs", "ru-xc-explained"], "src": [{"t": "Paragliding World Cup, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding_World_Cup"}, {"t": "Cross Country, PWC Super Final kicks off in Baixo Guandu", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/comps-and-events/pwc-super-final-kicks-off-in-baixo-guandu/"}]}, {"id": "ru-comp-task", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.46, "lon": -5.33, "name": "How does a competition task work?", "place": "Explained over Piedrahita, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "It is a sailing race around invisible buoys, except the buoys are cylinders in the sky.", "body": ["A competition is a series of daily tasks, usually one a day, with scores added up over the week. At the morning briefing the task setter puts up a route: a start cylinder, a sequence of GPS turnpoints, each a cylinder you must enter, and a goal. Total distance is measured through the cylinder centres, while the optimised distance clips the edge of each one, and pilots programme the task into their instruments and check it against the board.", "The launch window opens for about two hours. Pilots climb and wait near the start, then at the race start time everyone crosses the start cylinder together. Timing runs from the start to the End of Speed Section, usually a turnpoint a kilometre or so before goal, which stops people racing low over the last field. You still have to cross the goal line to earn your time points.", "The first pilot into goal scores up to 1,000 points. Others get time points if they make goal, or distance points for how far round the course they got. Leading points reward pilots who push out in front and find the thermals, because that is where the risk is. If few pilots make goal the task is devalued. Piedrahita, host of the 2011 Worlds, still hosts open competitions on this format."], "rel": ["ru-pwc", "ru-world-champs", "ru-superfinal", "ru-thermal-etiquette"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Competition briefing: understanding competition tasks", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/comp-series-understanding-competition-tasks/"}, {"t": "World Paragliding Championships, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Paragliding_Championships"}]}, {"id": "ru-acro-world-tour", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 46.4, "lon": 6.93, "name": "Acro World Tour", "place": "Villeneuve, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Ten pilots, a lake, a tow boat and a judging panel: the Sonchaux Acroshow is the sport's Wimbledon.", "body": ["The Acro World Tour is the top competition series for aerobatic paragliding. It is sanctioned by the FAI as a category 2 event and is run by its own organisation rather than by CIVL. Pilots fly over water, usually a lake, and perform runs of manoeuvres in front of judges who score technical difficulty, execution and choreography. Height is gained by tow or by launching from a mountain above the lake.", "Villeneuve, at the eastern end of Lake Geneva below the Sonchaux launch, has hosted the finals. In 2018 the tour ran qualifying rounds at Voss in Norway, Molveno and Trasaghis in Italy before the Sonchaux Acroshow decided the title. Today the format is a closed tour for the top ten pilots, fed by open Acro World Qualifier events; the best three qualifiers earn a place for the following year.", "In 2025 the tour was flown over five events with a Superfinal at Ölüdeniz in Turkey. Théo de Blic of France won the men's title and Maud Perrin the women's, and de Blic also holds three synchro titles. Acro competition is where the infinity tumble, the helicopter and their combinations are judged, and where new manoeuvres first appear."], "rel": ["ru-acro-manoeuvres", "oludeniz", "ru-world-champs", "ru-camera-drones"], "src": [{"t": "FAI, The Acro World Tour 2018 kicks off in Norway", "u": "https://www.fai.org/node/179567"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Théo de Blic wins Acro World Tour 2025", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/comps-and-events/theo-de-blic-wins-acro-world-tour-2025/"}]}, {"id": "ru-acro-manoeuvres", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.16, "lon": -3.53, "name": "What are the SAT, the helicopter and the infinity tumble?", "place": "Explained over Cenes de la Vega, Granada, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Three manoeuvres invented by one Spanish pilot turned paragliding into an aerobatic sport.", "body": ["The SAT is a spiral in which the centre of rotation sits between the wing and the pilot, so the glider flies forwards while the pilot swings backwards around it. Raul Rodriguez discovered it in Argentina in 1999 and named it after his Safety Acro Team, formed the year before. It sinks slowly, around 2 to 6 m/s depending on the wing, and became the root of a family of moves: asymmetric SAT, rhythmic SAT and tumbling.", "The helicopter is a controlled flat spin. One side of the wing is stalled and held with the brake while the other keeps flying, so the canopy rotates above the pilot like a rotor while sinking almost vertically. Badly entered, it becomes a spin with riser twists. The infinity tumble came later: Rodriguez entered a rhythmic SAT high over Cenes de la Vega, released the brakes and let the wing loop end over end above him, recovering with a full stall after eight tumbles. Judges called it the hardest manoeuvre.", "None of these belong over a hill. Acro is learned over water, with a boat, a reserve or two and an instructor, after an SIV course has taught the basic stalls and spins. Rodriguez himself threw his reserve twice in his first hundred infinity attempts. The usual mistake is trying a SAT on a wing or at a height that cannot forgive an early entry."], "rel": ["ru-acro-world-tour", "oludeniz", "ru-camera-drones", "ru-liability"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, How Raul invented the Infinite Tumble", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/infinite-tumbling/"}, {"t": "Justacro, SAT", "u": "https://justacro.com/book/export/html/27"}]}, {"id": "ru-hike-and-fly", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.4, "lon": 8.13, "name": "What is hike and fly?", "place": "Explained over Fiesch, Switzerland", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Walk up with a wing lighter than a bag of sugar, fly down, and the mountain becomes a loop.", "body": ["Hike and fly means carrying your paraglider up a mountain on your back and flying down, instead of driving to a launch with a road. The whole kit has shrunk to make it possible. Ozone's XXLite 2, built for alpinists, weighs about 1.3 kg in the 16 m² size, and a harness and reserve to match weigh little more. Those wings carry an EN D rating and are for experienced pilots; heavier EN A and B light wings exist for everyone else.", "The culture grew out of the Alps, where lifts close in the shoulder seasons and many summits have no road. Fiesch in the Valais, with its cable car to the Eggishorn and long valley glides, is a hub for it, and the Red Bull X-Alps turned the idea into a race across the mountains. Local events such as the Eigertour and the Bornes to Fly use mountain huts as turnpoints.", "The safety trade is clear. You arrive tired at a launch you may not know, often late in the day, with a wing that is less forgiving than your usual one. Learn the site, check the wind at the top as well as the forecast, and be willing to walk down. A rolled up wing in the pack weighs nothing compared with a flight in the lee."], "rel": ["ru-vol-biv", "ru-eigertour", "ru-bornes-to-fly", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Ozone XXLite 2 review", "u": "https://xcmag.com/gear-guide/paraglider-reviews/ozone-xxlite-2-review/"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Chrigel Maurer wins Eigertour 2020", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/chrigel-maurer-wins-eigertour-2020/"}]}, {"id": "ru-vol-biv", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 35.3, "lon": 75.63, "name": "What is vol bivouac?", "place": "Explained over Skardu, Pakistan", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Fly all day, sleep where you land, fly on: a month of it took one pilot above 8,000 m.", "body": ["Vol bivouac, or vol biv, is French for flying and camping. The pilot carries a tent, food and the wing, flies as far as the day allows, lands, sleeps on the mountain and walks or flies on the next morning. It is hike and fly stretched into a journey, and it rewards reading weather and terrain over days rather than hours.", "The defining trip is Antoine Girard's in 2016. Over a month, self supported, he flew through the Karakoram past Nanga Parbat and across the Deosai plateau. On 23 July he climbed through cloudbase at 6,500 m and soared to about 8,100 m above Broad Peak during a seven hour flight, the first time a paraglider had reached an 8,000 m summit, before landing at 4,400 m. Skardu, the town below the range, is where such expeditions start.", "Vol biv is the most committing form of the sport. You are alone, high, cold and far from rescue, and the ground you land on is unknown. Pilots who do it well carry a satellite messenger, a conservative wing, warm layers and enough food to wait out two days of bad weather. The common error is carrying too much and then launching tired into conditions a rested pilot would decline."], "rel": ["ru-hike-and-fly", "ru-eigertour", "bar-headed-goose", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Antoine Girard flies over Broad Peak", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=47546"}, {"t": "Paragliding, Wikipedia (gain of height record)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding"}]}, {"id": "ru-bornes-to-fly", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 45.84, "lon": 6.21, "name": "Bornes to Fly", "place": "Talloires, Annecy, France", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Three days, nine turnpoints, a cat's cradle around the Aravis, and fewer than one pilot in five makes goal.", "body": ["The Bornes to Fly is a hike and fly race in the Bornes and Aravis massifs above Lake Annecy. It starts and finishes in Talloires on the eastern shore and weaves back and forth around a set of turnpoints that may include the Parmelan plateau, the Aravis ridge and the valley town of Thônes. Pilots walk when they cannot fly and may use roads, which in a wet spring turns the race into a long run between short flights.", "The 2023 edition, celebrated as the tenth anniversary, gave 97 pilots from 8 am on Saturday 6 May until 4.30 pm on Monday 8 May to round nine turnpoints. Only 18 reached goal. Maxime Pinot won ahead of Tanguy Renaud-Goud and Patrick von Känel, and Laurie Genovese was the first woman, 15th overall. Arthur Moindrot won in 2024, and Lucas Bonin and Silvia Galli won the 2026 edition.", "It is a step between local hike and fly and the X-Alps: short enough to enter on a long weekend, serious enough that leading pilots use it as training. The lake, the Forclaz launch and the Annecy sites are a few kilometres away, so a visitor can watch the race and fly the same air."], "rel": ["ru-hike-and-fly", "annecy", "ru-eigertour", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Maxime Pinot wins Bornes to Fly 2023", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/comps-and-events/maxime-pinot-wins-bornes-to-fly-2023/"}, {"t": "Ozone, Lucas and Silvia win Bornes to Fly 2026", "u": "https://flyozone.com/paragliders/news/lucas-and-silvia-win-bornes-to-fly-2026"}]}, {"id": "ru-eigertour", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 46.62, "lon": 8.04, "name": "Eigertour", "place": "Grindelwald, Switzerland", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Up to 200 km around the Eiger with mountain huts as turnpoints, and Chrigel Maurer won the first three.", "body": ["The Eigertour is a hike and fly race based in Grindelwald, under the north face of the Eiger. It began in 2018 and the 2026 edition, run from 1 to 4 July, was its ninth. The course is set each year through the Bernese Alps with 14 to 18 turnpoints over roughly 100 to 200 km, and the turnpoints are alpine huts, so competitors eat and sleep indoors and need no support crew.", "There are two categories. Pro pilots fly the full course, while Challenger pilots have slightly fewer turnpoints but must still prove alpine flying experience to enter. Chrigel Maurer, the X-Alps champion from the next valley, won the first three editions through 2020, when Sepp Inniger was second and Aline Schäfer was the only woman in the field. A winter edition followed in 2022.", "Grindelwald's lifts make it a fine ordinary flying destination too, with launches high above the valley and the Jungfrau in view. The race adds rules the holiday pilot can borrow: carry minimal kit, respect the hut wardens and the farmers, and land early when the afternoon wind rises off the glaciers."], "rel": ["ru-hike-and-fly", "ru-bornes-to-fly", "ru-vol-biv", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "MySwitzerland, Eigertour Hike & Fly", "u": "https://www.myswitzerland.com/en/experiences/events/eigertour-hike-fly/"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Chrigel Maurer wins Eigertour 2020", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/chrigel-maurer-wins-eigertour-2020/"}]}, {"id": "ru-dolomitenmann", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 46.83, "lon": 12.77, "name": "Dolomitenmann", "place": "Lienz, Austria", "icon": "runner", "hidden": true, "hook": "The paraglider is the second leg of a four person relay a retired ski racer invented in 1988.", "body": ["The Dolomitenmann is a relay race held every September at Lienz in East Tyrol, founded in 1988 by Werner Grissmann, a former World Cup downhill skier. Each team has four athletes: a mountain runner, a paraglider pilot, a mountain biker and a whitewater kayaker. Red Bull sponsors it, and the organisers bill it as the world's toughest team relay. Entry has grown from 51 teams in the first year to around 110 to 128, with waiting lists.", "The runner leaves the town square at 674 m and climbs about 12 km to the Kühbodentörl at 2,441 m. There the paraglider pilot takes over, runs with the full kit to a launch, flies down to the Moosalm, runs to a second launch and flies again to Leisach to hand over to the biker, who climbs 1,600 m or more over about 27 km. The kayaker swims to the boat, drops 7 m off a ramp into the Drau and sprints through the Isel rapids to the finish.", "The fastest winning time is 3 hours 0 minutes 17 seconds, set in 2008. Only men race in the main event; a women's competition was added in 2017. For pilots the lesson is in the flying leg: a fast launch in a tight window, a glide chosen for speed, and a top landing on a small target with the heart rate of a runner."], "rel": ["ru-hike-and-fly", "ru-eigertour", "ru-launch-etiquette", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "Dolomitenmann, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolomitenmann"}, {"t": "Tyrol.com, Red Bull Dolomitenmann Lienz", "u": "https://tyrol.com/things-to-do/events/all-events/e-dolomitenmann-lienz"}]}, {"id": "ru-launch-etiquette", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 4.41, "lon": -76.15, "name": "What is launch etiquette?", "place": "Explained over Roldanillo, Colombia", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A busy launch is the most dangerous place in paragliding, and most of the danger is other people.", "body": ["Launch etiquette is the set of habits that let thirty pilots share a patch of hillside without hurting each other. The core rule is that the pilot laid out and ready at the front has the slot, and nobody kites a wing above or behind them. Lay out behind the active area, not on it. Clip in, check in, then move forward only when the pilot ahead has gone, and call clearly before you inflate so people know a wing is coming up.", "Watch the pilots already in the air, not just the windsock. If gliders are soaring in front of launch, you must not launch into their path, and the pilot in the air has priority over the one leaving the ground. Do not ground handle for practice on a launch others are waiting for. Carry your bag off to the side, keep dogs and spectators behind the line and help a stranger with a line check when asked.", "Roldanillo in the Cauca valley, host of the 2015 World Championships, is a good place to watch it done well: a packed launch, a marshal calling the window and pilots cycling through in order. What goes wrong is usually impatience, a wing pulled up into the back of someone else's lines, or a pilot launching into a sinking cycle because the queue behind was sighing."], "rel": ["ru-thermal-etiquette", "ru-landing-etiquette", "ru-comp-task", "k-window"], "src": [{"t": "DHV, Rules of the air for paragliders (English)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/08_en/GermanFlyingRules/DHV-Ausweichregeln-A2-engl.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA, Cooperative to the Core", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/cooperative-to-the-core.aspx"}]}, {"id": "ru-thermal-etiquette", "t": 2, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 19.19, "lon": -100.13, "name": "Which way do I turn in a thermal?", "place": "Explained over Valle de Bravo, Mexico", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "The first pilot in the thermal chooses the direction, and everyone who joins has to agree.", "body": ["The first glider in a thermal sets the turning direction, and every pilot who joins turns the same way. That one rule prevents most mid-air collisions in a gaggle. Join on a tangent to the circle, slot in behind someone rather than cutting in front, and if there is no gap fly a wider circle outside the group until one opens. Pilots already in the thermal have priority over those joining, and the lower pilot has priority because the wing above blocks their view.", "On a ridge the rules change. Two gliders meeting head on both turn right. When soaring a slope, the glider with the ridge on its right has right of way and the one with the ridge on its left moves out to pass. Overtake with a safe gap and never force the other pilot to turn toward the hill. These are the German DHV rules, and most countries use the same ones, but check the local variations before you fly abroad.", "Valle de Bravo's El Peñón launch, host of the 2009 Worlds, sees big winter gaggles and is where many pilots first learn to share a core. The errors are predictable: joining against the direction, flattening a turn into someone's path, and assuming the other pilot has seen you. Assume they have not, and fly actively so your wing stays open in the gaggle."], "rel": ["ru-launch-etiquette", "k-thermal", "ru-comp-task", "ru-flarm"], "src": [{"t": "DHV, Rules of the air for paragliders (English)", "u": "https://www.dhv.de/media/seiten/08_en/GermanFlyingRules/DHV-Ausweichregeln-A2-engl.pdf"}, {"t": "USHPA, Cooperative to the Core", "u": "https://ushpa.org/page/cooperative-to-the-core.aspx"}]}, {"id": "ru-landing-etiquette", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 54.29, "lon": -2.17, "name": "How do I keep farmers happy when I land in a field?", "place": "Explained over Wether Fell, Yorkshire Dales, England", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "Every field belongs to somebody, and a trampled hay crop can close a site faster than an accident.", "body": ["Almost every flying site sits on private land that the owner allows a club to use. Clubs lose sites over small things: a gate left open, a dog among ewes, a car blocking a track. The Dales club's rule for Wether Fell is that the gate at the start of the Cam High Road must be closed behind you even if you found it open. Use gates and stiles, never climb dry stone walls, park only where the site guide says and take litter home.", "Fields are crops and animals, not grass. Long grass in summer is winter feed, and a wing dragged across it flattens what cannot then be cut. Pregnant ewes can miscarry if frightened, so around lambing in April give sheep a wide berth, land well away from them and give horses more space still. If you land out, walk the edge of the field, not the middle, fold up at the gate and leave as you found it.", "When you land somewhere you had no permission to be, go and find the farmer, say hello and apologise. Report any damage honestly. Clubs say a visit to the farmhouse does more for site access than any letter, and a pilot who talks to the landowner after a field landing is the reason the next pilot is still welcome. The thermal downwind of a popular site is where this matters most."], "rel": ["ru-clubs-site-guides", "ru-launch-etiquette", "ru-xc-explained", "ru-liability"], "src": [{"t": "Dales Hang Gliding and Paragliding Club, Countryside Code", "u": "https://www.dhpc.org.uk/sites/countryside-code"}, {"t": "Derbyshire Soaring Club, Pilot information", "u": "https://derbyshiresoaringclub.org.uk/pilot-information/"}]}, {"id": "ru-flying-abroad", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.48, "lon": 11.77, "name": "What insurance do I need to fly abroad?", "place": "Explained over Canazei, Dolomites, Italy", "icon": "letter", "hidden": false, "hook": "Your health card covers the hospital but not the helicopter, and the helicopter is what you will need.", "body": ["Three covers matter. Third party liability pays if you injure someone or damage property; in Britain it comes with BHPA membership, £5 million of it, and most federations bundle it the same way. Personal accident and travel insurance pay for your own injuries, and a standard travel policy usually excludes paragliding unless you add it. Rescue and repatriation cover is the one pilots forget: a European health card gives state healthcare only, not a mountain rescue flight or a flight home.", "Host countries set their own minimums. Switzerland requires visiting pilots to carry at least CHF 1 million of liability cover and sells a guest policy, CHF 51 for 30 days, to pilots who lack it; commercial tandem pilots need CHF 5 million and a Swiss licence. Germany asks for valid liability insurance, an IPPI 4 or 5 card, a helmet and a reserve. Carry the IPPI card and your federation card together; the BHPA issues IPPI for £20.", "Local rules travel with the insurance. In the Dolomites around Canazei, Col Rodella and the Marmolada, the Italian federation asks pilots to install the 112 Where ARE U app, listen on PMR channel 8 subchannel 16, and clear a 2 km radius or land at once when a rescue helicopter appears. At Dune du Pilat pilots must register online before flying. Failing to do these is how visiting nationals get banned."], "rel": ["ru-liability", "k-ippi", "ru-ratings-compared", "ru-tandem-rules"], "src": [{"t": "BHPA, UK Pilots Flying Abroad", "u": "https://www.bhpa.co.uk/safety/overseas/"}, {"t": "SHV/FSVL, Foreign licence", "u": "https://shv-fsvl.ch/en/education/foreign-licence"}, {"t": "BHPA membership", "u": "https://www.bhpa.co.uk/bhpa"}]}, {"id": "ru-clubs-site-guides", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 53.35, "lon": -1.81, "name": "Why join a club and read the site guide?", "place": "Explained over Mam Tor, Peak District, England", "icon": "home", "hidden": false, "hook": "The site guide is where the club wrote down every way the hill has hurt someone.", "body": ["A club holds the site. It negotiates with landowners, pays for insurance and gates, posts the restrictions and runs coaching. In Britain the BHPA's roughly 7,000 pilots fly through local clubs, and many sites are members only, with no reciprocal access. The Derbyshire Soaring Club, which looks after Mam Tor and Stanage, allows guests only when the committee invites them, typically for competitions.", "The site guide is the condensed memory of that club. For Mam Tor it tells you the site code, that pay and display applies at Mam Nick, that theft from cars is high, that model aircraft must keep 1 km clear when the hill is in use and that midweek flying must be notified to the RAF. It says pilots with under ten hours fly with red streamers on their risers and should use the coaching scheme, and that you must check NOTAMs before every flight.", "Most of a guide is the hill's own character: where the rotor sits, which wind direction turns the bowl into a trap, where the landing field floods. Reading it is the cheapest safety equipment you will buy. The mistake visitors make is treating a famous site as public space and flying it on a forecast and a photo."], "rel": ["ru-landing-etiquette", "ru-notam", "ru-flying-abroad", "ru-community-online"], "src": [{"t": "Derbyshire Soaring Club, Pilot information", "u": "https://derbyshiresoaringclub.org.uk/pilot-information/"}, {"t": "BHPA membership", "u": "https://www.bhpa.co.uk/bhpa"}]}, {"id": "ru-instructor", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 40.48, "lon": -111.9, "name": "How do you become a paragliding instructor?", "place": "Explained over Point of the Mountain, Utah, United States", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "Three hundred flights and two hundred flying days before you may start the course, and that is entry level.", "body": ["Every federation makes instructing a separate qualification above the top pilot rating, with its own course, apprenticeship and exam. In the United States a USHPA Basic Instructor must hold at least an Intermediate rating, log 200 flying days and 300 flights, complete a reserve clinic plus two of thermal, ridge soaring, tandem or towing, hold an in-person first aid and CPR card and pass the FAA's Fundamentals of Instruction test. Then comes a three phase programme: seminar, apprenticeship and evaluation.", "Elsewhere the shape is the same. France requires the Brevet de Pilote Confirmé before federal instructor training. Switzerland insists on a Swiss instructor licence to teach commercially, whatever you hold at home. Germany trains instructors through DHV schools under its own training order. Britain's BHPA runs trainee instructor and instructor grades through its registered schools.", "Point of the Mountain south of Salt Lake City, a smooth ridge soarable most days where American schools train many students, shows what the job really is: standing in wind for eight hours, watching for the moment a student's wing surges, and deciding when to say no. The most common failure in new instructors is not skill but the inability to cancel a day with paying customers on the hill."], "rel": ["ru-ratings-compared", "ru-tandem-rules", "ru-liability", "ru-clubs-site-guides"], "src": [{"t": "USHPA, How to become an instructor", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/page/how-to-become-an-instructor"}, {"t": "SHV/FSVL, Foreign licence", "u": "https://shv-fsvl.ch/en/education/foreign-licence"}, {"t": "Les Brevets de Pilote en Parapente (FFVL summary)", "u": "https://unilim.fr/suaps/wp-content/uploads/sites/14/2013/03/Les-Brevets-de-Pilote-en-Parapente_C.LAFON_.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-tandem-rules", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 46.7, "lon": 7.77, "name": "What are the rules for commercial tandem flights?", "place": "Explained over Beatenberg, Interlaken, Switzerland", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Switzerland wants a Swiss licence and five million francs for a tourist flight; America bans paid joyrides.", "body": ["Tandem flying for money is the most regulated corner of the sport, and the rules differ sharply by country. Switzerland, where the Interlaken launches above Beatenberg fly passengers all year, allows occasional non-commercial tandems on a foreign tandem licence but requires a Swiss licence for any commercial flight, with liability cover of at least CHF 5 million. Teaching also needs a Swiss instructor licence.", "In the United States two-seat paragliders are only legal under FAA Exemption 4721, which USHPA holds. Every flight must be for training, both occupants need a current USHPA rating, one must be a Tandem Instructor, and the passenger must be told the aircraft meets no FAA certification standard. The exemption runs to October 2028. Germany requires a separate passenger licence after at least 40 flights, 30 of them height flights with licensed passengers. France's biplace qualification sits on top of the Brevet de Pilote Confirmé.", "Passengers can check two things anywhere: that the pilot holds the national tandem licence and that the operator carries liability insurance. Pilots should know that a tandem is heavier, faster and slower to react than a solo wing, and that most tandem accidents happen on launch and landing with a passenger who was never briefed to keep running."], "rel": ["ru-liability", "ru-instructor", "ru-flying-abroad", "ru-ratings-compared"], "src": [{"t": "SHV/FSVL, Foreign licence", "u": "https://shv-fsvl.ch/en/education/foreign-licence"}, {"t": "USHPA, FAA Exemption 4721 Tandem Operations", "u": "https://www.ushpa.org/Public/PilotResources/faa-exemption-4721.aspx"}, {"t": "DHV, Zusammenfassung Änderung APO 2019", "u": "https://service.dhv.de/dbfiles/aus/Arbeitsordner%20Seli/Zusammenfassung_nderung_APO_2019.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-community-online", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 50.87, "lon": 0.01, "name": "Where do pilots talk online?", "place": "Explained over Lewes, Sussex, England", "icon": "book", "hidden": false, "hook": "A magazine started by an artist in 1988 is still where the sport argues with itself.", "body": ["Cross Country magazine is the closest thing paragliding has to a paper of record. Sherry Thevenot, an artist who had married into the hang gliding scene, founded it in 1988, and it is still produced from near Lewes in East Sussex, eight issues a year in print and digital, with a weekly newsletter, gear reviews, travel guides and a news site that reports records, accidents and competitions within hours.", "The Paragliding Forum is the long running message board, and it hosts Leonardo, the flight database that many clubs use as a league. Alongside it sit national forums in German, French and Spanish, the XContest comment threads, a large Reddit community and the podcasts that interview competition pilots. Manufacturers and schools run their own channels, which are useful but are also selling something.", "Use the community for what it is good at: site knowledge, retrieve offers, weather debate and used gear. Treat it carefully for safety advice. A forum thread can argue for a week about a manoeuvre that an SIV instructor would settle in a minute, and the loudest voices are rarely the most current. Read the accident reports; they are the most honest writing in the sport."], "rel": ["ru-xc-leagues", "ru-clubs-site-guides", "ru-flying-abroad", "ru-women"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country hits 25 years old, issue 150", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/cross-country-hits-25-years-old-and-celebrates-it-in-issue-150/"}, {"t": "IHPA, What is Leonardo?", "u": "https://ihpa.ie/index.php/competitions/how-to-use-and-manage-the-comps-leagues"}]}, {"id": "ru-environment", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.57, "lon": 10.77, "name": "What is the environmental impact of paragliding?", "place": "Explained over Tegelberg, Bavaria, Germany", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wing makes no noise and burns no fuel, which is why a chamois does not see it coming.", "body": ["A paraglider leaves no exhaust and little noise, so its footprint on the ground is mostly the car to launch and the boots on the path. In the air the issue is wildlife. A silent shape overhead reads as a predator to grazing animals and nesting birds, and the response is flight, lost feeding time and abandoned nests. The German federation commissioned a study by Zeitler and Georgii in 1994 that still shapes Alpine site rules.", "The study found that over regularly flown descent routes the critical height for chamois and red deer was about 100 m, and seldom below 50 m, as long as the pilot passed over quickly. Animals reacted more strongly on exposed ridges and open meadows than on bushy slopes, and more strongly in areas that were rarely overflown. Golden eagles may defend nesting cliffs during brooding, typically between 1,200 and 1,600 m. Red deer were more sensitive than chamois.", "The advice is practical. Stay on normal routes and cross wildlife areas high and fast. Do not circle low over the same meadow, and respect seasonal closures that clubs post for nesting raptors and grouse. Talk to hunters and conservationists, who know where the animals are through the year. The Tegelberg above Schwangau, one of Bavaria's busiest sites, flies under exactly this kind of agreement."], "rel": ["ru-national-parks", "ru-landing-etiquette", "ruppells-vulture", "ru-clubs-site-guides"], "src": [{"t": "DHV, Icarus and Wildlife (Zeitler and Georgii study summary)", "u": "https://dhv.de/en/sites-nature/nature-conservation/icarus-and-wildlife"}, {"t": "Effects of paragliding on alpine chamois, Wildlife Biology", "u": "https://bioone.org/journals/wildlife-biology/volume-7/issue-3/wlb.2001.033/Effects-of-paragliding-on-alpine-chamois-Rupicapra-rupicapra-rupicapra/10.2981/wlb.2001.033.short"}]}, {"id": "ru-national-parks", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 37.73, "lon": -119.57, "name": "Can I fly in a national park?", "place": "Explained over Glacier Point, Yosemite, United States", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": true, "hook": "Hang gliders launch from Glacier Point under a permit a paraglider cannot get, and the rule predates the sport.", "body": ["It depends entirely on the park and the country. In the United States the National Park Service regulation 36 CFR 2.17, written in 1983, forbids delivering or retrieving a person by parachute, helicopter or other airborne means except in emergencies or under a permit. Superintendents may issue permits and mostly do not. Yosemite is the famous exception: the Yosemite Hang Gliders Association, a USHPA chapter, flies from Glacier Point under a park permit with a monitor on launch and tightly regulated launch times and landing zones.", "That programme is a hang gliding one, and the park's own accident reports, including a 2012 crash 400 ft below the lookout, show how carefully the agency watches it. Elsewhere in the world the picture varies: many European national parks allow free flight with seasonal and altitude restrictions to protect wildlife, some ban launches inside the boundary, and some permit flying over the park from launches outside it.", "Before travelling, ask the local club rather than the park website, because agreements are often unwritten and recent. The mistake that closes areas is the quiet launch from a viewpoint, filmed and posted. One video of a wing over a prohibited canyon can end a decade of negotiation by the club that flies next door."], "rel": ["ru-environment", "ru-clubs-site-guides", "ru-camera-drones", "ru-flying-abroad"], "src": [{"t": "NPS Yosemite, Hang gliding accident near Glacier Point", "u": "https://www.nps.gov/yose/blogs/Hang-Gliding-Accident-Resulting-in-Injuries-Near-Glacier-Point.htm"}, {"t": "NPS, letter on 36 CFR 2.17 airborne delivery", "u": "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/policy/upload/BASE_Letter.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-camera-drones", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 32.89, "lon": -117.25, "name": "Can I fly with a camera, and what about drones?", "place": "Explained over Torrey Pines, California, United States", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A drone wrapped itself in a paraglider's lines over Torrey Pines and cut two of them.", "body": ["Cameras are part of the sport now, but every mount is a snag point. Swiss pilot and doctor Reto Reiser, who films for Cross Country, uses a pole from the harness karabiner or the waist strap, never on the reserve handle side, and stows a thigh mounted pole well before landing. A follow camera on a line should attach to the rearmost riser level, never the brakes, and he rules out helicopters, full stalls and big wingovers with one trailing. Launch with the camera stowed.", "Drones are the newer hazard. In January 2025 a civilian drone collided with a paraglider over Torrey Pines in San Diego at about 400 ft, tore the canopy in two places and cut two lines before the pilot landed unhurt. EASA rules limit open category drones in Europe to 120 m above the surface and oblige the operator to keep it in sight, scan for other aircraft and land if it heads toward any aircraft or parachute. In the US the limit is 400 ft and drones give way.", "Paragliders and drones share the same few hundred metres above a ridge. If you fly drones, do not fly them at an active launch. If you fly wings, assume a hovering drone cannot see you and give it the same berth as a bird. And whichever you hold, a camera that makes you do a manoeuvre for the shot has already cost you the margin you flew with before it."], "rel": ["ru-acro-manoeuvres", "ru-launch-etiquette", "ru-national-parks", "ru-liability"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Reto Reiser on filming yourself", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=67365"}, {"t": "Drone collides with Torrey Pines paraglider (Yahoo News)", "u": "https://www.yahoo.com/news/drone-collides-torrey-pines-hang-014308989.html"}, {"t": "EASA Easy Access Rules for UAS, UAS.OPEN.010", "u": "https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/document-library/easy-access-rules/online-publications/easy-access-rules-unmanned-aircraft-systems?page=5"}]}, {"id": "ru-women", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 42.05, "lon": 0.76, "name": "How many paragliding pilots are women?", "place": "Explained over Ager, Catalonia, Spain", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Women are one in twenty to one in seven World Cup pilots, and one holds a 531 km record.", "body": ["Fewer than most sports. At Paragliding World Cup events in 2022 and 2023 women made up between 5.1 and 13.6 per cent of the field, the highest share in South Korea and the lowest in Romania and France. Recreational numbers are higher but still a small minority. Research with pilots in India found the barriers were money, family expectations after marriage, concern about risk during pregnancy and childcare, few visible role models and almost no women among organisers and judges.", "The flying tells another story. Judy Leden of Britain was world champion in 1995, Louise Crandal in 2001 and 2005, and France's Seiko Fukuoka, Meryl Delferriere and Constance Mettetal have taken the recent titles. Yael Margelisch of Switzerland holds the women's straight distance record of 531.7 km from Caicó in Brazil, and Marcella Uchoa of Brazil the declared goal record of 457.3 km. The women's world records are within 15 per cent of the men's.", "Ager, below the Montsec ridge in Catalonia, hosted the Women's Paragliding Open in August 2010 with around 80 entrants expected, one of a series of women's competitions that give newer pilots a first taste of racing. Federations now run women's coaching weeks, and the FAI's Women with Wings series profiles pilots. The practical difference on the hill is small: lighter pilots fly smaller wings, and good schools fit the harness rather than the stereotype."], "rel": ["ru-world-champs", "ru-xc-explained", "ru-community-online", "ru-adaptive"], "src": [{"t": "Soaring Beyond Limits: women's prospects and challenges in paragliding (Mizoram University)", "u": "https://mzu.edu.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/3.Soaring-Beyond-Limits_A-Qualitative-Exploration-of-Women_s-Prospects-and-Challenges-in-Paragliding.pdf"}, {"t": "World Paragliding Championships, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Paragliding_Championships"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Women's Paragliding Open registration starts", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/womens-paragliding-open-registration-starts/"}]}, {"id": "ru-olympics", "t": 4, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 52.53, "lon": 13.12, "name": "Should paragliding be in the Olympics?", "place": "Explained over Berlin-Staaken, Germany", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": true, "hook": "Gliding was an Olympic demonstration sport in 1936 and was accepted for the 1940 Games, which never happened.", "body": ["Air sport has been to the Olympics once. On 4 August 1936, at the Staaken airfield outside Berlin, 21 glider pilots from seven countries, Hanna Reitsch among them, flew a demonstration with no contest and no prizes, as the IOC required. The day before, Austrian pilot Ignaz Stiefsohn had died when a wing broke during aerobatics. At the 1938 Cairo conference the IOC accepted gliding as an optional sport for 1940, and a standard glider, the Olympia Meise, was chosen before war cancelled the Games.", "Gliding stayed on the optional list until the IOC abolished it in 1956, and no host ever picked it. The FAI has been recognised by the IOC since 1986, so the door is formally open, and campaigners have argued for gliding and paragliding ever since. The case for is simple: a global sport with world championships, a professional circuit and a clean, silent image.", "The case against is the weather. A task needs thermals, a safe wind and a launch that works on the day, and an Olympic schedule cannot wait a week for a front to pass. Accuracy landing and acro are more schedulable, which is why they lead the discussion. Most pilots are relaxed about it: the sport's World Cup and World Championships already give it the competition it needs."], "rel": ["ru-fai", "ru-world-champs", "ru-acro-world-tour", "lilienthal"], "src": [{"t": "Gliding at the 1936 Summer Olympics, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gliding_at_the_1936_Summer_Olympics"}, {"t": "About FAI, letter from the Secretary General, 2020", "u": "https://fai.org/sites/default/files/about-fai_to-whom-it-may-concern_2020_secgen.pdf"}]}, {"id": "ru-adaptive", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": 47.17, "lon": 11.86, "name": "What is adaptive paragliding?", "place": "Explained over Mayrhofen, Austria", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "A wheelchair with a harness bolted to it lets a paraplegic pilot launch, fly and land alone.", "body": ["Adaptive paragliding is flying with equipment and methods changed for a disability. The commonest change is a wheeled harness: a frame with wheels that replaces the pilot's legs on launch and landing, so a person who cannot run can still be pulled into the air on a slope or by tow and roll to a stop. Tandem versions put the passenger in the chair with the pilot behind; solo versions put the pilot in command.", "Wheels4Flying, a non-profit in Mayrhofen in the Zillertal, began in autumn 2022 offering wheelchair assisted tandem flights, and in summer 2023 Mario Graus became its first solo pilot, flying four times on his first day on a BGD Magic wing paired with a Handivol chair. In France, Back Bone at Tallard builds the Hand'Expert, a solo wheelchair harness with adjustable tilt, a neoprene cover and a frame that folds into a car, after years making tandem chairs for passengers.", "The flying is the same; the ground is different. Wheeled launches need a smooth slope, more wind or a tow, and a landing field without ditches, so site choice does most of the safety work. Visual and hearing impairments are handled with radio and a guide pilot. What adaptive pilots say they want is the same instruction and the same standards, not a softer version of the sport."], "rel": ["ru-tandem-rules", "ru-women", "ru-instructor", "ru-hike-and-fly"], "src": [{"t": "BGD, Wheels4Flying: wheelchair-assisted paragliding", "u": "https://www.flybgd.com/en/paragliders/wheels4flying-wheelchair-assisted-paragliding--news-6161-2075-0.html"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Back Bone Hand'Expert wheelchair harness", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/handexpert-solo-wheelchair-harness-back-bone/"}]}, {"id": "ru-africa", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -33.93, "lon": 18.39, "name": "Where can you fly in Africa?", "place": "Explained over Lion's Head, Cape Town, South Africa", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Launch from Lion's Head at sunset, land by a beachfront pub, then drive to thermals that cross the Karoo.", "body": ["South Africa is the continent's established flying nation. Cape Town has two city sites, Lion's Head and Signal Hill, where pilots launch above Table Mountain's flank in the evening and land on the Sea Point promenade, and Sir Lowry's Pass and Porterville's Dassklip Pass lie within two hours. Wilderness on the Garden Route claims 24 sites within an hour, from coastal soaring over whales to inland thermals, and Bulwer in the southern Drakensberg has had a school since 1993.", "All of it is licensed through SAHPA, the South African Hang Gliding and Paragliding Association, under the Civil Aviation Act, and visiting pilots fly under its rules. The dry interior of the Northern Cape produces the strong, high thermals behind South Africa's distance records. Further north, Kenya's Kerio Valley escarpment has become the continent's other record site, with a tour industry growing around it.", "Beyond those two, Africa is largely unexplored sky. Morocco, Tanzania, Namibia, Ethiopia and Madagascar all have launches used by a few pilots, mostly on expeditions with local fixers. The constraints are the ones a visiting pilot should expect: little rescue infrastructure, uncertain airspace information and sites without a club to ask. Fly with people who know the place, carry a tracker and treat every landing field as someone's crop."], "rel": ["kerio-valley", "ruppells-vulture", "ru-flying-abroad", "ru-xc-explained"], "src": [{"t": "Brand South Africa, Paragliding in South Africa", "u": "https://www.brandsouthafrica.com/stay-updated/quick-facts/2017/paragliding-in-south-africa/"}, {"t": "SAHPA, IPPI card", "u": "https://sahpa.co.za/membership/ippi-card/"}]}, {"id": "ru-india-history", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 18.76, "lon": 73.55, "name": "Paragliding in India", "place": "Kamshet, Maharashtra, India", "icon": "kite", "hidden": false, "hook": "Hang gliders reached Billing in 1984, and a hill town between Mumbai and Pune taught India to fly.", "body": ["Flying in India began with hang gliders. Billing, above the village of Bir in Himachal Pradesh, hosted its first international hang gliding rally in 1984, and foreign pilots' enthusiasm persuaded the state government to run annual events. Local young people who had watched them took up the sport, and paragliders followed. Bir Billing staged a Pre-World Cup in 2013 and then, from 23 to 31 October 2015, the first Paragliding World Cup in India, with about 130 pilots from more than 40 countries.", "The training side grew in the west. Kamshet, a small town in the Sahyadri hills about 100 km from Mumbai and an hour from Pune, saw paragliding pioneered in the 1990s and became what Cross Country calls the training capital of India. Its two sites are Tower Hill, an east facing launch at 857 m that works from November to February, and Shelar, west facing at 720 m, which flies from January to May before the monsoon.", "India now has a tandem industry at Bir and other hill stations, a competition scene and a Himalayan cross country season each autumn. The challenges are the ones a young flying nation faces: uneven regulation of commercial tandems, few qualified instructors for the number of students, and sites where the landing field is also a farmer's winter crop. The upside is some of the biggest mountains anyone gets to fly."], "rel": ["bir-billing", "himalayan-griffon", "ru-pwc", "ru-nepal-history"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Paragliding in Kamshet, India", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/asia/paragliding-in-kamshet-india/"}, {"t": "The Better India, India's first Paragliding World Cup in October", "u": "https://www.thebetterindia.com/33535/indias-first-paragliding-world-cup-in-october/"}, {"t": "Scroll.in, Bir Billing video report", "u": "https://thereel.scroll.in/813501/scroll_in"}]}, {"id": "ru-nepal-history", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 28.245, "lon": 83.95, "name": "Paragliding in Nepal", "place": "Sarangkot, Pokhara, Nepal", "icon": "bird", "hidden": true, "hook": "Nepal's first tandem company launched off a cliff at Sarangkot, and a falconer later taught vultures to fly alongside.", "body": ["Commercial paragliding in Nepal started at Pokhara with Sunrise Paragliding, the first Nepali company to offer flights, run by Biru Bomjom. Under the 1994 Civil Aviation Policy the authority approved flights from the Torepani cliff at Sarangkot over Lakeside, the Mahendra caves and the Phewa plain, with landings near Naudanda, and pilots had to watch for the Pokhara to Jomsom aeroplanes. By early 2002 more than 1,000 passengers had flown, at about $70 an hour.", "There were almost no Nepali pilots and no school, so Sunrise hired foreign pilots and trained locals informally on the FAI's three level course. Rajesh Bomjom became the first Nepali to receive a paragliding licence, issued from London, and won the solo event at the company's third international competition in December 2001. Sarangkot has since become one of the busiest tandem sites in Asia, with many companies launching below the Annapurna skyline.", "Pokhara is also where parahawking was born. British falconer Scott Mason coined the name for flying with trained birds of prey that led pilots to thermals, and ran it from a raptor rescue centre in the town until the government confiscated endangered birds in 2017. The industry's growth has brought the usual problems of crowding and regulation, and the usual answer: a licensed pilot, a briefed passenger and a wide landing field."], "rel": ["pokhara", "himalayan-griffon", "ru-india-history", "ru-tandem-rules"], "src": [{"t": "Nepali Times, Soaring over Nepal", "u": "https://nepalitimes.com/soaring-over-nepal"}, {"t": "Parahawking, Wikipedia", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parahawking"}]}, {"id": "ru-liability", "t": 3, "dom": "knowledge", "lat": -22.99, "lon": -43.28, "name": "Who is liable when a paraglider crashes?", "place": "Explained over Pedra Bonita, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "When a tandem went into the sea off São Conrado in 2026, the first questions were about paperwork.", "body": ["In law a paraglider is an aircraft and its pilot is responsible for it. If you hit another pilot, a walker or a car, you are liable, which is why federations bundle third party cover with membership: the BHPA's £5 million is typical, and host countries set minimums such as Switzerland's CHF 1 million for solo visitors and CHF 5 million for commercial tandems. Landowners are usually covered under the same policies, which is what keeps sites open.", "Commercial flying raises the stakes. On 21 February 2026 a tandem hang glider on approach to the Pedra Bonita landing hit the water nose first; the pilot died at the scene and his American passenger in hospital. The club that runs the site, where marshals check documents before every launch, said the pilot was a qualified instructor, the equipment inspected and the weather good, and suspended flying for three days while investigators worked. Liability in such cases turns on licence, insurance, maintenance records and whether the passenger was properly briefed.", "Insurance is fragile. In 2003 the BHPA's insurers cut cover for schools, instructors and tandem pilots from £2 million to £25,000 because a multi-million pound claim was expected every few years, and schools considered closing. Pilots protect the system by flying within their rating, keeping logbooks and gear checks current, and never flying a passenger for money without the licence and cover the country requires."], "rel": ["ru-flying-abroad", "ru-tandem-rules", "ru-instructor", "ru-landing-etiquette"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country, Rio de Janeiro sees tandem hang gliding double fatality", "u": "https://xcmag.com/news/rio-de-janeiro-sees-tandem-hang-gliding-double-fatality/"}, {"t": "Cross Country, Hang gliding and paragliding: politics of insurance", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=184"}, {"t": "SHV/FSVL, Foreign licence", "u": "https://shv-fsvl.ch/en/education/foreign-licence"}]}, {"id": "cs-quixada-records", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -4.97, "lon": -39.02, "name": "The Brazilian sertão distance records", "place": "Quixadá, Ceará, Brazil", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "Nearly every paragliding distance record since 2007 has been set on the dry plains of north-east Brazil.", "body": ["Quixadá is a small town in the dry interior of Ceará, and its launch sits on a granite hill at about 460 m. The land in front drops to around 300 m and then runs flat for hundreds of kilometres to the west. From September to December the trade wind blows hard from the east, often above 50 km/h, and cloudbase reaches 3,500 m. A pilot who launches at mid-morning is pushed downwind all day.", "On 14 November 2007 Marcelo Prieto, Frank Brown and Rafael Saladini flew 461.6 km together from Quixadá and shared the world record. In October 2012 four pilots flew over 460 km on the same day. From 2014 the action moved to Tacima in Paraíba, further east, which gives an extra hundred kilometres of runway. A three-pilot team flew 514 km in October 2015, then 564 km on 13 October 2016 in 11 hours 48 minutes, and 588 km on 10 October 2019.", "These flights are not casual. The launch is gusty and pilots use helpers to hold the wing. Landing fields can be swamps or stubble with sharp maize stalks, and a pilot who lands away from a road in 40 degree heat may wait hours for help. Record teams fly as a group, share lift, and carry water and satellite trackers. The lesson for everyone else is simpler: in Brazil the wind, not the pilot, decides the distance, so respect it."], "rel": ["cs-quixada-monoliths", "cs-cedro-dam", "cs-cangaco", "kerio-valley"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Guide to Quixadá", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-quixada-brazil/"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Tacima rocks, 2014 season roundup", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=41586"}, {"t": "Cross Country: The Dream Team 564 km world record", "u": "https://xcmag.com/magazine-articles/the-dream-team-564km-paragliding-world-record-in-brazil/"}]}, {"id": "cs-quixada-monoliths", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -5.12, "lon": -39.1, "name": "Monoliths of Quixadá", "place": "Quixadá, Ceará, Brazil", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "The granite domes pilots launch from were already old when the first animals crawled onto land.", "body": ["The hills around Quixadá are inselbergs: lumps of hard granite left standing after softer rock around them wore away. The granite formed about 600 million years ago, deep in the crust, and the semi-arid climate of the sertão has since stripped the land down to it. The result is a plain studded with smooth domes, the most famous shaped like a brooding hen and called Pedra da Galinha Choca.", "The state of Ceará protected 16,635 hectares of these hills as the Quixadá Monoliths Natural Monument on 25 October 2002. The domes are more than scenery for a pilot. Bare rock heats faster than the surrounding caatinga scrub, so each monolith is a thermal trigger, and the morning's first climbs usually start off the sunlit faces. They are also hard, unlandable ground, so the glide between them should always be planned with a field in reach.", "The caatinga itself is worth a look from the air. It is a dry forest found only in Brazil, and most of its trees drop their leaves between May and December, turning the land silver-grey. After the first rains the whole plain goes green in a week."], "rel": ["cs-quixada-records", "cs-cedro-dam", "cs-serra-da-capivara", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Quixadá Monoliths Natural Monument", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quixad%C3%A1_Monoliths_Natural_Monument"}, {"t": "UNESCO Tentative List: Cedro Dam and Quixadá monoliths", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5998/"}]}, {"id": "cs-cedro-dam", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": -4.99, "lon": -39.05, "name": "Cedro Dam", "place": "Quixadá, Ceará, Brazil", "icon": "fort", "hidden": true, "hook": "An emperor's answer to drought sits under the launch hill, and it has been dry for years.", "body": ["Below Pedra da Galinha Choca lies a curved stone wall 415 m long and 20 m high. This is the Açude do Cedro, the first large public work the Brazilian state ever built in the sertão. After the great drought of 1877 to 1879 devastated Ceará, Emperor Dom Pedro II ordered a reservoir. Work ran from 1890 to 1906, under the Empire and then the Republic, to a design by the English engineer John Julian Revy.", "The dam was built of local crystalline rock, 8 m wide at the base and 4 m at the crest, and could hold 126 million cubic metres from a catchment of about 224 square kilometres. Brazil placed it on UNESCO's Tentative List in January 2015 as a monument to the long fight against drought. The irony is that in recent dry years the reservoir has stood almost empty.", "For pilots it is a landmark and a safety reference. The wall and the lake bed sit directly under the Quixadá launch, so the view on the first glide is of a 19th century engineering project laid out like a map, and the flat, dry reservoir floor is the obvious bail-out if the first thermal does not work."], "rel": ["cs-quixada-records", "cs-quixada-monoliths", "cs-cangaco"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO Tentative List: Cedro Dam", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/5998/"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Quixadá Monoliths Natural Monument", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quixad%C3%A1_Monoliths_Natural_Monument"}]}, {"id": "cs-jericoacoara", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": -2.8, "lon": -40.51, "name": "Jericoacoara dunes", "place": "Jericoacoara, Ceará, Brazil", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "The same trade wind that carries record flights inland builds walking sand dunes on the coast.", "body": ["Jericoacoara National Park covers 8,850 hectares of dunes, lagoons and beach on the north coast of Ceará, about 300 km north-west of Fortaleza. It was a state protected area from 1984 and became a national park on 4 February 2002. The village inside it was a fishing hamlet with sand streets until the 1980s, and it still has no paved roads.", "The dunes exist because of the wind. From roughly July to January the south-east trade wind blows onto this coast almost without a break, and it pushes the sand inland in long crescent dunes that bury and uncover the lagoons behind the beach. The same steady wind made Jericoacoara one of the world's best windsurfing and kitesurfing spots, and each evening crowds climb the Duna do Pôr do Sol to watch the sun drop into the sea.", "Pilots know this wind as the system that drives the Quixadá distance flights 300 km to the south-east. On the coast it is too strong and too smooth for free flight, but a visit shows where the energy comes from: an ocean of air moving west, day after day."], "rel": ["cs-quixada-records", "cs-jangada", "k-sea-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Jericoacoara National Park", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jericoacoara_National_Park"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Guide to Quixadá", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-quixada-brazil/"}]}, {"id": "cs-serra-da-capivara", "t": 2, "dom": "heritage", "lat": -8.83, "lon": -42.55, "name": "Serra da Capivara rock art", "place": "Serra da Capivara National Park, Piauí, Brazil, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "scroll", "hidden": false, "hook": "Painted deer, jaguars and dancers cover cliffs that may rewrite when humans reached the Americas.", "body": ["Serra da Capivara National Park lies in southern Piauí, around 550 km south-west of Quixadá, in the same dry caatinga country. The park was created in 1979, covers roughly 129,000 hectares, and was listed by UNESCO in 1991. Its sandstone cliffs and rock shelters hold more than 300 painted sites recognised by UNESCO, and the research foundation that runs the park has recorded well over a thousand archaeological sites in all.", "The paintings are mostly red ochre. Red deer are the commonest subject, with armadillo, capybara, jaguar, tapir and the extinct giant rhea, and there are scenes of hunting, dancing, fighting and ceremony. The archaeologist Niède Guidon revealed the Pedra Furada site in 1973 and led digs there from 1978 to 1987. Her team dated hearths and painted fragments to well over 30,000 years ago, far older than the standard date for the first people in the Americas, and the claim is still argued over.", "The park has a visitor centre and museum at São Raimundo Nonato and guided trails to the shelters. It is a long drive from any flying site, but it is the single most important piece of human heritage in north-east Brazil."], "rel": ["cs-quixada-monoliths", "cs-cariri-fossils", "cs-cangaco"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO: Serra da Capivara National Park", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/606"}, {"t": "Bradshaw Foundation: Serra da Capivara", "u": "https://www.bradshawfoundation.com/south_america/serra_da_capivara/index.php"}]}, {"id": "cs-jangada", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": -3.72, "lon": -38.48, "name": "The jangada raft", "place": "Mucuripe, Fortaleza, Ceará, Brazil", "icon": "dhow", "hidden": true, "hook": "In 1941 four fishermen sailed a log raft 1,650 miles to the capital to see the president, and won.", "body": ["The jangada is the sailing raft of the north-east coast. Traditionally it was six light logs pegged and lashed together without a single nail, with a triangular sail on a mast that can be turned, a steering oar, and a daggerboard pushed down between the logs to stop it sliding sideways. The sail works exactly like a wing: a pressure difference between its two faces pulls the raft along, and it can sail upwind.", "In September 1941 four fishermen led by Manoel Olimpio Meira, known as Jacaré, left Fortaleza on the jangada São Pedro to protest a system in which raft owners took half the catch and fishermen had no pension or medical care. They sailed 61 days and 1,650 miles down the coast to Rio de Janeiro, then the capital, with no instruments. President Getúlio Vargas received them and signed a law giving jangadeiros the same benefits as unionised workers.", "Orson Welles filmed the story in 1942 for It's All True, and on 19 May 1942, while a launch towed the São Pedro for the Rio arrival scene, the raft overturned and Jacaré was lost at sea. The film was never finished, but the footage was found in 1981. Rafts still race off Mucuripe in Fortaleza, though most are now built from sawn planks rather than logs."], "rel": ["cs-jericoacoara", "cs-cangaco", "cs-quixada-records"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Jangada", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jangada"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: It's All True (film)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/It%27s_All_True_(film)"}]}, {"id": "cs-cariri-fossils", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -7.19, "lon": -39.74, "name": "Araripe fossil beds", "place": "Santana do Cariri, Ceará, Brazil, UNESCO Global Geopark", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "The best-preserved pterosaurs on Earth came out of a limestone plateau three hours south of Quixadá.", "body": ["The Araripe Basin covers about 8,000 square kilometres where Ceará, Piauí and Pernambuco meet, around the towns of Crato, Juazeiro do Norte and Santana do Cariri. Its Crato and Santana formations were laid down in the Early Cretaceous, in the Aptian and Albian stages, roughly 100 to 125 million years ago, when the area was a shallow lagoon on the edge of the opening South Atlantic.", "The finely laminated limestone preserved fish, insects, plants, crocodiles, turtles and pterosaurs in extraordinary detail, sometimes with skin, muscle and gut contents intact. Flying reptiles such as Araripesaurus were first described from here, and the basin has given palaeontologists many of the best-known pterosaur species. In 2006 UNESCO recognised the Araripe Geopark, the first geopark in the Americas. The Museum of Palaeontology in Santana do Cariri displays the local finds.", "The pterosaurs matter to anyone who flies. They were the first vertebrates to achieve powered flight, with wings of skin stretched on a single enormously long finger, and the Araripe specimens are the ones that show how those wings were built."], "rel": ["cs-serra-da-capivara", "cs-cangaco", "cs-quixada-monoliths"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Araripe Basin", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Araripe_Basin"}, {"t": "O Povo: Geopark Araripe, first in the Americas, 15 years", "u": "https://www.opovo.com.br/noticias/cariri/2021/09/24/geopark-araripe-primeiro-parque-das-americas-reconhecido-pela-unesco-faz-15-anos.html"}]}, {"id": "cs-cangaco", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": -9.56, "lon": -37.85, "name": "Lampião and the cangaço", "place": "Angico, Poço Redondo, Sergipe, Brazil", "icon": "skull", "hidden": false, "hook": "The outlaw bands of the sertão rode the country pilots now fly over, hunted for twenty years.", "body": ["Cangaço was the armed banditry of the Brazilian north-east. It grew out of family feuds and land disputes in the late 19th century and peaked in the 1920s and 1930s, when bands of mounted men in leather hats and studded jackets raided towns across seven states. The most famous leader was Virgulino Ferreira da Silva, called Lampião, born on 7 June 1897 near Serra Talhada in Pernambuco.", "Lampião took command of a band in 1922 and led it for sixteen years, at times with over a hundred followers. His companion from 1930 was Maria Déia, Maria Bonita. On 28 July 1938 police surrounded their camp at the Angico farm in Poço Redondo, Sergipe, after a tip from an informant, and killed Lampião, Maria Bonita and nine others. Their heads were displayed in the town of Piranhas and were only buried in 1969.", "The cangaceiros survive today in cordel ballads, films and museums, and in the place names of the sertão. Lampião himself revered Padre Cícero of Juazeiro do Norte, the priest whose shrine still draws pilgrims to the Cariri, and the roads the bands rode between Ceará, Paraíba and Pernambuco are the same ones record pilots now follow westward for retrieves."], "rel": ["cs-cariri-fossils", "cs-serra-da-capivara", "cs-quixada-records"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Lampião", "u": "https://www.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lampiao"}, {"t": "eBiografia: Lampião", "u": "https://www.ebiografia.com/lampiao/"}]}, {"id": "cs-wisnierska-storm", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -30.68, "lon": 150.61, "name": "Ewa Wiśnierska and the Manilla storm", "place": "Mount Borah, Manilla, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "A storm cloud lifted a paraglider to 10,000 m over Manilla and she lived; a pilot nearby did not.", "body": ["Mount Borah rises about 500 m above the plains near Manilla, 350 km north of Sydney, with four turfed launches between 820 and 880 m. It hosted the 2007 Paragliding World Championships, and on 14 February 2007, during a training flight before the event, the German pilot Ewa Wiśnierska was caught by a developing cumulonimbus. Her GPS recorded climbs of up to 21 m/s and a maximum altitude of 10,054 m, higher than Everest.", "She lost consciousness in the cloud, froze, was battered by hail and lightning, and came to still under a flying wing. She landed more than three hours after launch, about 60 km north of where she started, with frostbite and ice in her harness. A Chinese pilot, He Zhongpin, aged 42, was pulled into the same storm and killed by lightning.", "The lesson is not that storms can be survived. It is that a pilot must never be near one. Cumulus that grows tall, dark bases, a sudden drop in temperature, gust fronts on the ground and rain curtains are all reasons to land now, and in competition a task should be stopped before the first pilot has to decide. Most pilots who die in clouds flew into them while thinking they still had time."], "rel": ["cs-lake-keepit", "cs-wedge-tailed-eagle", "cs-bushfire-smoke", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Ewa Wiśnierska", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ewa_Wi%C5%9Bnierska"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Guide to Manilla", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-manilla-australia/"}, {"t": "SAFA site guide: Manilla, Mt Borah", "u": "https://siteguide.org.au/Sites/Details/Manilla%20-%20Mt%20Borah"}]}, {"id": "cs-lake-keepit", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": -30.9, "lon": 150.52, "name": "Lake Keepit Soaring Club", "place": "Keepit, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "plane", "hidden": false, "hook": "Half an hour from Manilla, sailplanes fly the same thermals as paragliders, only three times faster.", "body": ["Lake Keepit Soaring Club sits beside the Keepit Dam on the north-west plains of New South Wales, about 40 minutes from Tamworth airport and a similar drive south of Manilla. It is a dedicated glider airfield with few airspace restrictions, open seven days a week all year, with two aerotow tugs and a fleet from two-seat trainers to high-performance gliders. The club hosted the 10th Women's World Gliding Championships.", "The site works for the same reason Mount Borah does. The flat farmland of the Liverpool Plains heats hard in summer, thermals of 3 to 5 m/s are normal, and cloudbase sits at 2,500 to 3,500 m. A sailplane with a 40 to 1 glide can string those thermals together for 500 km and more in a day, while a paraglider on the same sky manages 100 to 300 km. Watching gliders work the same cumulus is a good lesson in how big and how high the lift really is.", "Keep an eye out for them. Gliders cruise at 150 km/h or more between thermals and are white and hard to see. A paraglider pilot sharing a thermal with one should turn the same way and hold a steady circle so the faster aircraft can plan around it."], "rel": ["cs-wisnierska-storm", "cs-warrumbungles", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Lake Keepit Soaring Club: About us", "u": "https://keepitsoaring.com/about-us/"}, {"t": "Soaring Society of America: Lake Keepit, 10th Women's Worlds", "u": "https://www.ssa.org/10th-womens-world-championships/"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Guide to Manilla", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-manilla-australia/"}]}, {"id": "cs-warrumbungles", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -31.33, "lon": 148.86, "name": "Warrumbungle National Park", "place": "Coonabarabran, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A collapsed volcano, a 90 m stone blade and Australia's darkest official sky, two hours from Manilla.", "body": ["The Warrumbungles are the eroded core of a shield volcano that was active about 13 to 18 million years ago. Soft lava flows have gone and only the hard plugs and dykes remain, so the range is a cluster of spires rising from the flat plain west of Coonabarabran. The best known is the Breadknife, a dyke of solidified lava 90 m high and only a few metres thick. Mount Exmouth, at 1,206 m, is the highest point.", "The park was first reserved in 1953 with 3,360 hectares and has grown to over 23,000. The traditional owners are the Gamilaraay, Wayilwan and Wiradjuri peoples. A bushfire in January 2013 burned more than 90 per cent of the park. In 2016 the International Dark-Sky Association named it Australia's first International Dark Sky Park, with gold-tier status, which is why the national observatory sits on its eastern edge.", "The classic walk is the Breadknife and Grand High Tops circuit, 14.5 km and five to six hours, which puts you on the rim of the old caldera looking across the spires. The park has 19 of Australia's 55 parrot species and a population of brush-tailed rock wallabies."], "rel": ["cs-siding-spring", "cs-lake-keepit", "cs-great-dividing-range"], "src": [{"t": "NSW National Parks: Warrumbungle National Park brochure", "u": "https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/-/media/visitor/files/pdf/brochures/warrumbungle-national-park-pdf-brochure.pdf"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Warrumbungles", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warrumbungles"}]}, {"id": "cs-siding-spring", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": -31.27, "lon": 149.07, "name": "Siding Spring Observatory", "place": "Mount Woorut, Coonabarabran, New South Wales, Australia", "icon": "rocket", "hidden": true, "hook": "The biggest optical telescope in Australia sits on a volcanic peak in a park that nearly burned around it.", "body": ["Siding Spring Observatory stands at 1,165 m on Mount Woorut, on the eastern edge of the Warrumbungles. The Australian National University chose the site in 1962 for its dark, dry, high skies, and by the mid-1960s three telescopes were running. In 1969 the Australian and British governments agreed to build the Anglo-Australian Telescope, a 3.9 m instrument that began work in 1974 and is still the largest optical telescope in the country. More than 60 telescopes now share the mountain.", "On 13 January 2013 a firestorm swept over the site. Eighteen staff were evacuated, the lodge, the director's cottage and the fire station were destroyed, and the visitor centre was badly damaged, but every telescope survived. The observatory and the surrounding Dark Sky Park are among the reasons the region has strict limits on outdoor lighting.", "Pilots flying the plains to the east share something with the astronomers: both depend on dry, stable, cloud-free air. The same high-pressure days that give Manilla its blue thermals give Siding Spring its clearest nights, and the smoke that grounds one also blinds the other. The site has a visitor centre and the Anglo-Australian Telescope dome can be viewed by day."], "rel": ["cs-warrumbungles", "cs-bushfire-smoke", "cs-lake-keepit"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Siding Spring Observatory", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siding_Spring_Observatory"}, {"t": "Australian Geographic: Bushfire hits Australia's largest observatory", "u": "https://www.australiangeographic.com.au/news/2013/01/bushfire-hits-australias-largest-observatory/"}]}, {"id": "cs-mount-buffalo", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": -36.72, "lon": 146.82, "name": "Mount Buffalo and the Gorge", "place": "Mount Buffalo National Park, Victoria, Australia", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A granite plateau above Bright with a hang gliding ramp on a cliff 975 m above the valley floor.", "body": ["Mount Buffalo is a block of granite that rises out of the Ovens Valley near Bright and Porepunkah to a plateau at 1,723 m. The park was reserved on 4 November 1898, one of Victoria's oldest, and covers 310 square kilometres of granite tors, snow gum woodland above 1,300 m, and the Gorge, where the plateau breaks off in a sheer cliff. The Mount Buffalo Chalet at the Gorge lookout was built in 1910, soon after the first road reached the top.", "The hang gliding ramp at the Gorge is at 1,380 m and stands 975 m above the landing. It is rated advanced, and the Victorian site guide is blunt: there is no room for mistakes, the cliff is sheer, south-easterly winds throw turbulence off the launch, and paragliders are not permitted under any circumstances. Summer thermals here give climbs and sinks of 5 m/s and more. Pilots land at the Porepunkah airstrip or in the Burrs paddock below, where a powerline crosses the field.", "Paraglider pilots fly from Mystic Hill above Bright instead, and the Buffalo plateau is a goal and a turnpoint for cross-country flights up the Ovens Valley. Top landings on the plateau are forbidden, and the granite holds cold air at night, so the morning valley wind can run down off it before the thermals start."], "rel": ["cs-wedge-tailed-eagle", "cs-great-dividing-range", "cs-bushfire-smoke"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Mount Buffalo National Park", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Buffalo_National_Park"}, {"t": "SAFA site guide: Mt Buffalo, The Gorge HG ramp", "u": "https://www.siteguide.org.au/Sites/Details/Mt%20Buffalo%20-%20The%20Gorge%20HG%20Ramp"}]}, {"id": "cs-great-dividing-range", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -36.46, "lon": 148.26, "name": "Great Dividing Range", "place": "Mount Kosciuszko, Snowy Mountains, Australia", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Australia's spine is 3,500 km long, 300 million years old, and never reaches 2,300 m.", "body": ["The Great Dividing Range runs about 3,500 km from Dauan Island in the Torres Strait down the whole east coast of Australia to Victoria. It is not a single chain but a belt of highlands between 160 and 400 km wide that separates the rivers draining to the Pacific from those draining inland. Its highest point is Mount Kosciuszko at 2,228 m in the Snowy Mountains, and the highest areas of southern New South Wales and eastern Victoria are called the Australian Alps.", "The range is old. It formed over 300 million years ago, in the Carboniferous, when the eastern edge of Australia was crushed against other land masses, and it has been wearing down ever since. That is why the peaks are rounded plateaus rather than sharp horns, and why granite blocks such as Mount Buffalo and Mount Bogong stand out where harder rock resisted erosion.", "For pilots the range makes two very different kinds of flying. On the western side, at Manilla, the plains heat and the thermals are smooth and high. In the Victorian Alps around Bright the valleys are deep, the breezes are strong, and the flying is mountain flying. The barrier also bends weather: westerly winds compress over the tablelands east of Manilla, which the local guide warns is where the air gets rough."], "rel": ["cs-mount-buffalo", "cs-warrumbungles", "cs-bushfire-smoke", "k-valley-breeze"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Great Dividing Range", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Dividing_Range"}, {"t": "Cross Country: Guide to Manilla", "u": "https://xcmag.com/travel-guide/guide-to-manilla-australia/"}]}, {"id": "cs-wedge-tailed-eagle", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -36.68, "lon": 147.1, "name": "Wedge-tailed eagle", "place": "Tawonga Gap, Bright, Victoria, Australia", "icon": "bird", "hidden": false, "hook": "Australia's largest raptor thinks your glider is a rival eagle, and it will come and check.", "body": ["The wedge-tailed eagle is the biggest bird of prey in Australia. Adults span 182 to 232 cm across the wings, and the largest verified bird measured 2.84 m. Females weigh 3 to 5.8 kg, males 2 to 4 kg. The long diamond-shaped tail that gives the bird its name is easy to pick out from a glider, and so is the way it flies: long minutes of soaring without a flap, circling up in thermals in the heat of the day to patrol its territory.", "They regularly climb to about 1,800 m and have been met by glider pilots above 3,000 m. They also attack. A paraglider or hang glider circling in their airspace reads as an intruding eagle, and the bird will swoop, scream and sometimes rake the canopy with its talons. Torn sail cloth is well documented. No pilot has been reported injured, but a strike on the lines or a collapse close to a ridge is a real risk.", "The advice from Australian clubs is to leave the thermal, fly away from the bird's territory, and not to turn towards it. The species was shot and poisoned in huge numbers through the 20th century but is now listed as Least Concern, with numbers back to or above pre-settlement levels."], "rel": ["cs-mount-buffalo", "cs-wisnierska-storm", "cs-great-dividing-range", "ruppells-vulture"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Wedge-tailed eagle", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wedge-tailed_eagle"}, {"t": "SAFA site guide: Mt Buffalo, The Gorge HG ramp", "u": "https://www.siteguide.org.au/Sites/Details/Mt%20Buffalo%20-%20The%20Gorge%20HG%20Ramp"}]}, {"id": "cs-bushfire-smoke", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": -36.73, "lon": 147.31, "name": "Bushfire smoke and flying", "place": "Mount Bogong, Alpine National Park, Victoria, Australia", "icon": "cloud", "hidden": false, "hook": "In a fire summer the sky looks clear from the ground and has no horizon from 300 m up.", "body": ["Mount Bogong, at 1,986 m the highest peak in Victoria, was badly burned in the alpine fires of 2003, and the Bright and Mount Beauty valleys were smoke-filled again through the Black Summer of 2019 to 2020. Fire is part of flying in south-east Australia, and smoke is its quietest hazard. The Australian aviation safety regulator's magazine gives the key fact: a pilot can often see straight down but not sideways, because looking horizontally means looking through kilometres of particles.", "Smoke pools under temperature inversions, in valleys and bowls, especially morning and evening. It blocks the sun, which cools the ground, which strengthens the inversion and traps more smoke. In one case a pilot at Mount Beauty lost the horizon at circuit height with visibility later estimated at 2 km. Without a horizon a paraglider pilot cannot judge bank angle or glide, and in a mountain valley that is how people fly into terrain.", "Big fires also make weather. Rising smoke can build pyrocumulus clouds that grow into thunderstorms with downbursts and lightning, and they are hard to spot through haze. Firefighting aircraft work low and on their own radio frequencies. The safe answer is simple: if fires are active, check the restrictions, keep well clear, and if the horizon is going, land while you can still see the field."], "rel": ["cs-mount-buffalo", "cs-wisnierska-storm", "cs-siding-spring", "k-cloudbase"], "src": [{"t": "Flight Safety Australia: Smoke is no joke", "u": "https://flightsafetyaustralia.com/?p=207674"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Mount Bogong", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Bogong"}, {"t": "CASA: Don't fly near bushfires", "u": "https://www.casa.gov.au/resources-and-education/publications-and-resources/industry-guides-and-publications/dont-fly-near-bushfires-poster"}]}, {"id": "cs-monarch-reserve", "t": 2, "dom": "nature", "lat": 19.59, "lon": -100.27, "name": "Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve", "place": "El Rosario, Michoacán, Mexico, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "Up to a billion butterflies fly 3,000 miles to winter in a few fir forests near Valle de Bravo.", "body": ["Every autumn monarch butterflies leave the north-eastern United States and Canada and fly south, upwards of 3,000 miles, to the same few mountain forests on the border of Michoacán and the State of Mexico. They begin arriving in early November and hang in dense clusters on oyamel fir trees at around 3,000 m until mid-March. UNESCO listed the reserve in 2008. It covers 56,259 hectares and holds 14 known overwintering colonies, eight of them inside the protected core.", "The butterflies that arrive have never been here before. The autumn generation lives up to eight months and makes the whole journey; the return north takes four or five short-lived generations. They navigate by the sun and ride the autumn winds south, a 3,000 mile cross-country made on four wings a few centimetres across.", "El Rosario and Sierra Chincua in Michoacán have the easiest trails and the biggest weekend crowds. Cerro Pelón and Piedra Herrada are on the State of Mexico side, nearer Valle de Bravo. All the colonies are at about 3,000 m, so take the walk slowly, go on a weekday, and visit in the late morning when the sun warms the clusters and the air fills with wings."], "rel": ["cs-piedra-herrada", "cs-nevado-de-toluca", "cs-valle-pwc", "cs-el-penon"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO: Monarch Butterfly Biosphere Reserve", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1290"}, {"t": "National Geographic: Monarch butterflies migrate 3,000 miles", "u": "https://www.nationalgeographic.com/animals/article/monarch-butterfly-migration"}, {"t": "Monarch Joint Venture: Visiting the Mexican overwintering sites", "u": "https://monarchjointventure.org/faq/overwintering/visiting-mexican-overwintering-sites"}]}, {"id": "cs-piedra-herrada", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 19.19, "lon": -99.95, "name": "Piedra Herrada sanctuary", "place": "Los Saucos, State of Mexico, Mexico", "icon": "leaf", "hidden": false, "hook": "The nearest monarch colony to Valle de Bravo is 25 km up the Toluca road, reachable on horseback.", "body": ["Piedra Herrada is the monarch sanctuary closest to Valle de Bravo, about 25 km east on the road toward Toluca at Los Saucos. It sits on the State of Mexico side of the biosphere reserve and is run by the local community, who provide guides and hire out horses for the climb through the fir forest. The trail varies from steep to fairly easy depending on where the colony has settled that season.", "The butterflies are in residence from December to mid-March, and the colony is most active from mid-February, when warming days send clouds of them into the air to drink at streams before the flight north. Because it is close to both Valle and Mexico City, weekends are crowded, so a weekday visit is better. The roost is at roughly 3,000 m, and the walk is a real climb for anyone fresh from sea level.", "For flying visitors the timing is a gift. The monarch season and the Valle flying season are the same months, December to March, and the sanctuary is a morning's outing before the valley wind starts. Go early, carry warm clothes for the forest, and stay on the marked paths; the butterflies cluster low and a careless step can crush dozens."], "rel": ["cs-monarch-reserve", "cs-nevado-de-toluca", "cs-el-penon"], "src": [{"t": "Monarch Joint Venture: Visiting the Mexican overwintering sites", "u": "https://monarchjointventure.org/faq/overwintering/visiting-mexican-overwintering-sites"}, {"t": "México Desconocido: Santuario Piedra Herrada", "u": "https://www.mexicodesconocido.com.mx/el-santuario-piedra-herrada-estado-de-mexico.html"}]}, {"id": "cs-nevado-de-toluca", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 19.1, "lon": -99.75, "name": "Nevado de Toluca", "place": "Nevado de Toluca, State of Mexico, Mexico", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Two lakes sit inside a volcano's crater at 4,200 m, in sight of the Valle launches on clear days.", "body": ["Nevado de Toluca, called Xinantécatl in Nahuatl, is the fourth highest mountain in Mexico at 4,680 m, after Pico de Orizaba, Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl. It stands about 40 km east of Valle de Bravo and dominates the eastern skyline from El Peñón. The last major eruption was about 10,500 years ago, and the summit is a broken caldera holding two lakes, the Lake of the Sun and the Lake of the Moon, at about 4,200 m.", "The mountain is a national park and was long a straightforward day trip, with a road almost to the crater floor. The road is now gated about 2 km below the lakes, and the park was closed indefinitely in August 2025 after an accident, so check the current status before planning a visit. The lakes were a ritual site for pre-Hispanic peoples, who left offerings in the water.", "For pilots the volcano is a weather sign and a boundary. Cloud building on its flanks early in the day warns of overdevelopment in the Valle bowl, and the high ground between the two is part of the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt, the chain that gives Valle its 2,000 m launches and its deep, warm valleys."], "rel": ["cs-el-penon", "cs-piedra-herrada", "cs-teotihuacan"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Nevado de Toluca", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nevado_de_Toluca"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Valle de Bravo", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valle_de_Bravo"}]}, {"id": "cs-teotihuacan", "t": 2, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 19.69, "lon": -98.84, "name": "Teotihuacan", "place": "Teotihuacan, State of Mexico, Mexico, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "temple", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 75 m pyramid in a city of 25,000, burned and abandoned seven centuries before the Aztecs found it.", "body": ["Teotihuacan lies about 45 km north-east of Mexico City, three hours' drive from Valle de Bravo. It was built between the 1st and 7th centuries AD, grew to cover 36 square kilometres with at least 25,000 inhabitants, and was the largest city in the Americas of its time. Around the 7th century it was burned and abandoned. The Aztecs who arrived centuries later gave it its name, 'the place where the gods were made', and did not know who had built it.", "The city is laid out on a grid around the Avenue of the Dead, a straight north-south axis lined with monumental buildings. The Pyramid of the Sun on its east side is 75 m high on a base of 225 by 222 m. The Pyramid of the Moon closes the north end and the Temple of the Feathered Serpent, with its carved stone heads, sits in the citadel to the south. The builders diverted the San Juan river to cross the avenue. UNESCO listed the site in 1987.", "Hot air balloons fly over the pyramids at dawn most mornings, launching from fields just outside the site. It is one of the few places in the world where a passenger can see a planned ancient city whole, from above, the way its builders imagined it."], "rel": ["cs-voladores", "cs-nevado-de-toluca", "cs-cappadocia-balloons", "montgolfier"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO: Pre-Hispanic City of Teotihuacan", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/414"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Valle de Bravo", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valle_de_Bravo"}]}, {"id": "cs-valle-pwc", "t": 3, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 19.19, "lon": -100.15, "name": "Valle de Bravo: Worlds and Superfinals", "place": "Valle de Bravo, State of Mexico, Mexico", "icon": "wing", "hidden": false, "hook": "The lakeside town at 1,850 m has hosted a world championship and three World Cup Superfinals in winter sun.", "body": ["Valle de Bravo was founded in 1530 as a Franciscan mission called El Pino, and the lake that gives it its postcard view is artificial, filled when the Villa Victoria dam was finished in 1947. The town sits at 1,850 m and was named a Pueblo Mágico in 2005. Its winter flying, dry from November to May with cloudbase above 3,500 m, made it the home of the Monarca Paragliding Open and, from the 2000s, the regular venue for the biggest competitions in the sport.", "The 11th FAI World Paragliding Championships were flown here from 23 January to 7 February 2009. Ten tasks were valid, the longest 117 km, and Switzerland's Andy Aebi won with Elisa Houdry of France first among the women. The Paragliding World Cup Superfinal, the season decider for the top pilots in the world, came to Valle in 2011, 2015 and 2022; Honorin Hamard and Constance Mettetal of France won the last of these.", "The town also hosts the El Peñón Classic hang gliding race every February and has a Wills Wing hang glider factory. Competition flying here is fast and crowded. Local pilots warn of hidden power lines in the fields, of launches blowing out around midday, and of the gaggles that gather over the well-known thermal points. A visiting pilot should fly the morning and watch the afternoon."], "rel": ["cs-el-penon", "cs-avandaro-festival", "cs-monarch-reserve", "red-bull-x-alps"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Andy Aebi wins Paragliding World Championships", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=2296"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Paragliding World Cup", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paragliding_World_Cup"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Valle de Bravo", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valle_de_Bravo"}]}, {"id": "cs-el-penon", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 19.06, "lon": -100.1, "name": "El Peñón del Diablo", "place": "El Peñón, Valle de Bravo, State of Mexico, Mexico", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A rock launch at 2,340 m on the volcanic belt, flyable almost every day from November to June.", "body": ["El Peñón, the Devil's Rock, is the main launch for Valle de Bravo, about half an hour south of town on the road toward Temascaltepec. The take-off sits at roughly 2,340 m and the lakeside landing at about 1,790 m, a drop of 550 to 600 m. Local guides claim 330 flyable days a year between November and June, and in the dry heart of the season, December to May, cloudbase passes 4,500 m and flights of 100 km are routine.", "The geology explains the flying. Valle lies in the Trans-Mexican Volcanic Belt, the chain of volcanoes that crosses the country from the Pacific to the Gulf and includes Nevado de Toluca to the east. The belt lifts the launches to over 2,000 m while the valleys between stay warm and sheltered, and the dark volcanic rock of El Peñón heats fast. The result is strong, early thermals off a cliff that faces the morning sun.", "Strength is the catch. The site is calm at dawn and dusk and can be launched in nil wind, but from February until the rains the air is rough and the wind strong almost all day, and the daytime landing is turbulent. Avoid the saddle behind the rock, expect hidden power lines in the fields, and in the rainy season watch the clouds: afternoons overdevelop fast."], "rel": ["cs-valle-pwc", "cs-nevado-de-toluca", "cs-monarch-reserve", "k-thermal"], "src": [{"t": "Cross Country: Valle de Bravo guide", "u": "https://xcmag.com/?p=38844"}, {"t": "APPI: El Peñón flight site", "u": "https://flyappi.org/flight_site/732"}]}, {"id": "cs-avandaro-festival", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 19.16, "lon": -100.11, "name": "Avándaro 1971", "place": "Avándaro, Valle de Bravo, State of Mexico, Mexico", "icon": "home", "hidden": true, "hook": "Mexico's Woodstock happened by the lake at Valle, and the government made sure it never happened again.", "body": ["On 11 and 12 September 1971 a car race meeting beside Lake Avándaro, in the hamlet of Tenantongo just outside Valle de Bravo, turned into the biggest rock festival Mexico had ever seen. The Festival de Rock y Ruedas was planned to revive the Avándaro road race with 12 bands as a sideshow. Somewhere between 100,000 and 500,000 people arrived, the race was cancelled on the Saturday morning, and 18 acts played through the night in the rain.", "The reaction was swift. President Luis Echeverría condemned it, the state governor said the organisers had permission for a sporting event and not a concert, and a senator declared there would be no more Avándaros in the Republic. Songs about the festival were banned from radio, the magazine Piedra Rodante was shut down in early 1972, and Mexican rock was pushed into illegal shows in abandoned warehouses for a decade.", "There is no monument. The golf club is still there, the lake still fills the valley, and the landing fields where paragliders come down each winter are a few kilometres from the spot. Ask an older local about Avándaro and you will get a story."], "rel": ["cs-valle-pwc", "cs-el-penon", "cs-teotihuacan"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Festival Rock y Ruedas de Avándaro", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Festival_Rock_y_Ruedas_de_Av%C3%A1ndaro"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Valle de Bravo", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valle_de_Bravo"}]}, {"id": "cs-voladores", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 20.45, "lon": -97.32, "name": "Voladores de Papantla", "place": "Papantla, Veracruz, Mexico, UNESCO Intangible Heritage", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "Four men fall from a 30 m pole on unwinding ropes, 52 turns, as they have for centuries.", "body": ["The Danza de los Voladores is a flying ritual from the Gulf coast of Mexico. Five men climb a pole about 30 m high. Four tie ropes to their waists and launch backwards from a small frame at the top, and as the frame turns the ropes unwind and lower them head first in slow circles to the ground. The fifth, the caporal, stays on top playing a flute and a small drum, turning to salute the four directions, beginning with the east.", "Each rope winds 13 times round the pole, so the four flyers make 52 turns together, matching the 52-year cycle of the Mesoamerican calendar. The flyers stand for the four directions and the elements earth, air, fire and water; the caporal is the sun. The ritual is older than the Totonac people who keep it today and is thought to have begun as a plea to end a drought. The pole itself is a tree chosen in the forest with offerings to the mountain god before it is cut.", "UNESCO inscribed the ceremony on its Intangible Cultural Heritage list in 2009, and Papantla in Veracruz, beside the ruined city of El Tajín, is its home. Voladores also perform daily outside the anthropology museum in Mexico City. It is a controlled descent on a line, centuries older than any parachute, and watching it is a strange experience for anyone who flies a harness."], "rel": ["cs-teotihuacan", "cs-valle-pwc", "cs-cappadocia-balloons"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Danza de los Voladores", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danza_de_los_Voladores"}, {"t": "MexPro: The Dance of the Pole Flyers", "u": "https://www.mexpro.com/blog/ancient-ritual-los-voladores-de-papantla/"}]}, {"id": "cs-lycian-way", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 36.26, "lon": 29.32, "name": "The Lycian Way", "place": "Patara, Antalya Province, Turkey", "icon": "runner", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 500 km footpath through the ruins of an ancient federation starts above the Ölüdeniz landing.", "body": ["The Lycian Way begins at Ovacık, on the hill above Ölüdeniz, and runs south and east along the Mediterranean coast to the edge of Antalya. It was conceived by Kate Clow, a British expatriate, who had it waymarked by volunteers and opened in 1999. Red and white paint stripes mark the route every 50 to 100 m, and a red cross means do not enter. Most sources give the length as a little over 500 km, though later extensions push it toward 760 km.", "Lycia was a league of independent cities in the first millennium BC, and the trail links their ruins: Xanthos and Letoon, listed by UNESCO, Pinara, Tlos, Myra, Olympos, Phaselis, and Patara, the league's capital, where the ruins run down to a long beach that loggerhead turtles still use. Rock-cut tombs with carved doors stand above almost every valley. The inland sections climb past 1,800 m over Mount Tahtalı.", "Pilots see the first days of the trail from the air every flight: the path drops from Ovacık to the Butterfly Valley cliffs and Kabak, directly under the Babadağ glide. The Lycian Way Ultramarathon runs from Ölüdeniz toward Antalya in about six days. Walk it in spring or autumn; summer on this coast is too hot for the ridges."], "rel": ["cs-kayakoy", "cs-saklikent", "cs-taurus-geology", "oludeniz"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Lycian Way", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lycian_Way"}, {"t": "Antalya tourism: Lycian Way", "u": "https://antalya.com.tr/en/discovery/activities/must-see/lycian-way"}]}, {"id": "cs-kayakoy", "t": 4, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 36.57, "lon": 29.09, "name": "Kayaköy ghost village", "place": "Kayaköy, Fethiye, Turkey", "icon": "ruin", "hidden": true, "hook": "Five hundred roofless stone houses on a hillside behind Ölüdeniz, empty since a treaty in 1923.", "body": ["Kayaköy lies in a valley 8 km south of Fethiye, on the road over the hill from Ölüdeniz. Until the early 1920s it was Livissi, a Greek Orthodox town of several thousand people with two large churches, schools and chapels climbing the slope. The Greco-Turkish war ended in 1923 with a treaty that exchanged populations: over a million Orthodox Christians left Turkey for Greece and about half a million Muslims came the other way.", "The people of Livissi went to Greece. Muslim families from Greece were settled in their place but did not stay; the rocky valley did not suit farmers used to the plains, and they moved on. The empty town was shaken by a magnitude 7.1 earthquake in 1957 that brought down most roofs, and it has been a ruin since. Around 500 house shells remain, preserved as an open-air museum. Louis de Bernières set his novel Birds Without Wings here.", "From Babadağ the village is easy to find: a grey smudge of walls on the hillside between the Ölüdeniz landing and Fethiye. On the ground it is a half-day walk, and the path from Ölüdeniz over the ridge to Kayaköy is one of the first stages of the Lycian Way."], "rel": ["cs-lycian-way", "cs-dalyan", "oludeniz"], "src": [{"t": "Amusing Planet: Kayakoy, a Greek ghost town in Turkey", "u": "https://www.amusingplanet.com/2016/09/kayakoy-greek-ghost-town-in-turkey.html"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Lycian Way", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lycian_Way"}]}, {"id": "cs-dalyan", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 36.83, "lon": 28.64, "name": "Dalyan turtles and rock tombs", "place": "Dalyan, Muğla Province, Turkey", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 1987 hotel plan nearly ended a turtle beach; instead it gave Turkey a protected coast.", "body": ["Dalyan is a river town an hour west of Fethiye. Boats push through reed channels from the town to İztuzu beach, a sand bar between the river delta and the open sea where loggerhead turtles, Caretta caretta, come ashore to nest from May to October. In 1987 developers planned a luxury hotel on the beach. A campaign by local and foreign conservationists, among them June Haimoff, who lived in a hut on the sand, drew international attention, and in 1988 the beach and its hinterland were declared protected.", "The area is now the Köyceğiz-Dalyan Special Environmental Protection Area. During the nesting season the beach is closed from 20:00 to 08:00, lights are kept off, and nests are fenced. Hatchlings emerge at night and follow the light of the sea, which is why a torch on the beach can kill them. A rescue centre at the beach treats turtles injured by boat propellers.", "Across the river from the town, Lycian tombs from about 400 BC are cut into the cliff with carved temple facades, and the ruins of the trading port of Kaunos, silted in by the river since antiquity, lie a short boat ride beyond. The sulphur mud baths upstream are a tourist ritual."], "rel": ["cs-kayakoy", "cs-lycian-way", "cs-pamukkale"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Dalyan", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dalyan"}, {"t": "Go Türkiye: İztuzu Beach and the Caretta caretta", "u": "https://prod.goturkiye.com/blog/iztuzu-beach"}]}, {"id": "cs-pamukkale", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 37.92, "lon": 29.12, "name": "Pamukkale and Hierapolis", "place": "Pamukkale, Denizli, Turkey, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "A 200 m cliff of white terraces built by hot water, with a Roman spa town on top.", "body": ["Pamukkale means 'cotton castle'. Hot springs rise at the top of a cliff almost 200 m high above the Menderes valley, and as the water cools and loses carbon dioxide it drops calcium carbonate. Over thousands of years this has built a cascade of white travertine basins, some less than a metre high and others about 6 m, with stalactite fringes and petrified waterfalls. Fresh deposits keep the surface a dazzling white.", "The kings of Pergamon founded the spa town of Hierapolis at the top of the terraces at the end of the 2nd century BC, and the Romans enlarged it with a theatre, baths, a large necropolis and a hydraulic system that carried the water some 70 km. Fallen columns lie in the warm sacred pool, and visitors now swim among the marble. UNESCO listed Hierapolis-Pamukkale in 1988.", "Shoes are banned on the terraces and the water is channelled in rotation to keep them white. Paragliders fly tandems over the site from a ridge above the town, so the travertines can be seen from the air as well as from the pools. It is about three hours' drive north of Fethiye."], "rel": ["cs-cappadocia-balloons", "cs-dalyan", "cs-taurus-geology"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO: Hierapolis-Pamukkale", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/485"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Taurus Mountains", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurus_Mountains"}]}, {"id": "cs-cappadocia-balloons", "t": 2, "dom": "aviation", "lat": 38.64, "lon": 34.83, "name": "Cappadocia balloons", "place": "Göreme, Nevşehir, Turkey, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "balloon", "hidden": false, "hook": "Around 150 balloons lift off together at dawn over a valley of rock churches and chimneys of volcanic ash.", "body": ["Cappadocia is a plateau of soft volcanic tuff laid down by the eruptions of Erciyes Dağ, 3,916 m, and Hasan Dağ, 3,253 m. Rain and wind have cut it into cones, ridges and pinnacles called fairy chimneys, and people have been carving homes into the rock for at least sixteen centuries. Monks cut cells here from the 4th century, the painted churches of Göreme date from the 10th, and the underground towns of Kaymaklı and Derinkuyu sheltered whole communities. UNESCO listed Göreme National Park and the rock sites in 1985.", "The ballooning is a product of the geography. The valleys are sheltered, the dawn air is still, and the ground is a sculpture garden, so a balloon drifting at tree height sees more than any aircraft. By 2015 nearly half a million passengers flew in a year on 266 flying days, and recent seasons have passed 750,000. About 160 balloons from 27 companies work the area and up to 150 can be aloft on a good morning.", "Flights are early, usually before sunrise, and the civil aviation authority cancels them whenever wind or visibility is marginal; in 2015, 99 days were lost to weather. That caution is the point. A balloon cannot steer, only climb and sink into different winds, and 150 of them in one valley is a crowd that only works in calm air."], "rel": ["cs-teotihuacan", "cs-pamukkale", "cs-mount-nemrut", "montgolfier"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO: Göreme National Park and the Rock Sites of Cappadocia", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/357"}, {"t": "Hürriyet Daily News: Half a million flew in Cappadocia balloons in 2015", "u": "https://www.hurriyetdailynews.com/amp/half-a-million-people-flew-in-cappadocia-balloons-in-2015-96231"}, {"t": "Daily Sabah: Cappadocia's balloon boom continues", "u": "https://www.dailysabah.com/life/fairy-chimneys-from-above-cappadocias-balloon-boom-continues/news"}]}, {"id": "cs-mount-nemrut", "t": 3, "dom": "heritage", "lat": 37.98, "lon": 38.74, "name": "Mount Nemrut", "place": "Nemrut Dağ, Adıyaman, Turkey, UNESCO World Heritage", "icon": "temple", "hidden": false, "hook": "A king built a 50 m gravel mountain on a 2,150 m summit and ringed it with stone gods.", "body": ["Nemrut Dağ is a summit of about 2,150 m in the eastern Taurus, in Adıyaman province, a long day's drive east of Cappadocia. On top of it Antiochus I of Commagene, who reigned from 69 to 34 BC over a small kingdom squeezed between Rome and Parthia, built his own tomb: a tumulus of crushed stone 50 m high and 145 m across, originally perhaps 60 m. Nobody has found the burial chamber inside.", "Terraces on the east and west sides carry rows of giant seated limestone statues of Antiochus beside Greek and Persian gods, with lions and eagles for guards. Earthquakes have thrown the heads to the ground, where they sit in a line at the statues' feet, staring across the terraces. A relief on the west terrace shows a lion with stars and planets, read as a horoscope of the day the monument was founded. UNESCO listed the site in 1987.", "The road ends below the summit and visitors walk the last stretch for sunrise or sunset, when the stone glows. It is cold up there even in summer, and snow closes the site in winter. The tumulus is slowly shrinking under weathering and foot traffic, so stay on the paths."], "rel": ["cs-cappadocia-balloons", "cs-taurus-geology", "cs-pamukkale"], "src": [{"t": "UNESCO: Nemrut Dağ", "u": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/448"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Taurus Mountains", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurus_Mountains"}]}, {"id": "cs-taurus-geology", "t": 3, "dom": "nature", "lat": 36.55, "lon": 29.6, "name": "Taurus Mountains", "place": "Akdağ, Lycian Taurus, Turkey", "icon": "mountain", "hidden": false, "hook": "Babadağ is one tooth in a limestone wall raised by Africa pushing into Europe; hence the flying.", "body": ["The Taurus Mountains curve across southern Turkey from the lakes near Eğirdir in the west to the headwaters of the Euphrates and Tigris in the east. They are part of the Alpide belt, the same chain as the Alps and the Himalaya, and were pushed up by the collision of the African and Eurasian plates. The rock is overwhelmingly limestone laid down in the Mesozoic sea that lay between the two continents. The highest peak is Demirkazık, 3,756 m, in the central Taurus.", "The western, Lycian, section behind Fethiye is a block of Mesozoic limestone broken by steep faults, and Akdağ, just over 3,000 m, is its roof. Ice carved its summits above about 2,300 m in the last glaciation, and water has dissolved the rest into karst: sinkholes, dry valleys, underground rivers, and some of the largest caves in Asia further east in the Aladağlar and Bolkar ranges. Saklıkent Gorge is one of these cuts.", "Pilots feel the geology every day. Babadağ is a 1,969 m limestone peak standing straight out of the sea, so the sea breeze meets it with nothing in the way and the thermals off its pale, bare upper slopes are strong and early. The same steepness means the lee of every ridge is rough, and the faulted valleys funnel wind. Height here comes from the rock, and so does the turbulence."], "rel": ["cs-saklikent", "cs-lycian-way", "cs-mount-nemrut", "oludeniz"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Taurus Mountains", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurus_Mountains"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Akdağlar", "u": "https://www.wikipedia.com/wiki/Akda%C4%9Flar"}]}, {"id": "cs-saklikent", "t": 4, "dom": "nature", "lat": 36.47, "lon": 29.4, "name": "Saklıkent Gorge", "place": "Saklıkent National Park, Seydikemer, Turkey", "icon": "wave", "hidden": false, "hook": "An 18 km slot in the limestone, 300 m deep and cold with Taurus snowmelt even in August.", "body": ["Saklıkent, 'the hidden city', is a gorge cut into the limestone of the western Taurus about 50 km from Fethiye. It is 18 km long and up to 300 m deep, and in places the walls close to a few metres apart so that the sun never reaches the floor. The river at the bottom is snowmelt from the mountains above, cold and fast in spring and a relief from the heat in summer. The gorge became a national park on 6 June 1996 and covers 1,643 hectares.", "Visitors enter on a boardwalk bolted to the cliff, cross the main spring where the river surges out of the rock, and then wade upstream. About 4 km is walkable once the water drops after April; the full length needs ropes and canyoning experience. Flash floods are the danger, and the gorge should be left alone after heavy rain in the mountains, even on a sunny day at the entrance.", "It is a half-day trip from Ölüdeniz and sits a few kilometres from the ruins of Tlos, one of the Lycian cities, which makes a good pairing. Pilots on long flights east from Babadağ can sometimes pick out the slot as a dark line in the pale rock."], "rel": ["cs-taurus-geology", "cs-lycian-way", "cs-dalyan"], "src": [{"t": "Wikipedia: Saklıkent National Park", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakl%C4%B1kent_National_Park"}, {"t": "Wikipedia: Taurus Mountains", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taurus_Mountains"}]}]);
